## ⚡ Short Answer
In ASP.NET Core's built-in DI container, **Singleton** creates one instance for the entire application lifetime, **Scoped** creates one instance per HTTP request (or per scope), and **Transient** creates a new instance every time the service is requested. Choosing the wrong lifetime is one of the most common sources of subtle bugs in .NET applications.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Service lifetimes control how long a dependency injection container holds onto and reuses instances of registered services.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without lifetime management, you'd either waste memory creating thousands of redundant objects, or accidentally share stateful objects across requests, causing race conditions and data leaks between users.

**How it works internally:** The DI container (IServiceProvider) maintains internal dictionaries keyed by service type. Singleton instances are stored at the root container level. Scoped instances are stored in a child container (IServiceScope) created per request — ASP.NET Core middleware creates and disposes this scope automatically per HTTP request. Transient services are newed up on every Resolve call and not tracked (unless they implement IDisposable, in which case the scope disposes them).

**Benefits:**
- Singleton: lowest allocation cost, ideal for stateless or thread-safe shared services (caches, config)
- Scoped: safe per-request state sharing (e.g. DbContext, Unit of Work), automatically cleaned up
- Transient: safest for stateful/non-thread-safe lightweight objects; no shared state risk

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Singleton holding a Scoped dependency = "captive dependency" bug (scope leaks into singleton lifetime)
- Transient IDisposable objects held by root container are never disposed (memory leak risk)
- Scoped services cannot be safely used outside of a scope (e.g. in background threads) without explicitly creating one

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// --- Registration (Program.cs / Startup.cs) ---
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IConfigService, ConfigService>();   // one instance ever
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();        // one per HTTP request
builder.Services.AddTransient<IEmailFormatter, EmailFormatter>(); // new every time

// --- Service Implementations ---

// SINGLETON: Must be thread-safe. Shared across ALL requests.
public class ConfigService : IConfigService
{
    private readonly Dictionary<string, string> _cache = new();
    // Fine here: populated once, read-only after that
    public string Get(string key) => _cache.TryGetValue(key, out var v) ? v : string.Empty;
}

// SCOPED: One instance per request. Safe to hold request-level state.
public class OrderService : IOrderService
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db; // DbContext is also Scoped — safe
    public OrderService(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;
    public Task<Order> GetOrderAsync(int id) => _db.Orders.FindAsync(id).AsTask();
}

// TRANSIENT: New instance every resolve. Keep lightweight; avoid heavy constructors.
public class EmailFormatter : IEmailFormatter
{
    // Stateful per-call formatting — transient is correct here
    public string Format(string to, string body) => $"To: {to}\n\n{body}";
}

// --- ❌ CAPTIVE DEPENDENCY (Singleton capturing Scoped) ---
// This will throw at runtime in development (scope validation is on by default):
// builder.Services.AddSingleton<IBadService, BadService>();
// public class BadService(IOrderService orders) { } // IOrderService is Scoped — DANGER

// --- ✅ Correct: Create a scope manually in a background service ---
public class BackgroundWorker : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly IServiceScopeFactory _scopeFactory;
    public BackgroundWorker(IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory) => _scopeFactory = scopeFactory;

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await using var scope = _scopeFactory.CreateAsyncScope();
        var orderService = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IOrderService>();
        // Now safe to use scoped service inside a singleton-lifetime background service
    }
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: What is a "captive dependency" and why is it dangerous?** A: It's when a longer-lived service (e.g. Singleton) depends on a shorter-lived one (e.g. Scoped). The Scoped instance gets trapped in the Singleton for the app's lifetime, causing stale data, connection leaks, and cross-request contamination. ASP.NET Core throws an InvalidOperationException at startup in Development to catch this.
- **Q: How do you safely use a Scoped service from a Singleton (e.g. in IHostedService)?** A: Inject IServiceScope
