## ⚡ Short Answer
`AsNoTracking()` tells EF Core to fetch entities without registering them in the DbContext's change tracker, making reads faster and less memory-intensive. Use tracked queries when you intend to modify and save entities back to the database; use `AsNoTracking()` for read-only scenarios like API responses, reports, or lookups where you never call `SaveChanges()` on those entities.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A LINQ extension method in Entity Framework Core that bypasses the change tracking mechanism for a specific query. Tracked queries register every loaded entity in the `ChangeTracker`, while `AsNoTracking()` queries return plain, detached entity instances.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** EF Core's change tracker snapshots every entity it loads so it can detect mutations and generate the correct `UPDATE` SQL on `SaveChanges()`. This snapshot work has real CPU and memory cost — for read-only workloads (dashboards, API GET endpoints, reports) you're paying that cost for zero benefit.

**How it works internally:** When a query runs without `AsNoTracking()`, EF Core calls `ChangeTracker.TrackGraph()` on each result, creating an `EntityEntry` that stores the original property values. On `SaveChanges()`, EF compares current vs. original values to detect what changed. With `AsNoTracking()`, none of that graph-walking or snapshotting happens — EF materializes the POCO and returns it immediately. Identity resolution (deduplication of the same entity appearing in multiple result rows) is also skipped by default, which further speeds things up.

**Benefits:** Noticeably faster queries (typically 20–40% in benchmarks for large result sets), lower memory pressure, no risk of accidentally persisting a "read" entity, simpler reasoning in read-heavy services.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** You cannot call `SaveChanges()` on a no-tracking entity without first attaching it (and specifying its state). Lazy loading navigation properties won't work (they rely on proxy tracking). Identity resolution is skipped, so the same row referenced by multiple relations will produce duplicate in-memory objects, which can surprise you if you expect reference equality.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

public class OrderService
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    public OrderService(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    // ✅ READ-ONLY: use AsNoTracking() — no SaveChanges() needed
    public async Task<List<OrderDto>> GetRecentOrdersAsync(int customerId)
    {
        return await _db.Orders
            .AsNoTracking()                          // skip change tracker overhead
            .Where(o => o.CustomerId == customerId)
            .OrderByDescending(o => o.CreatedAt)
            .Take(50)
            .Select(o => new OrderDto              // project to DTO = even better
            {
                Id       = o.Id,
                Total    = o.Total,
                Status   = o.Status,
                Created  = o.CreatedAt
            })
            .ToListAsync();
    }

    // ✅ WRITE: tracked query — EF must detect the change
    public async Task UpdateOrderStatusAsync(int orderId, string newStatus)
    {
        // No AsNoTracking here — EF tracks the entity so it can generate UPDATE
        var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(orderId);
        if (order is null) return;

        order.Status = newStatus;          // change tracker detects this mutation
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();      // generates: UPDATE Orders SET Status=... WHERE Id=...
    }

    // ✅ GLOBAL default: set AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution for read-heavy contexts
    // In DbContext constructor or factory:
    //   optionsBuilder.UseQueryTrackingBehavior(QueryTrackingBehavior.NoTracking);
    // Then opt IN to tracking only where you need writes:
    //   _db.Orders.AsTracking().FirstAsync(...)

    // ⚠️ WRONG: reading with no-tracking then trying to save (will throw or silently do nothing)
    public async Task BadExampleAsync(int orderId)
    {
        var order = await _db.Orders
            .AsNoTracking()
            .FirstAsync(o => o.Id == orderId);

        order.Status = "Cancelled";
        // SaveChanges() won't update this — entity is detached!
        // You'd need: _db.Orders.Update(order) first (issues a blind UPDATE for ALL columns)
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); // ← dangerous if you forgot .Update()
    }
}
```

