## ⚡ Short Answer
`IEnumerable<T>` executes queries in memory on the client side, pulling all data first and then filtering it with LINQ-to-Objects. `IQueryable<T>` builds an expression tree that is translated into a native query (e.g., SQL) and executed on the data source, so filtering, sorting, and paging happen server-side. Choosing the wrong one can mean the difference between fetching 10 rows and fetching 10 million rows.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `IEnumerable<T>` lives in `System.Collections.Generic` and represents a forward-only, in-memory sequence. `IQueryable<T>` lives in `System.Linq` and extends `IEnumerable<T>` by adding an `Expression` tree and a `Provider` that can translate that tree into a target query language.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without `IQueryable<T>`, every LINQ operator applied to a database-backed collection would force a full table scan into memory before filtering. `IQueryable<T>` was introduced so that ORMs like Entity Framework can compose queries lazily and ship the final, optimised query to the database rather than the application layer.

**How it works internally:** When you chain `.Where()`, `.OrderBy()`, or `.Select()` on an `IQueryable<T>`, each operator appends a node to an `Expression<Func<...>>` tree stored on the object — nothing runs yet. When you materialise (call `.ToList()`, `.FirstOrDefault()`, iterate with `foreach`, etc.), the `IQueryProvider` (e.g., EF Core's `EntityQueryProvider`) visits the tree, translates it to SQL, sends it to the database, and streams the results back. `IEnumerable<T>` uses compiled delegates instead of expression trees, so all filtering is done in the .NET runtime.

**Benefits:**
- `IQueryable<T>` pushes work to the database engine, which is optimised for set-based operations, uses indexes, and avoids transferring unnecessary rows over the network.
- Composability: you can build a query across multiple layers of your application before it is ever executed.
- Supports `Skip`/`Take` as true SQL `OFFSET/FETCH`, enabling efficient pagination.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- `IQueryable<T>` only supports expressions that the provider can translate; complex C# logic (custom methods, certain string operations) will throw a runtime `InvalidOperationException`.
- Debugging is harder — the final SQL is generated at runtime, not compile time.
- `IEnumerable<T>` is correct and simpler for in-memory collections (lists, arrays); using `IQueryable<T>` there adds overhead with no benefit.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

// --- Assume this DbContext and entity ---
// public class AppDbContext : DbContext { public DbSet<Order> Orders { get; set; } }
// public class Order { public int Id { get; set; } public string Status { get; set; } public decimal Total { get; set; } }

public class QueryDemo
{
    // ❌ BAD: Returns IEnumerable<T> — EF Core fetches ALL orders from the DB,
    //         then filters in memory. With 1M rows this is catastrophic.
    public IEnumerable<Order> GetPendingOrders_Bad(AppDbContext db)
    {
        IEnumerable<Order> orders = db.Orders; // immediately loads everything!
        return orders.Where(o => o.Status == "Pending"); // filtered in-memory
    }

    // ✅ GOOD: Returns IQueryable<T> — the Where clause becomes part of the SQL.
    //          Only matching rows travel over the network.
    public IQueryable<Order> GetPendingOrders_Good(AppDbContext db)
    {
        return db.Orders.Where(o => o.Status == "Pending"); // no DB call yet
    }

    // ✅ COMPOSABLE: Build query across layers, materialise once at the end.
    public async Task<List<Order>> GetPagedHighValuePendingOrders(
        AppDbContext db, int page, int pageSize)
    {
        IQueryable<Order> query = db.Orders                  // SQL: FROM Orders
            .Where(o => o.Status == "Pending")               // SQL: WHERE Status = 'Pending'
            .Where(o => o.Total > 1000m)                     // SQL: AND Total > 1000
            .OrderByDescending(o => o.Total)                 // SQL: ORDER BY Total DESC
            .Skip((page - 1) * pageSize)                     // SQL: OFFSET ...
            .Take(pageSize);                                  // SQL: FETCH NEXT ...

        // Single SQL query is sent here:
        return await query.
