## ⚡ Short Answer
`First()` returns the first element and throws if the sequence is empty. `FirstOrDefault()` returns the first element or a default value if empty. `Single()` returns the one-and-only element, throwing if empty **or** if more than one exists. `SingleOrDefault()` does the same but returns a default value instead of throwing when the sequence is empty — it still throws if multiple elements are found.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Four LINQ extension methods on `IEnumerable<T>` / `IQueryable<T>` for retrieving a single element from a sequence.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** They encode your *intent* about how many results you expect, making code self-documenting and letting the runtime enforce your assumptions rather than silently returning wrong data.

**How it works internally:** Each method iterates the sequence. `First`/`FirstOrDefault` stop at the first match. `Single`/`SingleOrDefault` must scan the entire sequence to confirm exactly one match exists — making them potentially more expensive on large, unfiltered sets. With `IQueryable` (EF Core), they translate to SQL `TOP 1` or `LIMIT 1` with appropriate exception handling on the C# side.

**Benefits:** Explicit intent, early failure on violated assumptions, clean code without manual count checks.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** `Single`/`SingleOrDefault` always scan the full set when there is no DB-level unique constraint or index to short-circuit. Overusing `First` when you expect uniqueness hides bugs silently.

