## ⚡ Short Answer
`GroupJoin()` is a LINQ operator that performs a left outer join between two sequences, grouping all matching elements from the inner sequence under each outer element. Unlike `Join()`, it preserves every outer element even when there are zero matching inner elements, making it ideal for hierarchical or parent-child data projections.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A LINQ extension method that joins two sequences and groups the inner sequence results under each outer element, returning an `IEnumerable<TResult>` where each result contains the outer element paired with a (possibly empty) collection of matching inner elements.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Standard `Join()` discards outer elements with no matches (inner join semantics). `GroupJoin()` solves the need for left outer join semantics and hierarchical projections — e.g., "give me every customer along with all their orders, including customers with no orders."

**How it works internally:** The runtime builds a lookup (hash map) of the inner sequence keyed by the join key. It then iterates the outer sequence and for each outer element, looks up all matching inner elements. The result selector receives the outer element plus an `IEnumerable<TInner>` of matches (empty if none). No deferred Cartesian explosion occurs since results are grouped, not flattened.

**Benefits:** Preserves all outer elements (left join semantics); produces nested/hierarchical results naturally; composes easily with `SelectMany()` to flatten if needed; efficient O(n+m) due to the internal lookup.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Returns nested collections, which may be unfamiliar to developers expecting flat rows; slightly more verbose than SQL LEFT JOIN; using it with EF Core translates to SQL LEFT JOIN — ensure indexes exist on join keys or you'll get slow full scans.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

// --- Domain models ---
public record Customer(int Id, string Name);
public record Order(int Id, int CustomerId, string Product, decimal Amount);

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var customers = new List<Customer>
        {
            new(1, "Alice"),
            new(2, "Bob"),
            new(3, "Carol")   // Carol has NO orders — left join keeps her
        };

        var orders = new List<Order>
        {
            new(101, 1, "Laptop",  1200m),
            new(102, 1, "Mouse",     25m),
            new(103, 2, "Keyboard",  75m)
        };

        // ── GroupJoin: each customer paired with their orders collection ──
        var results = customers.GroupJoin(
            inner:            orders,
            outerKeySelector: c => c.Id,           // key from outer (customer)
            innerKeySelector: o => o.CustomerId,   // key from inner (order)
            resultSelector:   (customer, customerOrders) => new
            {
                Customer     = customer.Name,
                Orders       = customerOrders.ToList(),   // may be empty
                TotalSpent   = customerOrders.Sum(o => o.Amount)
            });

        foreach (var r in results)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{r.Customer} | Total: {r.TotalSpent:C}");
            if (r.Orders.Any())
                foreach (var o in r.Orders)
                    Console.WriteLine($"   - {o.Product} ({o.Amount:C})");
            else
                Console.WriteLine("   (no orders)");
        }

        // ── Flattening GroupJoin to simulate a LEFT OUTER JOIN flat result ──
        var flatLeftJoin = customers
            .GroupJoin(
                orders,
                c => c.Id,
                o => o.CustomerId,
                (customer, customerOrders) => new { customer, customerOrders })
            .SelectMany(
                x => x.customerOrders.DefaultIfEmpty(),   // key: DefaultIfEmpty keeps nulls
                (x, order) => new
                {
                    Customer = x.customer.Name,
                    Product  = order?.Product ?? "—",
                    Amount   = order?.Amount ?? 0m
                });

        Console.WriteLine("\n── Flat Left Join ──");
        foreach (var row in flatLeftJoin)
            Console.WriteLine($"{row.Customer,-10} | {row.Product,-10} | {row.Amount:C}");
    }
}

