## ⚡ Short Answer
These five generic collections each solve a different data-access problem. `List<T>` is a resizable random-access array; `LinkedList<T>` is a doubly-linked list optimised for cheap insertions/deletions anywhere; `Queue<T>` enforces FIFO order; `Stack<T>` enforces LIFO order; and `HashSet<T>` stores only unique elements with O(1) lookup. Choosing the wrong one is a classic source of hidden performance bugs.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Five BCL (Base Class Library) generic collections in C# / .NET, each backed by a different data structure and exposing a different interface contract.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** No single data structure is optimal for every access pattern. A random-access array wastes time on mid-list inserts; a linked list wastes time on index lookups; a set wastes time if you actually need duplicates. The BCL ships purpose-built types so you can match the structure to the workload.

**How it works internally:**
`List<T>` — backed by a T[] array. Doubles its capacity when full (amortised O(1) add). Index access is O(1); mid-list insert/remove is O(n) because elements must shift.
`LinkedList<T>` — each element is a `LinkedListNode<T>` holding Value, Previous, and Next pointers. Insert/remove at a known node is O(1); finding a node by value or index is O(n). Higher per-element memory cost (two extra pointers per node).
`Queue<T>` — circular buffer (array) with head/tail pointers. Enqueue and Dequeue are both amortised O(1). Resizes like List when full.
`Stack<T>` — array with a top pointer. Push and Pop are O(1). No random access.
`HashSet<T>` — hash table (buckets of linked entries). Add, Remove, Contains are average O(1). Duplicates are silently rejected. Iteration order is not guaranteed.

**Benefits:**
`List<T>` — best general-purpose collection; fast index reads; LINQ-friendly.
`LinkedList<T>` — O(1) arbitrary insert/delete when you already hold the node; good for LRU caches, undo stacks with splice.
`Queue<T>` — communicates intent clearly (FIFO); safe producer/consumer boundary.
`Stack<T>` — communicates intent clearly (LIFO); ideal for DFS, expression parsing, undo.
`HashSet<T>` — uniqueness enforcement; fastest membership test; set operations (Union, Intersect, Except).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
`List<T>` — avoid when you need frequent mid-list inserts/deletes on large lists.
`LinkedList<T>` — poor cache locality; avoid for read-heavy or iteration-heavy workloads.
`Queue<T>` / `Stack<T>` — limited API; no random access by design.
`HashSet<T>` — unordered; no key-value pairs; hash collisions degrade to O(n) in worst case.

