## ⚡ Short Answer
Both `SortedDictionary<K,V>` and `SortedList<K,V>` maintain key-value pairs in sorted key order, but they differ fundamentally in their underlying data structures. Use `SortedDictionary` when you need frequent insertions and deletions with O(log n) performance, and use `SortedList` when memory efficiency and index-based access matter more, especially for mostly-read or bulk-loaded datasets.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `SortedDictionary<K,V>` is backed by a Red-Black tree (BST), while `SortedList<K,V>` is backed by two parallel sorted arrays — one for keys, one for values.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Plain `Dictionary<K,V>` gives O(1) lookups but no ordering. Both sorted variants let you iterate keys in order and do range-based queries, but each optimizes for different workloads — one for mutability, one for memory and index access.

**How it works internally:** `SortedDictionary` uses a self-balancing BST, so inserts and deletes are O(log n) without shifting elements. `SortedList` stores keys in a sorted array, so binary search gives O(log n) lookups, but insertions into the middle require O(n) array shifting. `SortedList` also exposes `IndexOfKey`, `Keys[i]`, and `Values[i]` for positional access.

**Benefits:**
- `SortedDictionary`: Fast O(log n) inserts/deletes, no memory spikes, handles frequent mutations well
- `SortedList`: Lower memory footprint (no tree node objects), supports index-based access, better cache locality, faster iteration

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- `SortedDictionary`: Higher memory per entry (tree node overhead), no index-based access
- `SortedList`: O(n) inserts/deletes in the middle make it poor for high write volumes; pre-sizing needed for large bulk loads to avoid repeated resizing

