## ⚡ Short Answer
The Singleton pattern ensures a class has only one instance throughout the application's lifetime and provides a global access point to it. In C#, thread-safety is critical because without proper synchronization, multiple threads can simultaneously create separate instances, breaking the pattern's guarantee.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A creational design pattern that restricts instantiation of a class to exactly one object, exposing it through a static property or method.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Some resources should logically exist only once — configuration managers, connection pools, loggers, or caches. Without Singleton, every `new MyService()` call creates a separate object, wasting memory and causing inconsistent state across the app.

**How it works internally:** The class hides its constructor (`private`), stores the single instance in a `static` field, and exposes it via a `static` property. Thread-safety is achieved via `Lazy<T>` (which uses `LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication` internally — a lock on first access) or `double-checked locking` with `volatile`. The CLR also guarantees that static field initializers run only once, making the static initializer approach inherently thread-safe.

**Benefits:** Controlled single access, lazy or eager initialization, reduced memory overhead for expensive objects, consistent shared state.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Introduces global state (makes unit testing hard — tight coupling), violates Single Responsibility Principle if overused, can hide dependencies (prefer DI containers registering as singleton scope in production), and creates concurrency bugs if implemented naively.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ✅ APPROACH 1: Lazy<T> — Recommended, clean, thread-safe, lazy
public sealed class AppConfig
{
    // Lazy<T> guarantees thread-safe, single initialization on first access
    private static readonly Lazy<AppConfig> _lazy =
        new Lazy<AppConfig>(() => new AppConfig());

    public static AppConfig Instance => _lazy.Value;

    public string ConnectionString { get; private set; }

    // Private constructor prevents external instantiation
    private AppConfig()
    {
        // Simulate loading config from environment/file
        ConnectionString = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("DB_CONN")
                           ?? "Server=localhost;Database=AppDb;";
        Console.WriteLine("AppConfig initialized once.");
    }
}

// ✅ APPROACH 2: Static initializer — Simple, thread-safe (CLR guarantee), eager
public sealed class Logger
{
    // CLR guarantees static field initializers run exactly once, before first use
    private static readonly Logger _instance = new Logger();

    public static Logger Instance => _instance;

    private Logger() { }

    public void Log(string message) =>
        Console.WriteLine($"[{DateTime.UtcNow:O}] {message}");
}

// ✅ APPROACH 3: Double-checked locking — Useful when you need lazy + custom lock logic
public sealed class CacheManager
{
    // volatile ensures visibility across CPU cores (prevents instruction reordering)
    private static volatile CacheManager? _instance;
    private static readonly object _lock = new object();

    private readonly Dictionary<string, object> _store = new();

    public static CacheManager Instance
    {
        get
        {
            if (_instance is null) // First check (no lock — fast path)
            {
                lock (_lock)
                {
                    if (_instance is null) // Second check (inside lock — safe)
                        _instance = new CacheManager();
                }
            }
            return _instance;
        }
    }

    private CacheManager() { }

    public void Set(string key, object value) => _store[key] = value;
    public object? Get(string key) => _store.GetValueOrDefault(key);
}

// ✅ APPROACH 4 (Production Best Practice): Register as singleton in DI container
// In Program.cs / Startup.cs:
// builder.Services.AddSingleton<AppConfig>();
// This gives you testability, lifetime management, and DI injection — not global state.

// --- Usage ---
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Both calls return the same instance
        var cfg1 = AppConfig.Instance;
        var cfg2 = AppConfig.Instance;
        Console.WriteLine(ReferenceEquals(cfg1, cfg2)); // True

        Logger.Instance.Log("Application started");

        CacheManager.Instance.Set("userId", 42);
        Console.WriteLine(CacheManager.Instance.Get("userId")); // 42
    }
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Why is `volatile` needed in double-checked locking?** A: Without `volatile`, the CPU or compiler can reorder instructions so `_instance` appears non-null to other threads before its constructor finishes running — causing a partially constructed object to be returned. `volatile` enforces memory barriers preventing this reordering
