## ⚡ Short Answer
The Factory Method pattern defines an interface for creating an object, but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate. It defers object creation to subclasses, promoting loose coupling between the creator and the concrete products it creates. This is one of the core GoF creational design patterns.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A creational design pattern that provides an abstract method (`CreateProduct()`) in a base class or interface, which subclasses override to produce objects of a specific type — without the caller needing to know the concrete class.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** When you have a framework or base class that needs to create objects, but the exact type shouldn't be hardcoded — because it varies by context, configuration, or runtime conditions — the Factory Method lets you extend creation logic via inheritance rather than modification, honoring the Open/Closed Principle.

**How it works internally:** The base "Creator" class calls its own factory method to get a product instance. Concrete creator subclasses override this method to return a specific product type. The product returned always implements a shared interface, so the creator's logic works uniformly regardless of which concrete product was made.

**Benefits:** Eliminates tight coupling between creator and concrete products; new product types can be added by creating a new subclass without touching existing code; promotes single responsibility by isolating creation logic.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Can lead to class explosion — every new product type needs a new creator subclass. If you don't need runtime polymorphism over the creator itself, simpler alternatives (like static factory methods or DI containers) are often preferred.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ─── Product Interface ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public interface INotification
{
    void Send(string message);
}

// ─── Concrete Products ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public class EmailNotification : INotification
{
    public void Send(string message) =>
        Console.WriteLine($"[Email] {message}");
}

public class SmsNotification : INotification
{
    public void Send(string message) =>
        Console.WriteLine($"[SMS] {message}");
}

public class PushNotification : INotification
{
    public void Send(string message) =>
        Console.WriteLine($"[Push] {message}");
}

// ─── Creator (abstract) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Declares the factory method that subclasses must override.
public abstract class NotificationService
{
    // Factory Method — subclasses decide which product to create
    protected abstract INotification CreateNotification();

    // Template method — uses the factory method internally
    public void Notify(string message)
    {
        INotification notification = CreateNotification(); // polymorphic creation
        notification.Send(message);
    }
}

// ─── Concrete Creators ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public class EmailNotificationService : NotificationService
{
    protected override INotification CreateNotification() =>
        new EmailNotification();
}

public class SmsNotificationService : NotificationService
{
    protected override INotification CreateNotification() =>
        new SmsNotification();
}

public class PushNotificationService : NotificationService
{
    protected override INotification CreateNotification() =>
        new PushNotification();
}

// ─── Client Code ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The client works with the abstract NotificationService — no concrete types visible.
NotificationService service = GetServiceForUser(userPreference);
service.Notify("Your order has shipped!");

// Factory selection can be driven by config, DI, or runtime logic
static NotificationService GetServiceForUser(string preference) => preference switch
{
    "email" => new EmailNotificationService(),
    "sms"   => new SmsNotificationService(),
    _       => new PushNotificationService()
};
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: How does Factory Method differ from Abstract Factory?** A: Factory Method uses inheritance — one factory method per creator subclass produces one product. Abstract Factory uses composition — one factory object produces a *family* of related products across multiple types.
- **Q: How does Factory Method differ from a simple static factory method?** A: A static factory method (e.g., `Notification.Create("email")`) is just a convenience method — there's no subclassing, no polymorphism over the creator. The GoF Factory Method is specifically about delegating creation to subclasses via overriding.
- **Q: How does Factory Method interact with Dependency Injection?** A: In modern applications, DI containers often replace the need for explicit Factory Method hierarchies by resolving concrete types at runtime. Factory Method remains useful when creation logic is complex, stateful, or needs to be encapsulated in the creator itself.

