## ⚡ Short Answer
The Abstract Factory pattern is a creational design pattern that provides an interface for creating **families of related objects** without specifying their concrete classes. It groups object factories that share a common theme, ensuring that the created objects are compatible with each other. Think of it as a "factory of factories."

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A creational pattern that defines an abstract interface (`IAbstractFactory`) with multiple factory methods, each responsible for creating a different type of related object. Concrete factories implement this interface to produce a consistent family of products.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** When a system needs to work with multiple families of related products (e.g., UI components for Windows vs. macOS), hard-coding concrete classes causes tight coupling. Abstract Factory enforces consistency — you can't accidentally mix a Windows button with a macOS checkbox.

**How it works internally:** The client depends only on the abstract factory and abstract product interfaces. A concrete factory (e.g., `WindowsFactory`) is injected at runtime and creates all related concrete products (`WindowsButton`, `WindowsCheckbox`). The client never references concrete types directly.

**Benefits:**
- Guarantees product compatibility within a family
- Open/Closed Principle — add new families without changing existing client code
- Single Responsibility — product creation is isolated in one place
- Easy to swap entire product families at runtime via dependency injection

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Adding a new *product type* (not just a new family) requires changing the abstract interface and all concrete factories — this is the main pain point
- Can lead to many classes/interfaces for simple scenarios — overkill for small projects
- Adds indirection that can obscure what concrete types are being used

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ─── Abstract Products ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public interface IButton
{
    void Render();
}

public interface ICheckbox
{
    void Render();
}

// ─── Abstract Factory ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public interface IUIFactory
{
    IButton  CreateButton();
    ICheckbox CreateCheckbox();
}

// ─── Concrete Products: Windows Family ───────────────────────────────────────
public class WindowsButton : IButton
{
    public void Render() => Console.WriteLine("Rendering Windows Button");
}

public class WindowsCheckbox : ICheckbox
{
    public void Render() => Console.WriteLine("Rendering Windows Checkbox");
}

// ─── Concrete Products: macOS Family ─────────────────────────────────────────
public class MacButton : IButton
{
    public void Render() => Console.WriteLine("Rendering macOS Button");
}

public class MacCheckbox : ICheckbox
{
    public void Render() => Console.WriteLine("Rendering macOS Checkbox");
}

// ─── Concrete Factories ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public class WindowsFactory : IUIFactory
{
    public IButton   CreateButton()   => new WindowsButton();
    public ICheckbox CreateCheckbox() => new WindowsCheckbox();
}

public class MacFactory : IUIFactory
{
    public IButton   CreateButton()   => new MacButton();
    public ICheckbox CreateCheckbox() => new MacCheckbox();
}

// ─── Client ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Client only depends on abstractions — never on concrete types
public class Application
{
    private readonly IButton   _button;
    private readonly ICheckbox _checkbox;

    public Application(IUIFactory factory)
    {
        // Factory guarantees button and checkbox are from the SAME family
        _button   = factory.CreateButton();
        _checkbox = factory.CreateCheckbox();
    }

    public void RenderUI()
    {
        _button.Render();
        _checkbox.Render();
    }
}

// ─── Composition Root ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Swap entire UI family by changing one line — client code is untouched
        IUIFactory factory = Environment.OSVersion.Platform == PlatformID.Win32NT
            ? new WindowsFactory()
            : new MacFactory();

        var app = new Application(factory);
        app.RenderUI();
        // Output (Windows): Rendering Windows Button
        //                   Rendering Windows Checkbox
    }
}
```

