## ⚡ Short Answer
The Builder pattern is a creational design pattern that separates the construction of a complex object from its representation, allowing the same construction process to create different representations. It solves the "telescoping constructor" problem by providing a fluent, step-by-step API for assembling objects with many optional or required parameters. Use it when an object requires many configuration options, has complex validation logic, or needs to be built in a controlled sequence.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A creational pattern that uses a dedicated "Builder" object to construct a complex target object incrementally, typically exposing a fluent interface and a terminal `Build()` / `Create()` method that produces the final result.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Constructors with many parameters become unreadable and error-prone (easy to swap arguments of the same type). Named parameters help somewhat, but they can't enforce ordering, conditional rules, or multi-step assembly. The Builder encapsulates that complexity behind a clean, discoverable API.

**How it works internally:** The Builder holds mutable state mirroring the target object's fields. Each setter/with-method mutates that state and returns `this` (enabling method chaining). The `Build()` method validates invariants, constructs the immutable target object, and returns it. A Director class is optional — it encodes common construction recipes using the builder.

**Benefits:** Readable, self-documenting object construction; enforces invariants in one place; produces immutable objects; enables step-by-step or conditional assembly; supports multiple representations from one builder.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds boilerplate (a whole extra class per built type); overkill for simple objects with 2–3 fields; mutable builder state means it's not thread-safe to share across goroutines/threads without synchronization; easy to forget calling `Build()`, leaving an incomplete object.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ── Target: immutable HTTP request object ──────────────────────────────────
public sealed class HttpRequest
{
    public string Method      { get; }
    public string Url         { get; }
    public Dictionary<string, string> Headers { get; }
    public string? Body       { get; }
    public int TimeoutSeconds { get; }

    // Private ctor — only the builder can create instances
    private HttpRequest(Builder b)
    {
        Method        = b.Method;
        Url           = b.Url;
        Headers       = new Dictionary<string, string>(b.Headers); // defensive copy
        Body          = b.Body;
        TimeoutSeconds = b.TimeoutSeconds;
    }

    // ── Nested Builder ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    public sealed class Builder
    {
        internal string Method        { get; private set; } = "GET";
        internal string Url           { get; private set; } = string.Empty;
        internal Dictionary<string, string> Headers { get; } = new();
        internal string? Body         { get; private set; }
        internal int TimeoutSeconds   { get; private set; } = 30;

        // Required parameter passed via constructor to enforce it up-front
        public Builder(string url)
        {
            if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(url))
                throw new ArgumentException("URL is required.", nameof(url));
            Url = url;
        }

        public Builder WithMethod(string method)
        {
            Method = method;
            return this; // fluent — enables chaining
        }

        public Builder WithHeader(string key, string value)
        {
            Headers[key] = value;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder WithBody(string body)
        {
            Body   = body;
            Method = "POST"; // conditional logic lives in the builder, not the caller
            return this;
        }

        public Builder WithTimeout(int seconds)
        {
            if (seconds <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(seconds));
            TimeoutSeconds = seconds;
            return this;
        }

        // Terminal method — validates & constructs the immutable target
        public HttpRequest Build()
        {
            if (Body != null && Method == "GET")
                throw new InvalidOperationException("GET requests cannot have a body.");

            return new HttpRequest(this);
        }
    }
}

// ── Usage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
var request = new HttpRequest.Builder("https://api.example.com/orders")
    .WithMethod("POST")
    .WithHeader("Authorization", "Bearer abc123")
    .WithHeader("Content-Type", "application/json")
    .WithBody("{\"item\":\"book\"}")
    .WithTimeout(10)
    .Build();

Console.WriteLine($"{request.Method} {request.Url} (timeout: {request.TimeoutSeconds}s)");
// Output: POST https://api.example.com/orders (timeout: 10s)

// ── Optional
