## ⚡ Short Answer
The Observer pattern defines a one-to-many dependency so that when one object (the subject) changes state, all its dependents (observers) are notified automatically. In C#, events and delegates are the language-level implementation of this pattern — delegates act as type-safe function pointers, and events wrap them to enforce proper encapsulation so only the publisher can raise them.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A behavioral design pattern where a subject maintains a list of observers and notifies them of state changes, without the subject needing to know who or what those observers are.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Tight coupling — without this pattern, an object that changes state must call specific methods on every interested party directly, making the code rigid and hard to extend. The Observer pattern inverts this dependency.

**How it works internally:** The subject holds a multicast delegate (in C#, an `event`). Observers subscribe by adding their callback method using `+=`. When the subject fires the event with `Invoke()` or `()`, the delegate's invocation list calls every subscribed method in registration order on the same thread. The `event` keyword restricts external callers from invoking or replacing the delegate — only the declaring class can do that.

**Benefits:** Loose coupling between publisher and subscriber; easy to add new observers without changing the subject; supports broadcast communication; built natively into .NET with `EventHandler<T>`.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Subscribers can cause memory leaks if they don't unsubscribe (the subject holds a reference to them). Ordering of notification is not guaranteed across refactors. Can be hard to debug since the call chain is implicit. Not ideal for high-frequency events where allocation of `EventArgs` matters.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;

// --- EventArgs payload ---
public class StockPriceChangedEventArgs : EventArgs
{
    public string Symbol { get; }
    public decimal NewPrice { get; }
    public StockPriceChangedEventArgs(string symbol, decimal price)
        => (Symbol, NewPrice) = (symbol, price);
}

// --- Subject (Publisher) ---
public class StockTicker
{
    // 'event' keyword ensures only StockTicker can invoke this delegate
    public event EventHandler<StockPriceChangedEventArgs>? PriceChanged;

    private decimal _price;
    public string Symbol { get; }

    public StockTicker(string symbol) => Symbol = symbol;

    public decimal Price
    {
        get => _price;
        set
        {
            if (_price == value) return;
            _price = value;
            // Raise the event — notify all observers
            OnPriceChanged(new StockPriceChangedEventArgs(Symbol, _price));
        }
    }

    // Protected virtual: allows subclasses to override notification logic
    protected virtual void OnPriceChanged(StockPriceChangedEventArgs e)
        => PriceChanged?.Invoke(this, e);
}

// --- Observer 1: Alert system ---
public class PriceAlertService
{
    private readonly decimal _threshold;

    public PriceAlertService(decimal threshold) => _threshold = threshold;

    // This method signature matches EventHandler<StockPriceChangedEventArgs>
    public void OnPriceChanged(object? sender, StockPriceChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.NewPrice > _threshold)
            Console.WriteLine($"[ALERT] {e.Symbol} exceeded threshold: {e.NewPrice:C}");
    }
}

// --- Observer 2: Logging ---
public class AuditLogger
{
    public void OnPriceChanged(object? sender, StockPriceChangedEventArgs e)
        => Console.WriteLine($"[LOG] {DateTime.UtcNow:u} | {e.Symbol} = {e.NewPrice:C}");
}

// --- Wire-up (composition root / program entry) ---
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var ticker = new StockTicker("AAPL");

        var alertService = new PriceAlertService(threshold: 180m);
        var logger = new AuditLogger();

        // Subscribe observers using +=
        ticker.PriceChanged += alertService.OnPriceChanged;
        ticker.PriceChanged += logger.OnPriceChanged;

        ticker.Price = 175m;  // Both observers notified
        ticker.Price = 185m;  // Alert fires + log fires

        // Unsubscribe to prevent memory leaks
        ticker.PriceChanged -= alertService.OnPriceChanged;

        ticker.Price = 190m;  // Only logger notified now

        // COMPILE ERROR (demonstrating event encapsulation):
        // ticker.PriceChanged.Invoke(ticker, null!); // ❌ Cannot invoke from outside
        // ticker.PriceChanged = null;               // 
