## ⚡ Short Answer
The Decorator pattern wraps an existing object with new behavior without modifying the original class, adhering to the Open/Closed Principle. Each decorator implements the same interface as the component it wraps, forwarding calls while adding pre/post logic. In ASP.NET Core, it's commonly used to layer cross-cutting concerns — like caching, logging, or validation — onto services registered in the DI container.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A structural design pattern where a "decorator" class wraps a "component" class, both sharing the same interface, so decorators can be stacked transparently and the caller never knows how many layers exist.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Inheritance for adding behavior causes class explosion and tightly couples concerns. The Decorator pattern lets you compose behavior at runtime, keeping each class focused on a single responsibility.

**How it works internally:** The decorator holds a reference to the inner component (via constructor injection). When a method is called, the decorator intercepts it, executes its own logic (before and/or after), and delegates to the inner instance. Multiple decorators can be nested like Russian dolls — each layer only knows about the interface, not the concrete type beneath it.

**Benefits:** Follows Open/Closed and Single Responsibility Principles; behavior is composable at runtime; clean separation of cross-cutting concerns; works naturally with DI containers.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Stacking many decorators adds indirection and can make debugging/stack traces harder to read. Order of decoration matters and can introduce subtle bugs. Not ideal when the behavior truly belongs inside the core class logic.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ── 1. The interface both the real service and decorators implement ──
public interface IProductRepository
{
    Task<IEnumerable<Product>> GetAllAsync();
    Task<Product?> GetByIdAsync(int id);
}

// ── 2. The "real" implementation ──
public class SqlProductRepository : IProductRepository
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    public SqlProductRepository(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    public async Task<IEnumerable<Product>> GetAllAsync() =>
        await _db.Products.AsNoTracking().ToListAsync();

    public async Task<Product?> GetByIdAsync(int id) =>
        await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);
}

// ── 3. Logging decorator ──
public class LoggingProductRepository : IProductRepository
{
    private readonly IProductRepository _inner;   // wrapped component
    private readonly ILogger<LoggingProductRepository> _logger;

    public LoggingProductRepository(
        IProductRepository inner,
        ILogger<LoggingProductRepository> logger)
    {
        _inner = inner;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async Task<IEnumerable<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Fetching all products");
        var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
        var result = await _inner.GetAllAsync();          // delegate
        _logger.LogInformation("Fetched {Count} products in {Ms}ms",
            result.Count(), sw.ElapsedMilliseconds);
        return result;
    }

    public async Task<Product?> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Fetching product {Id}", id);
        return await _inner.GetByIdAsync(id);             // delegate
    }
}

// ── 4. Caching decorator (wraps the logging decorator) ──
public class CachingProductRepository : IProductRepository
{
    private readonly IProductRepository _inner;
    private readonly IMemoryCache _cache;
    private static readonly TimeSpan Ttl = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5);

    public CachingProductRepository(IProductRepository inner, IMemoryCache cache)
    {
        _inner = inner;
        _cache = cache;
    }

    public async Task<IEnumerable<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        const string key = "products:all";
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(key, out IEnumerable<Product>? cached))
            return cached!;

        var products = await _inner.GetAllAsync();
        _cache.Set(key, products, Ttl);
        return products;
    }

    public async Task<Product?> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        var key = $"products:{id}";
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(key, out Product? cached))
            return cached;

        var product = await _inner.GetByIdAsync(id);
        if (product is not null) _cache.Set(key, product, Ttl);
        return product;
    }
}

