## ⚡ Short Answer
The Command pattern is a behavioral design pattern that encapsulates a request or operation as a standalone object, allowing you to parameterize methods with different requests, queue or log operations, and support undoable actions. It decouples the sender of a request from the object that knows how to execute it, turning method calls into first-class objects.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A behavioral pattern where each action or request is wrapped in a Command object containing all the information needed to perform or reverse that action — typically the receiver, method, and arguments.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without it, the code that triggers an action is tightly coupled to the code that performs it. This makes it hard to queue operations, add undo/redo, log history, or support transactional rollback. The Command pattern solves all of these by treating operations as data.

**How it works internally:** Four key participants work together — the Command interface declares `Execute()` (and optionally `Undo()`), Concrete Commands implement it and hold a reference to the Receiver, the Invoker stores and triggers commands without knowing what they do, and the Client creates and configures command objects and hands them to the Invoker.

**Benefits:** Enables undo/redo stacks, operation queuing, scheduled/deferred execution, logging/auditing, and macro recording. Also supports the Open/Closed Principle — new commands are added without changing existing code.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Increases the number of classes significantly (one class per operation). Can feel over-engineered for simple CRUD apps with no undo requirement. Command state must be carefully managed if commands are long-lived or replayed.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// === COMMAND PATTERN — Text Editor with Undo/Redo ===

// 1. Command interface
public interface ICommand
{
    void Execute();
    void Undo();
}

// 2. Receiver — the object that knows how to do the real work
public class TextDocument
{
    private readonly StringBuilder _content = new();

    public void InsertText(string text, int position)
    {
        _content.Insert(position, text);
        Console.WriteLine($"Document: \"{_content}\"");
    }

    public void DeleteText(int position, int length)
    {
        _content.Remove(position, length);
        Console.WriteLine($"Document: \"{_content}\"");
    }

    public string GetContent() => _content.ToString();
}

// 3. Concrete Command — Insert
public class InsertTextCommand : ICommand
{
    private readonly TextDocument _doc;
    private readonly string _text;
    private readonly int _position;

    public InsertTextCommand(TextDocument doc, string text, int position)
    {
        _doc = doc;
        _text = text;
        _position = position;
    }

    public void Execute() => _doc.InsertText(_text, _position);
    public void Undo()    => _doc.DeleteText(_position, _text.Length);
}

// 4. Concrete Command — Delete
public class DeleteTextCommand : ICommand
{
    private readonly TextDocument _doc;
    private readonly int _position;
    private readonly int _length;
    private string _deletedText = string.Empty; // saved for undo

    public DeleteTextCommand(TextDocument doc, int position, int length)
    {
        _doc = doc;
        _position = position;
        _length = length;
    }

    public void Execute()
    {
        // Snapshot before deleting — required for undo
        _deletedText = _doc.GetContent().Substring(_position, _length);
        _doc.DeleteText(_position, _length);
    }

    public void Undo() => _doc.InsertText(_deletedText, _position);
}

// 5. Invoker — manages the command history stack
public class CommandHistory
{
    private readonly Stack<ICommand> _history = new();
    private readonly Stack<ICommand> _redoStack = new();

    public void Execute(ICommand command)
    {
        command.Execute();
        _history.Push(command);
        _redoStack.Clear(); // new action clears redo
    }

    public void Undo()
    {
        if (_history.TryPop(out var command))
        {
            command.Undo();
            _redoStack.Push(command);
        }
    }

    public void Redo()
    {
        if (_redoStack.TryPop(out var command))
        {
            command.Execute();
            _history.Push(command);
        }
    }
}

// 6. Client
var doc     = new TextDocument();
var history = new CommandHistory();

history.Execute(new InsertTextCommand(doc, "Hello", 0));     // "Hello"
history.Execute(new InsertTextCommand(doc, " World", 5));    // "Hello World"
