## ⚡ Short Answer
The Adapter pattern is a structural design pattern that allows incompatible interfaces to work together by wrapping an existing class with a new interface. It acts as a translator between two incompatible interfaces without modifying their source code. This is especially useful when integrating third-party libraries or legacy systems into a new codebase.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A structural pattern that converts the interface of a class into another interface that clients expect, enabling classes with incompatible interfaces to collaborate.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** You often can't modify existing (legacy or third-party) code, but you need it to conform to an interface your system expects. The Adapter bridges that gap without touching the original class.

**How it works internally:** The Adapter holds a reference (composition) to the Adaptee — the class with the incompatible interface. When the client calls a method on the Adapter, the Adapter translates and delegates the call to the Adaptee. In class adapters (via inheritance, common in languages that support multiple inheritance), the Adapter extends the Adaptee directly.

**Benefits:** Enables open/closed principle — existing code is untouched. Promotes reusability of legacy or third-party components. Separates interface conversion logic into a single place.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds an extra layer of indirection and a new class. If overused, can mask poor architectural decisions. Object adapters using composition are preferred over class adapters because inheritance creates tighter coupling.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// === SCENARIO ===
// Our app expects ILogger interface.
// We have a third-party LegacyFileLogger that we cannot modify.
// We create an Adapter to bridge the gap.

// --- Target Interface (what our app expects) ---
public interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message);
}

// --- Adaptee (third-party / legacy class we CANNOT modify) ---
public class LegacyFileLogger
{
    public void WriteToFile(string timestamp, string text)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[FILE LOG] {timestamp} - {text}");
    }
}

// --- Adapter (wraps LegacyFileLogger, implements ILogger) ---
public class LegacyLoggerAdapter : ILogger
{
    private readonly LegacyFileLogger _legacyLogger;

    public LegacyLoggerAdapter(LegacyFileLogger legacyLogger)
    {
        _legacyLogger = legacyLogger;
    }

    // Translates ILogger.Log() → LegacyFileLogger.WriteToFile()
    public void Log(string message)
    {
        string timestamp = DateTime.UtcNow.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss");
        _legacyLogger.WriteToFile(timestamp, message);
    }
}

// --- Client code (only knows about ILogger) ---
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;

    public OrderService(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public void PlaceOrder(string orderId)
    {
        // Client doesn't know or care about LegacyFileLogger
        _logger.Log($"Order placed: {orderId}");
    }
}

// --- Usage / Composition Root ---
class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var legacyLogger  = new LegacyFileLogger();
        ILogger adapter   = new LegacyLoggerAdapter(legacyLogger); // plug in adapter
        var orderService  = new OrderService(adapter);

        orderService.PlaceOrder("ORD-9981");
        // Output: [FILE LOG] 2025-01-15 10:30:00 - Order placed: ORD-9981
    }
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: What is the difference between the Adapter and Facade patterns?** A: Adapter makes two incompatible interfaces work together (1-to-1 translation). Facade simplifies a complex subsystem by providing a unified, higher-level interface (many-to-1 simplification). Adapter doesn't change complexity; Facade hides it.
- **Q: What is the difference between object adapter and class adapter?** A: Object adapter uses composition (holds a reference to the Adaptee) — preferred because it's loosely coupled and works at runtime. Class adapter uses inheritance to extend the Adaptee — only possible in languages supporting multiple inheritance and creates tighter coupling.
- **Q: When would you use Adapter vs. just rewriting the legacy class?** A: Use Adapter when you can't modify the source (third-party libraries, sealed classes, legacy systems with no tests). Rewrite when you own the code, the Adaptee is simple, and maintenance of the extra layer outweighs the risk of refactoring.

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes
❌ Confusing Adapter with Facade — thinking they both just "wrap" things and are interchangeable.
