## ⚡ Short Answer
The Proxy pattern provides a surrogate or placeholder object that controls access to another object, allowing you to add behavior (lazy loading, caching, access control, logging) before or after forwarding requests to the real object. In .NET, proxies appear extensively in DispatchProxy, RealProxy (legacy), Castle DynamicProxy, and framework features like Entity Framework lazy loading and WCF service proxies.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A structural design pattern where a proxy class wraps a real subject and implements the same interface, intercepting calls to add cross-cutting behavior without modifying the original class.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** You often need to add behavior like authorization checks, logging, caching, or lazy initialization around an object you don't own or shouldn't modify. The proxy acts as a transparent intermediary that the caller never needs to know about.

**How it works internally:** The proxy and the real subject share the same interface or base class. The proxy holds a reference to the real subject and delegates calls to it, optionally executing logic before/after each call. At runtime, dynamic proxies (Castle DynamicProxy, DispatchProxy) use IL emission or reflection to generate proxy types on the fly without compile-time boilerplate.

**Benefits:** Separates cross-cutting concerns cleanly, enables lazy initialization, supports open/closed principle, and is the foundation for AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming) in .NET DI containers.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds indirection and potential latency; dynamic proxies only intercept virtual or interface methods; debugging through proxy layers can be harder; overuse leads to confusing call stacks.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ── 1. Contract ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public interface IOrderService
{
    Order GetOrder(int id);
    void PlaceOrder(Order order);
}

// ── 2. Real Subject ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public class OrderService : IOrderService
{
    public Order GetOrder(int id)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[OrderService] Fetching order {id} from DB");
        return new Order { Id = id, Total = 99.99m };
    }

    public void PlaceOrder(Order order)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[OrderService] Saving order {order.Id} to DB");
    }
}

// ── 3a. Static Proxy (Logging + Caching) ──────────────────────────────────
public class CachingOrderServiceProxy : IOrderService
{
    private readonly IOrderService _inner;
    private readonly Dictionary<int, Order> _cache = new();

    public CachingOrderServiceProxy(IOrderService inner) => _inner = inner;

    public Order GetOrder(int id)
    {
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(id, out var cached))
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[Proxy] Cache hit for order {id}");
            return cached;
        }

        Console.WriteLine($"[Proxy] Cache miss — forwarding to real service");
        var order = _inner.GetOrder(id);
        _cache[id] = order;
        return order;
    }

    public void PlaceOrder(Order order)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[Proxy] Invalidating cache and forwarding PlaceOrder");
        _cache.Remove(order.Id);
        _inner.PlaceOrder(order);
    }
}

// ── 3b. Dynamic Proxy via DispatchProxy (intercepts ANY method) ────────────
public class LoggingProxy<T> : DispatchProxy
{
    private T _target = default!;

    // Factory method — DispatchProxy.Create<T, TProxy>() calls this internally
    public static T CreateFor(T target)
    {
        // DispatchProxy generates a runtime type that inherits LoggingProxy<T>
        // and implements interface T via IL emission
        var proxy = Create<T, LoggingProxy<T>>() as LoggingProxy<T>;
        proxy!._target = target;
        return (T)(object)proxy;
    }

    // Every method call on the proxy funnels through Invoke()
    protected override object? Invoke(MethodInfo? targetMethod, object?[]? args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[Log] Calling {targetMethod!.Name} with args: " +
                          $"{string.Join(", ", args ?? Array.Empty<object?>())}");

        var stopwatch = Stopwatch.StartNew();
        try
        {
            var result = targetMethod.Invoke(_target, args);
            stopwatch.Stop();
            Console.WriteLine($"[Log] {targetMethod.Name} completed in {stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds}ms");
            return result;
        }
        catch (TargetInvocationException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine
