## ⚡ Short Answer
The Chain of Responsibility pattern passes a request along a chain of handlers, where each handler decides to process the request, pass it along, or stop the chain. It decouples the sender of a request from its receivers by giving multiple objects a chance to handle the request. This avoids hard-wiring the sender to a specific handler and allows the chain to be configured dynamically at runtime.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A behavioral design pattern that creates a linked chain of handler objects, each capable of processing a request or forwarding it to the next handler in the chain.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without it, the sender must know which specific object handles its request, creating tight coupling. It solves scenarios where multiple handlers may be relevant (e.g., middleware pipelines, approval workflows, event bubbling) and where the set of handlers should be configurable without changing the sender.

**How it works internally:** Each handler holds a reference to the next handler (the successor). When a request arrives, the handler checks if it can process it. If yes, it handles it (and optionally still forwards it). If no, it calls `next.Handle(request)`. The chain terminates either when a handler fully handles the request or the end of the chain is reached with no handler claiming it.

**Benefits:** Loose coupling between sender and receivers; handlers are independently reusable and reorderable; open/closed principle — add new handlers without changing existing ones; supports both exclusive handling (first match wins) and cumulative handling (all handlers participate).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** A request can go unhandled if no handler claims it (requires explicit fallback or logging); debugging long chains can be difficult; performance overhead if chains are very long; ordering of handlers matters and must be managed carefully.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// === Chain of Responsibility: Expense Approval Workflow ===

// 1. Abstract Handler
public abstract class ApprovalHandler
{
    protected ApprovalHandler? _next;

    // Fluent API: allows chaining like teamLead.SetNext(manager).SetNext(director)
    public ApprovalHandler SetNext(ApprovalHandler next)
    {
        _next = next;
        return next;
    }

    public abstract void Handle(ExpenseRequest request);
}

// 2. Request object
public record ExpenseRequest(string Requester, decimal Amount, string Description);

// 3. Concrete Handlers
public class TeamLeadHandler : ApprovalHandler
{
    public override void Handle(ExpenseRequest request)
    {
        if (request.Amount <= 500)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[TeamLead] Approved ${request.Amount} for '{request.Description}'");
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[TeamLead] Amount too large. Escalating...");
            _next?.Handle(request);
        }
    }
}

public class ManagerHandler : ApprovalHandler
{
    public override void Handle(ExpenseRequest request)
    {
        if (request.Amount <= 5_000)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[Manager] Approved ${request.Amount} for '{request.Description}'");
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[Manager] Exceeds my limit. Escalating...");
            _next?.Handle(request);
        }
    }
}

public class DirectorHandler : ApprovalHandler
{
    public override void Handle(ExpenseRequest request)
    {
        if (request.Amount <= 50_000)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"[Director] Approved ${request.Amount} for '{request.Description}'");
        }
        else
        {
            // End of chain — no one can approve this
            Console.WriteLine($"[Director] ${request.Amount} requires Board approval. Request DENIED at this level.");
        }
    }
}

// 4. Client: builds and uses the chain
public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Build the chain
        var teamLead = new TeamLeadHandler();
        var manager  = new ManagerHandler();
        var director = new DirectorHandler();

        teamLead.SetNext(manager).SetNext(director);

        // Fire requests — sender doesn't know who will handle them
        var requests = new[]
        {
            new ExpenseRequest("Alice",    200m,    "Office supplies"),
            new ExpenseRequest("Bob",    3_500m,    "Team offsite event"),
            new ExpenseRequest("Carol", 20_000m,    "Server hardware"),
            new ExpenseRequest("Dave", 100_000m,    "Office renovation"),
        };

        foreach (var req in requests)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"\nRequest from {req.Requester}:");
            teamLead.Handle(req); // Always enters at the top of the chain
        }
    }
}

