## ⚡ Short Answer
The `init` accessor is a C# 9 property accessor that allows a property to be set only during object initialization — either in an object initializer or a constructor — and becomes read-only after the object is fully constructed. It gives you immutability without forcing all assignments into a constructor, making records and immutable DTOs far cleaner to write. It is the middle ground between a mutable `set` and a fully `readonly` field.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A property accessor keyword (`init`) introduced in C# 9 that restricts assignment to the initialization phase of an object's lifetime. After construction completes, the property behaves like a get-only property.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before `init`, you had two bad choices for immutable properties: use `private set` (mutable via internal code) or use constructor-only assignment (which forces verbose constructors and breaks object initializer syntax). `init` lets consumers use the clean `new MyType { Prop = value }` syntax while still guaranteeing immutability post-construction.

**How it works internally:** The compiler emits the setter with a `modreq(System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit)` modifier on the setter method. The runtime enforces that this setter can only be called from constructors, `init` accessors of other types, or object initializer contexts. It is not truly enforced at the IL level alone — the C# compiler rejects calls outside those contexts at compile time.

**Benefits:** Enables non-destructive mutation via `with` expressions on records, supports object initializer syntax for immutable types, works perfectly with `record` types, makes DTOs and value objects clean and safe, and pairs well with serializers like `System.Text.Json` that need a settable path during deserialization.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Reflection can still bypass `init` enforcement (e.g., serializers using `BindingFlags`). Cannot be used on `readonly` structs without care. Older serializers (Newtonsoft.Json < 13) may not honor `init`. Does not prevent the object itself from being replaced (reference reassignment is still possible).

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;

// ── 1. Basic init accessor ──────────────────────────────────────────────
public class OrderLine
{
    public int ProductId  { get; init; }  // settable only during init
    public int Quantity   { get; init; }
    public decimal Price  { get; init; }

    // Computed, fully read-only — init not needed here
    public decimal Total => Quantity * Price;
}

// ── 2. Using object initializer syntax (allowed) ────────────────────────
var line = new OrderLine
{
    ProductId = 42,
    Quantity  = 3,
    Price     = 9.99m
};

Console.WriteLine(line.Total); // 29.97

// line.Quantity = 5; // ❌ CS8852: Init-only property cannot be assigned to

// ── 3. Constructor + init — both work together ──────────────────────────
public class Customer
{
    public string Name  { get; init; }
    public string Email { get; init; }

    public Customer(string name)
    {
        Name = name;   // ✅ allowed — inside constructor
    }
}

var customer = new Customer("Alice") { Email = "alice@example.com" };
// customer.Email = "other@example.com"; // ❌ not allowed after init

// ── 4. Records use init implicitly ──────────────────────────────────────
public record Product(int Id, string Name, decimal Price);

var product  = new Product(1, "Widget", 4.99m);
var updated  = product with { Price = 5.49m }; // non-destructive mutation
Console.WriteLine(updated); // Product { Id = 1, Name = Widget, Price = 5.49 }

// ── 5. Works with System.Text.Json deserialization ──────────────────────
using System.Text.Json;

public class UserDto
{
    public int    Id       { get; init; }
    public string Username { get; init; } = string.Empty;
}

string json    = """{"Id":7,"Username":"bob"}""";
var    userDto = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<UserDto>(json)!;
Console.WriteLine($"{userDto.Id} — {userDto.Username}"); // 7 — bob

// ── 6. Struct with init ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
public struct Point
{
    public double X { get; init; }
    public double Y { get; init; }
}

var p = new Point { X = 1.0, Y = 2.0 };
// p.X = 3.0; // ❌ compile error
```

