## ⚡ Short Answer
Global using directives (C# 10) let you declare a `using` statement once in any file and have it apply across the entire project, eliminating repetitive imports. File-scoped namespaces allow you to declare a namespace for an entire file with a single line ending in `;`, removing the need for a wrapping block and reducing indentation by one level.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `global using` is a modifier on a `using` directive that makes the import project-wide. File-scoped namespace syntax (`namespace MyApp.Services;`) declares the namespace for the whole file without curly braces.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before C# 10, every file repeated the same 5–15 `using` statements (e.g., `System`, `System.Collections.Generic`, `System.Linq`), creating noise and drift between files. File-scoped namespaces existed to reduce the extra indentation level that a namespace block forces on all class members.

**How it works internally:** The compiler treats `global using` directives as if they were implicitly present at the top of every source file in the compilation unit. They are typically placed in a dedicated file (e.g., `GlobalUsings.cs`). For SDK-style projects targeting .NET 6+, the SDK auto-generates a set of implicit global usings (e.g., `System`, `System.Linq`) unless you opt out via `<ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>`. File-scoped namespaces are purely syntactic sugar — the compiler emits identical IL to the block-body form.

**Benefits:** Dramatically reduces boilerplate, keeps files focused on logic, enforces a consistent set of imports across the project, and the file-scoped namespace reclaims one level of indentation for the entire file body.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Global usings can obscure where a type comes from if a developer is unfamiliar with the project's setup. Mixing file-scoped and block-scoped namespaces in the same file is a compile error; you must choose one style per file. Over-use of global usings can lead to ambiguous type references if two namespaces export the same type name.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// File: GlobalUsings.cs  (one file for the whole project)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// These usings are automatically available in EVERY .cs file in the project.
global using System;
global using System.Collections.Generic;
global using System.Linq;
global using System.Threading.Tasks;
global using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

// You can also alias globally:
global using ProjectLogger = Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger<MyApp.Startup>;


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// File: OrderService.cs  — file-scoped namespace
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// One semicolon replaces the opening brace + closing brace.
// Everything below belongs to this namespace.
namespace MyApp.Services;   // ← file-scoped namespace (C# 10)

// No extra indentation level needed — the class sits at column 0.
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly ILogger<OrderService> _logger;   // ILogger from global using
    private readonly List<Order> _orders = new();     // List<T> from global using

    public OrderService(ILogger<OrderService> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    // System.Linq (Sum) and System.Threading.Tasks (Task) come from global usings.
    public async Task<decimal> GetTotalAsync()
    {
        await Task.Delay(10); // simulated async work
        var total = _orders.Sum(o => o.Amount);
        _logger.LogInformation("Total calculated: {Total}", total);
        return total;
    }
}

public record Order(int Id, decimal Amount);


