## ⚡ Short Answer
Required members in C# 11 (the `required` modifier) force callers to set specific properties or fields during object initialization using object initializers. If a required member is not set, the compiler emits an error — this shifts initialization validation from runtime to compile time, eliminating a whole class of "forgetting to set a property" bugs.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** The `required` modifier is a keyword applied to properties or fields in a class, struct, or record, enforcing that any code constructing the type must supply a value for that member in the object initializer.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before C# 11, the only way to guarantee a property was set was through a constructor parameter. This was verbose, especially for types with many optional + a few mandatory properties. Alternatively, developers left it to runtime checks (throwing `ArgumentNullException` in a constructor or method), which meant bugs appeared at runtime. Required members make mandatory initialization a compile-time contract without forcing constructor boilerplate.

**How it works internally:** The compiler emits a `RequiredMemberAttribute` on the type and a `CompilerFeatureRequiredAttribute` on any constructor. When the compiler sees an object initializer, it checks that all `required` members are assigned. Constructors can opt out of this check using `[SetsRequiredMembers]` from `System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis`, signaling that the constructor itself guarantees initialization.

**Benefits:** Compile-time safety instead of runtime null/validation errors; less boilerplate than constructor-only patterns; works naturally with `init`-only setters; improves discoverability — IDEs highlight required members in completion lists.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Only works with object initializers, not with positional construction (unless `[SetsRequiredMembers]` is used). Adds friction when subclassing — derived classes must also satisfy or re-declare required members. Not a full replacement for constructor validation (no logic/conditional checks). Reflection-based creation (e.g., deserialization via `Activator.CreateInstance`) bypasses the check entirely.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;

// --- Basic Usage ---
public class UserProfile
{
    // These MUST be set in the object initializer
    public required string Username { get; init; }
    public required string Email { get; init; }

    // Optional — no required modifier
    public string? DisplayName { get; init; }
    public int Age { get; init; }
}

// ✅ Compiles: all required members are set
var user = new UserProfile
{
    Username = "jdoe",
    Email = "jdoe@example.com",
    DisplayName = "John Doe"  // optional, fine to omit too
};

// ❌ Compile error CS9035: Required member 'UserProfile.Email' must be set
// var broken = new UserProfile { Username = "jdoe" };


// --- Using [SetsRequiredMembers] on a constructor ---
public class Product
{
    public required string Sku { get; init; }
    public required string Name { get; init; }
    public decimal Price { get; init; }

    // This constructor satisfies all required members itself.
    // [SetsRequiredMembers] suppresses the compile-time check for callers
    // using this constructor — the dev promises the ctor sets them.
    [SetsRequiredMembers]
    public Product(string sku, string name, decimal price)
    {
        Sku = sku;
        Name = name;
        Price = price;
    }

    // Parameterless ctor WITHOUT [SetsRequiredMembers]:
    // callers MUST use object initializer to set Sku and Name
    public Product() { }
}

// ✅ Via constructor (required members satisfied by [SetsRequiredMembers])
var p1 = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);

// ✅ Via object initializer (required members set explicitly)
var p2 = new Product { Sku = "SKU-002", Name = "Gadget", Price = 19.99m };

// ❌ Compile error — parameterless ctor without [SetsRequiredMembers]
// var p3 = new Product { Price = 5.00m };


// --- Inheritance ---
public class AdminUser : UserProfile
{
    // Derived class adds its own required member
    public required string AdminRole { get; init; }
}

// ✅ Must satisfy both base and derived required members
var admin = new AdminUser
{
    Username = "admin",
    Email = "admin@example.com",
    AdminRole = "SuperAdmin"
};
```

