## ⚡ Short Answer
Primary constructors in C# 12 allow you to define constructor parameters directly in the class or struct declaration, making them available throughout the entire type body. They reduce boilerplate by eliminating the need to manually declare private fields and write constructor body assignments. This feature was previously available only for records, and C# 12 extends it to all classes and structs.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A syntax feature that lets you declare constructor parameters on the class/struct declaration line (e.g., `class MyService(ILogger logger, string name)`), with those parameters in scope for the entire type body.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Traditional constructors required repetitive boilerplate: declare a private field, add a constructor parameter, write an assignment. For classes with many dependencies (especially DI-heavy services), this added significant noise. Primary constructors eliminate that ceremony.

**How it works internally:** The compiler captures primary constructor parameters as unseen, synthesized fields when they are referenced in instance members. If a parameter is only used in field/property initializers and never in instance methods, the compiler may not generate a backing field at all, optimizing memory usage. Unlike records, primary constructor parameters in classes are NOT automatically exposed as public properties.

**Benefits:** Dramatically reduces boilerplate for DI and simple data-carrying classes, improves readability, works naturally with `required` properties and field initializers, and is fully compatible with additional constructors (which must chain via `this(...)`).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Parameters are not automatically properties (a common gotcha). Mutable capture can lead to subtle bugs if a parameter is reassigned. Debugging can be harder since synthesized fields have compiler-generated names. Overuse in complex classes can hurt readability when the type body is long and the parameter origin becomes unclear.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

// ── 1. Basic primary constructor on a class ──────────────────────────────────
// Parameters are available throughout the class body
public class OrderService(ILogger<OrderService> logger, IOrderRepository repository)
{
    // Primary constructor parameters used directly — no private fields needed
    public async Task<Order?> GetOrderAsync(int id)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching order {Id}", id);
        return await repository.GetByIdAsync(id);
    }
}

// ── 2. Capturing into an explicit field (best practice for mutation safety) ──
// If you need to reassign or clearly signal "this is a field", capture it.
public class PricingEngine(decimal taxRate)
{
    // Explicit capture: makes intent clear and prevents accidental parameter mutation
    private readonly decimal _taxRate = taxRate;

    public decimal Calculate(decimal amount) => amount * (1 + _taxRate);
}

// ── 3. Primary constructor + property initializer ────────────────────────────
public class UserProfile(string firstName, string lastName, string email)
{
    // Parameters used to initialize auto-properties
    public string FirstName { get; } = firstName;
    public string LastName  { get; } = lastName;
    public string Email     { get; init; } = email;

    public string FullName => $"{FirstName} {LastName}";
}

// ── 4. Additional constructors must chain to the primary constructor ──────────
public class ReportGenerator(string title, string author)
{
    // Convenience overload — must call the primary constructor via this(...)
    public ReportGenerator(string title) : this(title, "System") { }

    public string Header => $"{title} — by {author}";
}

// ── 5. Struct with primary constructor ───────────────────────────────────────
public struct Point(double x, double y)
{
    public double X { get; } = x;
    public double Y { get; } = y;

    public double DistanceTo(Point other)
        => Math.Sqrt(Math.Pow(X - other.X, 2) + Math.Pow(Y - other.Y, 2));
}

// ── 6. GOTCHA: parameter captured in instance member creates a hidden field ──
public class Counter(int start)
{
    // 'start' is referenced in an instance method → compiler synthesizes a field
    // That hidden field is MUTABLE — reassigning 'start' anywhere changes it!
    public int Current => start;
    public void Increment() => start++; // mutates the synthesized field — be careful!
}

// Usage
var order   = new OrderService(logger, repo);
var profile = new UserProfile("Jane", "Doe", "jane@example.com");
var report  = new ReportGenerator("Q1 Summary"); // uses overload → chains to primary
var p1      = new Point(0, 0);
var p2      = new Point(3, 4);
Console.WriteLine(p1.DistanceTo(p2)); // 5
```

