## ⚡ Short Answer
Collection expressions in C# 12 introduce a unified, concise syntax using square brackets `[...]` to create and initialize any collection type — arrays, lists, spans, and more — without needing `new` keywords or type-specific constructors. They also support the spread operator `..` to inline elements from existing collections. The compiler translates them into the most efficient code possible for the target type.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A new literal syntax `[elem1, elem2, ...]` that works across arrays, `List<T>`, `Span<T>`, `ReadOnlySpan<T>`, `IEnumerable<T>`, and any type decorated with `[CollectionBuilder]`. The spread element `..otherCollection` inlines all elements from another collection inline.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before C# 12, creating different collection types required inconsistent, verbose syntax — `new int[] { }`, `new List<int> { }`, `Array.Empty<T>()`, `Enumerable.Empty<T>()`. Collection expressions unify all of these behind one minimal syntax and eliminate empty-collection boilerplate.

**How it works internally:** The compiler performs target-type inference to decide what kind of collection to produce. For `List<T>` it calls `Add` or uses a `CollectionsMarshal`-friendly path; for arrays it emits an array literal; for `Span<T>`/`ReadOnlySpan<T>` it can use stack allocation. The spread operator `..` is lowered to an iteration or a `CopyTo`/`AddRange` call depending on the target. Custom types opt in via the `[CollectionBuilder]` attribute pointing to a factory method.

**Benefits:** Dramatically less boilerplate, consistent syntax across collection types, compiler-chosen optimal allocation strategy, works naturally with `var` via target typing, and enables `Span<T>` stack-allocation paths that previously required unsafe code or manual `stackalloc`.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Requires C# 12 / .NET 8+. Target-type inference can occasionally be ambiguous and need a cast. The `[CollectionBuilder]` path for custom types adds some setup complexity. Very large inline literals may hurt readability — in those cases a dedicated factory or data file is cleaner.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Collections.Immutable;

// ── 1. Basic collection expressions ─────────────────────────────────────────

// Array — compiler emits a classic int[]
int[] squares = [1, 4, 9, 16, 25];

// List<T> — compiler calls CollectionsMarshal-optimised path
List<string> fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"];

// Span<T> — compiler may use stackalloc; no heap allocation!
Span<byte> header = [0xFF, 0xFE, 0x00];

// ReadOnlySpan<T> — same benefit, immutable view
ReadOnlySpan<char> vowels = ['a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'];

// Empty collections — replaces Array.Empty<T>() / new List<T>()
int[]       emptyArray = [];
List<float> emptyList  = [];

// ── 2. Spread operator (..) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

int[] odds  = [1, 3, 5];
int[] evens = [2, 4, 6];

// Combine two arrays inline — no Concat() needed
int[] combined = [..odds, ..evens, 7];   // [1, 3, 5, 2, 4, 6, 7]

// Spread into a List<T>
List<int> asList = [0, ..odds, ..evens]; // [0, 1, 3, 5, 2, 4, 6]

Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", combined)); // 1, 3, 5, 2, 4, 6, 7

// ── 3. Immutable collections ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ImmutableArray<T> supports collection expressions out of the box
ImmutableArray<string> tags = ["dotnet", "csharp", "performance"];

// ── 4. Target-typed via method parameter ─────────────────────────────────────

static void PrintAll(IEnumerable<string> items)
{
    foreach (var item in items) Console.WriteLine(item);
}

// Compiler infers the right type from the parameter
PrintAll(["hello", "world"]);

// ── 5. Custom type opt-in via [CollectionBuilder] ────────────────────────────

// The attribute tells the compiler: "use MyCollectionBuilder.Create<T>(ReadOnlySpan<T>)"
[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CollectionBuilder
