## ⚡ Short Answer
`class` is a reference type with full OOP support; `struct` is a value type optimized for small, stack-allocated data; `record class` is a reference type with built-in value equality and immutability; `record struct` is a value type that also gets value equality and with-expression support. Choose based on identity vs. value semantics, mutability needs, and allocation context.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Four distinct type declarations in C# that differ in memory model, equality semantics, mutability defaults, and intended use cases.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before records (C# 9), writing immutable data models required boilerplate `Equals`, `GetHashCode`, `ToString`, and copy constructors by hand. Records eliminate that ceremony. Structs exist for performance-sensitive, small value types to avoid heap allocation. Classes remain the default for complex, identity-based objects.

**How it works internally:** Classes live on the heap; references are passed around. Structs are copied on assignment and typically stack-allocated (though boxed when stored as `object` or interface). `record class` generates a primary constructor, positional deconstruction, `==` using value equality across all properties, a `PrintMembers` chain, and a protected copy constructor for `with` expressions. `record struct` does the same but as a value type — and unlike `record class`, it is **mutable by default** unless declared `readonly record struct`.

**Benefits:**
- `record class` — concise immutable DTOs, pattern matching, structural equality out of the box
- `record struct` — same syntax sugar as records but zero heap allocation; great for hot-path value objects
- `struct` — fine-grained control, no compiler-generated equality magic, explicit mutability
- `class` — full inheritance hierarchy, reference semantics, nullability, DI-friendly

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Records can't be used as base for non-record classes; inheritance chain must be all records
- `record struct` is mutable by default — easy to accidentally mutate a copy thinking you mutated the original
- Structs larger than ~16 bytes can hurt performance due to copying overhead
- Struct arrays of interfaces cause boxing; avoid when performance matters

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ── 1. CLASS ── reference type, identity equality
public class PersonClass
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
}

// ── 2. STRUCT ── value type, manual equality, mutable
public struct PersonStruct
{
    public string Name;
    public int Age;
}

// ── 3. RECORD CLASS (C# 9) ── reference type, value equality, immutable by default
public record PersonRecord(string Name, int Age);
// Compiler generates: Equals, GetHashCode, ToString, operator==, with-expression support

// ── 4. RECORD STRUCT (C# 10) ── value type + record features
public record struct PersonRecordStruct(string Name, int Age);
// Mutable by default! Use 'readonly' to enforce immutability:
public readonly record struct ImmutablePoint(double X, double Y);

// ════════════════════════════════════════════════
// EQUALITY BEHAVIOR DEMO
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════
var c1 = new PersonClass { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var c2 = new PersonClass { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
Console.WriteLine(c1 == c2);        // False — different references

var r1 = new PersonRecord("Alice", 30);
var r2 = new PersonRecord("Alice", 30);
Console.WriteLine(r1 == r2);        // True  — structural/value equality

var rs1 = new PersonRecordStruct("Bob", 25);
var rs2 = new PersonRecordStruct("Bob", 25);
Console.WriteLine(rs1 == rs2);      // True  — value equality

// ── WITH-EXPRESSION (records only) ──
var r3 = r1 with { Age = 31 };      // New record, Alice aged 31; r1 unchanged
Console.WriteLine(r1);              // PersonRecord { Name = Alice, Age = 30 }
Console.WriteLine(r3);              // PersonRecord { Name = Alice, Age = 31 }

// ── STRUCT COPY TRAP ──
var s1 = new PersonStruct { Name = "Carol", Age = 22 };
var s2 = s1;          // full copy
s2.Age = 99;
Console.WriteLine(s1.Age); // 22 — s1 is unchanged; s2 is a separate copy

// ── RECORD STRUCT MUTATION TRAP ──
var p1 = new PersonRecordStruct("Dave", 40);
var p2 = p1;          // also a full copy
p2.Age = 99;          // mutates only p2
