## ⚡ Short Answer
The .NET Garbage Collector divides the managed heap into three generations (0, 1, 2) based on object lifetime, using the generational hypothesis that most objects die young. Gen 0 holds newly allocated, short-lived objects and is collected most frequently; Gen 1 acts as a buffer between short- and long-lived objects; Gen 2 holds long-lived objects and is collected least often. This design dramatically improves GC performance by avoiding full heap scans on every collection.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A generational memory management strategy where the .NET CLR organizes heap-allocated objects into three "generations" (Gen 0, Gen 1, Gen 2) plus a separate Large Object Heap (LOH, also called Gen 3).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Scanning the entire heap on every GC cycle would be prohibitively expensive. The generational hypothesis observes that most allocations are short-lived (e.g., temporaries, request-scoped objects), so collecting only young objects frequently is far cheaper than sweeping everything. This makes GC pauses short and infrequent for the common case.

**How it works internally:**
- **Gen 0** — All new small object allocations land here. When Gen 0 fills its budget (typically ~256 KB–a few MB), a Gen 0 collection triggers. Surviving objects are promoted to Gen 1.
- **Gen 1** — Acts as a survivor buffer. When Gen 1 fills, a Gen 1 collection also collects Gen 0. Survivors of Gen 1 are promoted to Gen 2.
- **Gen 2** — Holds long-lived objects (static data, caches, singletons). A Gen 2 collection is a *full GC* — it collects all generations and is the most expensive. It can trigger Stop-the-World (STW) pauses.
- **LOH (Large Object Heap)** — Objects ≥ 85,000 bytes go directly here and are collected with Gen 2. LOH is not compacted by default, which can cause fragmentation.
- In **Background GC** (default in .NET 4.5+, Server GC), Gen 2 collections are done concurrently to reduce STW pauses.

**Benefits:**
- Short Gen 0/1 collections run in microseconds to low milliseconds, keeping throughput high.
- Full GCs (Gen 2) are rare when objects are properly scoped.
- Compaction of Gen 0/1 keeps memory dense and improves cache locality.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Promoting objects to Gen 2 is expensive long-term — avoid caching large numbers of short-lived references in long-lived structures.
- LOH fragmentation can cause `OutOfMemoryException` even when total free memory exists.
- Gen 2 / full GC pauses can still be disruptive in latency-sensitive apps (e.g., real-time trading, gaming).
- Finalizers cause objects to survive one extra GC cycle, promoting them unnecessarily.

