## ⚡ Short Answer
`IDisposable` is a .NET interface with a single `Dispose()` method that allows objects to release unmanaged resources (file handles, database connections, native memory) deterministically — meaning you control *when* cleanup happens, not the GC. The dispose/finalize pattern combines `Dispose()` for explicit cleanup with a finalizer (`~ClassName()`) as a safety net in case the caller forgets to dispose, following a well-established dual-path cleanup structure.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `IDisposable` defines a contract (`void Dispose()`) that any class holding unmanaged or expensive resources should implement. The full dispose/finalize pattern extends this with a `protected virtual Dispose(bool disposing)` overload and an optional finalizer.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** The .NET GC only manages *managed* memory. Unmanaged resources — OS handles, sockets, COM objects, native allocations — won't be freed by the GC unless you explicitly tell the runtime how to do it. Without `IDisposable`, resource leaks occur silently.

**How it works internally:** When you call `Dispose()`, it invokes `Dispose(true)`, which frees both managed and unmanaged resources, then calls `GC.SuppressFinalize(this)` to tell the GC "don't bother running my finalizer — I already cleaned up." The finalizer (`~ClassName()`) is a fallback that calls `Dispose(false)`, which frees *only* unmanaged resources (because managed objects may already be collected when the finalizer runs). Finalizers run on the GC's finalizer thread, non-deterministically, and add pressure to the GC — so suppressing them when not needed is important.

**Benefits:** Deterministic resource release, `using` statement support, composable with dependency injection, enables safe wrapping of native resources.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Forgetting to call `Dispose()` causes leaks (mitigated by finalizer fallback but not eliminated). Finalizers delay GC collection by one generation. The full pattern is boilerplate-heavy; simpler classes may only need `IDisposable` without a finalizer. Thread safety of `Dispose()` must be considered explicitly.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

// Full dispose/finalize pattern for a class that wraps an unmanaged resource
public class NativeFileWrapper : IDisposable
{
    // Simulated unmanaged handle (e.g. from P/Invoke or SafeHandle)
    private IntPtr _nativeHandle;
    
    // A managed resource that is also IDisposable
    private System.IO.StreamWriter _logWriter;
    
    // Track whether we've already been disposed
    private bool _disposed = false;

    public NativeFileWrapper(string logPath)
    {
        // Acquire unmanaged resource (simulated)
        _nativeHandle = Marshal.AllocHGlobal(256);
        
        // Acquire managed resource
        _logWriter = new System.IO.StreamWriter(logPath, append: true);
        
        Console.WriteLine("Resources acquired.");
    }

    // Public entry point — called by consumer or 'using' statement
    public void Dispose()
    {
        Dispose(disposing: true);
        
        // Tell GC: "Don't run the finalizer — we already cleaned up."
        // This is critical for performance; avoids finalizer queue overhead.
        GC.SuppressFinalize(this);
    }

    // Core cleanup method with dual-path logic
    protected virtual void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (_disposed) return; // Guard against double-dispose

        if (disposing)
        {
            // Safe to clean up MANAGED resources here
            // (we are still in a valid managed state)
            _logWriter?.Dispose();
            _logWriter = null;
            Console.WriteLine("Managed resources released.");
        }

        // Always clean up UNMANAGED resources, regardless of 'disposing'
        // (because finalizer also takes this path with disposing=false)
        if (_nativeHandle != IntPtr.Zero)
        {
            Marshal.FreeHGlobal(_nativeHandle);
            _nativeHandle = IntPtr.Zero;
            Console.WriteLine("Unmanaged resources released.");
        }

        _disposed = true;
    }

    // Finalizer — safety net ONLY. Runs if caller forgot to Dispose().
    // NOTE: Only add a finalizer if you directly hold unmanaged resources.
    ~NativeFileWrapper()
    {
        // disposing = false: do NOT touch managed objects here —
        // they may already be collected.
        Dispose(disposing: false);
        Console.WriteLine("Finalizer ran — Dispose() was not called by consumer!");
    }

    // Guard public methods against use after disposal
    public void Write(string message)
    {
        if (_disposed)
