## ⚡ Short Answer
You should implement a finalizer in C# **only when your class directly holds unmanaged resources** (like raw OS handles, native memory via `Marshal.AllocHGlobal`, or P/Invoke resources) that won't be freed otherwise. In virtually all modern code, you should prefer implementing `IDisposable` with the full dispose pattern instead, and only add a finalizer as a safety net when unmanaged resources are involved directly.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A finalizer (written as `~ClassName()`) is a special method called by the Garbage Collector (GC) before reclaiming an object's memory. It maps to `Object.Finalize()` under the hood and cannot be called directly by user code.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Managed memory is handled automatically by the GC, but unmanaged resources (file handles, sockets, native memory, COM objects) are not. A finalizer acts as a last-resort cleanup mechanism to prevent leaks if a consumer forgets to call `Dispose()`.

**How it works internally:** Objects with finalizers are placed on the **finalization queue** at creation time. When the GC determines an object is unreachable, instead of immediately collecting it, it moves it to the **freachable queue** and a dedicated Finalizer thread calls the finalizer. This means finalized objects survive at least one extra GC cycle (Gen 0 → Gen 1 or higher), adding memory pressure and latency. After finalization, the object is eligible for collection on the next GC pass.

**Benefits:**
- Safety net to prevent unmanaged resource leaks if `Dispose()` is never called
- Runs automatically without consumer cooperation

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Significant GC performance cost — objects are promoted and live longer
- Finalizer thread is single-threaded; slow finalizers block all other finalizations
- Non-deterministic execution — you don't control when it runs
- Finalizers cannot safely reference other managed objects (they may already be collected)
- Almost never needed directly — use `SafeHandle` or existing wrappers instead

