## ⚡ Short Answer
A `WeakReference<T>` holds a reference to an object without preventing the garbage collector from reclaiming it. When the GC needs memory, it can collect the target object even if a weak reference still points to it. They're useful for caches, event listeners, and other scenarios where you want to observe or optionally use an object without being responsible for keeping it alive.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `WeakReference<T>` is a generic class in .NET that wraps a reference to an object without rooting it in the GC graph. The referenced object can be collected at any time if no strong references remain.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Strong references prevent GC collection, which can cause memory leaks in caches, event systems, and object pools. A classic example is event handlers keeping subscribers alive longer than intended (the "lapsed listener" problem). Weak references let you hold a handle to an object without artificially extending its lifetime.

**How it works internally:** The CLR maintains a separate weak reference table. During GC, after marking all strongly-reachable objects, it scans the weak reference table. Any entry pointing to an unmarked (unreachable) object has its slot zeroed out — the target becomes `null`. `TryGetTarget()` checks this slot atomically: if the object was collected it returns `false`; otherwise it promotes the reference to a strong one for the duration of your use, preventing collection mid-operation.

**Benefits:** Prevents memory leaks in caches and pub/sub systems; allows objects to be GC'd naturally; enables memory-sensitive caching strategies (`WeakReference` entries are collected under memory pressure).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** You must always check `TryGetTarget()` before use — no guaranteed availability. Adds code complexity and a small performance overhead. Not suitable when you need the object to definitely survive. Short-lived or high-churn objects are poor candidates since they may be collected before you can use them.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// ── 1. Basic usage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
void BasicUsage()
{
    var data = new byte[1024 * 1024]; // 1 MB object
    var weakRef = new WeakReference<byte[]>(data);

    // Strong reference 'data' still alive → TryGetTarget succeeds
    if (weakRef.TryGetTarget(out var target))
        Console.WriteLine($"Alive: {target.Length} bytes");

    data = null!;           // Drop the strong reference
    GC.Collect();           // Force collection (demo only — never do in prod)
    GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();

    if (!weakRef.TryGetTarget(out _))
        Console.WriteLine("Object was collected by GC");
}

// ── 2. Memory-sensitive cache (practical pattern) ─────────────────────────────
public sealed class WeakCache<TKey, TValue> where TValue : class
{
    private readonly Dictionary<TKey, WeakReference<TValue>> _cache = new();

    public TValue? GetOrAdd(TKey key, Func<TKey, TValue> factory)
    {
        // Try to retrieve an existing live reference
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(key, out var weakRef) &&
            weakRef.TryGetTarget(out var cached))
        {
            return cached; // Object still alive — cache hit
        }

        // Object was collected or never created — rebuild
        var value = factory(key);
        _cache[key] = new WeakReference<TValue>(value);
        return value;
    }

    // Periodically purge dead entries to avoid key-leak
    public void Purge()
    {
        var dead = new List<TKey>();
        foreach (var (key, weakRef) in _cache)
            if (!weakRef.TryGetTarget(out _))
                dead.Add(key);

        foreach (var key in dead)
            _cache.Remove(key);
    }
}

