## ⚡ Short Answer
The Circuit Breaker pattern prevents an application from repeatedly trying to execute an operation that is likely to fail, allowing it to recover gracefully instead of cascading failures across services. Polly is a .NET resilience library that implements this pattern through a state machine with three states — Closed, Open, and Half-Open — letting you define thresholds for failure counts or durations before tripping the circuit.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A resilience pattern modeled after an electrical circuit breaker that "trips" (opens) when too many failures are detected, short-circuiting further calls and returning a fast failure until the system recovers.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In distributed systems, a downstream service (database, third-party API, microservice) can become slow or unavailable. Without a circuit breaker, callers keep hammering the failing service, exhausting thread pools, connection pools, and propagating latency upstream — the classic cascading failure. The circuit breaker acts as a protective gate.

**How it works internally:** Polly maintains a state machine with three states. In the **Closed** state (healthy), calls pass through normally and failures are counted. Once failures exceed the configured threshold within a sampling window, the circuit transitions to **Open** — all subsequent calls immediately throw `BrokenCircuitException` without hitting the downstream service. After a configured `durationOfBreak` timeout, it moves to **Half-Open**, where a limited number of probe calls are allowed through. If those succeed, the circuit resets to Closed; if they fail, it snaps back to Open.

**Benefits:** Fails fast instead of waiting for timeouts; reduces load on the struggling downstream service; gives it time to recover; improves overall system responsiveness; supports fallback strategies.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds complexity; requires careful tuning of thresholds and break duration — too sensitive and you trip on transient noise, too lenient and you don't protect fast enough. Shared circuit breaker state across instances requires distributed state (e.g., Redis) which most teams skip, meaning each pod has independent state.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
