## ⚡ Short Answer
Eventual consistency is a consistency model used in distributed systems where, after a write, all replicas will *eventually* converge to the same value — but reads may temporarily return stale data. It trades strong consistency for higher availability and partition tolerance, as described by the CAP theorem. Systems like Cassandra, DynamoDB, and Redis Cluster operate this way by default.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A guarantee that, given no new updates, all replicas in a distributed system will *eventually* return the same value. There is no guarantee of *when* convergence happens — only that it will.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In distributed systems, enforcing strong consistency (every read sees the latest write) requires coordination between nodes (locks, quorums, two-phase commit). This coordination introduces latency and reduces availability — if a node is unreachable, the system must block or reject the operation. Eventual consistency removes this requirement, allowing nodes to accept reads/writes independently and sync later, which is critical for globally distributed, high-throughput systems.

**How it works internally:** Writes are accepted by one or more nodes and propagated asynchronously via anti-entropy protocols (gossip, change data capture, replication logs). Each node applies updates independently. Conflict resolution strategies — Last Write Wins (LWW), vector clocks, CRDTs — determine what happens when divergent versions are reconciled. DynamoDB uses versioning + LWW; Cassandra uses timestamps; CouchDB uses MVCC with manual conflict resolution.

**Benefits:** High availability, low write latency, partition tolerance, horizontal scalability. Allows "always-on" writes even during network partitions.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Stale reads are possible. Complex conflict resolution. Hard to reason about in business logic involving money, inventory, or sequencing. Not suitable for operations requiring linearizability (e.g., bank transfers, ticket booking seat locks).

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// Scenario: User profile service backed by an eventually consistent store (e.g., DynamoDB)
// Problem: Read-your-own-writes anomaly — user updates name but immediately reads stale data

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Strategy 1: Read-Your-Own-Writes via client-side caching
// After a successful write, cache locally so the next read is fresh
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public class UserProfileService
{
    private readonly IDistributedStore _store;
    private readonly IMemoryCache _localCache;
    private static readonly TimeSpan CacheTtl = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10);

    public UserProfileService(IDistributedStore store, IMemoryCache localCache)
    {
        _store = store;
        _localCache = localCache;
    }

    public async Task UpdateUsernameAsync(string userId, string newName)
    {
        await _store.PutAsync($"user:{userId}:name", newName);

        // Cache the write locally so we satisfy read-your-own-writes
        _localCache.Set($"user:{userId}:name", newName, CacheTtl);
    }

    public async Task<string> GetUsernameAsync(string userId)
    {
        // Check local cache first (satisfies read-your-own-writes)
        if (_localCache.TryGetValue($"user:{userId}:name", out string cached))
            return cached;

        // Fall back to the eventually consistent store
        return await _store.GetAsync($"user:{userId}:name");
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Strategy 2: Idempotent writes with version tokens
// Use a monotonic version to detect and discard stale overwrites
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
public record UserProfile(string UserId, string Name, long Version);

public class VersionedProfileService
{
    private readonly IDistributedStore _store;

    public async Task<bool> TryUpdateAsync(UserProfile updated)
    {
        var existing = await _store.GetAsync<UserProfile>($"profile:{updated.UserId}");

        // Discard stale writes — last-write-wins with version guard
        if (existing != null && existing.Version >= updated.Version)
        {
            // Stale update; skip silently (idempotent)
            return false;
        }

        await _store.PutAsync($"profile:{updated.UserId}", updated);
        return true;
    }
}

