## ⚡ Short Answer
The Outbox pattern is a technique for reliably publishing messages/events alongside database writes by storing them in an "outbox" table within the same transaction, then having a separate process relay them to the message broker. It solves the dual-write problem — the impossibility of atomically writing to a database and publishing to a message broker in a single operation. Without it, you risk events being lost or duplicated whenever a failure occurs between the two writes.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A microservices integration pattern where, instead of directly publishing an event to a message broker (Kafka, RabbitMQ, etc.), you insert the event as a row into an `outbox` table inside the same local database transaction as your business data change. A separate relay process (poller or CDC listener) then reads unpublished rows and forwards them to the broker.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In distributed systems, you cannot atomically commit to two different systems (your DB and your broker) without a distributed transaction. If you write to the DB first and the app crashes before publishing, the event is lost. If you publish first and the DB write fails, you've emitted a phantom event. The Outbox pattern eliminates this by making event storage part of the same ACID transaction as the business write.

**How it works internally:** Two-phase flow — (1) Application writes business entity + outbox row in one DB transaction. (2) A relay agent (poller using SELECT/UPDATE or a CDC tool like Debezium reading the DB transaction log) reads committed outbox rows and publishes them to the broker, then marks them as processed. The relay guarantees at-least-once delivery; idempotency on the consumer side handles duplicates.

**Benefits:** Atomic consistency between state and events; no distributed transactions needed; works with any broker; decouples the write path from broker availability (broker can be down temporarily).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds latency (slight delay between commit and publish); extra DB table and relay infrastructure to operate; outbox table can grow large without pruning; at-least-once delivery means consumers must be idempotent; CDC-based approaches require access to DB replication logs (not always available on managed DBs).

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// --- 1. Domain entities and Outbox table model ---

public class Order
{
    public Guid Id { get; set; }
    public string Status { get; set; }
}

public class OutboxMessage
{
    public Guid Id { get; set; }           // Unique message ID (used for idempotency)
    public string EventType { get; set; }  // e.g. "OrderPlaced"
    public string Payload { get; set; }    // JSON-serialized event body
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }
    public bool Processed { get; set; }    // or use ProcessedAt DateTime?
}

// --- 2. Application service: write business data + outbox row atomically ---

public class OrderService
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;

    public OrderService(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    public async Task PlaceOrderAsync(Guid orderId)
    {
        var order = new Order { Id = orderId, Status = "Placed" };

        var outboxMessage = new OutboxMessage
        {
            Id = Guid.NewGuid(),
            EventType = "OrderPlaced",
            Payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { OrderId = orderId, Status = "Placed" }),
            CreatedAt = DateTime.UtcNow,
            Processed = false
        };

        // Both writes happen in ONE transaction — atomically
        _db.Orders.Add(order);
        _db.OutboxMessages.Add(outboxMessage);
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); // If this fails, neither row is persisted
    }
}

// --- 3. Relay agent: poll outbox and publish to broker (runs as background service) ---

public class OutboxRelayWorker : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    private readonly IMessageBroker _broker;

    public OutboxRelayWorker(AppDbContext db, IMessageBroker broker)
    {
        _db = db;
        _broker = broker;
    }

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        while (!ct.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Fetch a batch of unprocessed messages
            var pending = await _db.OutboxMessages
                .Where(m => !m.Processed)
                .OrderBy(m => m.CreatedAt)
                .Take(50)
                .ToListAsync(ct);

            foreach (var msg in pending)
            {
                try
                {
                    // Publish to broker — at-least-once; consumers must be idempotent
                    await _broker.PublishAsync(msg.EventType, msg.Payload, msg.Id);
                    msg.Processed =
