## ⚡ Short Answer
A health check endpoint is a dedicated HTTP route (typically `GET /health` or `GET /healthz`) that returns the operational status of a service, including its dependencies like databases, caches, and downstream APIs. Every microservice needs one because orchestrators like Kubernetes and load balancers rely on it to route traffic only to healthy instances, automatically restart failing pods, and enable zero-downtime deployments.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A lightweight HTTP endpoint that reports whether a service is alive and capable of serving traffic. It typically returns a structured JSON payload with status codes (healthy/degraded/unhealthy) and optionally details about each dependency's state.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In distributed systems, services fail silently — a pod may be running but unable to connect to its database. Without health checks, load balancers send traffic to broken instances, causing cascading failures. Health endpoints give the infrastructure a programmatic way to detect and react to these partial failures before users are impacted.

**How it works internally:** There are two distinct probe types in production systems. A liveness probe answers "is this process alive?" (if it fails, restart the container). A readiness probe answers "is this service ready to accept traffic?" (if it fails, remove it from the load balancer rotation but don't restart it). A third type, the startup probe, handles slow-starting services. Each probe hits a specific route, checks internal state and dependencies, and returns an HTTP 200 (healthy) or 503 (unhealthy).

**Benefits:** Enables automatic recovery via orchestrator restarts, prevents traffic routing to degraded instances, supports graceful rolling deployments, integrates with monitoring dashboards and alerting, and makes dependency failures visible and diagnosable.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Health checks that are too aggressive (checking every downstream service) can cause cascading failures — if Service A's health check calls Service B, and B is slow, A marks itself unhealthy and amplifies the outage. Checks must be fast (under 100ms ideally) to avoid timing out the orchestrator. Sensitive internal details (connection strings, error messages) must never be leaked in the response body.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ASP.NET Core 8 — Production-grade health check setup
// Program.cs

using Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.HealthChecks;
using System.Text.Json;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddHealthChecks()
    // Built-in SQL Server check (nuget: AspNetCore.HealthChecks.SqlServer)
    .AddSqlServer(
        connectionString: builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("Default")!,
        name: "sqlserver",
        failureStatus: HealthStatus.Degraded,   // degraded, not unhealthy — still serve traffic
        tags: ["db", "readiness"])
    // Built-in Redis check (nuget: AspNetCore.HealthChecks.Redis)
    .AddRedis(
        redisConnectionString: builder.Configuration["Redis:Connection"]!,
        name: "redis",
        failureStatus: HealthStatus.Degraded,
        tags: ["cache", "readiness"])
    // Custom business-logic check
    .AddCheck<ExternalPaymentApiHealthCheck>(
        name: "payment-api",
        failureStatus: HealthStatus.Unhealthy,
        tags: ["external", "readiness"]);

var app = builder.Build();

// LIVENESS — is the process alive? No dependency checks.
// Kubernetes restarts the pod if this returns non-200.
app.MapHealthChecks("/healthz/live", new()
{
    Predicate = _ => false,   // Run NO checks — just return 200 if app is running
    ResponseWriter = WriteJsonResponse
});

// READINESS — can this instance serve traffic?
// Kubernetes removes pod from LB rotation if this returns non-200.
app.MapHealthChecks("/healthz/ready", new()
{
    Predicate = check => check.Tags.Contains("readiness"),
    ResponseWriter = WriteJsonResponse
});

// FULL — human-readable dashboard endpoint (protect this in prod!)
app.MapHealthChecks("/health", new()
{
    ResponseWriter = WriteJsonResponse
}).RequireAuthorization("InternalOnly");  // Never expose full details publicly

app.Run();

// -------------------------------------------------------
// Custom health check: wraps an external HTTP dependency
// -------------------------------------------------------
public class ExternalPaymentApiHealthCheck : IHealthCheck
{
