## ⚡ Short Answer
Designing a high-throughput .NET API for 10,000 req/sec requires combining async I/O throughout the stack, minimizing allocations to reduce GC pressure, leveraging caching aggressively, and scaling horizontally behind a load balancer. No single technique achieves this — it's a layered strategy covering the runtime, application code, infrastructure, and observability.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A set of architectural and code-level patterns that allow an ASP.NET Core API to sustain 10,000+ requests per second with low latency and high reliability.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Default .NET APIs out of the box handle hundreds to low thousands of req/sec before hitting bottlenecks: thread pool exhaustion, GC pauses from excessive allocations, database connection saturation, and synchronous blocking code. At 10k req/sec these issues become service-killers.

**How it works internally:** ASP.NET Core uses Kestrel, a non-blocking I/O server built on `System.IO.Pipelines`. With `async/await` end-to-end, threads are freed back to the pool during I/O waits, allowing a small number of threads to multiplex thousands of concurrent requests. The CLR thread pool auto-tunes, but you need to ensure nothing blocks it. Reducing allocations keeps the GC in Gen0, avoiding expensive Gen2 collections that cause stop-the-world pauses on hot paths.

**Benefits:**
- Async I/O eliminates thread-per-request overhead
- Object pooling and `Span<T>`/`ArrayPool<T>` reduce GC pressure dramatically
- Response caching and distributed caching (Redis) absorb repeated read traffic
- Horizontal scaling via Kubernetes or load-balanced VMs provides near-linear throughput scaling
- Output caching and rate limiting are now first-class ASP.NET Core middleware

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Async code adds complexity; mistakes (`.Result`, `.Wait()`) cause deadlocks
- Caching introduces staleness; invalidation logic is hard
- Pooling objects requires careful reset logic to avoid state leaks
- Horizontal scaling needs stateless design — sessions must live in Redis, not in-process

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// Program.cs — high-throughput ASP.NET Core 8 setup
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.OutputCaching;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.StackExchangeRedis;
using System.Buffers; // ArrayPool

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// 1. Use Kestrel with tuned limits
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
{
    options.Limits.MaxConcurrentConnections = 10_000;
    options.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 1_048_576; // 1 MB
    options.Limits.MinRequestBodyDataRate = null;   // disable for high-burst
});

// 2. Add Redis distributed cache (absorb read traffic)
builder.Services.AddStackExchangeRedisCache(options =>
    options.Configuration = builder.Configuration["Redis:ConnectionString"]);

// 3. Add output caching (ASP.NET Core 7+ built-in)
builder.Services.AddOutputCache(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("products", p => p.Expire(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30)));
});

// 4. Register a pooled DB context (reduces connection overhead)
builder.Services.AddDbContextPool<AppDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseNpgsql(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("Db")),
    poolSize: 256);

// 5. Add rate limiting (protect from thundering herd)
builder.Services.AddRateLimiter(options =>
{
    options.AddFixedWindowLimiter("global", limiter =>
    {
        limiter.Window = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1);
        limiter.PermitLimit = 12_000; // allow burst headroom
        limiter.QueueProcessingOrder = QueueProcessingOrder.OldestFirst;
        limiter.QueueLimit = 2_000;
    });
});

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseRateLimiter();
app.UseOutputCache();

