## ⚡ Short Answer
Concurrent updates to a shared resource in .NET Web API are handled using concurrency control strategies — primarily optimistic concurrency (using ETags or row versions) or pessimistic concurrency (using locks or database-level locks). Optimistic concurrency is preferred for high-read, low-conflict scenarios, while pessimistic concurrency suits high-contention writes. The choice depends on conflict frequency, acceptable latency, and data consistency requirements.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Concurrency control is a set of mechanisms that ensure multiple simultaneous requests don't corrupt shared state by interleaving reads and writes in ways that produce incorrect results (lost updates, dirty reads, phantom reads).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In a Web API, multiple clients can send requests at the same time. Without concurrency control, two users can read the same record, both modify it independently, and the second write silently overwrites the first — the classic "lost update" problem. This causes data corruption without any error being raised.

**How it works internally:** Optimistic concurrency works by attaching a version token (a `RowVersion` byte array in SQL Server, or an ETag in HTTP) to each resource. When a client sends an update, it includes the token it received. The server checks whether the token still matches the current state — if not, a conflict is detected and the update is rejected with HTTP 409 Conflict. Pessimistic concurrency uses database transactions with locking hints (`SELECT ... WITH (UPDLOCK)`) or .NET synchronization primitives (`SemaphoreSlim`, `lock`) to block concurrent access entirely until the holder finishes.

**Benefits:** Optimistic concurrency scales well because no locks are held between read and write — threads are never blocked waiting. Pessimistic concurrency guarantees no conflicts ever happen, suitable for financial or inventory systems where retries are unacceptable.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Optimistic concurrency requires clients to handle 409 responses and implement retry logic, adding client-side complexity. Pessimistic concurrency reduces throughput under load, risks deadlocks if not carefully ordered, and can cause timeouts at scale. In-process locks (`lock`, `SemaphoreSlim`) don't work across multiple API instances — a distributed lock (Redis, Azure Blob leases) is needed in multi-node deployments.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// === ENTITY with RowVersion for Optimistic Concurrency ===
public class Product
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public decimal Price { get; set; }

    // SQL Server automatically increments this on every update
    [Timestamp]
    public byte[] RowVersion { get; set; } = Array.Empty<byte>();
}

// === EF CORE CONFIGURATION ===
// In DbContext or Fluent API:
// modelBuilder.Entity<Product>()
//     .Property(p => p.RowVersion).IsRowVersion();

// ============================================================
// OPTIMISTIC CONCURRENCY — Controller
// ============================================================
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class ProductsController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;

    public ProductsController(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    // GET: Returns ETag header alongside the product
    [HttpGet("{id}")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id)
    {
        var product = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);
        if (product is null) return NotFound();

        // Convert RowVersion to ETag string so client can send it back
        var etag = Convert.ToBase64String(product.RowVersion);
        Response.Headers.ETag = $"\"{etag}\"";

        return Ok(product);
    }

    // PUT: Client must send back the ETag in If-Match header
    [HttpPut("{id}")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> Update(int id, [FromBody] ProductUpdateDto dto)
    {
        // 1. Read the If-Match header the client echoed back
        if (!Request.Headers.TryGetValue("If-Match", out var ifMatch))
            return BadRequest("If-Match header is required for updates.");

        var product = await _db.Products.FindAsync(id);
        if (product is null) return NotFound();

        // 2. Compare client's version with current DB version
        var clientVersion = Convert.FromBase64String(ifMatch.ToString().Trim('"'));
        if (!clientVersion.SequenceEqual(product.RowVersion))
            return Conflict("The resource was modified by another request. Fetch the latest version and retry.");

        // 3. Apply changes — EF Core will issue UPDATE ... WHERE RowVersion = @original
        product.Name = dto.Name;
        product.Price = dto.Price;

        try
        {
            await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
        }
        catch (DbUpdateConcurrencyException ex)
