## ⚡ Short Answer
A caching layer sits between your application and the database, storing frequently read data in fast in-memory storage (like Redis or Memcached) to reduce database load and cut latency from milliseconds to microseconds. The core challenges are cache invalidation, consistency, and choosing the right eviction and expiration strategy for your access patterns.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A caching layer is an intermediary store that holds copies of database query results in memory so future reads can be served without hitting the database.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Databases are I/O-bound and expensive to scale. When a table is read thousands of times per second (e.g., a product catalog or user profile table), the DB becomes a bottleneck. A cache absorbs the read amplification and lets the DB handle writes and complex queries only.

**How it works internally:** The most common pattern is Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading): the app checks the cache first; on a miss, it queries the DB, writes the result to the cache with a TTL, and returns the data. Other patterns include Read-Through (cache fetches from DB automatically), Write-Through (writes go to cache and DB together), and Write-Behind (writes go to cache first, DB is updated asynchronously). Under the hood, Redis uses an in-memory hash map with optional persistence (RDB snapshots / AOF logs) and supports LRU/LFU eviction policies when memory is full.

**Benefits:** Dramatic latency reduction (sub-millisecond vs. 10-100ms DB queries), reduced DB CPU/IO load, higher throughput, and the ability to handle traffic spikes without scaling the database.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Cache invalidation is notoriously hard — stale data is a real risk. Write-heavy workloads see little benefit and add complexity. Cold starts (empty cache after deploy or crash) can cause a thundering herd problem. Memory is finite and expensive. Distributed caches add network hops and operational overhead.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// Production-grade Cache-Aside pattern with Redis in .NET
// Uses IDistributedCache abstraction for testability

public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly IDistributedCache _cache;
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    private readonly ILogger<ProductRepository> _logger;

    // TTL strategy: shorter for volatile data, longer for stable data
    private static readonly TimeSpan DefaultTtl = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(10);

    public ProductRepository(IDistributedCache cache, AppDbContext db, ILogger<ProductRepository> logger)
    {
        _cache = cache;
        _db = db;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async Task<Product?> GetByIdAsync(int productId)
    {
        string cacheKey = $"product:{productId}";

        // 1. Try cache first
        byte[]? cachedBytes = await _cache.GetAsync(cacheKey);
        if (cachedBytes is not null)
        {
            _logger.LogDebug("Cache HIT for key {Key}", cacheKey);
            return JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Product>(cachedBytes);
        }

        _logger.LogDebug("Cache MISS for key {Key}", cacheKey);

        // 2. Fallback to DB
        var product = await _db.Products
            .AsNoTracking()
            .FirstOrDefaultAsync(p => p.Id == productId);

        // 3. Populate cache (avoid caching nulls unless desired)
        if (product is not null)
        {
            var options = new DistributedCacheEntryOptions
            {
                // AbsoluteExpiration: hard expiry — prevents permanently stale data
                AbsoluteExpirationRelativeToNow = DefaultTtl,
                // SlidingExpiration: resets TTL on access — good for active sessions
                SlidingExpiration = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2)
            };
            await _cache.SetAsync(cacheKey, JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes(product), options);
        }

        return product;
    }

    // CRITICAL: Invalidate cache on writes to prevent stale reads
    public async Task UpdateProductAsync(Product product)
    {
        // Write-Through: update DB first, then invalidate (or re-populate) cache
        _db.Products.Update(product);
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();

        // Invalidate — safest approach vs. trying to update in-place
        string cacheKey = $"product:{product.Id}";
        await _cache.RemoveAsync(cacheKey);

        // Also invalidate any list/collection caches that included this product
        await _cache.RemoveAsync("products:list:all");
    }

    // THUNDERING HERD mitigation: use a distributed lock on cache miss
    // Pattern: only one process fetches from DB; others wait for cache to populate
    public async Task<Product?> GetBy
