## ⚡ Short Answer
Real-time notifications in ASP.NET Core are best implemented using **SignalR**, which abstracts over WebSockets, Server-Sent Events, and Long Polling to maintain persistent connections between server and clients. The server pushes messages to connected clients instantly without polling. For scaled-out deployments, a backplane like Redis or Azure SignalR Service is used to fan messages across multiple server instances.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** SignalR is a library built into ASP.NET Core that enables full-duplex, real-time communication. It exposes a Hub abstraction where the server can invoke methods on connected clients and clients can call server methods.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Traditional HTTP is request/response — the client must poll for updates. This wastes bandwidth and adds latency. SignalR solves this by keeping a persistent connection alive so the server can push data the moment it's ready.

**How it works internally:** SignalR negotiates the best transport automatically — WebSockets first, then SSE, then Long Polling. Each connected client gets a unique connection ID. The Hub tracks these connections (in-memory by default, or via a distributed backplane for multi-server setups). When you call `Clients.All.SendAsync(...)`, SignalR serializes the payload and writes it to every active connection's transport stream.

**Benefits:** Low latency push, built-in reconnection logic, strongly typed hubs, supports groups and user targeting, scales with Redis or Azure SignalR Service.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Persistent connections consume server resources (threads/sockets); in-memory connection state doesn't survive server restarts; requires a backplane for load-balanced deployments; WebSockets may be blocked by some corporate firewalls or proxies.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ─── 1. Define the Hub ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR;

public class NotificationHub : Hub
{
    // Clients can call this to join a named group (e.g. per-user or per-tenant)
    public async Task JoinGroup(string groupName)
    {
        await Groups.AddToGroupAsync(Context.ConnectionId, groupName);
    }

    public async Task LeaveGroup(string groupName)
    {
        await Groups.RemoveFromGroupAsync(Context.ConnectionId, groupName);
    }
}

// ─── 2. Wire up SignalR in Program.cs ──────────────────────────────────────
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddSignalR()
    // Optional: scale out with Redis backplane
    // .AddStackExchangeRedis("localhost:6379");
    ;

// Register a background service that simulates sending notifications
builder.Services.AddHostedService<NotificationDispatcher>();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapHub<NotificationHub>("/hubs/notifications");
app.Run();

// ─── 3. Push notifications from anywhere via IHubContext ───────────────────
public class NotificationDispatcher : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly IHubContext<NotificationHub> _hubContext;

    public NotificationDispatcher(IHubContext<NotificationHub> hubContext)
    {
        _hubContext = hubContext;
    }

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        int counter = 0;

        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5), stoppingToken);

            var payload = new
            {
                Id      = ++counter,
                Message = $"Server event #{counter}",
                At      = DateTime.UtcNow
            };

            // Push to ALL connected clients
            await _hubContext.Clients.All
                .SendAsync("ReceiveNotification", payload, stoppingToken);

            // Or push to a specific group (e.g. per-user targeting)
            // await _hubContext.Clients.Group("user-42")
            //     .SendAsync("ReceiveNotification", payload, stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}

