## ⚡ Short Answer
`null` is an intentional absence of a value assigned by a developer, `undefined` means a variable has been declared but not yet assigned a value, and `NaN` ("Not a Number") is a numeric value returned when a mathematical operation fails to produce a valid number. They are three distinct primitives with different types, behaviors, and use cases that are frequently confused in JavaScript interviews.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:**
`null` is a deliberate assignment indicating "no value here." `undefined` is JavaScript's default state for uninitialized variables, missing object properties, or omitted function arguments. `NaN` is a special value of type `number` that represents an invalid numeric result.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:**
JavaScript needed a way to distinguish between "I explicitly set this to nothing" (`null`), "this was never set" (`undefined`), and "I tried to do math but it didn't work" (`NaN`). These distinctions help developers communicate intent and handle edge cases gracefully.

**How it works internally:**
`typeof null` returns `"object"` — a well-known historical bug in JavaScript that was never fixed to avoid breaking the web. `typeof undefined` returns `"undefined"`. `typeof NaN` returns `"number"`. `NaN` is the only value in JavaScript not equal to itself (`NaN !== NaN`), which is why `Number.isNaN()` exists. `null == undefined` is `true` (loose equality), but `null === undefined` is `false` (strict equality).

**Benefits:**
Using them correctly makes intent explicit, prevents subtle bugs in type checks, and aligns with JavaScript's loose-typing system when handled deliberately.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
The `typeof null === "object"` bug causes confusion. Loose equality between `null` and `undefined` creates unpredictable comparisons. `NaN` comparisons with `===` always fail, which trips up newcomers. Relying on `undefined` over `null` for intentional "no value" makes APIs unclear to consumers.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── null ───────────────────────────────────────────────
let user = null;                  // Explicitly "no user"
console.log(typeof null);         // "object" (historical bug!)
console.log(null === undefined);  // false (different types)
console.log(null == undefined);   // true  (loose equality quirk)

// ─── undefined ──────────────────────────────────────────
let score;                        // Declared but never assigned
console.log(score);               // undefined
console.log(typeof score);        // "undefined"

function greet(name) {
  console.log(name);              // undefined if called as greet()
}

const obj = { a: 1 };
console.log(obj.b);               // undefined — missing property

// ─── NaN ────────────────────────────────────────────────
const result = "hello" * 2;      // Invalid math → NaN
console.log(result);              // NaN
console.log(typeof NaN);          // "number" (!)
console.log(NaN === NaN);         // false — NaN is never equal to itself

// ✅ Correct way to check for NaN
console.log(Number.isNaN(result));    // true  (strict, recommended)
console.log(isNaN("hello"));          // true  (coerces first — avoid!)
console.log(Number.isNaN("hello"));   // false (no coercion)

// ─── Practical guard patterns ────────────────────────────
function processUser(user) {
  if (user === null) {
    return "No user provided (intentional)";
  }
  if (user === undefined) {
    return "User was never set";
  }
  return `Hello, ${user.name}`;
}

function safeDivide(a, b) {
  const result = a / b;
  if (Number.isNaN(result) || !isFinite(result)) {
    return 0; // fallback for bad input
  }
  return result;
}

console.log(safeDivide(10, 0));      // 0  (Infinity guard)
console.log(safeDivide(10, "x"));    // 0  (NaN guard)
console.log(safeDivide(10, 2));      // 5
```

