## ⚡ Short Answer
The `typeof` operator in JavaScript is a unary operator that returns a string indicating the primitive type of its operand. It is evaluated at runtime and returns one of eight possible string values, making it the primary tool for type-checking primitives — though it has a well-known quirk with `null`.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A built-in JavaScript unary operator (not a function) that takes any expression and returns a lowercase string describing its type.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** JavaScript is dynamically typed, meaning variables have no declared type at definition time. `typeof` gives developers a lightweight, safe way to inspect what kind of value a variable holds at runtime — especially useful for feature detection, guard clauses, and handling optional parameters.

**How it works internally:** The JS engine inspects the internal type tag stored in the value's memory representation. Because `typeof` operates before evaluation of the operand, it is uniquely safe to use on undeclared variables without throwing a `ReferenceError` — it returns `"undefined"` instead.

**Benefits:** Zero-cost runtime type check, works safely on undeclared identifiers, no imports needed, covers all primitive types in one operation.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Cannot distinguish between different object subtypes (arrays, dates, plain objects all return `"object"`), and the infamous `typeof null === "object"` bug is a permanent language quirk preserved for backward compatibility. For objects, `instanceof`, `Array.isArray()`, or `Object.prototype.toString` are better choices.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ── All 8 possible return values ──────────────────────────────────────

typeof undefined          // "undefined"
typeof 42                 // "number"
typeof NaN                // "number"  ← NaN is still type number!
typeof "hello"            // "string"
typeof true               // "boolean"
typeof Symbol("id")       // "symbol"
typeof 9007199254740991n  // "bigint"
typeof function(){}       // "function"

// Objects (plain, array, date, null) all return "object"
typeof {}                 // "object"
typeof []                 // "object"  ← use Array.isArray() instead
typeof new Date()         // "object"
typeof null               // "object"  ← historical bug, NOT truly object

// ── Safe check on an undeclared variable ─────────────────────────────
// This does NOT throw ReferenceError — unique to typeof
if (typeof myUndeclaredVar === "undefined") {
  console.log("Variable not declared or not yet assigned");
}

// ── Practical guard clause pattern ───────────────────────────────────
function greet(name) {
  if (typeof name !== "string") {
    throw new TypeError(`Expected string, got ${typeof name}`);
  }
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}

// ── Distinguishing objects properly ──────────────────────────────────
const value = [1, 2, 3];

typeof value === "object" && value !== null && Array.isArray(value)
  ? console.log("It's an array")      // ✅ correct check
  : console.log("Not an array");

// ── Object.prototype.toString for precise object type ────────────────
Object.prototype.toString.call([])        // "[object Array]"
Object.prototype.toString.call(new Date()) // "[object Date]"
Object.prototype.toString.call(null)       // "[object Null]"
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Why does `typeof null` return `"object"`?** A: It's a bug from JavaScript's 1995 implementation where null's internal type tag was `000` — the same prefix used for objects. It was never fixed to avoid breaking existing web pages.
- **Q: How do you safely check for null specifically?** A: Use a strict equality check: `value === null`. Since `null` is the only value that is both `typeof "object"` and falsy in a meaningful way, `=== null` is the only reliable test.
- **Q: Can `typeof` be used before a `let` or `const` declaration?** A: No — unlike `var`, `let` and `const` are in the temporal dead zone (TDZ) before their declaration, so `typeof myLetVar` will throw a `ReferenceError` if used before the declaration line.

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes
❌ Using `typeof value === "object"` to confirm something is a real object, then calling methods on it — forgetting that `null` passes this check and will cause a `TypeError`.
✅ Always pair the check with a null guard: `typeof value === "object" && value !== null`.

❌ Expecting `typeof []` to return `"array"` — arrays are objects in JavaScript.
✅ Use `Array.isArray(value)` for array detection; it works across iframes and is the idiomatic standard.

