## ⚡ Short Answer
In JavaScript, primitive types (numbers, strings, booleans, null, undefined, Symbol, BigInt) are passed by value — the function gets a copy, so mutations don't affect the original. Objects and arrays are passed by reference — the function receives a reference to the same memory location, so property mutations affect the original. However, reassigning the parameter itself never affects the caller's variable, which is a common source of confusion.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A distinction in how function arguments are handed off — either as independent copies (by value) or as pointers to the same memory location (by reference).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** JavaScript's engine optimizes memory by copying small primitives cheaply while sharing large objects by reference, avoiding expensive deep copies on every function call.

**How it works internally:** When you pass a primitive, the JS engine allocates a new slot on the call stack and copies the bit pattern of the value. When you pass an object, the engine copies the *reference* (the memory address/pointer), not the object itself — both the caller and callee now hold a reference to the same heap object. Critically, this is sometimes called "pass-by-sharing" or "pass-by-value-of-the-reference," because you can mutate the object's properties but cannot replace the object in the caller's scope.

**Benefits:** Objects shared by reference avoid costly deep copies, enabling efficient mutation patterns and large data passing. Primitives copied by value provide isolation and predictability.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Reference sharing causes unexpected side effects when functions mutate object arguments. Reassigning a parameter (e.g. `param = {}`) feels like it should affect the caller but silently doesn't, leading to subtle bugs.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── 1. PASS BY VALUE (primitives) ───────────────────────────────────────────
function incrementAge(age) {
  age += 1;           // modifies the LOCAL copy only
  console.log("inside:", age); // 31
}

let myAge = 30;
incrementAge(myAge);
console.log("outside:", myAge); // still 30 — original unchanged ✅


// ─── 2. PASS BY REFERENCE (objects) ──────────────────────────────────────────
function birthdayMutate(person) {
  person.age += 1;    // mutates the SHARED object on the heap
}

const user = { name: "Alice", age: 30 };
birthdayMutate(user);
console.log(user.age); // 31 — original IS changed ⚠️


// ─── 3. THE COMMON TRAP: reassignment doesn't affect caller ──────────────────
function tryReplace(person) {
  // This only rebinds the LOCAL parameter — caller's variable is unaffected
  person = { name: "Bob", age: 99 };
  console.log("inside:", person.name); // "Bob"
}

const dev = { name: "Alice", age: 30 };
tryReplace(dev);
console.log("outside:", dev.name); // still "Alice" — reassign had no effect ✅


// ─── 4. DEFENSIVE: avoid mutating caller's object — use spread ────────────────
function birthdaySafe(person) {
  return { ...person, age: person.age + 1 }; // return a new object (immutable style)
}

const original = { name: "Alice", age: 30 };
const updated  = birthdaySafe(original);

console.log(original.age); // 30 — untouched ✅
console.log(updated.age);  // 31 ✅


// ─── 5. ARRAYS behave the same as objects ────────────────────────────────────
function pushItem(arr, item) {
  arr.push(item); // mutates caller's array
}

const nums = [1, 2, 3];
pushItem(nums, 4);
console.log(nums); // [1, 2, 3, 4] — mutated ⚠️

// Safe version: return a new array
const safePush = (arr, item) => [...arr, item];
console.log(safePush(nums, 5)); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
console.log(nums);              // [1, 2, 3, 4] — original unchanged ✅
```

