## ⚡ Short Answer
Synchronous code executes line by line, blocking further execution until each operation completes. Asynchronous code allows the program to initiate an operation and continue executing other work while waiting for the result, improving throughput and responsiveness — especially for I/O-bound tasks like network calls or file reads.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Synchronous (sync) code runs sequentially — each statement must finish before the next begins. Asynchronous (async) code defers long-running operations, registering a callback, promise, or continuation to handle the result when it's ready, without blocking the calling thread.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Blocking a thread while waiting for a database query or HTTP response wastes CPU cycles and limits scalability. Async programming lets a single thread (or a small thread pool) handle thousands of concurrent operations, which is critical for web servers, APIs, and UI applications.

**How it works internally:** In JavaScript, async is powered by the event loop — async operations are offloaded to the runtime (e.g., libuv), and their callbacks are queued in the microtask or macrotask queue when done. In .NET, `async/await` uses a state machine compiled by the C# compiler; awaiting a task suspends the method and returns the thread to the pool, resuming on completion via a continuation. In both cases, no thread sits idle waiting.

**Benefits:** Higher throughput under concurrent load, better resource utilization, responsive UIs (no frozen screens), and scalability without proportionally adding threads.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** More complex control flow (harder to reason about, debug, and test), risk of async/await misuse (e.g., deadlocks in .NET, unhandled promise rejections in JS), and overhead from state machine allocation or context switching — making async overkill for purely CPU-bound work.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
import axios from "axios";

// ─── SYNCHRONOUS analogy (blocking, not how JS HTTP works, shown for contrast) ───
function fetchUserSync(id: number): string {
  // Imagine this blocks the thread for 500ms — nothing else can run
  const result = blockingHttpCall(`/api/users/${id}`); // ❌ freezes everything
  return result;
}

// ─── ASYNCHRONOUS with async/await (non-blocking) ────────────────────────────
async function fetchUser(id: number): Promise<{ name: string; email: string }> {
  // Thread is NOT blocked here — event loop can handle other requests
  const response = await axios.get(`/api/users/${id}`);
  return response.data;
}

// ─── PARALLEL async: run multiple async operations concurrently ───────────────
async function fetchDashboardData(userId: number) {
  console.time("parallel");

  // ✅ Fire all three requests at the same time, await all results together
  const [user, orders, notifications] = await Promise.all([
    fetchUser(userId),              // ~200ms
    axios.get(`/api/orders/${userId}`),     // ~300ms
    axios.get(`/api/notifications/${userId}`), // ~150ms
  ]);
  // Total wait ≈ 300ms (slowest), NOT 650ms (sequential sum)

  console.timeEnd("parallel");
  return { user: user, orders: orders.data, notifications: notifications.data };
}

// ─── SEQUENTIAL async: when order matters ────────────────────────────────────
async function createOrderForUser(userId: number, item: string) {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);           // Must have user first
  const order = await placeOrder(user, item);     // Then create order
  await sendConfirmationEmail(user.email, order); // Then send email
  return order;
}

// ─── COMMON MISTAKE: async in a loop (sequential instead of parallel) ────────
async function fetchAllUsersWrong(ids: number[]) {
  const users = [];
  for (const id of ids) {
    users.push(await fetchUser(id)); // ❌ Waits for each one — totally sequential
  }
  return users;
}

async function fetchAllUsersCorrect(ids: number[]) {
  return Promise.all(ids.map(id => fetchUser(id))); // ✅ All fired concurrently
}

// ─── Always handle errors in async code ──────────────────────────────────────
async function safeFetch(id: number) {
  try {
    return await fetchUser(id);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`Failed to fetch user ${id}:`, err);
    throw err; // Re-throw or return a fallback
  }
}

