## ⚡ Short Answer
A **pure function** always returns the same output for the same input and has no side effects — it doesn't modify anything outside its own scope. An **impure function** either depends on external state, modifies external state, or produces side effects like I/O, making its behavior less predictable and harder to test.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Pure functions are deterministic, self-contained units of computation. Impure functions introduce dependencies on or mutations of the outside world.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** As codebases grow, unpredictable state mutations cause bugs that are hard to trace. Pure functions eliminate this class of bug entirely by guaranteeing referential transparency — you can replace a function call with its return value and nothing breaks.

**How it works internally:** A pure function reads only its arguments and writes only to its return value. No global variables, no database calls, no random numbers, no logging, no mutations of passed-in objects. The runtime has no hidden inputs or outputs. Impure functions break one or more of these constraints.

**Benefits:** Pure functions are trivially unit-testable (no mocks needed), safely cacheable/memoizable, parallelizable without race conditions, and easy to reason about in isolation.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Real software must have side effects (DB writes, HTTP calls, UI rendering). You can't build a useful system from pure functions alone — the goal is to *isolate* impurity at the edges, not eliminate it entirely. Over-purifying can also lead to awkward data threading through many layers.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── PURE FUNCTIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────

// ✅ Same input ALWAYS gives same output. No side effects.
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

// ✅ Pure even with objects — returns a NEW object, no mutation
function applyDiscount(product, discountPct) {
  return { ...product, price: product.price * (1 - discountPct / 100) };
}

// ✅ Easy to test — no setup, no mocks needed
console.log(add(2, 3));          // always 5
console.log(add(2, 3));          // always 5

// ─── IMPURE FUNCTIONS ─────────────────────────────────────────────

let taxRate = 0.2; // external state

// ❌ Impure: depends on external variable — output changes if taxRate changes
function calcTax(price) {
  return price * taxRate;
}

// ❌ Impure: mutates the passed-in object (side effect)
function applyDiscountMutating(product, discountPct) {
  product.price = product.price * (1 - discountPct / 100); // mutation!
  return product;
}

// ❌ Impure: I/O side effect (logging, network, DB, random, Date.now(), etc.)
function saveUser(user) {
  console.log("Saving:", user);       // side effect: I/O
  fetch("/api/users", { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify(user) }); // side effect: network
}

// ─── ARCHITECTURAL PATTERN: Push impurity to the edges ────────────

// Pure core — all business logic, easily tested
function buildUserPayload(name, email) {
  return { name: name.trim(), email: email.toLowerCase(), createdAt: null };
}

// Impure shell — thin layer that calls the pure core then does I/O
async function registerUser(name, email) {
  const payload = buildUserPayload(name, email); // pure
  payload.createdAt = new Date().toISOString();  // impure (Date)
  await fetch("/api/users", { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify(payload) }); // impure (I/O)
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: What is referential transparency?** A: A function call is referentially transparent if you can replace it with its return value without changing program behavior — pure functions have this property, impure ones don't.
- **Q: Is a function that throws an error pure?** A: Debatable, but generally considered impure — throwing an exception is a side effect that breaks the normal return-value contract.
- **Q: How does this relate to memoization?** A: Memoization (caching results by input) is only safe on pure functions. If a function has side effects or depends on external state, caching its result will return stale or incorrect values.

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes
❌ Believing a function is pure just because it doesn't use global variables — if it calls `Math.random()`, `Date.now()`, or makes a network call, it's impure.
✅ Check all three: (1) no external reads, (2) no external writes/mutations, (3) no I/O or non-determin
