## ⚡ Short Answer
JavaScript's `try/catch/finally` mechanism lets you intercept runtime errors, respond to them gracefully, and guarantee cleanup code runs regardless of outcome. You can throw any value (though `Error` objects are conventional) and catch it in the nearest enclosing handler up the call stack. Unhandled rejections in async code require additional handling via `Promise.catch()` or `try/catch` inside `async` functions.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A structured exception-handling system composed of four keywords — `try` (guarded block), `catch` (error handler), `finally` (unconditional cleanup), and `throw` (raise an error manually).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without structured error handling, a single unexpected runtime error crashes the entire execution context. `try/catch` lets the program recover, log meaningfully, and keep running instead of silently failing or showing a raw stack trace to users.

**How it works internally:** JavaScript uses a single-threaded call stack. When an exception is thrown, the engine unwinds the stack frame by frame looking for the nearest `catch` block. If none is found, the error becomes an *uncaught exception* (browser: `window.onerror`; Node: `process.on('uncaughtException')`). `finally` is appended to both the normal return path and every exception path in the bytecode, so it always executes — even if `catch` re-throws.

**Benefits:** Separates happy-path logic from error logic; enables typed error discrimination; `finally` prevents resource leaks; custom `Error` subclasses allow structured, machine-readable errors in APIs.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Overusing `try/catch` for control flow is an anti-pattern and degrades performance in hot loops (V8 de-optimises try blocks). Swallowing errors silently (`catch(e) {}`) hides bugs. Async code with callbacks requires extra care — `try/catch` does NOT catch errors thrown inside plain callbacks passed to setTimeout, etc.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── 1. Custom error hierarchy ───────────────────────────────────────────────
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode = 500) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'AppError';
    this.statusCode = statusCode;
    // Maintains proper stack trace in V8 (Node/Chrome)
    Error.captureStackTrace?.(this, AppError);
  }
}

class NotFoundError extends AppError {
  constructor(resource) {
    super(`${resource} not found`, 404);
    this.name = 'NotFoundError';
  }
}

// ─── 2. Sync try / catch / finally ───────────────────────────────────────────
function parseConfig(jsonString) {
  try {
    const config = JSON.parse(jsonString);          // may throw SyntaxError
    if (!config.version) throw new AppError('Config missing version field', 400);
    return config;
  } catch (err) {
    // Discriminate by type — don't swallow, re-throw if unknown
    if (err instanceof SyntaxError) {
      throw new AppError(`Invalid JSON: ${err.message}`, 400);
    }
    throw err; // re-throw unknown errors unchanged
  } finally {
    // Runs whether we returned normally OR threw — good for cleanup
    console.log('[parseConfig] attempt complete');
  }
}

// ─── 3. Async / await — try/catch works naturally ────────────────────────────
async function fetchUser(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);

    if (res.status === 404) throw new NotFoundError(`User #${id}`);
    if (!res.ok)            throw new AppError(`HTTP ${res.status}`, res.status);

    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof NotFoundError) {
      console.warn(err.message);   // handle gracefully
      return null;
    }
    throw err;                     // re-throw unexpected errors to caller
  }
}

// ─── 4. Promise chain style (equivalent) ─────────────────────────────────────
fetch('/api/data')
  .then(res => {
    if (!res.ok) throw new AppError('Fetch failed', res.status);
    return res.json();
  })
  .catch(err => console.error('Caught in chain:', err.message));

// ─── 5. Global safety nets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Browser
window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', event => {
  console.error('Unhandled promise rejection:', event.reason);
  event.preventDefault(); // suppress default console noise if you log it yourself
});

// Node.js
process.on('uncaughtException',       err => { /* log, then exit */
