## ⚡ Short Answer
These four static Promise combinators handle multiple async operations differently based on how they respond to resolution and rejection. `Promise.all()` requires all to succeed, `Promise.race()` takes the first to settle, `Promise.allSettled()` waits for all regardless of outcome, and `Promise.any()` succeeds with the first resolved promise.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Static methods on the Promise constructor that coordinate multiple promises running concurrently, each with different semantics for success and failure.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** When you have multiple async operations, you need different coordination strategies — sometimes you need all results, sometimes just the fastest, sometimes you want to handle partial failures gracefully. These combinators eliminate manual tracking with counters or flags.

**How it works internally:** All four accept an iterable of promises and start all of them concurrently (not sequentially). They differ in their settlement logic: `all` short-circuits on first rejection, `race` short-circuits on first settlement (resolve or reject), `allSettled` never short-circuits and collects `{status, value/reason}` objects, `any` short-circuits on first resolution and only rejects with an `AggregateError` if all fail.

**Benefits:** Clean declarative async coordination, no manual promise chaining, built-in error aggregation with `allSettled` and `any`, and true concurrent execution for performance.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** `Promise.all` can waste work if one of many long-running promises fails early (no cancellation). `Promise.race` has the same issue — "losing" promises still run to completion. None of these combinators support cancellation natively, which can lead to resource leaks.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// Simulated async tasks
const fast = () => new Promise(res => setTimeout(() => res('fast ✅'), 100));
const slow = () => new Promise(res => setTimeout(() => res('slow ✅'), 500));
const failing = () => new Promise((_, rej) => setTimeout(() => rej(new Error('boom 💥')), 200));

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. Promise.all() — ALL must resolve; fails fast on first rejection
// Use when: you need every result and one failure means the whole operation fails
try {
  const results = await Promise.all([fast(), slow()]);
  console.log('all:', results); // ['fast ✅', 'slow ✅']

  // With a rejection — rejects immediately when failing() rejects
  await Promise.all([fast(), failing(), slow()]);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('all failed:', err.message); // 'boom 💥'
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. Promise.race() — First to SETTLE (resolve OR reject) wins
// Use when: you want a timeout pattern or the fastest response wins
const timeout = (ms) => new Promise((_, rej) =>
  setTimeout(() => rej(new Error(`Timed out after ${ms}ms`)), ms)
);

try {
  const result = await Promise.race([slow(), timeout(300)]);
  console.log('race:', result); // 'slow ✅' (resolves at 500ms — but timeout fires at 300ms)
} catch (err) {
  console.error('race timed out:', err.message); // Timed out after 300ms
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. Promise.allSettled() — Waits for ALL, never rejects, gives full picture
// Use when: you want a report of what succeeded and what failed (batch jobs, dashboards)
const results = await Promise.allSettled([fast(), failing(), slow()]);
results.forEach(({ status, value, reason }) => {
  if (status === 'fulfilled') console.log('✅ fulfilled:', value);
  else console.log('❌ rejected:', reason.message);
});
// ✅ fulfilled: fast ✅
// ❌ rejected: boom 💥
// ✅ fulfilled: slow ✅

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. Promise.any() — First to RESOLVE wins; only rejects if ALL fail
// Use when: you have redundant sources and just need one to succeed (CDN fallback, etc.)
try {
  const first = await Promise.any([failing(), fast(), slow()]);
  console.log('any:', first); // 'fast ✅' — ignores the rejection

  // All failing → AggregateError
  await Promise.any([
    Promise.reject(new Error('err1')),
    Promise.reject(new Error('err2')),
  ]);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err instanceof AggregateError); // true
  console.log(err.errors.map(e => e.message)); // ['err1', 'err2
