## ⚡ Short Answer
ES2020 introduced optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`), while ES2021 added logical assignment operators (`||=`, `&&=`, `??=`). These features reduce boilerplate for null/undefined checks, default value assignment, and conditional property access, making code safer and more expressive without nested ternaries or verbose guard clauses.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A set of modern JavaScript operators that handle nullish values, safe property traversal, and conditional assignment in a concise, readable syntax.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before these operators, developers wrote repetitive patterns like `obj && obj.a && obj.a.b` or `value !== null && value !== undefined ? value : default`. These patterns were error-prone, verbose, and hard to scan quickly.

**How it works internally:** `?.` short-circuits evaluation and returns `undefined` if the left-hand side is `null` or `undefined`, instead of throwing a TypeError. `??` only falls back to the right-hand side when the left is strictly `null` or `undefined` (unlike `||`, which also triggers on `0`, `''`, `false`). Logical assignment operators (`||=`, `&&=`, `??=`) are short-circuit assignments — they only assign if the condition is met, avoiding unnecessary writes.

**Benefits:** Eliminates deeply nested null checks, prevents accidental overwrite of falsy-but-valid values (`0`, `''`, `false`), and reduces lines of code significantly in real-world data-access patterns.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Overuse of `?.` can silently swallow errors — if a property is `undefined` due to a bug, `?.` hides it rather than crashing loudly. Requires transpilation (Babel/esbuild) for older browser targets. `??` and `?.` cannot be mixed with `&&`/`||` without explicit parentheses.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. Optional Chaining (?.)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

const user = {
  profile: {
    address: {
      city: "New York"
    }
  }
};

// Old way — verbose and fragile
const city1 = user && user.profile && user.profile.address && user.profile.address.city;

// New way — clean and safe
const city2 = user?.profile?.address?.city;       // "New York"
const zip   = user?.profile?.address?.zip;        // undefined (no throw)
const ghost = user?.nonexistent?.deep?.value;     // undefined (no throw)

// Works on method calls too
const upper = user?.profile?.getName?.();         // undefined if getName doesn't exist

// Works on dynamic keys
const key = "city";
const val = user?.profile?.address?.[key];        // "New York"

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. Nullish Coalescing (??)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

const config = {
  timeout: 0,       // falsy but intentional
  retries: null,    // explicitly unset
  label: "",        // empty string but valid
};

// ❌ || treats 0 and "" as falsy — wrong defaults applied
const timeout1 = config.timeout  || 3000;   // 3000 (BUG: overrides intentional 0)
const label1   = config.label    || "N/A";  // "N/A" (BUG: overrides intentional "")

// ✅ ?? only falls back on null / undefined
const timeout2 = config.timeout  ?? 3000;   // 0     (correct)
const retries2 = config.retries  ?? 3;      // 3     (correct default)
const label2   = config.label    ?? "N/A";  // ""    (correct)

// Chaining ?. with ?? — very common pattern
const displayCity = user?.profile?.address?.city ?? "City not set";
// → "New York"

const displayZip  = user?.profile?.address?.zip  ?? "ZIP not set";
// → "ZIP not set"

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. Logical Assignment (||=, &&=, ??=)  — ES2021
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// ||=  Assign if left side is falsy
let a = null;
a ||= "default";        // a = "default"

let b = "existing";
b ||= "default";        // b = "existing" (no assignment — short-circuits)

// &&=  Assign only if left side is truthy
let isLoggedIn = true;
let username = "alice";
username &&= username.toUpperCase();  // username = "ALICE" (was truthy, updated)
