## ⚡ Short Answer
Prototypal inheritance is JavaScript's native object inheritance model where objects inherit directly from other objects via a prototype chain, rather than from classes. Every object has an internal `[[Prototype]]` link pointing to another object, and property lookups traverse this chain until found or until `null` is reached. ES6 `class` syntax is syntactic sugar built on top of this same mechanism.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A system where objects inherit properties and methods directly from other objects through a linked chain (`__proto__` / `[[Prototype]]`), not through blueprint-style class instantiation as in classical OOP.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** JavaScript was designed for dynamic, flexible scripting where heavyweight class hierarchies felt unnecessary. Prototypal inheritance enables code reuse and shared behavior without rigid class trees, making it easy to compose and extend objects at runtime.

**How it works internally:** When you access a property on an object, the JS engine first looks at the object itself. If not found, it follows the `[[Prototype]]` link to the parent object, then that object's `[[Prototype]]`, and so on up the chain until it hits `null`. Functions have a `.prototype` property; when called with `new`, the created object's `[[Prototype]]` is set to that `.prototype` object. `Object.create(proto)` directly sets the prototype of a new object without invoking a constructor.

**Benefits:** Highly flexible and dynamic — you can modify prototypes at runtime. Enables memory-efficient method sharing (methods live on the prototype, not copied per instance). Allows delegation patterns and mixins that classical inheritance can't express as cleanly.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** The implicit chain can be hard to trace and debug. Mutating a shared prototype affects all instances unexpectedly. Deep chains hurt property lookup performance. `instanceof` checks and `hasOwnProperty` guards add boilerplate. TypeScript's structural typing somewhat masks underlying prototype behavior.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── 1. Raw prototypal chain with Object.create ───────────────────────────
const animal = {
  type: "Animal",
  describe() {
    return `I am a ${this.type} named ${this.name}`;
  },
};

const dog = Object.create(animal); // dog's [[Prototype]] === animal
dog.type = "Dog";
dog.bark = function () {
  return `${this.name} says: Woof!`;
};

const rex = Object.create(dog); // rex -> dog -> animal -> Object.prototype -> null
rex.name = "Rex";

console.log(rex.describe()); // "I am a Dog named Rex"  (inherited from animal)
console.log(rex.bark());     // "Rex says: Woof!"       (inherited from dog)
console.log(rex.hasOwnProperty("name")); // true  — own property
console.log(rex.hasOwnProperty("bark")); // false — lives on dog prototype

// ─── 2. Constructor function pattern (pre-ES6) ────────────────────────────
function Vehicle(make, speed) {
  this.make  = make;   // own properties set per instance
  this.speed = speed;
}
// Shared methods live on the prototype — NOT copied per instance
Vehicle.prototype.accelerate = function (by) {
  this.speed += by;
  return `${this.make} now at ${this.speed} km/h`;
};

function Car(make, speed, doors) {
  Vehicle.call(this, make, speed); // borrow parent constructor
  this.doors = doors;
}
// Wire up the prototype chain: Car -> Vehicle -> Object
Car.prototype = Object.create(Vehicle.prototype);
Car.prototype.constructor = Car; // fix broken constructor reference

Car.prototype.honk = function () {
  return `${this.make}: Beep!`;
};

const tesla = new Car("Tesla", 0, 4);
console.log(tesla.accelerate(100)); // "Tesla now at 100 km/h"
console.log(tesla.honk());          // "Tesla: Beep!"
console.log(tesla instanceof Car);     // true
console.log(tesla instanceof Vehicle); // true — prototype chain check

// ─── 3. ES6 class — same prototype chain, nicer syntax ───────────────────
class Shape {
  constructor(color) {
    this.color = color;
  }
  describe() {
    return `A ${this.color} ${this.constructor.name}`;
  }
}

class Circle extends Shape {
  constructor(color, radius) {
    super(color);        // calls Shape constructor
    this.radius = radius;
  }
  area() {
    return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2;
  }
}

const c = new Circle("red", 5);
console.log(c.describe()); // "A red Circle"  (delegated to Shape.prototype)
console.log(c.area().toFixed(2)); //
