## ⚡ Short Answer
Event bubbling and event capturing are the two phases of event propagation in the DOM. When an event fires on an element, it travels through the DOM tree in a specific order: capturing goes **top-down** (from `document` to the target), and bubbling goes **bottom-up** (from the target back up to `document`). Understanding both phases lets you control exactly where and when your event handlers execute.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Event propagation describes how a DOM event travels through the element hierarchy. There are three phases: capturing (top → target), target (the element itself), and bubbling (target → top).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** The DOM is a tree, and when you click a nested element, multiple ancestors could legitimately claim that click. The W3C model resolves this ambiguity by defining a deterministic two-phase traversal, letting developers intercept events at any level.

**How it works internally:** When an event fires, the browser first runs the **capture phase** — traversing from `window` down through every ancestor to the target element. Then it runs the **bubble phase** — traversing back up from the target to `window`. Handlers registered with `addEventListener(event, fn, true)` fire during capture; those registered with `false` (the default) fire during bubble. `stopPropagation()` halts traversal at any point; `stopImmediatePropagation()` also prevents other handlers on the same element from running.

**Benefits:** Enables **event delegation** — attaching one handler on a parent instead of hundreds on children — which improves performance and handles dynamically added elements automatically.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Propagation can cause unexpected handler triggers if you're unaware of the chain. Over-relying on `stopPropagation()` can break third-party libraries or analytics tools listening higher in the tree.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── HTML Structure ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// <div id="grandparent">
//   <div id="parent">
//     <button id="child">Click Me</button>
//   </div>
// </div>

const grandparent = document.getElementById('grandparent');
const parent      = document.getElementById('parent');
const child       = document.getElementById('child');

// ── BUBBLING (default: 3rd arg = false) ───────────────────────────────
// Fires BOTTOM-UP: child → parent → grandparent
grandparent.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('Grandparent BUBBLE'), false);
parent.addEventListener('click',      () => console.log('Parent BUBBLE'),      false);
child.addEventListener('click',       () => console.log('Child BUBBLE'),       false);

// Click output (bubbling only):
// Child BUBBLE
// Parent BUBBLE
// Grandparent BUBBLE

// ── CAPTURING (3rd arg = true) ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Fires TOP-DOWN: grandparent → parent → child
grandparent.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('Grandparent CAPTURE'), true);
parent.addEventListener('click',      () => console.log('Parent CAPTURE'),      true);
child.addEventListener('click',       () => console.log('Child CAPTURE'),       true);

// Click output (both phases together):
// Grandparent CAPTURE  ← capture phase
// Parent CAPTURE       ← capture phase
// Child CAPTURE        ← target (capture registered)
// Child BUBBLE         ← target (bubble registered)
// Parent BUBBLE        ← bubble phase
// Grandparent BUBBLE   ← bubble phase

// ── STOP PROPAGATION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
parent.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  console.log('Parent caught it, stopping here');
  e.stopPropagation(); // grandparent bubble handler will NOT fire
});

// ── EVENT DELEGATION (practical real-world use) ───────────────────────
// Instead of adding a listener to every <li>, add ONE to the <ul>
const list = document.getElementById('task-list');

list.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  // e.target is the actual element clicked
  if (e.target.matches('li.task-item')) {
    console.log('Task clicked:', e.target.dataset.id);
    // Works even for <li> elements added dynamically later!
  }
});
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: What is event delegation and why is it useful?** A: Event delegation means attaching a single listener to a parent element and using `e.target` to identify which child was clicked. It's more performant than N listeners, and it automatically handles dynamically inserted children.
- **Q: What's the difference between `stopPropagation()` and `preventDefault()`?** A: `stopPropagation()` halts the event from traveling further up/down the DOM tree. `preventDefault()` cancels the browser's default
