## ⚡ Short Answer
WeakMap and WeakSet are JavaScript collections that hold **weak references** to their keys (WeakMap) or values (WeakSet), meaning the garbage collector can reclaim those objects even while they're still in the collection. Unlike Map and Set, they are not enumerable and only accept objects (or registered symbols) as keys/members. This makes them ideal for associating metadata with objects without preventing garbage collection.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** WeakMap is a key-value store where keys must be objects and are held weakly. WeakSet is a collection of unique objects held weakly. Both are part of the ES6 specification.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In a normal Map or Set, holding a reference to an object prevents the GC from ever collecting it — a common source of memory leaks, especially in DOM-heavy or long-lived applications. WeakMap/WeakSet solve this by not counting as a reference for GC purposes.

**How it works internally:** The JavaScript engine tracks WeakMap/WeakSet entries using ephemeron tables or similar GC-aware structures. When no other strong reference to a key/member exists, the GC can collect it and the corresponding entry is automatically removed from the collection. This is why iteration is impossible — the collection's contents are non-deterministic from the runtime's perspective.

**Benefits:** Automatic memory management, no manual cleanup needed, great for caching, private data storage, and tracking object state without memory leaks.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Not iterable (no `.forEach`, `.keys()`, `.size`), only objects as keys/values, not serializable, and harder to debug since you can't inspect all entries.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── WeakMap: Associate private data with an object ───────────────────────

const _private = new WeakMap();

class BankAccount {
  constructor(owner, balance) {
    // Store sensitive data outside the object — no public access
    _private.set(this, { owner, balance });
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    const data = _private.get(this);
    data.balance += amount;
  }

  getBalance() {
    return _private.get(this).balance;
  }
}

const account = new BankAccount("Alice", 1000);
account.deposit(500);
console.log(account.getBalance()); // 1500
console.log(account.owner);        // undefined — truly private!

// When `account` goes out of scope, GC collects it AND removes it from _private.
// No memory leak. No manual cleanup.


// ─── WeakSet: Track "visited" objects without preventing cleanup ──────────

const visited = new WeakSet();

function processNode(node) {
  if (visited.has(node)) {
    console.log("Already processed, skipping.");
    return;
  }
  visited.add(node);
  // ... do work ...
  console.log("Processing node:", node.id);
}

let nodeA = { id: "A" };
let nodeB = { id: "B" };

processNode(nodeA); // Processing node: A
processNode(nodeA); // Already processed, skipping.
processNode(nodeB); // Processing node: B

// Once nodeA is no longer referenced elsewhere, it's GC'd
// and automatically removed from `visited`. No leak.
nodeA = null; // eligible for GC


// ─── Contrast: Map causes memory leak ────────────────────────────────────

const cache = new Map();
function loadUser(userObj) {
  cache.set(userObj, { loaded: true }); // ⚠️ Keeps userObj alive forever
}

// Use WeakMap instead for cache keyed by objects:
const safeCache = new WeakMap();
function loadUserSafe(userObj) {
  safeCache.set(userObj, { loaded: true }); // ✅ GC-friendly
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Why can't you iterate over a WeakMap or WeakSet?** A: Because their entries can be removed at any time by the GC (non-deterministically), exposing iteration would produce inconsistent, unreliable results — so the spec forbids it entirely.
- **Q: Can you use primitive values as WeakMap keys?** A: No, only objects (and registered Symbols in modern engines per ES2023). Primitives are not garbage-collected in the same way, so a weak reference to them is meaningless.
- **Q: What's a real production use case for WeakMap?** A: Storing per-instance private state in classes (as shown above), DOM node metadata in frameworks (e.g., React's internal fiber map), and memoization caches keyed by object identity.

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes
❌ Using a regular Map to cache data keyed by DOM elements or objects, then wondering why memory usage grows unboundedly.
✅ Use WeakMap instead — when the DOM element
