## ⚡ Short Answer
JavaScript uses automatic memory management via a garbage collector, so developers don't manually allocate or free memory. The engine allocates memory when objects are created and reclaims it when objects are no longer reachable from the root (the global object or active call stack). The primary algorithm used in modern engines (V8, SpiderMonkey) is **mark-and-sweep**, often enhanced with generational collection.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** JavaScript's memory lifecycle has three phases: allocation (when you declare variables or create objects), usage (reading/writing), and release (garbage collection reclaims unreachable memory automatically).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Manual memory management (as in C/C++) is error-prone — developers forget to free memory (leaks) or free it too early (dangling pointers). JavaScript abstracts this away to reduce bugs and cognitive load.

**How it works internally:** The garbage collector starts from a set of "roots" (global variables, the current call stack, active closures) and traverses all object references, marking everything reachable. Any object not marked is considered unreachable and its memory is swept/freed. Modern engines like V8 use **generational GC**: the heap is divided into a Young Generation (short-lived objects, collected frequently and cheaply via "Scavenge") and an Old Generation (long-lived objects, collected less often via full mark-and-sweep or incremental/concurrent marking). V8 also uses **incremental marking** and **concurrent sweeping** to avoid long "stop-the-world" pauses that block the main thread.

**Benefits:** No manual `malloc`/`free`, prevents most dangling pointer bugs, heap compaction reduces fragmentation, generational collection is highly optimized for typical allocation patterns.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** GC pauses can cause jank in animations or real-time apps, you can't force or precisely time collection, and memory leaks are still possible (unintentional references keeping objects alive — e.g., forgotten event listeners, closures capturing large objects, global variable accumulation).

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. Normal allocation & automatic collection
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
function createUser(name) {
  // Allocated on the heap when called
  return { name, createdAt: Date.now() };
}
let user = createUser("Alice");
user = null; // Reference dropped → eligible for GC

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. MEMORY LEAK — Forgotten event listener
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
function leakySetup() {
  const bigData = new Array(100_000).fill("leak");

  // ❌ Listener keeps `bigData` alive via closure
  //    even after the component is "destroyed"
  document.getElementById("btn").addEventListener("click", () => {
    console.log(bigData[0]);
  });
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. FIX — Remove the listener when done
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
function safeSetup() {
  const bigData = new Array(100_000).fill("ok");
  const btn = document.getElementById("btn");

  const handler = () => console.log(bigData[0]);
  btn.addEventListener("click", handler);

  // ✅ Call this in componentWillUnmount / cleanup
  return () => btn.removeEventListener("click", handler);
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. WeakMap — Allows GC to collect keys
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
const cache = new WeakMap();

function process(obj) {
  if (!cache.has(obj)) {
    // ✅ When `obj` has no other references,
    //    GC can collect it AND its cache entry
    cache.set(obj, { result: heavyComputation(obj) });
  }
  return cache.get(obj).result;
}

function heavyComputation(obj) {
  return JSON.stringify(obj).length; // placeholder
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 5. Detecting leaks — Chrome DevTools snippet
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// In DevTools > Memory tab:
// 1. Take Heap Snapshot (baseline)
// 2. Perform the suspect action
// 3. Take another Heap Snapshot
// 4. Use "Comparison" view to find objects
//    with growing retained size
```

