## ⚡ Short Answer
Structural sharing is a technique where immutable data structures reuse unchanged portions of the old structure when creating a "new" version, rather than deep-copying everything. This makes immutability practical by keeping memory usage and copy costs O(log n) instead of O(n). It matters because immutability eliminates an entire class of bugs caused by shared mutable state while remaining performant.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Immutability means data cannot be changed after creation — any "update" produces a new value. Structural sharing is the implementation strategy that makes this efficient: new and old versions share the unchanged subtrees of a persistent data structure (typically a tree or trie).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Mutable shared state is the root cause of race conditions, unexpected side effects, and bugs that are hard to reproduce. Pure copying solves the correctness problem but is prohibitively expensive. Structural sharing threads the needle — you get value semantics (safe to share freely) at near-mutation cost.

**How it works internally:** Immutable maps and vectors are usually implemented as Hash Array Mapped Tries (HAMTs) or persistent balanced trees. When you "update" a key, only the path from root to the changed leaf is rebuilt (O(log n) nodes). All sibling branches are referenced, not copied. Equality checks can short-circuit at the first shared reference: if `a === b` (reference equality), their subtrees are identical by definition.

**Benefits:** Predictable state changes (old references are never invalidated), trivial undo/redo (just keep old root references), safe concurrent reads without locks, cheap change detection (reference equality as a proxy for deep equality — the basis of React's `shouldComponentUpdate` and Redux's selectors).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Small constant-factor overhead vs raw mutation (extra pointer indirections). Not suitable for tight numerical loops or large typed arrays where cache locality matters. Garbage collector pressure increases if many intermediate versions are created rapidly. Interop with APIs that expect mutable objects requires explicit conversion.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── Conceptual illustration with a plain JS object ───────────────────────
// (React/Redux pattern — pretend these are Redux state updates)

const stateV1 = {
  user: { id: 1, name: "Alice", prefs: { theme: "dark" } },
  posts: [{ id: 10, title: "Hello" }],
};

// ❌ MUTABLE – breaks referential equality checks, causes stale UI
stateV1.user.name = "Bob"; // same reference, no way to detect change cheaply

// ✅ IMMUTABLE with structural sharing – only the changed path is new
const stateV2 = {
  ...stateV1,                         // re-use 'posts' reference (shared!)
  user: {
    ...stateV1.user,                  // re-use 'prefs' reference (shared!)
    name: "Bob",                      // only this leaf is new
  },
};

console.log(stateV1.posts === stateV2.posts); // true  → shared, 0 cost
console.log(stateV1.user  === stateV2.user);  // false → path was rebuilt
console.log(stateV1.user.prefs === stateV2.user.prefs); // true → shared

// ─── Immer makes this ergonomic while keeping immutability guarantees ──────
import produce from "immer";

const stateV3 = produce(stateV2, (draft) => {
  draft.user.prefs.theme = "light"; // write as if mutable; Immer does sharing
});

console.log(stateV2.posts === stateV3.posts); // true  → untouched, shared
console.log(stateV2.user  === stateV3.user);  // false → path rebuilt
console.log(stateV2.user.name === stateV3.user.name); // "Bob" — shared leaf

// ─── Immutable.js — fully persistent HAMT-based structure ─────────────────
import { Map } from "immutable";

const mapV1 = Map({ a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 });
const mapV2 = mapV1.set("b", 99);       // only 'b' path is new

console.log(mapV1.get("b")); // 2  — original untouched
console.log(mapV2.get("b")); // 99 — new version
// Internal trie shares all unchanged nodes between mapV1 and mapV2
```

