## ⚡ Short Answer
An **iterator** is any object with a `next()` method that returns `{ value, done }` pairs on each call. The **iterable protocol** says an object is iterable if it exposes a `[Symbol.iterator]()` method that returns an iterator. Together they power `for...of`, spread syntax, destructuring, and `Promise.all`.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Two complementary protocols defined in ES2015. The *iterator protocol* defines a standard way to produce a sequence of values. The *iterable protocol* defines how an object advertises that it can be iterated.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before ES2015, every collection (Array, NodeList, arguments, Map, Set, custom linked-list…) needed its own iteration API. The protocols provide a single, unified contract so any consumer (`for...of`, `...spread`, `Array.from`, destructuring, generators) works with any producer automatically.

**How it works internally:** When `for...of` starts, the engine calls `obj[Symbol.iterator]()` to get an iterator object, then calls `.next()` in a loop. Each call returns `{ value: <current>, done: false }` until the sequence ends, at which point `{ value: undefined, done: true }` is returned and the loop exits. If the iterator also has a `return()` method, it is called on early exit (break/throw) for cleanup.

**Benefits:** Universal interoperability — write once, works everywhere; lazy evaluation (values produced on demand, not all up-front); composable (generators can `yield*` other iterables); enables infinite sequences without blowing memory.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Iterator objects are stateful and single-use — you cannot rewind or replay without recreating them. Naive chaining of multiple iterator adapters can be hard to debug. For simple array-only code, plain index loops are still faster in tight inner loops due to less abstraction overhead.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── 1. Consuming the built-in iterable protocol ───────────────────────────
const map = new Map([["a", 1], ["b", 2]]);
for (const [key, val] of map) {           // Map is iterable
  console.log(key, val);                  // a 1 / b 2
}

// ─── 2. Implementing both protocols on a custom object ─────────────────────
class Range {
  constructor(start, end) {
    this.start = start;
    this.end   = end;
  }

  // Iterable protocol: [Symbol.iterator]() must return an iterator
  [Symbol.iterator]() {
    let current = this.start;
    const end   = this.end;

    // Iterator protocol: object with next() returning { value, done }
    return {
      next() {
        return current <= end
          ? { value: current++, done: false }
          : { value: undefined, done: true };
      },
      // Optional: called on early exit (break / throw)
      return(val) {
        console.log("Iterator closed early");
        return { value: val, done: true };
      }
    };
  }
}

const r = new Range(1, 5);
console.log([...r]);          // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]  – spread uses iterable protocol
const [first, second] = r;    // destructuring also uses iterable protocol
console.log(first, second);   // 1 2

for (const n of r) {
  if (n === 3) break;         // triggers return() → "Iterator closed early"
}

// ─── 3. Generator shorthand (iterator + iterable in one) ───────────────────
function* fibonacci() {       // infinite sequence – lazy, no memory issue
  let [a, b] = [0, 1];
  while (true) {
    yield a;
    [a, b] = [b, a + b];
  }
}

const fib = fibonacci();
console.log(fib.next().value);  // 0
console.log(fib.next().value);  // 1
console.log(fib.next().value);  // 1
console.log(fib.next().value);  // 2

// Take first N values from ANY iterable using the protocol
function take(iterable, n) {
  const result = [];
  for (const val of iterable) {
    result.push(val);
    if (result.length === n) break;
  }
  return result;
}

console.log(take(fibonacci(), 8)); // [0,1,1,2,3,5,8,13]
console.log(take(new Range(10, 99), 4)); // [10,11,12,13]

// ─── 4. Iterator helpers (ES2025 – available in modern runtimes) ────────────
// Iterator.from
