## ⚡ Short Answer
TypeScript's basic types are the building blocks of its type system: `string`, `number`, and `boolean` mirror JavaScript primitives; `any` opts out of type checking; `unknown` is the type-safe alternative to `any`; `void` represents the absence of a return value; and `never` represents values that can never occur (unreachable code or always-throwing functions).

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A set of primitive and special types that form the foundation of TypeScript's static type system, layered on top of JavaScript's runtime types.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** JavaScript is dynamically typed, meaning type errors surface only at runtime. TypeScript's basic types allow the compiler to catch mismatches at build time, document intent, and enable editor tooling like autocomplete and inline docs.

**How it works internally:** TypeScript erases all type annotations at compile time — the emitted JavaScript has none of them. The type checker runs a structural analysis pass before emission, comparing assigned values and usage against declared types. `any` tells the checker to skip analysis entirely; `unknown` forces a type guard before use; `never` is inferred when the checker determines a code path is unreachable or a function always throws.

**Benefits:** Catches bugs at compile time, self-documents code, enables safe refactoring, and powers IDE intelligence.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Overusing `any` silently disables safety. `unknown` requires extra narrowing boilerplate. `never` can confuse developers new to the type system. Type annotations add syntactic overhead that pure JS doesn't have.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─── PRIMITIVES ───────────────────────────────────────────────
const username: string = "alice";
const age: number = 30;           // covers int, float, NaN, Infinity
const isAdmin: boolean = true;

// ─── ANY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Opt out of type checking — escape hatch, avoid in production code
let payload: any = JSON.parse('{"id":1}');
payload.foo.bar.baz;              // no compiler error — dangerous!

// ─── UNKNOWN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Type-safe alternative to any — must narrow before use
function parseInput(input: unknown): string {
  if (typeof input === "string") {
    return input.toUpperCase();   // ✅ safe after narrowing
  }
  if (typeof input === "number") {
    return input.toFixed(2);      // ✅ safe after narrowing
  }
  throw new Error("Unsupported input type");
}

// ─── VOID ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// For functions that return nothing (return value is undefined)
function logMessage(msg: string): void {
  console.log(msg);
  // returning a value here would be a compile error
}

// ─── NEVER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A function that NEVER returns (always throws or infinite loop)
function throwError(message: string): never {
  throw new Error(message);
}

// Exhaustiveness checking with never — compiler ensures all cases handled
type Shape = "circle" | "square" | "triangle";

function describeShape(shape: Shape): string {
  switch (shape) {
    case "circle":   return "round";
    case "square":   return "four equal sides";
    case "triangle": return "three sides";
    default:
      // If you add a new Shape without handling it here,
      // TypeScript will error: Type 'newShape' is not assignable to 'never'
      const exhaustiveCheck: never = shape;
      return exhaustiveCheck;
  }
}

// ─── PRACTICAL: API RESPONSE HANDLER ──────────────────────────
interface ApiResponse {
  status: number;
  data: unknown;   // unknown, not any — force callers to narrow
}

function handleResponse(res: ApiResponse): string {
  if (typeof res.data === "string") return res.data;
  if (res.data && typeof res.data === "object") return JSON.stringify(res.data);
  return String(res.data);
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: What is the difference between `unknown` and `any`?** A: Both accept any value, but `unknown` requires a type guard before you can use the value — the compiler enforces narrowing. `any` bypasses the type checker entirely, so operations on `any` values are never checked.
- **Q: When would you actually use `never`?** A: Two main cases — functions that always throw or loop forever (explicit annotation), and exhaustiveness checks in `switch`/`if` chains to ensure every union member is handled; the compiler errors if a new case is added but not covered.
- **Q: Is `void` the same as `undefined`?** A: Not quite.
