## ⚡ Short Answer
Both `type` aliases and `interface` declarations in TypeScript describe the shape of an object, and for most everyday use cases they are interchangeable. The key differences are that interfaces are open and can be merged/extended via declaration merging, while type aliases are closed, more flexible (they can represent unions, primitives, tuples, and mapped types), and cannot be reopened after declaration.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** An `interface` is a named contract describing an object's structure. A `type` alias is a name given to any TypeScript type expression — objects, unions, intersections, primitives, tuples, conditional types, and more.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** TypeScript needed two complementary tools: `interface` to support object-oriented, class-like patterns with extensibility and declaration merging (important for augmenting third-party library types), and `type` to cover the full breadth of TypeScript's structural type system including union and conditional types.

**How it works internally:** Both produce no JavaScript output — they are purely compile-time constructs. The compiler uses them for type-checking only. Interfaces participate in declaration merging: two `interface` blocks with the same name in the same scope are automatically merged into one. Type aliases do not merge; redeclaring the same alias name is a compile error.

**Benefits:**
- `interface`: Natural for public API contracts, supports declaration merging, slightly cleaner error messages for object shapes, works well with `implements` in classes.
- `type`: Supports unions, intersections, tuples, conditional types, mapped types, template literal types — far more expressive for complex type manipulations.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- `interface` cannot express union types (`type Result = Success | Failure` is impossible with `interface`).
- `type` aliases cannot be merged, so augmenting third-party module types requires `interface`.
- Overusing `type` for simple object shapes in a large codebase can reduce readability for developers expecting interface-style contracts.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ── 1. Basic object shape — nearly identical ──────────────────────────────
interface UserInterface {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

type UserType = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
};

// ── 2. Extension ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Interface uses 'extends'
interface AdminInterface extends UserInterface {
  role: string;
}

// Type uses intersection
type AdminType = UserType & { role: string };

// ── 3. Declaration Merging (interface ONLY) ───────────────────────────────
// Useful for augmenting third-party types (e.g. Express Request)
interface Window {
  myCustomProp: string; // merged into the global Window interface
}

// type Window = { myCustomProp: string }; // ❌ ERROR: Duplicate identifier

// ── 4. Union types (type ONLY) ────────────────────────────────────────────
type Status = "active" | "inactive" | "pending"; // ✅ only possible with type
// interface Status = "active" | ...              // ❌ not possible

// ── 5. Tuple (type ONLY) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
type Coordinates = [number, number];
const point: Coordinates = [51.5, -0.1];

// ── 6. Conditional / Mapped types (type ONLY) ────────────────────────────
type Readonly<T> = { readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] };
type IsString<T> = T extends string ? true : false;

// ── 7. Implements works equally well with both ───────────────────────────
class UserEntity implements UserInterface {
  constructor(public id: number, public name: string) {}
}

class UserEntity2 implements UserType {
  constructor(public id: number, public name: string) {}
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: When should you prefer `interface` over `type`?** A: Prefer `interface` when defining the public contract of an object or class, especially in library code where consumers may need to extend or augment it via declaration merging.
- **Q: Can a `type` alias extend an `interface` and vice versa?** A: Yes — an `interface` can `extends` a `type` alias (as long as it resolves to an object shape), and a `type` can intersect (`&`) an `interface`. They are largely interoperable.
- **Q: Are there performance differences between the two?** A: Interfaces can be more efficient to check in large projects because the TypeScript compiler can cache named interface shapes. Complex `type` expressions (especially recursive or conditional ones) may increase compilation time.

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes
❌ Believing `interface` and `type` are completely identical and always interchangeable.
✅ They overlap heavily for object shapes, but only
