## ⚡ Short Answer
Union types (`A | B`) represent a value that can be **one of several types**, while intersection types (`A & B`) represent a value that is **all of those types simultaneously**. Union types are about flexibility ("this OR that"), while intersection types are about composition ("this AND that"). Both are foundational to TypeScript's structural type system and enable powerful type-safe patterns without runtime overhead.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Union types are written as `T1 | T2 | T3` and mean the value can be any one of those types. Intersection types are written as `T1 & T2 & T3` and mean the value must satisfy all of those types at once.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before these constructs, you'd fall back to `any` or overloaded functions to handle values of multiple shapes. Union types let you model real-world "variant" data (e.g., a result that's either a success or an error). Intersection types let you compose types the way you compose behaviors (e.g., a logger that is also a serializer).

**How it works internally:** TypeScript uses **structural subtyping** — it checks the shape of objects, not their declared names. A union type narrows at runtime via type guards (`typeof`, `instanceof`, discriminant fields). An intersection type is resolved by merging all member properties into one flat shape; if the same property key exists in multiple members with incompatible types, the result is `never`.

**Benefits:** No runtime cost (types are erased). Enables exhaustive checks with discriminated unions. Intersection types allow mixin-style composition. Both integrate cleanly with generics.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Deep union types can produce confusing error messages. Intersecting primitive types (e.g., `string & number`) yields `never`. Intersection types don't model prototype chains — only shape. Overuse of unions without discriminants makes narrowing cumbersome.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─── UNION TYPES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// 1. Simple literal union
type Direction = "north" | "south" | "east" | "west";

// 2. Discriminated union (preferred pattern for variants)
type ApiResult<T> =
  | { status: "success"; data: T }
  | { status: "error";   message: string; code: number };

function handleResult(result: ApiResult<string[]>): void {
  // TypeScript narrows automatically based on `status`
  if (result.status === "success") {
    console.log("Items:", result.data.join(", ")); // data is string[]
  } else {
    console.error(`Error ${result.code}: ${result.message}`); // code & message available
  }
}

// 3. Exhaustiveness check — catches missing cases at compile time
function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error("Unhandled variant: " + JSON.stringify(x));
}

type Shape =
  | { kind: "circle";    radius: number }
  | { kind: "rectangle"; width: number; height: number };

function area(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case "circle":    return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
    case "rectangle": return shape.width * shape.height;
    default:          return assertNever(shape); // compile error if a case is missing
  }
}

// ─── INTERSECTION TYPES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// 4. Composing independent capability interfaces
interface Serializable {
  serialize(): string;
}

interface Loggable {
  log(message: string): void;
}

// Intersection: must satisfy BOTH interfaces
type LoggableService = Serializable & Loggable;

class UserService implements LoggableService {
  serialize(): string {
    return JSON.stringify({ service: "UserService" });
  }
  log(message: string): void {
    console.log(`[UserService] ${message}`);
  }
}

// 5. Utility: merging two plain objects (generic mixin helper)
function merge<A extends object, B extends object>(a: A, b: B): A & B {
  return { ...a, ...b };
}

const config   = { host: "localhost", port: 5432 };
const metadata = { version: 2, readonly: true };
const full     = merge(config, metadata);
// full is { host: string; port: number; version: number; readonly: boolean }
console.log(full.host, full.version); // TS knows both properties exist

// 6. Edge case — conflicting types collapse to `never`
type Impossible = string & number; // → never; no value can satisfy both
```

