## ⚡ Short Answer
Enums in TypeScript are a way to define a set of named constants, either numeric or string-based, that make code more readable and self-documenting. While they seem like a natural fit for representing fixed sets of values, they come with surprising pitfalls around type safety, bundle size, and JavaScript interop that often lead experienced developers to prefer union types instead.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** An `enum` is a TypeScript construct that groups related named constants under a single type. They come in three flavors: numeric enums (default), string enums, and const enums.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Enums replace magic strings or numbers scattered throughout code (e.g., `status === 2`) with readable named constants (e.g., `status === Status.Active`), reducing bugs from typos and improving discoverability via IntelliSense.

**How it works internally:** Regular numeric enums compile to a JavaScript object with a bidirectional mapping — both `Enum.Key → value` and `Enum[value] → "Key"`. String enums compile to a one-way object. `const enum` is inlined at compile time and produces no runtime object at all, but this breaks in certain module systems.

**Benefits:** Named constants improve readability; TypeScript enforces only valid enum members are used; enums are iterable at runtime (for numeric/string enums); they work well as flags with bitwise numeric values.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Numeric enums are not type-safe (any `number` is assignable to a numeric enum type); const enums break with `isolatedModules` (used by Babel, esbuild, Vite); regular enums emit extra JavaScript and increase bundle size; string enums require exact string values and can't be used with plain string literals without a cast; they are a TypeScript-only concept that doesn't exist in JavaScript, creating a conceptual gap.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─── 1. Numeric Enum (default) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
enum Direction {
  Up,    // 0
  Down,  // 1
  Left,  // 2
  Right, // 3
}

// ⚠️ PITFALL: Numeric enums are NOT type-safe — any number is assignable
function move(dir: Direction) {
  console.log(dir);
}
move(Direction.Up); // ✅ fine
move(999);          // ✅ TypeScript allows this — that's a bug waiting to happen

// ─── 2. String Enum (recommended over numeric) ───────────────────────────────
enum Status {
  Active   = "ACTIVE",
  Inactive = "INACTIVE",
  Pending  = "PENDING",
}

function setStatus(s: Status) {
  console.log(s);
}
setStatus(Status.Active);   // ✅ "ACTIVE"
// setStatus("ACTIVE");     // ❌ Compile error — plain strings not assignable
// This is safer but can feel rigid when working with external APIs

// ─── 3. Const Enum (zero runtime cost, but has pitfalls) ─────────────────────
const enum LogLevel {
  Error = 1,
  Warn  = 2,
  Info  = 3,
}

// Compiles to: if (level === 1) — the enum is fully erased at runtime
function log(level: LogLevel, msg: string) {
  if (level === LogLevel.Error) console.error(msg);
}

// ⚠️ PITFALL: const enums break with isolatedModules (Babel, Vite, esbuild)
// tsconfig: "isolatedModules": true  → const enums cause a build error

// ─── 4. The Modern Alternative: Union Types ───────────────────────────────────
// Prefer this in most codebases — no runtime artifact, fully type-safe
type HttpMethod = "GET" | "POST" | "PUT" | "DELETE";

function request(method: HttpMethod, url: string) {
  console.log(`${method} ${url}`);
}
request("GET", "/api/users");    // ✅ string literal works directly
// request("PATCH", "/api/users"); // ❌ compile error

// ─── 5. As-Const Object (best of both worlds) ────────────────────────────────
// Gives you: named constants, iteration, type safety, AND plain JS compatibility
const Role = {
  Admin:  "admin",
  User:   "user",
  Guest:  "guest",
} as const;

type Role = typeof Role[keyof typeof Role]; // "admin" | "user" | "guest"

function assignRole(r: Role) {
  console.log(r);
}
assignRole(Role.Admin); // ✅
assignRole("user");     // ✅ plain string also works — great for APIs
