## ⚡ Short Answer
TypeScript strict mode is a compiler flag (`"strict": true` in `tsconfig.json`) that enables a collection of type-checking options that catch more potential runtime errors at compile time. It's essentially a "best practices" bundle that enforces sound typing discipline. Most modern TypeScript projects and frameworks (Next.js, Angular, NestJS) enable it by default because the safety gains far outweigh the initial migration cost.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A shorthand tsconfig flag that activates a group of strict type-checking compiler options simultaneously, rather than enabling each one individually.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without strict mode, TypeScript silently allows many unsafe patterns — implicit `any`, unchecked `null`/`undefined` access, unbound `this` — that compile fine but blow up at runtime. Strict mode closes these gaps, making the type system actually enforce correctness instead of just providing IDE hints.

**How it works internally:** Setting `"strict": true` is syntactic sugar that flips on a set of sub-flags. Each sub-flag instructs the TypeScript compiler (tsc) to apply additional type narrowing rules during its semantic analysis phase. You can still override individual flags after enabling `strict: true` if needed.

**Benefits:** Catches null-reference errors, implicit `any` leaks, unsafe `this` bindings, and incorrect function parameter handling at compile time — before tests or production. Greatly improves refactor safety and IDE intelligence.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds upfront migration cost on existing codebases (can generate hundreds of errors). Requires more explicit type annotations. Can feel overly pedantic for quick prototypes or scripts where safety isn't the priority.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// tsconfig.json — enabling strict mode
// {
//   "compilerOptions": {
//     "strict": true   // enables ALL flags below as a bundle
//   }
// }

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. strictNullChecks
//    null and undefined are NOT assignable to other types
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
function greet(name: string) {
  console.log(name.toUpperCase());
}

// Without strictNullChecks: this compiles fine, crashes at runtime
// greet(null); // ❌ ERROR with strictNullChecks: Argument of type 'null' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string'

// Fix: make the intent explicit
function greetSafe(name: string | null) {
  if (name === null) return "Hello, stranger";
  return name.toUpperCase(); // ✅ TypeScript now knows name is string here
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. noImplicitAny
//    Parameters/variables must have an explicit or inferable type
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// ❌ Without strict: TypeScript silently infers `any` — type safety gone
// function process(data) { return data.value; }

// ✅ Must be explicit
function process(data: { value: number }): number {
  return data.value;
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. strictFunctionTypes
//    Function parameter types are checked contravariantly (no unsafe narrowing)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
type Handler = (event: MouseEvent) => void;

// ❌ Without strictFunctionTypes, this unsafely assigns a narrower handler:
// const handler: Handler = (e: Event) => {}; // would fail — Event is wider than MouseEvent

// ✅ Safe assignment — types must align correctly
const clickHandler: Handler = (e: MouseEvent) => {
  console.log(e.clientX, e.clientY);
};

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. strictPropertyInitialization
//    Class properties must be initialized in the constructor (or be optional)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
class UserService {
  // ❌ Without strict: this compiles but `name` is undefined at runtime
  // name: string;

  // ✅ Option A: initialize in constructor
  name: string;

  // ✅ Option B: declare as possibly undefined
  email: string | undefined;

  // ✅ Option C: use definite assignment assertion (when you know it's set elsewhere)
  dbClient!: unknown; // the ! tells TS "trust me, this will be set"

  constructor(name: string) {
    this.name = name;
  }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// 5. noImplicitThis
//    'this' inside functions must have a declared type
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// ❌ Without strict: 'this' is implicitly
