## ⚡ Short Answer
Optional properties (marked with `?`) allow object members to be omitted when creating an object, while readonly properties (marked with `readonly`) prevent reassignment after initialization. Together they give you fine-grained control over object shape and mutability, helping catch bugs at compile time rather than runtime.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Optional properties use the `?` syntax to mark a property as possibly `undefined`, while `readonly` properties are compile-time enforced immutability constraints on object members.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In real APIs and domain models, not every field is always present (e.g., a user's middle name or optional address), and some fields should never change after creation (e.g., a database `id` or a config value). Without these modifiers, you'd either need overly permissive types or verbose runtime guards everywhere.

**How it works internally:** The TypeScript compiler erases both modifiers at runtime — they produce no JavaScript output. Optional properties widen the type to `T | undefined`, so TypeScript forces you to handle the missing case before using the value. `readonly` prevents the assignment operator from targeting that property after the object is constructed; it's structurally enforced, meaning two types with the same shape but different `readonly` modifiers are still assignable in many contexts.

**Benefits:** Catches missing-field bugs and accidental mutation bugs at compile time with zero runtime overhead. Makes API contracts self-documenting and reduces the need for defensive checks.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** `readonly` is shallow — it prevents reassigning the property reference but does NOT deep-freeze nested objects or arrays. Optional properties can proliferate `undefined` checks throughout your code if overused. Neither modifier affects the emitted JavaScript, so runtime mutation of `readonly` properties is still possible via type assertions or `as any`.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─── Domain Model ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

interface User {
  readonly id: number;          // Cannot be changed after creation
  readonly createdAt: Date;     // Structural immutability at compile time
  name: string;                 // Required, mutable
  email: string;                // Required, mutable
  middleName?: string;          // Optional: string | undefined
  address?: Address;            // Optional nested object
}

interface Address {
  street: string;
  city: string;
  zip?: string;                 // Optional within optional
}

// ─── Usage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

const user: User = {
  id: 42,
  createdAt: new Date(),
  name: "Alice",
  email: "alice@example.com",
  // middleName and address are omitted — perfectly valid
};

// ✅ Safe narrowing before using an optional property
function getDisplayName(user: User): string {
  // TypeScript forces us to handle undefined
  return user.middleName
    ? `${user.name} ${user.middleName}`
    : user.name;
}

// ✅ Optional chaining for nested optionals
function getCity(user: User): string | undefined {
  return user.address?.city;  // No error, returns undefined if address missing
}

// ─── Readonly Array ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

const ALLOWED_ROLES: readonly string[] = ["admin", "editor", "viewer"];
// ALLOWED_ROLES.push("superuser"); // ❌ Compile error: push does not exist on readonly array

// ─── Readonly in Classes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

class Product {
  readonly sku: string;
  name: string;
  price?: number;               // Optional: product might not have a price yet

  constructor(sku: string, name: string, price?: number) {
    this.sku = sku;             // ✅ Allowed in constructor
    this.name = name;
    this.price = price;
  }

  updatePrice(newPrice: number) {
    this.price = newPrice;      // ✅ Mutable property — allowed
    // this.sku = "NEW-SKU";   // ❌ Compile error: cannot assign to readonly property
  }
}

// ─── Utility Types leveraging these modifiers ─────────────────────────────────

type PartialUser = Partial<User>;         // All properties become optional
type ImmutableUser = Readonly<User>;      // All properties become readonly
type RequiredUser = Required<User>;       // All optional properties become required

// ─── SHALLOW readonly trap — be aware! ───────────────────────────────────────

const config: Readonly<{ db: { host: string } }> = {
  db: { host: "localhost" },
};
// config.db = { host: "other" };  // ❌ Compile error — reference is readonly
config.db.host = "hacked";         // ✅
