## ⚡ Short Answer
Utility types are built-in generic types in TypeScript that transform existing types into new ones without rewriting them from scratch. They handle common type transformations like making all fields optional, picking a subset of keys, or excluding certain types — reducing boilerplate and keeping your types DRY.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A set of generic type helpers built into TypeScript's standard library that operate on existing types to produce new, modified types at compile time.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before utility types, developers had to manually re-declare types with slight variations (e.g., a separate `UserUpdate` type with all optional fields). Utility types let you derive new types from existing ones, ensuring changes to the source type automatically propagate.

**How it works internally:** They use TypeScript's mapped types and conditional types under the hood. For example, `Partial<T>` is implemented as `{ [P in keyof T]?: T[P] }` — it iterates over every key in `T` and adds the `?` modifier. `Exclude<T, U>` uses conditional types: `T extends U ? never : T`.

**Benefits:** Eliminates repetitive type declarations, enforces single source of truth, improves maintainability, and makes refactoring safer — change one base type and all derived utility types update automatically.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Deeply nested or chained utility types can produce hard-to-read error messages and confusing hover tooltips in IDEs. Overuse can make types feel implicit and harder to understand for newcomers compared to explicit interface declarations.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// --- Base type ---
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  age: number;
  role: "admin" | "editor" | "viewer";
}

// --- Partial<T>: All fields become optional (great for PATCH/update payloads) ---
type UserUpdate = Partial<User>;
// { id?: number; name?: string; email?: string; age?: number; role?: ... }

function updateUser(id: number, changes: UserUpdate): void {
  console.log(`Updating user ${id}`, changes);
}
updateUser(1, { name: "Alice" }); // ✅ Only name, all others optional

// --- Required<T>: All fields become mandatory (opposite of Partial) ---
type StrictUser = Required<User>;
// All fields non-optional — useful when you know a fully-loaded object is returned

// --- Pick<T, K>: Select a subset of keys ---
type UserPreview = Pick<User, "id" | "name">;
// { id: number; name: string } — useful for list views or public APIs

const preview: UserPreview = { id: 42, name: "Bob" };

// --- Omit<T, K>: Remove specific keys from a type ---
type PublicUser = Omit<User, "email" | "age">;
// { id: number; name: string; role: "admin" | "editor" | "viewer" }
// Great for removing sensitive or internal fields

// --- Record<K, V>: Build a type with specific keys mapping to a value type ---
type RolePermissions = Record<User["role"], string[]>;
const permissions: RolePermissions = {
  admin:  ["read", "write", "delete"],
  editor: ["read", "write"],
  viewer: ["read"],
};

// Record with string index — useful for dynamic lookup maps
type CacheStore = Record<string, User>;
const cache: CacheStore = {};

// --- Exclude<T, U>: Remove types from a union ---
type NonAdminRole = Exclude<User["role"], "admin">;
// "editor" | "viewer"

function restrictToNonAdmin(role: NonAdminRole) {
  console.log("Non-admin action for role:", role);
}

// --- Extract<T, U>: Opposite of Exclude — keep only matching union members ---
type AdminOnly = Extract<User["role"], "admin">;
// "admin"

// --- NonNullable<T>: Remove null and undefined from a type ---
type SafeName = NonNullable<string | null | undefined>;
// string

// --- ReturnType<T>: Infer the return type of a function ---
function fetchUser(): User {
  return { id: 1, name: "Carol", email: "c@x.com", age: 30, role: "editor" };
}
type FetchedUser = ReturnType<typeof fetchUser>; // User

// --- Readonly<T>: All fields become read-only ---
type ImmutableUser = Readonly<User>;
const frozen: ImmutableUser = { id: 1, name: "Dan", email: "d@x.com", age: 25, role: "viewer" };
// frozen.name = "X"; // ❌ Error: Cannot assign to 'name' because it is a read-only property

// --- Combining
