## ⚡ Short Answer
Conditional types in TypeScript let you define types that resolve differently based on a condition, using the syntax `T extends U ? X : Y`. They enable expressive, logic-driven type transformations that adapt to the shape of the types passed in. This is the backbone of many of TypeScript's powerful built-in utility types like `NonNullable`, `ReturnType`, and `Exclude`.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A conditional type evaluates a type-level condition — `T extends U ? X : Y` — and resolves to `X` if `T` is assignable to `U`, otherwise resolves to `Y`. They work much like ternary operators, but at the type system level.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before conditional types, you couldn't write types that changed their shape based on what was passed to them. You'd be forced into union types or overloads that couldn't capture nuanced relationships. Conditional types let you encode "if this type looks like X, give me Y" logic directly in the type system, enabling much more precise type inference.

**How it works internally:** TypeScript evaluates `T extends U ? X : Y` at compile time. When `T` is a union type, conditional types distribute over the union automatically (distributive conditional types) — so `string | number extends string ? X : Y` becomes `(string extends string ? X : Y) | (number extends string ? X : Y)`. You can also use the `infer` keyword inside the condition to extract and capture parts of the matched type.

**Benefits:** Enables reusable, generic utility types; powers the `infer` keyword for type extraction; supports distributive behavior over unions; makes conditional API return types possible without overloads.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Can become deeply nested and hard to read; distributive behavior can surprise you if you don't expect it; complex conditional types can slow down TypeScript's type checker in large codebases; debugging failures requires understanding how TypeScript defers evaluation of unresolved generics.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// --- 1. Basic conditional type ---
type IsString<T> = T extends string ? "yes" : "no";

type A = IsString<string>;  // "yes"
type B = IsString<number>;  // "no"

// --- 2. Distributive conditional type (applies to each union member) ---
type Flatten<T> = T extends Array<infer Item> ? Item : T;

type C = Flatten<string[]>;        // string
type D = Flatten<number[]>;        // number
type E = Flatten<boolean>;         // boolean (not an array, passes through)

// --- 3. Using `infer` to extract a type ---
type ReturnType<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : never;

function greet(name: string): string {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}

type GreetReturn = ReturnType<typeof greet>; // string

// --- 4. Practical utility: strip null/undefined (like NonNullable) ---
type NotNullable<T> = T extends null | undefined ? never : T;

type F = NotNullable<string | null | undefined>; // string

// --- 5. Conditional return type based on input ---
type DataResponse<T extends "json" | "text"> =
  T extends "json" ? Record<string, unknown> : string;

function fetchData<T extends "json" | "text">(format: T): DataResponse<T> {
  if (format === "json") {
    return {} as DataResponse<T>;
  }
  return "" as DataResponse<T>;
}

const jsonResult = fetchData("json");  // Record<string, unknown>
const textResult = fetchData("text");  // string

// --- 6. Preventing distribution with a tuple wrapper ---
// Without wrapping, `string | number extends string` distributes.
// Wrapping stops distribution so the whole union is tested at once.
type IsExactlyString<T> = [T] extends [string] ? "yes" : "no";

type G = IsExactlyString<string | number>; // "no" (union tested as whole)
type H = IsExactlyString<string>;          // "yes"
```

