## ⚡ Short Answer
The `infer` keyword in TypeScript is used within conditional types to declare a type variable that TypeScript will infer at the point where the condition is evaluated. It lets you "capture" and reuse a type that would otherwise be inaccessible, such as extracting the return type of a function or the element type of an array.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `infer` is a contextual keyword that can only appear in the `extends` clause of a conditional type. It introduces a new type variable that TypeScript binds to whatever type fits in that position during pattern matching.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before `infer`, you couldn't programmatically extract sub-types from complex types. For example, given a function type, you had no way to pull out just its return type in a generic, reusable way. `infer` enables type-level destructuring, making powerful utility types possible.

**How it works internally:** When TypeScript evaluates a conditional type `T extends SomeType<infer U> ? U : never`, it checks whether `T` is assignable to `SomeType<infer U>`. If it is, TypeScript binds `U` to the matched type and the true branch resolves using that bound variable. It's essentially pattern matching on the type system.

**Benefits:** Enables extraction of component types (return types, parameter types, promise resolution types, array element types). Powers built-in utility types like `ReturnType<T>`, `Parameters<T>`, and `Awaited<T>`. Encourages reusable, composable type logic.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Only valid inside conditional types — you can't use `infer` in other positions. Complex `infer` chains can become difficult to read and debug. Overuse can make types harder for teammates to understand, increasing cognitive overhead in codebases with mixed TypeScript experience levels.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// --- 1. Basic: Extract the return type of any function ---
type MyReturnType<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : never;

function greet(name: string): string {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}

type GreetReturn = MyReturnType<typeof greet>; // string

// --- 2. Extract the first parameter type ---
type FirstParam<T> = T extends (first: infer P, ...rest: any[]) => any ? P : never;

function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}

type AddFirstParam = FirstParam<typeof add>; // number

// --- 3. Unwrap a Promise (like the built-in Awaited<T>) ---
type Unwrap<T> = T extends Promise<infer Value> ? Value : T;

type ResolvedUser = Unwrap<Promise<{ id: number; name: string }>>; // { id: number; name: string }
type PlainString  = Unwrap<string>;                                 // string (not a Promise, returns T)

// --- 4. Extract array element type ---
type ElementType<T> = T extends (infer Item)[] ? Item : never;

type NumElement = ElementType<number[]>; // number
type StrElement = ElementType<string[]>; // string

// --- 5. Real-world: infer inside a mapped/conditional utility ---
// Extract the resolved value from any async function
type AsyncReturnType<T extends (...args: any[]) => Promise<any>> =
  T extends (...args: any[]) => Promise<infer R> ? R : never;

async function fetchUser(): Promise<{ id: number; email: string }> {
  return { id: 1, email: "alice@example.com" };
}

type User = AsyncReturnType<typeof fetchUser>;
// type User = { id: number; email: string }

// --- 6. Infer with union distribution ---
// When T is a union, conditional types distribute over each member
type Flatten<T> = T extends Array<infer Item> ? Item : T;

type Mixed = Flatten<string[] | number[] | boolean>;
// string | number | boolean
```

