## ⚡ Short Answer
Template literal types in TypeScript allow you to construct new string literal types by embedding other types into string templates using backtick syntax. They work just like JavaScript template literals, but at the type level, enabling powerful string manipulation and type inference at compile time.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A TypeScript type-level feature that uses backtick syntax (`` `prefix_${T}_suffix` ``) to create new string union types by combining string literals with other types.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before template literal types, there was no ergonomic way to express relationships between string-based types — for example, that an event name `"onClick"` corresponds to a handler property `"onClickHandler"`, or that a CSS property `"margin"` maps to `"margin-top"`, `"margin-bottom"`, etc. They eliminate brittle manual type duplication.

**How it works internally:** TypeScript distributes template literal types over union members automatically. If `T = "a" | "b"`, then `` `get_${T}` `` resolves to `"get_a" | "get_b"`. TypeScript also provides four intrinsic utility types that work hand-in-hand: `Uppercase<S>`, `Lowercase<S>`, `Capitalize<S>`, and `Uncapitalize<S>`.

**Benefits:** Enables generating large string union types programmatically, enforces naming conventions at the type level, powers advanced mapped types and conditional inference patterns, and reduces manual repetition drastically.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Very large unions (thousands of members) can slow down the TypeScript compiler significantly. Complex nested template types can become hard to read and debug, and inference through multiple layers can sometimes surprise you with `never` or overly broad results.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// --- 1. Basic template literal type ---
type Direction = "top" | "right" | "bottom" | "left";
type MarginProp = `margin-${Direction}`;
// Resolves to: "margin-top" | "margin-right" | "margin-bottom" | "margin-left"

// --- 2. Generating event handler keys from entity names ---
type Entity = "user" | "product" | "order";
type EventType = "Created" | "Updated" | "Deleted";
type DomainEvent = `${Entity}${EventType}`;
// "userCreated" | "userUpdated" | "userDeleted" | "productCreated" | ...

// --- 3. Intrinsic string manipulation types ---
type Raw = "firstName";
type Upper = Uppercase<Raw>;       // "FIRSTNAME"
type Capped = Capitalize<Raw>;     // "FirstName"
type Lower = Lowercase<Capped>;    // "firstname"

// --- 4. Practical pattern: building a typed event emitter ---
type EventMap = {
  click: { x: number; y: number };
  keydown: { key: string };
  resize: { width: number; height: number };
};

// Generate "onClick" | "onKeydown" | "onResize" from EventMap keys
type HandlerName<K extends string> = `on${Capitalize<K>}`;
type Handlers = {
  [K in keyof EventMap as HandlerName<K>]: (payload: EventMap[K]) => void;
};
// Resolves to:
// { onClick: (payload: { x: number; y: number }) => void;
//   onKeydown: (payload: { key: string }) => void;
//   onResize: (payload: { width: number; height: number }) => void; }

// --- 5. Inferring parts of a string type ---
type ExtractRouteParam<T extends string> =
  T extends `${infer _Start}:${infer Param}/${infer Rest}`
    ? Param | ExtractRouteParam<`/${Rest}`>
    : T extends `${infer _Start}:${infer Param}`
    ? Param
    : never;

type Params = ExtractRouteParam<"/users/:userId/posts/:postId">;
// Resolves to: "userId" | "postId"

// Usage: type-safe route parameter access
function getParams<T extends string>(route: T): Record<ExtractRouteParam<T>, string> {
  // runtime implementation would parse the actual URL
  return {} as Record<ExtractRouteParam<T>, string>;
}

const params = getParams("/users/:userId/posts/:postId");
params.userId;   // ✅ string
params.postId;   // ✅ string
// params.fooBar; // ❌ compile error
```

