## ⚡ Short Answer
Discriminated unions (also called tagged unions or algebraic data types) are a TypeScript pattern where a union of types each share a common literal "discriminant" property that uniquely identifies each variant. The TypeScript compiler uses this tag to narrow the type inside conditionals, and when combined with a `never`-based exhaustive check, it guarantees at compile time that every possible variant is handled — so adding a new variant breaks the build if you forget to update your switch statement.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A discriminated union is a `type` or `interface` union where every member has a shared property (the discriminant) typed as a unique string or numeric literal, e.g. `kind: "circle" | "square"`.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Plain union types like `Circle | Square` give you no structural guarantee that a switch/if-else handles all cases. Discriminated unions let the compiler prove exhaustiveness, eliminating entire classes of runtime bugs when shapes or states evolve.

**How it works internally:** TypeScript's control flow analysis (CFA) tracks which literal values the discriminant property can hold inside each branch. After all known variants are handled, the remaining type in an `else` or `default` branch narrows to `never`. An `assertNever(x: never)` helper exploits this: if you pass anything to it, the compiler errors because only `never` is assignable to `never`.

**Benefits:** Compile-time exhaustiveness, excellent IDE autocomplete per variant, zero runtime overhead (pure type-level), self-documenting state machines, and safe refactoring when new variants are added.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Every member of the union must include the discriminant property, which can feel verbose. Deep nesting of unions gets complex. Not ideal when variants share large amounts of overlapping data (a class hierarchy may be cleaner). The pattern is TypeScript-specific; plain JavaScript has no enforcement.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// --- 1. Define the discriminated union ---
interface Circle {
  kind: "circle";   // discriminant: unique literal
  radius: number;
}

interface Rectangle {
  kind: "rectangle"; // discriminant: unique literal
  width: number;
  height: number;
}

interface Triangle {
  kind: "triangle";  // discriminant: unique literal
  base: number;
  height: number;
}

type Shape = Circle | Rectangle | Triangle;

// --- 2. Exhaustive-check helper ---
// If any variant is unhandled, TypeScript will error here at compile time
function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error(`Unhandled variant: ${JSON.stringify(x)}`);
}

// --- 3. Exhaustive switch using the discriminant ---
function area(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case "circle":
      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;   // shape is narrowed to Circle here

    case "rectangle":
      return shape.width * shape.height;    // narrowed to Rectangle

    case "triangle":
      return 0.5 * shape.base * shape.height; // narrowed to Triangle

    default:
      // If you add a new Shape variant and forget a case above,
      // TypeScript will error: Argument of type 'NewShape' is not
      // assignable to parameter of type 'never'.
      return assertNever(shape);
  }
}

// --- 4. Real-world: async action results (Redux-style) ---
type FetchState<T> =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "success"; data: T }
  | { status: "error";   error: string };

function renderUserState(state: FetchState<{ name: string }>): string {
  switch (state.status) {
    case "idle":    return "Waiting...";
    case "loading": return "Loading...";
    case "success": return `Hello, ${state.data.name}`;  // data only accessible here
    case "error":   return `Error: ${state.error}`;       // error only accessible here
    default:        return assertNever(state);
  }
}

// --- 5. Usage ---
console.log(area({ kind: "circle",    radius: 5 }));         // ~78.54
console.log(area({ kind: "rectangle", width: 4, height: 6 })); // 24
console.log(area({ kind: "triangle",  base: 3, height: 8 }));  // 12
```

