## ⚡ Short Answer
Declaration merging is TypeScript's ability to combine multiple declarations sharing the same name into a single definition. When the compiler encounters two or more declarations with the same name, it merges them into one entity. This is most commonly used to extend interfaces, augment modules, or combine namespaces with classes or functions.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A compile-time feature where TypeScript automatically merges multiple declarations of the same identifier (interfaces, namespaces, enums, or combinations thereof) into a single merged type definition.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** It enables incremental or distributed type definitions — you can extend third-party library types without forking them, split large interface definitions across files, and augment existing modules with new members. This is foundational to how DefinitelyTyped and module augmentation work.

**How it works internally:** The TypeScript compiler collects all declarations for a given name in a scope and merges their member sets. For interfaces, all properties and methods are unioned into one type. For namespaces merged with classes/functions, the namespace contributes static-like members. Non-function members must not conflict; function members become overload signatures ordered by declaration sequence (later declarations first, except string literals which go first).

**Benefits:** Allows non-breaking extension of existing types, enables module augmentation for third-party packages, supports the "extend an interface from different files" pattern cleanly, and keeps the type system open for extension without modification.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Can cause confusion when the same name is declared in multiple places — a developer may not realize a type has been augmented elsewhere. Merging classes is not supported (only interfaces and namespaces merge freely). Over-reliance on merging can make codebases harder to navigate and reason about statically.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─── 1. INTERFACE MERGING ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// Declare the same interface in two separate blocks (simulating separate files)
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

interface User {
  email: string;       // Merged: now User has id, name, AND email
  createdAt: Date;
}

const user: User = {
  id: 1,
  name: "Alice",
  email: "alice@example.com",
  createdAt: new Date(),
};

// ─── 2. FUNCTION + NAMESPACE MERGING ────────────────────────────────────
// A function and a namespace can share the same name.
// The namespace adds "static-like" members to the function object.
function createValidator(rules: string[]): boolean {
  return rules.length > 0;
}

namespace createValidator {
  export const defaultRules = ["required", "minLength"];
  export function fromConfig(config: object): string[] {
    return Object.keys(config);
  }
}

console.log(createValidator.defaultRules);          // ["required", "minLength"]
console.log(createValidator.fromConfig({ a: 1 }));  // ["a"]

// ─── 3. MODULE AUGMENTATION (real-world pattern) ─────────────────────────
// Extend a third-party library's types without modifying the original package.
// Imagine 'express' defines: interface Request { body: any }
// We want to add our own typed property.

// In a custom .d.ts or a dedicated augmentation file:
declare module "express" {
  interface Request {
    currentUser?: { id: string; role: string }; // merged into express's Request
  }
}

// Now in your route handler, TypeScript knows about currentUser:
// app.get("/profile", (req: Request, res: Response) => {
//   console.log(req.currentUser?.role); // ✅ No type error
// });

// ─── 4. ENUM MERGING (only with namespaces) ──────────────────────────────
enum Direction {
  Up = "UP",
  Down = "DOWN",
}

namespace Direction {
  // Add a helper function to the enum's namespace
  export function parse(value: string): Direction | undefined {
    return Object.values(Direction).find((v) => v === value) as Direction | undefined;
  }
}

console.log(Direction.parse("UP")); // Direction.Up → "UP"

// ─── 5. MERGING ORDER MATTERS FOR FUNCTION OVERLOADS ────────────────────
interface Logger {
  log(message: string): void;    // First declaration
}

interface Logger {
  log(message: string, level: "info" | "warn" | "error"): void; // Second — placed first in overloads
}

// Merged result behaves as:
// log(message: string, level: ...): void   ← later declaration goes first
// log(message: string): void
```

