## ⚡ Short Answer
When a third-party library lacks TypeScript definitions, you have three main options: install community-maintained types from the `@types` scope on npm, write your own ambient declaration file (`.d.ts`), or use a quick `declare module` stub to silence type errors. The right choice depends on how deeply you use the library and whether community types exist.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** TypeScript requires type information for every module it imports. When a library ships without a `types` field in its `package.json` and no bundled `.d.ts` files, the compiler has no idea what the library exports, so it throws `Could not find a declaration file for module 'xyz'`.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** The JavaScript ecosystem predates TypeScript by many years. Thousands of popular libraries were written in plain JS and never retrofitted with types. The community solved this via DefinitelyTyped — a massive open-source repo of hand-written declarations published under the `@types/` npm scope.

**How it works internally:** TypeScript resolves types in order: bundled `.d.ts` inside the package → `@types/<package>` in `node_modules` → ambient declarations you write yourself. An ambient declaration file uses `declare module 'library-name' { ... }` syntax to describe the public API without any implementation code. TypeScript merges these declarations at compile time; they produce zero runtime output.

**Benefits:** Full IntelliSense, safe refactors, catches API misuse at compile time, no runtime overhead, lets you incrementally type a library you only partially use.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Hand-written declarations can drift from the real library API and give false confidence. Broad stubs like `declare module 'foo'` that export `any` eliminate type safety entirely. Maintaining custom `.d.ts` files adds overhead when you upgrade the library.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// OPTION 1: Install community types (best first step)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// npm install --save-dev @types/lodash
// After install, TypeScript picks them up automatically.
import _ from 'lodash';
const result = _.chunk([1, 2, 3, 4], 2); // fully typed ✅


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// OPTION 2: Quick stub — suppress the error, use 'any'
// File: src/types/stubs.d.ts  (or any .d.ts picked up by tsconfig)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
declare module 'some-legacy-lib';
// Now `import x from 'some-legacy-lib'` compiles; x is typed as `any`.
// Use this as a temporary fix, not a long-term solution.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// OPTION 3: Proper ambient declaration (recommended for real usage)
// File: src/types/untyped-chart-lib.d.ts
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
declare module 'untyped-chart-lib' {
  export interface ChartOptions {
    width: number;
    height: number;
    title?: string;
    animate?: boolean;
  }

  export interface ChartInstance {
    render(data: number[]): void;
    destroy(): void;
    on(event: 'click' | 'hover', handler: (index: number) => void): void;
  }

  // Factory function — the library's default export
  export default function createChart(
    selector: string,
    options: ChartOptions
  ): ChartInstance;
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// OPTION 4: Module augmentation — extend an existing type definition
// Useful when @types/foo exists but is missing something
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
import 'express';

declare module 'express' {
  interface Request {
    currentUser?: { id: string; role: string }; // add custom field
  }
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// tsconfig.json — make sure TypeScript finds your .d.ts files
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// {
//   "compilerOptions": {
//     "typeRoots": ["./node_modules/@types", "./src/types"],
//     "strict": true
//   },
//   "include": ["src/**/*"]
// }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Usage of the custom-typed library
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
import createChart, { ChartOptions } from 'untyped-chart-lib';

const options: ChartOptions = { width: 800, height: 400, animate: true };
const chart = createChart('#container', options);
