## ⚡ Short Answer
A throttle function limits how often a function can execute by ensuring it fires at most once per specified time window, regardless of how many times it's invoked. Unlike debounce (which delays until calls stop), throttle guarantees regular execution — the first call fires immediately, then subsequent calls are ignored until the cooldown expires. It's essential for rate-limiting scroll handlers, resize listeners, and API calls.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A higher-order function that wraps another function and enforces a minimum time interval between successive executions.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without throttling, attaching a handler to `scroll` or `mousemove` events can trigger hundreds of function calls per second, destroying performance and hammering APIs with unnecessary requests.

**How it works internally:** On invocation, it checks whether enough time has elapsed since the last execution (using a timestamp or a timer flag). If yes, it executes immediately and records the new timestamp. If no, the call is silently dropped (or optionally queued for trailing-edge execution).

**Benefits:** Predictable, evenly-spaced execution cadence; immediate first response; protects downstream systems from call floods.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Some intermediate calls are permanently dropped — if every call carries unique payload data, you may lose information. Not the right tool when you need to react only after activity stops (use debounce then).

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THROTTLE — fires at most once per `limit` ms
// Options:
//   leading  (default true)  — fire on the FIRST call
//   trailing (default true)  — fire once MORE after the last call
//                              if calls came in during the cooldown
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function throttle(fn, limit = 300, { leading = true, trailing = true } = {}) {
  let lastCallTime = 0;   // timestamp of the last actual execution
  let trailingTimer = null; // timer for the optional trailing call

  return function throttled(...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - lastCallTime;

    // Helper: run the function and update bookkeeping
    const execute = () => {
      lastCallTime = Date.now();
      fn.apply(this, args);
    };

    // Clear any pending trailing call — we'll re-schedule if needed
    if (trailingTimer) {
      clearTimeout(trailingTimer);
      trailingTimer = null;
    }

    if (elapsed >= limit) {
      // ── LEADING EDGE ──────────────────────────────────────────
      // Enough time has passed; fire immediately (if leading enabled)
      if (leading) {
        execute();
      }
    } else {
      // ── THROTTLED ZONE ────────────────────────────────────────
      // Still inside the cooldown window.
      // Schedule a trailing call so the LAST invocation isn't lost.
      if (trailing) {
        trailingTimer = setTimeout(() => {
          trailingTimer = null;
          execute();
        }, limit - elapsed); // fire exactly when the window reopens
      }
    }
  };
}


