## ⚡ Short Answer
Deep cloning creates a fully independent copy of an object where nested references are also copied, not shared. Without `JSON.parse/stringify`, you can use `structuredClone()` (modern standard), a recursive function, or libraries like Lodash's `_.cloneDeep()`. Each approach handles edge cases like circular references, functions, and special types differently.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Deep cloning produces a new object graph that is structurally identical to the source but shares no references — mutating the clone cannot affect the original.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Shallow copies (spread, `Object.assign`) only copy the top level. Nested objects are still shared by reference, causing subtle bugs when one copy is mutated. `JSON.parse/stringify` solves depth but silently drops functions, `undefined`, `Date` objects (converts to strings), `Map`, `Set`, symbols, and circular references.

**How it works internally:** A recursive deep clone traverses every property of an object. For each value, if it's a primitive it copies it directly; if it's an object/array it recurses. To handle circular references, a `WeakMap` tracks already-visited objects and returns the previously created clone instead of recursing infinitely. Special types like `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, and `RegExp` need explicit handling since generic recursion won't reconstruct them correctly.

**Benefits:** Full independence of the clone; handles complex types; avoids JSON round-trip bugs; can be tailored to your domain.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Performance cost grows with object depth and size. Functions cannot be truly cloned (they close over scope). Prototype chains require extra care. For most production use cases, `structuredClone()` or Lodash is preferable to a hand-rolled solution.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Option 1: structuredClone() — PREFERRED (Node 17+, modern browsers)
// Handles: arrays, Date, Map, Set, RegExp, ArrayBuffer, circular refs
// Does NOT handle: functions, class instances (loses prototype)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
const original = {
  name: "Alice",
  born: new Date("1990-01-01"),
  scores: new Map([["math", 95]]),
  tags: new Set(["admin", "user"]),
  address: { city: "Berlin", zip: "10115" },
};

const clone1 = structuredClone(original);
clone1.address.city = "Munich";
console.log(original.address.city); // "Berlin" ✅ unchanged
console.log(clone1.born instanceof Date); // true ✅ real Date, not string


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Option 2: Manual recursive deep clone
// Handles: circular refs (WeakMap), Date, RegExp, Map, Set, Arrays
// Does NOT handle: functions, WeakMap/WeakSet, class instances cleanly
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
function deepClone(value, seen = new WeakMap()) {
  // Primitives (string, number, boolean, null, undefined, symbol, bigint)
  if (value === null || typeof value !== "object") return value;

  // Circular reference guard
  if (seen.has(value)) return seen.get(value);

  // Special built-in types
  if (value instanceof Date)   return new Date(value.getTime());
  if (value instanceof RegExp) return new RegExp(value.source, value.flags);

  if (value instanceof Map) {
    const mapClone = new Map();
    seen.set(value, mapClone);
    for (const [k, v] of value) mapClone.set(deepClone(k, seen), deepClone(v, seen));
    return mapClone;
  }

  if (value instanceof Set) {
    const setClone = new Set();
    seen.set(value, setClone);
    for (const item of value) setClone.add(deepClone(item, seen));
    return setClone;
  }

  // Arrays and plain objects — preserve prototype
  const clone = Array.isArray(value)
    ? []
    : Object.create(Object.getPrototypeOf(value));

  seen.set(value, clone); // register BEFORE recursing (handles circular refs)

  for (const key of Reflect.ownKeys(value)) { // includes symbol keys
    clone[key] = deepClone(value[key], seen);
  }

  return clone;
}

// ── Test: circular reference ──
const a = { x: 1 };
a.self = a; // circular!
const clonedA = deepClone(a);
console.log(clonedA.self === clonedA); // true ✅ circular ref preserved correctly
console.log(clonedA
