## ⚡ Short Answer
To flatten a deeply nested array without recursion, you can use an explicit stack (iterative DFS), JavaScript's built-in `Array.prototype.flat(Infinity)`, or a queue-based BFS approach. The iterative stack method is the most portable and avoids call-stack overflow risks that plague recursive solutions on deeply nested structures.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Flattening a nested array means converting `[1, [2, [3, [4]]]]` into `[1, 2, 3, 4]` — collapsing all nesting levels into a single-dimension array.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Recursive solutions are elegant but hit JavaScript's call stack limit (typically ~10,000–15,000 frames) on pathologically deep arrays. An iterative approach using an explicit stack on the heap sidesteps this entirely.

**How it works internally:** A stack is initialized with the input array's elements (reversed, so the first element is on top). Each iteration pops one item: if it's an array, its elements are pushed back onto the stack (again reversed); if it's a primitive, it's pushed to the result. This mimics DFS without using the call stack.

**Benefits:** No stack overflow risk, O(n) time where n is the total number of leaf elements, works in any environment (no ES2019 required), and easy to extend with a depth limit.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Slightly more verbose than `flat(Infinity)`. The reversal step on each spread can add a constant overhead for very wide arrays. `flat(Infinity)` is cleaner and optimized natively — prefer it when targeting modern environments.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── 1. Iterative Stack (most portable, no recursion) ───────────────────────
function flattenIterative(arr) {
  const stack = [...arr]; // shallow copy as our working stack
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const item = stack.pop(); // take from the top

    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      // Push children back (spread reverses order, so we reverse to maintain left-to-right)
      stack.push(...item);
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }

  // pop() processes right-to-left, so reverse the result
  return result.reverse();
}

// ─── 2. Iterative with explicit depth limit ──────────────────────────────────
function flattenToDepth(arr, maxDepth = Infinity) {
  // Each stack entry is [value, currentDepth]
  const stack = arr.map(item => [item, 0]);
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const [item, depth] = stack.pop();

    if (Array.isArray(item) && depth < maxDepth) {
      // Push children with incremented depth (reversed to preserve order)
      for (let i = item.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        stack.push([item[i], depth + 1]);
      }
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }

  return result;
}

// ─── 3. Modern JS built-in (ES2019+) ─────────────────────────────────────────
function flattenNative(arr) {
  return arr.flat(Infinity); // simplest — let the engine handle it
}

// ─── 4. Generator-based (lazy, memory-efficient for huge arrays) ──────────────
function* flattenLazy(arr) {
  const stack = [...arr];
  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const item = stack.pop();
    if (Array.isArray(item)) {
      stack.push(...item);
    } else {
      yield item;
    }
  }
  // Note: order will be reversed here — wrap with Array.from + .reverse() if needed
}

// ─── Tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const nested = [1, [2, [3, [4, [5]]]], 6, [7, 8]];

console.log(flattenIterative(nested));       // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
console.log(flattenToDepth(nested, 2));      // [1, 2, 3, [4, [5]], 6, 7, 8]
console.log(flattenNative(nested));          // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
console.log([...flattenLazy(nested)].reverse()); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]

