## ⚡ Short Answer
An Observable/EventEmitter is a publish-subscribe pattern implementation where objects can register listeners for named events, and a publisher can emit those events to notify all subscribers. It decouples producers from consumers, enabling reactive, event-driven architectures. Node.js's built-in `EventEmitter` is the canonical example, and this pattern underpins RxJS, DOM events, and most UI frameworks.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A class that maintains a registry of event names mapped to arrays of callback functions, allowing any code to subscribe to or publish named events.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Direct method calls between objects create tight coupling — the caller must know about the callee. An event emitter inverts this: publishers fire events without knowing who (or how many) listeners exist, and listeners register without knowing who will fire.

**How it works internally:** Internally, it holds a `Map` (or plain object) where each key is an event name and each value is an array of subscriber callbacks. `on()` pushes a callback into the array; `emit()` iterates that array and calls each callback with the provided arguments; `off()` filters the callback out; `once()` wraps the callback to auto-remove itself after the first invocation.

**Benefits:** Loose coupling between components, supports multiple listeners per event, easy to extend (wildcards, async emit, error events), and is the backbone of reactive and stream-based programming.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Memory leaks if listeners are never removed (especially in SPAs with component lifecycles), difficult to debug when event chains grow complex, no built-in type safety unless layered on (TypeScript generics help), and ordering of listener execution can be implicit and surprising.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
type Listener<T = unknown[]> = (...args: T extends unknown[] ? T : never[]) => void;

// ─── Core EventEmitter ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
class EventEmitter {
  // Map: eventName → ordered list of { listener, once }
  private _events: Map<string, Array<{ fn: Listener; once: boolean }>> = new Map();

  /** Subscribe to an event. Returns `this` for chaining. */
  on(event: string, listener: Listener): this {
    if (!this._events.has(event)) this._events.set(event, []);
    this._events.get(event)!.push({ fn: listener, once: false });
    return this;
  }

  /** Subscribe, but auto-remove after the first invocation. */
  once(event: string, listener: Listener): this {
    if (!this._events.has(event)) this._events.set(event, []);
    this._events.get(event)!.push({ fn: listener, once: true });
    return this;
  }

  /** Unsubscribe a specific listener from an event. */
  off(event: string, listener: Listener): this {
    const listeners = this._events.get(event);
    if (!listeners) return this;
    this._events.set(
      event,
      listeners.filter((entry) => entry.fn !== listener)
    );
    return this;
  }

  /** Fire an event, passing args to every subscribed listener. */
  emit(event: string, ...args: unknown[]): boolean {
    const listeners = this._events.get(event);
    if (!listeners || listeners.length === 0) return false;

    // Snapshot before iteration — a listener might call `off` mid-loop
    const snapshot = [...listeners];
    // Remove `once` listeners BEFORE calling them (avoids re-entry issues)
    this._events.set(event, listeners.filter((e) => !e.once));

    for (const { fn } of snapshot) {
      fn(...args);
    }
    return true;
  }

  /** Remove ALL listeners for an event, or every listener if no event given. */
  removeAllListeners(event?: string): this {
    if (event) {
      this._events.delete(event);
    } else {
      this._events.clear();
    }
    return this;
  }

  /** Introspection — useful for debugging memory leaks. */
  listenerCount(event: string): number {
    return this._events.get(event)?.length ?? 0;
  }
}

// ─── Usage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const emitter = new EventEmitter();

// Regular subscription
const onData = (payload: string) => console.log("data →", payload);
emitter.on("data", onData as Listener);

// One-shot subscription
emitter.once("connect", () => console.log("Connected! (fires once)"));

// Chained subscriptions
emitter
  .on("error", ((err: Error) => console.error("Handler 1:", err.message)) as Listener)
  .on("error", ((err: Error)
