## ⚡ Short Answer
The loop-closure-in-setTimeout bug occurs when a `var`-declared loop variable is captured by reference in a callback — by the time the callback fires, the loop has already finished and all closures share the same final value. Fix it by using `let` (block-scoped per iteration), an IIFE to create a new scope, or `Array.prototype.forEach`.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A subtle JavaScript closure bug where multiple async callbacks (setTimeout, event listeners, Promises) all read the *same* variable reference instead of the value it held when the callback was registered.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** `var` is function-scoped, not block-scoped. Every iteration of a `for` loop with `var` shares *one* binding in the enclosing function scope. When the event loop finally runs the callbacks, the loop is long done and the shared variable holds its terminal value (usually `n` or the last index).

**How it works internally:** JavaScript closures capture *variables* (bindings), not *values*. With `var i`, there is literally one `i` variable in memory. All three `setTimeout` callbacks close over a reference to that single memory location. When they execute (after ≥0 ms, meaning after the synchronous loop finishes), they all read whatever `i` is *now* — the post-loop value. With `let`, each iteration creates a *new lexical environment* containing its own fresh `i` binding, so each callback captures a different variable.

**Benefits of understanding this:**
- Eliminates an entire class of async/event-listener bugs
- Deepens understanding of JS scoping, closures, and the event loop
- Helps reason about captured state in React hooks, RxJS operators, and Node.js callbacks

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- `let` fixes the bug elegantly but requires ES6+; in legacy code you may still encounter IIFE patterns
- IIFE solution is verbose and harder to read for junior developers
- `bind` approach creates extra function objects per iteration (minor perf cost)

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ============================================================
// THE BUG — using var
// ============================================================
console.log("--- BUGGY (var) ---");
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(function () {
    // By the time this fires, the loop is done.
    // All three callbacks share the SAME `i` variable,
    // which is now 3 (the value that ended the loop).
    console.log("var i =", i); // prints 3, 3, 3
  }, 0);
}

// ============================================================
// FIX 1 — use `let` (preferred, ES6+)
// Each iteration creates a NEW block-scoped binding for `i`.
// ============================================================
console.log("--- FIX 1: let ---");
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(function () {
    console.log("let i =", i); // prints 0, 1, 2 ✅
  }, 0);
}

// ============================================================
// FIX 2 — IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression)
// Creates a new function scope per iteration; the value of `i`
// is passed in as argument `j`, freezing it for that closure.
// ============================================================
console.log("--- FIX 2: IIFE ---");
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  (function (j) {
    setTimeout(function () {
      console.log("IIFE j =", j); // prints 0, 1, 2 ✅
    }, 0);
  })(i); // `i` is evaluated NOW and passed by value
}

// ============================================================
// FIX 3 — forEach (functional style)
// Array methods create a new closure scope per element.
// ============================================================
console.log("--- FIX 3: forEach ---");
[0, 1, 2].forEach(function (i) {
  setTimeout(function () {
    console.log("forEach i =", i); // prints 0, 1, 2 ✅
  }, 0);
});

// ============================================================
// FIX 4 — setTimeout's built-in extra arguments
// Any arguments after the delay are passed to the callback.
// ============================================================
console.log("--- FIX 4: setTimeout extra args ---");
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(
    function (j) {
      console.log("extra arg j =", j); // prints 0, 1, 2 ✅
    },
    0,
    i // <-- passed as `j` to the callback
  );
}

// ============================================================
// REAL-WORLD ANALOGUE — event listeners in a loop
// (Same root cause; same fixes apply)
// ============================================================
//
