## ⚡ Short Answer
A plain object is one created via object literal `{}` or `Object.create(null/Object.prototype)` — not an Array, Date, Map, RegExp, or other built-in. The most reliable detection combines `typeof`, a null check, and inspecting the prototype chain via `Object.getPrototypeOf()` to confirm the prototype is either `Object.prototype` or `null`.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A plain object (also called a "POJO" — Plain Old JavaScript Object) is an instance whose prototype is `Object.prototype` or `null`, created without any custom constructor.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** JavaScript's `typeof` returns `"object"` for arrays, null, Date, Map, RegExp, and plain objects alike, so distinguishing them requires deeper inspection. Libraries like Lodash, Redux, and serialization tools need to know if a value is a safe, generic key-value container before iterating or cloning it.

**How it works internally:** Every JS object has an internal `[[Prototype]]`. `Object.getPrototypeOf(val)` exposes it. For `{}` or `new Object()`, that prototype is `Object.prototype`. For arrays it's `Array.prototype`, for Dates it's `Date.prototype`, and so on. `Object.create(null)` produces a "null-prototype object" (no prototype at all), which is also considered plain. Checking the constructor property is a weaker alternative — it can be overwritten — so prototype-level checks are more robust.

**Benefits:** Reliable across all built-in types; works across iframes (unlike `instanceof Object`); handles null-prototype objects correctly.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Instances of user-defined classes (e.g., `new MyClass()`) will have `MyClass.prototype` as their prototype, so they correctly fail the plain-object check — which is usually desired, but something to be aware of. Cross-realm class instances (from iframes) whose prototype chain doesn't reference the local `Object.prototype` are edge cases worth noting.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
// ─── Core utility ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Returns true only for plain objects:
 *   - {}  /  new Object()
 *   - Object.create(null)   (null-prototype objects)
 * Returns false for: null, arrays, Date, Map, Set, RegExp, class instances, primitives
 */
function isPlainObject(val) {
  // 1. Must be non-null and typeof "object"
  if (val === null || typeof val !== "object") return false;

  // 2. Get the prototype
  const proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(val);

  // 3. Null-prototype objects: Object.create(null)
  if (proto === null) return true;

  // 4. Prototype must be Object.prototype (standard plain object)
  //    We walk up to the top of the chain to handle deeply inherited cases.
  //    For a plain object, proto IS Object.prototype — no further ancestors.
  let baseProto = proto;
  while (Object.getPrototypeOf(baseProto) !== null) {
    baseProto = Object.getPrototypeOf(baseProto);
  }
  return proto === baseProto; // proto sits directly on Object.prototype level
}


// ─── Tests ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

console.log(isPlainObject({}));                    // ✅ true
console.log(isPlainObject(new Object()));          // ✅ true
console.log(isPlainObject(Object.create(null)));   // ✅ true  (null-proto)
console.log(isPlainObject(Object.create({}))); // ✅ true (proto IS Object.prototype)

console.log(isPlainObject([]));                    // ❌ false — Array
console.log(isPlainObject(new Date()));            // ❌ false — Date
console.log(isPlainObject(new Map()));             // ❌ false — Map
console.log(isPlainObject(/regex/));               // ❌ false — RegExp
