## ⚡ Short Answer
Functional components are plain JavaScript functions that return JSX, while class components are ES6 classes extending `React.Component` with lifecycle methods and `this`-based state. Since React 16.8 introduced Hooks, functional components can do everything class components can — and are now the industry standard. Class components are essentially legacy code in modern React.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Two different syntaxes for defining React UI components — functions vs. ES6 classes.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Class components were the original way to have stateful, lifecycle-aware components. Functional components were initially "dumb" (stateless) presentational components. Hooks were introduced to eliminate the complexity of classes while keeping full power in functions.

**How it works internally:** Class components instantiate an object, so React manages `this`, lifecycle methods (`componentDidMount`, `componentDidUpdate`, `componentWillUnmount`), and `this.state`. Functional components are called as plain functions on every render; Hooks like `useState` and `useEffect` store state and side-effect logic in React's internal Fiber linked-list, keyed by call order — not by `this`.

**Benefits:**
- Functional: simpler syntax, no `this` confusion, easier to test, better tree-shaking, composable logic via custom Hooks, smaller bundle size
- Class: still valid, widely documented, some teams maintain existing codebases with them

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Class components: verbose, `this` binding bugs, logic scattered across lifecycle methods, harder to share stateful logic (HOCs/render props were workarounds)
- Functional components: Hooks have rules (no conditional calls), `useEffect` dependency arrays require discipline, closures can cause stale-value bugs if mismanaged

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

// ─── CLASS COMPONENT (legacy style) ───────────────────────────────────────────
class CounterClass extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { count: 0 };
  }

  // Side effect: update document title on mount + update
  componentDidMount() {
    document.title = `Count: ${this.state.count}`;
  }

  componentDidUpdate() {
    document.title = `Count: ${this.state.count}`;
  }

  // Must manually bind or use arrow functions to avoid `this` bugs
  increment = () => {
    this.setState((prev) => ({ count: prev.count + 1 }));
  };

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <p>Class Count: {this.state.count}</p>
        <button onClick={this.increment}>Increment</button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}

// ─── FUNCTIONAL COMPONENT (modern style with Hooks) ────────────────────────────
function CounterFunctional() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // replaces this.state

  // useEffect combines componentDidMount + componentDidUpdate
  // dependency array [count] means: re-run whenever count changes
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = `Count: ${count}`;
  }, [count]);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Functional Count: {count}</p>
      {/* No `this` binding needed */}
      <button onClick={() => setCount((prev) => prev + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
}

// ─── CUSTOM HOOK: reusable stateful logic (impossible with classes) ─────────────
function useDocumentTitle(title) {
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = title;
    return () => { document.title = 'App'; }; // cleanup
  }, [title]);
}

// Now any component can use it with one line — no HOC or render prop needed
function AnotherComponent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  useDocumentTitle(`Count: ${count}`); // reused hook

  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>;
}

export { CounterClass, CounterFunctional, AnotherComponent };
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Can functional components replace class components 100%?** A: Almost — `getSnapshotBeforeUpdate` and `componentDidCatch` (error boundaries) still require class components as of React 18, though this may change.
- **Q: What are the rules of Hooks?** A: Only call Hooks at the top level (not inside loops/conditions/nested functions) and only from React functions — this ensures stable call order across renders.
- **Q: How do you migrate a class component to functional?** A: Replace `this.state` with `useState`, combine lifecycle methods into `useEffect` calls, move shared logic into custom Hooks,
