## ⚡ Short Answer
The React rendering lifecycle describes the sequence of phases a component goes through from creation to removal: mounting, updating, and unmounting. In class components, this maps to explicit lifecycle methods; in functional components with hooks, the same phases are managed via `useEffect`, `useState`, and related APIs. Understanding this lifecycle is critical for controlling side effects, optimizing performance, and avoiding memory leaks.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** The React rendering lifecycle is the ordered set of stages React uses to create, update, and destroy component instances in the virtual DOM and reconcile them with the real DOM.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** UI components need predictable points to fetch data, subscribe to events, animate, or clean up resources. Without a defined lifecycle, side effects would run unpredictably, causing race conditions, stale state, or memory leaks.

**How it works internally:** React's reconciler (Fiber architecture) breaks work into two phases. The **render phase** is pure and may be interrupted — React calls the component function (or `render()`) to compute the virtual DOM diff. The **commit phase** is synchronous and side-effect-safe — React applies DOM mutations, then fires layout effects (`useLayoutEffect`) and passive effects (`useEffect`). React 18 introduced concurrent features that can pause and resume the render phase, making the commit phase the only reliable place for DOM-dependent code.

**Benefits:** Predictable data flow, clear separation of rendering logic from side effects, and a hook-based model that composes far better than class lifecycle methods.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Misunderstanding the two-phase model leads to subtle bugs (e.g., running DOM measurements in the wrong effect). Strict Mode intentionally double-invokes render and effect setup/teardown in development to expose these issues, which confuses developers unfamiliar with the design.

## 💻 Code Example
```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect, useLayoutEffect, useRef } from 'react';

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Functional Component Lifecycle Demonstration
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const abortRef = useRef(null);

  // ── 1. MOUNT + UPDATE (dependency array controls when this runs)
  // Equivalent to: componentDidMount + componentDidUpdate
  useEffect(() => {
    // Setup: create an AbortController so we can cancel stale requests
    abortRef.current = new AbortController();

    async function fetchUser() {
      try {
        const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`, {
          signal: abortRef.current.signal,
        });
        const data = await res.json();
        setUser(data);
      } catch (err) {
        if (err.name !== 'AbortError') setError(err.message);
      }
    }

    fetchUser();

    // ── 2. CLEANUP (runs before next effect execution OR on unmount)
    // Equivalent to: componentWillUnmount
    return () => {
      abortRef.current.abort(); // cancel in-flight request — avoids setState on unmounted component
    };
  }, [userId]); // re-runs whenever userId changes

  // ── 3. LAYOUT EFFECT — runs synchronously AFTER DOM mutation, BEFORE paint
  // Use for: measuring DOM nodes, synchronous animations
  // Equivalent to: componentDidMount (synchronous variant)
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    document.title = user ? `Profile: ${user.name}` : 'Loading...';
  }, [user]);

  // ── 4. RENDER PHASE — pure, no side effects here
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error}</p>;
  if (!user)  return <p>Loading...</p>;
  return <h1>{user.name}</h1>;
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Class Component Lifecycle (for comparison / legacy codebases)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────

class UserProfileClass extends React.Component {
  state = { user: null };

  // MOUNT
  componentDidMount() {
    this.fetchUser(this.props.userId);
  }

  // UPDATE — only re-fetch when userId changes
  componentDidUpdate(prevProps) {
    if (prevProps.userId !== this.props.userId) {
      this.fetchUser(this.props.userId);
    }
  }

  // UNMOUNT — cleanup
  componentWillUnmount() {
    this.controller?.abort();
  }

  async fetchUser(id) {
    this.controller = new AbortController();
    const res  = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal: this.controller.signal });
    const user = await res.json();
    this.setState({ user });
  }

  render()
