## ⚡ Short Answer
React StrictMode is a development-only tool that intentionally invokes certain lifecycle methods and render functions twice to expose side effects and bugs caused by impure rendering. It doesn't affect the production bundle or performance — it only runs during development to surface problems that might otherwise go unnoticed until they cause subtle bugs in production.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A React component (`<React.StrictMode>`) that wraps part or all of your app and activates additional runtime checks and warnings in development mode only.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** As React moves toward concurrent rendering (where renders can be interrupted, restarted, or run multiple times), code that assumes render is called exactly once will break. StrictMode simulates this concurrency behavior early, forcing you to write components that are safe under those conditions before concurrent features are even enabled.

**How it works internally:** In development, StrictMode deliberately double-invokes render functions, function component bodies, state updater functions, and `useState`/`useReducer` initializers. It also intentionally mounts, unmounts, and re-mounts every component once to verify that effects are properly cleaned up. If your component behaves differently between the two invocations, it signals a hidden side effect or impurity.

**Benefits:** Catches impure render logic (e.g., mutations inside render), detects missing `useEffect` cleanup, warns about deprecated API usage (`componentWillMount`, `findDOMNode`, string refs), and future-proofs code for concurrent React.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** The double-invocation and double-mount behavior can confuse developers who don't know it's active — you may see console logs appear twice, network requests fire twice (if mistakenly placed outside `useEffect`), or third-party libraries behave unexpectedly. It only runs in development, so it adds zero production overhead but requires discipline to interpret warnings correctly.

## 💻 Code Example
```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from "react";

// ─── 1. Wrapping your app (or a subtree) in StrictMode ───────────────────────
function App() {
  return (
    <React.StrictMode>
      <UserProfile userId={42} />
    </React.StrictMode>
  );
}

// ─── 2. Component with a BUG StrictMode will expose ──────────────────────────
// BUG: mutating an external variable inside render — impure!
let renderCount = 0;

function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  renderCount++; // ❌ Side effect in render body — StrictMode double-renders,
                 //    so renderCount increments TWICE per "render" in dev.

  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  // ❌ Fetching without cleanup — StrictMode mounts → unmounts → remounts,
  //    so this fires twice, potentially causing a state update on an
  //    unmounted component.
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then((data) => setUser(data));
    // Missing cleanup! In React 18 StrictMode you'll see two requests.
  }, [userId]);

  return <div>{user?.name ?? "Loading..."}</div>;
}

// ─── 3. Fixed version StrictMode is happy with ───────────────────────────────
function UserProfileFixed({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false; // ✅ Cleanup flag — safe under double-mount

    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then((data) => {
        if (!cancelled) setUser(data); // Only update if still mounted
      });

    return () => {
      cancelled = true; // ✅ Cleanup function — StrictMode verifies this runs
    };
  }, [userId]);

  return <div>{user?.name ?? "Loading..."}</div>;
}

// ─── 4. State updater — must be pure (StrictMode calls it twice) ──────────────
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  function handleClick() {
    // ✅ Pure updater — calling it twice produces the same result
    setCount((prev) => prev + 1);

    // ❌ If you wrote: setCount(count + 1)
    //    And had side effects inside the updater, they'd fire twice in dev.
  }

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Count: {count}</button>;
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Does StrictMode affect production performance?** A: No. StrictMode wraps are completely stripped out (or become no-ops) in production builds — it's purely a development safety net with zero runtime cost in prod.
