## ⚡ Short Answer
Default props allow you to specify fallback values for props when they are not provided by the parent component, ensuring your component always has the data it needs to render. Prop types validation (via the `prop-types` library or TypeScript) lets you declare the expected type and shape of each prop, catching bugs at development time by warning you when the wrong type is passed.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `defaultProps` is a static property on a React component that defines fallback values for missing props. `PropTypes` is a runtime type-checking library that validates props against a schema you define and logs warnings in the browser console during development.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without defaults, missing props produce `undefined` errors or broken UI. Without type validation, mismatched data types (e.g., passing a string where a number is expected) silently cause bugs that are hard to trace. Both mechanisms act as a contract between the component author and its consumers.

**How it works internally:** At render time, React merges `defaultProps` into the received props object before passing them to the component function or class. The `prop-types` library runs its validators after rendering (in development builds only) and logs `console.error` warnings — it is stripped out or silenced in production for performance. TypeScript's static analysis catches type mismatches at compile time with zero runtime cost.

**Benefits:** Improves component resilience, acts as living documentation, catches integration bugs early, and makes components self-describing.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** `PropTypes` only validates at runtime in development, not at compile time. The `prop-types` library is effectively deprecated for new projects in favor of TypeScript. `defaultProps` for function components is also being soft-deprecated in React 19+ in favor of ES6 default parameters.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

// --- Modern approach: ES6 default parameters (recommended for function components) ---
function UserCard({
  name = 'Anonymous',       // default prop via destructuring
  age = 0,
  role = 'Viewer',
  isAdmin = false,
  onClick = () => {},       // default no-op for callbacks
}) {
  return (
    <div className={`user-card ${isAdmin ? 'admin' : ''}`}>
      <h2>{name}</h2>
      <p>Age: {age} | Role: {role}</p>
      <button onClick={onClick}>Select</button>
    </div>
  );
}

// --- PropTypes validation schema ---
// Runs in DEV only; logs console.error if violated
UserCard.propTypes = {
  name:    PropTypes.string,                  // optional string
  age:     PropTypes.number,                  // optional number
  role:    PropTypes.oneOf(['Admin', 'Editor', 'Viewer']),  // enum
  isAdmin: PropTypes.bool,
  onClick: PropTypes.func,
  // Use .isRequired to enforce presence:
  // id: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
};

// --- Legacy: defaultProps (still works, but prefer ES6 defaults above) ---
// UserCard.defaultProps = {
//   name: 'Anonymous',
//   age: 0,
//   role: 'Viewer',
//   isAdmin: false,
// };

export default UserCard;


// =========================================================
// --- TypeScript approach (preferred in modern codebases) ---
// =========================================================
interface UserCardProps {
  name?:    string;                          // '?' = optional
  age?:     number;
  role?:    'Admin' | 'Editor' | 'Viewer';  // union type as enum
  isAdmin?: boolean;
  id:       string;                          // required (no '?')
  onClick?: () => void;
}

function UserCardTS({
  name    = 'Anonymous',
  age     = 0,
  role    = 'Viewer',
  isAdmin = false,
  onClick = () => {},
  id,                                        // required — no default
}: UserCardProps) {
  return (
    <div>
      <span>{id}: {name}</span>
    </div>
  );
}

// Usage — TypeScript catches errors at COMPILE time:
// <UserCardTS />               ❌ Error: 'id' is required
// <UserCardTS id="1" age="x"/> ❌ Error: 'age' must be number
// <UserCardTS id="1" />        ✅ All optional props use defaults
```

