## ⚡ Short Answer
Hooks are special functions introduced in React 16.8 that let you use state and other React features inside functional components, without writing a class. They allow you to "hook into" React's internal lifecycle and state management systems from plain functions. Common built-in hooks include `useState`, `useEffect`, `useContext`, `useRef`, and `useMemo`.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Hooks are functions whose names start with `use` that connect functional components to React's core features — state, side effects, context, refs, memoization, and more.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before hooks, stateful logic could only live in class components, which led to verbose boilerplate (`this.setState`, lifecycle methods like `componentDidMount`), poor logic reuse across components, and the notorious "wrapper hell" of HOCs and render props. Hooks solve all three: they make components simpler, allow logic to be extracted into reusable custom hooks, and eliminate the need for classes entirely.

**How it works internally:** React maintains a linked list of hook state per fiber (component instance). Each time a component renders, hooks are called in the same order, and React matches each call to its slot in that list. This is why hooks must never be called conditionally or inside loops — the order must be stable across renders so React maps state correctly.

**Benefits:** Cleaner, more concise components; easy logic reuse via custom hooks; no `this` binding confusion; better TypeScript inference; and co-location of related logic (vs. class lifecycle methods splitting related logic across `componentDidMount`/`componentDidUpdate`/`componentWillUnmount`).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** The "rules of hooks" (only call at top level, only call from React functions) can be surprising. `useEffect` dependency arrays are error-prone and a common source of stale closure bugs. Over-reliance on `useEffect` for derived state can lead to unnecessary re-renders compared to computing values inline.

## 💻 Code Example
```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect, useCallback } from 'react';

// --- Built-in Hooks Demo ---
function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  // useState: local reactive state
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  // useEffect: side effects (data fetching, subscriptions, DOM mutations)
  // Runs after render; dependency array controls when it re-runs
  useEffect(() => {
    setLoading(true);
    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => {
        setUser(data);
        setLoading(false);
      });

    // Cleanup function — runs before next effect or on unmount
    return () => {
      // Cancel requests, clear subscriptions here
    };
  }, [userId]); // Re-run only when userId changes

  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  return <h1>{user?.name}</h1>;
}

// --- Custom Hook: extracting reusable logic ---
// Convention: name starts with "use"
function useFetch(url) {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false; // guard against stale updates after unmount

    fetch(url)
      .then(res => {
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Network error');
        return res.json();
      })
      .then(json => { if (!cancelled) setData(json); })
      .catch(err => { if (!cancelled) setError(err.message); })
      .finally(() => { if (!cancelled) setLoading(false); });

    return () => { cancelled = true; };
  }, [url]);

  return { data, error, loading };
}

// --- Using the custom hook ---
function PostList() {
  const { data: posts, loading, error } = useFetch('/api/posts');

  // useCallback: memoize a function reference to prevent child re-renders
  const handleClick = useCallback((id) => {
    console.log('Clicked post', id);
  }, []); // stable reference — no dependencies

  if (loading) return <p>Loading posts...</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error}</p>;

  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map(post => (
        <li key={post.id} onClick={() => handleClick(post.id)}>
          {post.title}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

