## ⚡ Short Answer
`useMemo` and `useCallback` are React hooks that memoize values and functions respectively, preventing unnecessary recalculations and re-renders. `useMemo` caches the *result* of a computation, while `useCallback` caches the *function itself*. Both accept a dependency array and only recompute when a dependency changes.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `useMemo(() => expensiveCalc(), [deps])` returns a memoized value. `useCallback(() => handler(), [deps])` returns a memoized function reference. In fact, `useCallback(fn, deps)` is essentially sugar for `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** React re-renders components on every state/prop change. Without memoization, expensive computations repeat on every render and child components that receive callbacks as props re-render even when nothing functionally changed (because `() => {}` creates a new reference each render).

**How it works internally:** React stores a "memo cache" per hook call in the fiber node. On each render it compares the new dependency array against the stored one using `Object.is` shallow equality. If all deps match, it returns the cached value/function without executing the factory again. If any dep changed, it runs the factory and stores the new result.

**Benefits:** Avoids redundant expensive computations, stabilizes function references (critical when passing callbacks to `React.memo`-wrapped children or as `useEffect` dependencies), improves perceived performance in large trees.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Memoization itself has a cost — memory for the cache and time for the comparison. Overusing them on cheap operations adds complexity with no benefit. They are optimization tools, not default best practices. They do not prevent re-renders on their own; the child must also be wrapped in `React.memo`.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
import React, { useState, useMemo, useCallback, memo } from "react";

// --- Expensive computation ---
function computeTotal(items: number[]): number {
  console.log("Computing total..."); // watch how often this runs
  return items.reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0);
}

// --- Child component: only re-renders if props change ---
const ItemList = memo(({ onRemove }: { onRemove: (id: number) => void }) => {
  console.log("ItemList rendered");
  return <button onClick={() => onRemove(1)}>Remove item 1</button>;
});

export default function Cart() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([10, 20, 30]);
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState("light");

  // ✅ useMemo: only recalculates when `items` changes, NOT when `theme` changes
  const total = useMemo(() => computeTotal(items), [items]);

  // ✅ useCallback: stable reference across re-renders unless `items` changes
  // Without this, ItemList would re-render on every theme toggle
  const handleRemove = useCallback(
    (id: number) => {
      setItems((prev) => prev.filter((_, i) => i !== id));
    },
    [items] // depends on items — or use functional update to remove this dep
  );

  // ❌ NO memo: new function on every render → ItemList always re-renders
  // const handleRemove = (id: number) => { ... };

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Total: {total}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setTheme(t => t === "light" ? "dark" : "light")}>
        Toggle theme (should NOT recompute total or re-render ItemList)
      </button>
      <ItemList onRemove={handleRemove} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: When should you NOT use useMemo or useCallback?** A: When the computation is trivial (e.g., adding two numbers) or when the child component is not wrapped in `React.memo` — the overhead of the hook itself outweighs any benefit.
- **Q: What is the difference between useMemo and useCallback?** A: `useMemo` memoizes a computed *value* (any type); `useCallback` memoizes a *function reference*. `useCallback(fn, deps)` === `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`.
- **Q: Does useMemo guarantee the cached value is never thrown away?** A: No. React may discard the memo cache in future versions (e.g., during offscreen work), so never use it for side effects — only for performance optimization.

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes
❌ Wrapping every function in `useCallback` by default, thinking it always improves performance.
✅ Only use `useCallback` when the function is passed as a prop to a `React.memo`-wrapped component or used as a `useEffect` dependency — otherwise it
