## ⚡ Short Answer
Lazy loading in React is a technique that defers the loading of components until they are actually needed, using `React.lazy()` and `Suspense`. Instead of bundling everything upfront, JavaScript chunks are split and downloaded on demand, reducing the initial bundle size and improving Time to Interactive (TTI).

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A built-in React mechanism (via `React.lazy()` + `Suspense`) that enables code-splitting at the component level, loading a component's JavaScript chunk only when it's first rendered.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In large SPAs, shipping one massive JavaScript bundle causes slow initial loads. Lazy loading lets you split your app into smaller chunks so users only download code for the features they actually visit.

**How it works internally:** `React.lazy()` accepts a function that returns a dynamic `import()` promise. When React encounters the lazy component in the render tree, it triggers the import, suspends rendering via the nearest `Suspense` boundary, shows the fallback UI, then re-renders with the loaded component once the chunk resolves. Under the hood, Webpack (or Vite) creates a separate chunk file for each lazy-imported module.

**Benefits:** Smaller initial bundle, faster TTI, better Core Web Vitals (LCP/FID), lower bandwidth usage for users who never visit certain routes, and natural alignment with route-based splitting.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds a loading flash (needs a fallback), introduces waterfall risk if overused at a granular level, SSR requires additional setup (Next.js `dynamic()`, or `loadable-components`), and error boundaries must be added to handle failed chunk fetches gracefully.

## 💻 Code Example
```jsx
import React, { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';
import { Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';

// ✅ Dynamically imported — Webpack creates separate chunks for each
const Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./pages/Dashboard'));
const Settings  = lazy(() => import('./pages/Settings'));
const Reports   = lazy(() => import('./pages/Reports'));

// Reusable fallback — swap with a real skeleton/spinner
function PageSpinner() {
  return <div className="page-spinner">Loading…</div>;
}

// ✅ Wrap lazy routes in Suspense; pair with ErrorBoundary in production
export default function AppRouter() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<PageSpinner />}>
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/dashboard" element={<Dashboard />} />
        <Route path="/settings"  element={<Settings />} />
        <Route path="/reports"   element={<Reports />} />
      </Routes>
    </Suspense>
  );
}

// ─── Production pattern: ErrorBoundary + lazy ────────────────────────────────
import { Component } from 'react';

class ChunkErrorBoundary extends Component {
  state = { hasError: false };

  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  // Chunk load failures (network error, stale deploy) land here
  componentDidCatch(error) {
    if (error.name === 'ChunkLoadError') {
      // Force a hard reload to fetch the latest chunks after a deploy
      window.location.reload();
    }
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) return <p>Failed to load page. Please refresh.</p>;
    return this.props.children;
  }
}

// Usage:
// <ChunkErrorBoundary>
//   <Suspense fallback={<PageSpinner />}>
//     <Dashboard />
//   </Suspense>
// </ChunkErrorBoundary>
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Can you use `React.lazy()` with named exports?** A: Not directly — `React.lazy()` requires a default export. Wrap it: `lazy(() => import('./Foo').then(m => ({ default: m.NamedExport })))`.
- **Q: How does lazy loading work with SSR (e.g., Next.js)?** A: `React.lazy()` doesn't support SSR out of the box. Use Next.js `dynamic(() => import(...), { ssr: false })` or the `loadable-components` library, which handles server-side chunk rendering.
- **Q: What is prefetching and how does it complement lazy loading?** A: Prefetching hints the browser to load a chunk before the user navigates to it (e.g., on hover). In Webpack, add `/* webpackPrefetch: true */` inside the import comment to emit a `<link rel="prefetch">` tag automatically.

