## ⚡ Short Answer
React reconciliation is the algorithm React uses to efficiently update the DOM by comparing the previous virtual DOM tree with the new one and computing the minimal set of changes needed. Instead of re-rendering the entire DOM on every state change, React diffs the two trees and applies only the necessary updates. This process is powered by the Fiber architecture (introduced in React 16), which makes the diffing incremental and interruptible.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Reconciliation is React's process of determining what changed between renders and syncing those changes to the real DOM as efficiently as possible.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Direct DOM manipulation is expensive. Without reconciliation, every state change would require tearing down and rebuilding the entire UI, causing severe performance bottlenecks. Reconciliation minimizes DOM operations by calculating the smallest diff between renders.

**How it works internally:** React maintains a virtual DOM — a lightweight JavaScript representation of the real DOM. On each render, it builds a new virtual DOM tree and runs the diffing algorithm against the previous tree using two key heuristics: (1) Elements of different types produce entirely different trees, so React destroys the old subtree and builds a new one from scratch. (2) The `key` prop is used to match children in lists across renders, allowing React to identify which items moved, were added, or were removed. React Fiber (the reconciler since v16) breaks reconciliation into two phases — the render/reconcile phase (interruptible, builds a work-in-progress fiber tree) and the commit phase (synchronous, flushes changes to the real DOM).

**Benefits:** Drastically reduces real DOM operations; enables features like concurrent rendering, Suspense, and transitions; keeps UI updates predictable and declarative.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** The diffing algorithm is O(n) but relies on heuristics that can be suboptimal if misused (e.g., unstable keys). Deeply nested trees or overly large component trees can still cause performance issues if components aren't memoized properly.

## 💻 Code Example
```jsx
import React, { useState, memo } from 'react';

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. KEY PROP — helps reconciler identify list items across renders
// -----------------------------------------------------------------

// ❌ BAD: using index as key — reconciler can't detect item identity
function BadList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item, index) => (
        <li key={index}>{item.name}</li> // reconciler may reuse wrong nodes
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// ✅ GOOD: using stable, unique ID — reconciler correctly tracks each item
function GoodList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li> // reconciler can diff efficiently
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. ELEMENT TYPE CHANGE — reconciler destroys + rebuilds subtree
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
function TypeSwitch({ isLoggedIn }) {
  // Changing the root element type (div → section) forces a full
  // subtree teardown. React will unmount div and mount section fresh.
  return isLoggedIn ? (
    <div className="dashboard">Welcome back!</div>
  ) : (
    <section className="login">Please log in.</section>
  );
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. MEMO — short-circuit reconciliation for pure components
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
const ExpensiveChild = memo(function ExpensiveChild({ value }) {
  // React skips reconciling this subtree entirely if `value` hasn't changed.
  // memo does a shallow comparison of props before triggering a re-render.
  console.log('ExpensiveChild rendered');
  return <div>{value}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [text, setText] = useState('hello');

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>
        Increment ({count})
      </button>
      {/* This re-renders only when `text` changes, not when `count` changes */}
      <ExpensiveChild value={text} />
    </div>
  );
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. KEY TRICK — force full remount to reset component state
// -----------------------------------------------------------------
function ResetableForm({ userId }) {
  // Changing `key` tells the reconciler this is a NEW component instance.
  // React will unmount the old Form and mount a fresh one — state is reset.
  return <UserForm key={userId} />;
}

function UserForm() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  return (
    <input
      value={name}
      onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)}
      placeholder="Enter name"
    />
  );
}
```

