## ⚡ Short Answer
Portals provide a way to render a React child component into a DOM node that exists outside the parent component's DOM hierarchy. Despite rendering outside the parent DOM tree, the portal child still behaves like a normal React child — events bubble up through the React component tree, not the DOM tree. They are created using `ReactDOM.createPortal(child, container)`.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A React Portal is a first-class mechanism for rendering children into a different DOM node than the one managed by the parent component, while preserving the React component hierarchy and context.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Certain UI elements — modals, tooltips, dropdowns, toasts — need to visually "escape" their parent container to avoid being clipped by `overflow: hidden`, `z-index` stacking contexts, or CSS transforms. Without portals, you'd have to move state up or use hacks like fixed positioning tied to global DOM nodes.

**How it works internally:** `ReactDOM.createPortal(child, domNode)` tells React's reconciler to mount the child's fiber into a different host DOM container while keeping it attached to the same React fiber tree. React's synthetic event system walks the fiber tree (not the DOM tree), so events bubble normally through React ancestors even though the DOM parent is elsewhere.

**Benefits:** Cleanly separates visual rendering location from logical component ownership; preserves context, state, and event bubbling through the React tree; no need to hoist state globally just to render something at the document root.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Can make debugging harder since the DOM structure diverges from the component tree; accessibility (focus management, `aria-*` attributes) must be handled manually; overusing portals for non-escape-hatch scenarios adds unnecessary complexity.

## 💻 Code Example
```jsx
import { useEffect, useRef } from "react";
import ReactDOM from "react-dom";

// --- Reusable Modal Portal ---
function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, children }) {
  // Target a dedicated <div id="modal-root"> in your public/index.html
  const modalRoot = document.getElementById("modal-root");

  // Prevent body scroll while modal is open
  useEffect(() => {
    if (isOpen) document.body.style.overflow = "hidden";
    return () => { document.body.style.overflow = ""; };
  }, [isOpen]);

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  // Even though this renders into #modal-root (outside #app-root),
  // onClick on the overlay still bubbles up through React's component tree.
  return ReactDOM.createPortal(
    <div
      className="modal-overlay"
      onClick={onClose}               // bubbles in React tree ✅
      role="dialog"
      aria-modal="true"
    >
      <div
        className="modal-content"
        onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()} // prevent overlay close on content click
      >
        {children}
        <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>
      </div>
    </div>,
    modalRoot // 👈 renders here in the DOM, but stays in React tree above
  );
}

// --- Usage ---
function App() {
  const [open, setOpen] = React.useState(false);

  return (
    <div style={{ overflow: "hidden" }}> {/* overflow: hidden WON'T clip the modal */}
      <button onClick={() => setOpen(true)}>Open Modal</button>

      {/* Modal escapes this overflow:hidden container via the portal */}
      <Modal isOpen={open} onClose={() => setOpen(false)}>
        <h2>I'm rendered in #modal-root, not inside this div!</h2>
      </Modal>
    </div>
  );
}

// public/index.html (simplified):
// <body>
//   <div id="root"></div>       ← React app mounts here
//   <div id="modal-root"></div> ← Portal target lives here
// </body>
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Do portals break React Context?** A: No. Since portals remain in the React fiber tree, they can still consume any Context provided by ancestor components, even though the DOM node is elsewhere.
- **Q: Do events bubble through a portal to its React parent?** A: Yes. React's synthetic event system follows the component tree, not the DOM tree, so a click inside a portal bubbles up to React ancestors as expected.
- **Q: How do portals work in React 18 with concurrent features?** A: Portals are fully compatible with concurrent rendering; the portal's fiber subtree participates in the same render/commit cycle as the rest of the app, including Suspense boundaries and transitions.

