## ⚡ Short Answer
`useContext` is a React hook that lets you read and subscribe to a React Context value directly inside a function component, eliminating the need for prop drilling. It returns the current context value provided by the nearest matching `Context.Provider` above the component in the tree, and re-renders the component whenever that value changes.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `useContext(MyContext)` is a hook that accepts a context object (created by `React.createContext()`) and returns the current context value for that context.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without context, sharing global-ish state (theme, auth user, locale, cart) requires threading props through every intermediate component — known as "prop drilling." Context + `useContext` lets you teleport data directly to any component that needs it, skipping the ones that don't.

**How it works internally:** React maintains a context stack per tree. When `useContext(Ctx)` is called, React walks up the fiber tree to find the nearest `<Ctx.Provider>` and subscribes the component to its `value` prop. Any time that `value` reference changes (referential equality check), every subscribed consumer re-renders. There is no selector mechanism built in — the whole consumer re-renders even if only one property of the value object changed.

**Benefits:** Zero boilerplate compared to prop drilling; works natively without external libraries; composable with `useReducer` for a lightweight Redux-like pattern; great for low-frequency updates (theme, auth, locale).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** No built-in selector optimization — large context objects with frequent updates can cause unnecessary re-renders across many consumers. Not a full replacement for dedicated state managers (Zustand, Redux) in high-frequency or large-scale scenarios. Overuse can make component reuse harder since components become implicitly coupled to a specific context.

## 💻 Code Example
```tsx
import React, { createContext, useContext, useState, ReactNode } from "react";

// ─── 1. Define shape & create context ────────────────────────────────────────
interface AuthContextValue {
  user: string | null;
  login: (name: string) => void;
  logout: () => void;
}

// Provide a sensible default (used when no Provider is above in the tree)
const AuthContext = createContext<AuthContextValue>({
  user: null,
  login: () => {},
  logout: () => {},
});

// ─── 2. Build a Provider component ───────────────────────────────────────────
export function AuthProvider({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState<string | null>(null);

  const login = (name: string) => setUser(name);
  const logout = () => setUser(null);

  // IMPORTANT: memoize the value object when updates are frequent
  // to avoid re-rendering all consumers on every parent render.
  const value: AuthContextValue = { user, login, logout };

  return <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AuthContext.Provider>;
}

// ─── 3. Custom hook — best practice ──────────────────────────────────────────
// Encapsulates the context, throws early if used outside the Provider.
export function useAuth(): AuthContextValue {
  const ctx = useContext(AuthContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error("useAuth must be used within <AuthProvider>");
  return ctx;
}

// ─── 4. Consumer components — no prop drilling ───────────────────────────────
function Navbar() {
  const { user, logout } = useAuth(); // only subscribes to what it destructures
  return (
    <nav>
      {user ? (
        <>
          <span>Hello, {user}</span>
          <button onClick={logout}>Log out</button>
        </>
      ) : (
        <span>Not logged in</span>
      )}
    </nav>
  );
}

function LoginForm() {
  const { login } = useAuth();
  return <button onClick={() => login("Alice")}>Log in as Alice</button>;
}

// ─── 5. App wiring ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
export default function App() {
  return (
    <AuthProvider>
      {/* Navbar and LoginForm can be deeply nested — no prop drilling needed */}
      <Navbar />
      <LoginForm />
    </AuthProvider>
  );
}
```

