## ⚡ Short Answer
Server-side rendering (SSR) in React means generating the full HTML of a page on the server for each request, sending that HTML to the browser, and then "hydrating" it — attaching React's event listeners and state to the already-rendered markup. This gives users a fully visible page before any JavaScript executes, improving perceived performance and enabling search engines to index content without running JS.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** SSR is the technique of running React's rendering pipeline on the server (Node.js) to produce a complete HTML string, which is sent as the HTTP response. The browser displays it immediately, then React "hydrates" the DOM by reconciling the server-rendered HTML with the client-side component tree.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Traditional client-side rendering (CSR) sends an empty `<div id="root">` and a large JS bundle; the user sees a blank screen until the bundle downloads, parses, and executes. SSR solves the blank-screen problem and makes content available to crawlers that don't execute JavaScript (important for SEO and social sharing previews).

**How it works internally:** On the server, `ReactDOMServer.renderToString()` (or the streaming `renderToPipeableStream()`) traverses the component tree synchronously/asynchronously, resolves data, and outputs an HTML string. That HTML is embedded in the response. In the browser, `ReactDOM.hydrateRoot()` attaches event handlers and reconciles the existing DOM nodes without re-creating them — a process called hydration.

**Benefits:** Faster First Contentful Paint (FCP) and Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), better SEO out of the box, content visible even on slow devices with poor JS execution, and improved link-preview metadata.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Every request hits the server, increasing infrastructure cost and server response time (TTFB). Hydration can cause a "hydration mismatch" if server and client render differently. Long server render times can block the response. Not suitable for highly dynamic, personalized, or real-time UIs where CSR or client-side state management is simpler.

## 💻 Code Example
