## ⚡ Short Answer
Hydration in React 18 is the process of attaching React's event listeners and state to server-rendered HTML that already exists in the DOM, making a static page interactive. React 18 introduced **selective hydration** and **concurrent hydration**, allowing the browser to hydrate parts of the UI incrementally and without blocking the main thread, dramatically improving Time to Interactive (TTI).

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Hydration is the client-side process where React "takes over" server-rendered HTML by reconciling the existing DOM nodes with the virtual DOM and wiring up event handlers, refs, and state — without re-rendering from scratch.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without SSR + hydration, users see a blank page until JavaScript loads and renders everything client-side. SSR sends pre-rendered HTML so users see content immediately, but the page isn't interactive until hydration completes. Before React 18, hydration was an all-or-nothing, synchronous blocking process — one slow component could delay the entire page becoming interactive.

**How it works internally:** React 18 hydration works in conjunction with Suspense and the new streaming SSR architecture (`renderToPipeableStream`). The server streams HTML in chunks as data becomes available. On the client, `hydrateRoot()` replaces the old `ReactDOM.hydrate()`. React uses concurrent rendering to hydrate components in priority order — if a user clicks on a not-yet-hydrated component, React will **selectively hydrate that subtree first** before continuing with the rest. Hydration work is broken into small units that can be interrupted and yielded back to the browser.

**Benefits:**
- Faster TTI because hydration is non-blocking and prioritized
- Selective hydration lets urgent interactions hydrate first
- Works seamlessly with React Suspense boundaries for deferred content
- Streaming SSR means HTML starts arriving sooner

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Hydration mismatches (server HTML ≠ client render) throw errors and cause full client re-renders — harder to debug
- Requires careful handling of browser-only APIs (window, document) that differ from server environment
- More complex mental model than pure CSR or old-style SSR

## 💻 Code Example
```tsx
// === SERVER (Next.js App Router / Node) ===
// React 18 streams HTML using renderToPipeableStream internally.
// Suspense boundaries tell React WHERE it can pause streaming.

// app/page.tsx (Next.js 13+ App Router — uses React 18 streaming SSR)
import { Suspense } from "react";
import HeavyDashboard from "./HeavyDashboard";
import LightHeader from "./LightHeader";

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <div>
      {/* Header hydrates immediately — small, fast */}
      <LightHeader />

      {/* Dashboard is wrapped in Suspense.
          Server streams a fallback first, then streams
          the real HTML once data is ready.
          Client hydrates this boundary independently. */}
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading dashboard...</p>}>
        <HeavyDashboard />
      </Suspense>
    </div>
  );
}

// === CLIENT ENTRY POINT ===
// React 18: hydrateRoot() instead of ReactDOM.hydrate()
import { hydrateRoot } from "react-dom/client";
import App from "./App";

const container = document.getElementById("root")!;

// hydrateRoot attaches React to the existing server-rendered HTML.
// It does NOT re-render from scratch — it reconciles in place.
const root = hydrateRoot(container, <App />);

// You can update after hydration just like createRoot:
// root.render(<App newProp={true} />);

// === HANDLING HYDRATION MISMATCHES ===
// Components that use browser-only values must be guarded.

// ❌ BAD — causes mismatch: server renders "" client renders "dark"
function ThemeBadge() {
  const theme = localStorage.getItem("theme") ?? "light"; // crashes on server
  return <span>{theme}</span>;
}

// ✅ GOOD — suppress or defer browser-only rendering
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

function ThemeBadge() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState("light"); // safe default for SSR

  useEffect(() => {
    // Runs only on client, after hydration is complete
    setTheme(localStorage.getItem("theme") ?? "light");
  }, []);

  return <span>{theme}</span>;
}

// ✅ ALSO VALID — suppressHydrationWarning for intentional mismatches
function Timestamp() {
  return (
    <time suppressHydrationWarning>
      {new Date().toLocaleTimeString()} {/* intentionally differs */}
    </time>
  );
}
```

