## ⚡ Short Answer
Error boundaries are React class components that catch JavaScript errors anywhere in their child component tree, log those errors, and display a fallback UI instead of crashing the entire application. They act like a try/catch block but for React's rendering pipeline, lifecycle methods, and constructors of child components.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A React class component that implements `componentDidCatch()` and/or `getDerivedStateFromError()` to intercept runtime errors during rendering and prevent the whole app from unmounting.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before error boundaries (pre-React 16), a JavaScript error inside a component would corrupt React's internal state and cause cryptic failures across the entire UI. Error boundaries allow you to isolate failures to a subtree, keeping the rest of the app functional.

**How it works internally:** React wraps the render phase in a fiber-level try/catch. When an error is thrown during rendering, a commit phase lifecycle, or a constructor, React walks up the fiber tree to find the nearest error boundary. It then calls `getDerivedStateFromError()` to update state (triggering a re-render with fallback UI) and `componentDidCatch()` for side effects like logging.

**Benefits:** Graceful degradation, scoped failure isolation, centralized error logging, better UX than a blank white screen.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Only work with class components (no Hook equivalent yet — `use` and `useErrorBoundary` are still evolving). They do NOT catch errors in event handlers, async code (setTimeout/fetch), or server-side rendering — those must be handled manually with try/catch.

## 💻 Code Example
```jsx
import React from "react";

// ─── 1. Define the Error Boundary ────────────────────────────────────────────
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false, error: null };
  }

  // Called during render phase — use to update state so next render shows fallback
  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    return { hasError: true, error };
  }

  // Called after render — use for side effects like logging to Sentry/Datadog
  componentDidCatch(error, info) {
    console.error("ErrorBoundary caught:", error);
    console.error("Component stack:", info.componentStack);
    // e.g. Sentry.captureException(error, { extra: info });
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      // Render custom fallback UI
      return (
        <div role="alert" style={{ padding: 24, color: "red" }}>
          <h2>Something went wrong.</h2>
          <pre style={{ fontSize: 12 }}>{this.state.error?.message}</pre>
          <button onClick={() => this.setState({ hasError: false, error: null })}>
            Try Again
          </button>
        </div>
      );
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}

// ─── 2. A Buggy Child Component ───────────────────────────────────────────────
function BuggyWidget({ shouldCrash }) {
  if (shouldCrash) {
    throw new Error("Widget exploded during render!"); // Caught by boundary
  }
  return <div>✅ Widget loaded successfully</div>;
}

// ─── 3. App — wrap only the risky subtree, not the whole page ─────────────────
export default function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>

      {/* This boundary isolates the widget — header/sidebar stay intact */}
      <ErrorBoundary>
        <BuggyWidget shouldCrash={true} />
      </ErrorBoundary>

      <footer>Footer still renders fine ✅</footer>
    </div>
  );
}

// ─── 4. Modern helper: react-error-boundary (community standard) ──────────────
// import { ErrorBoundary } from "react-error-boundary";
//
// <ErrorBoundary
//   FallbackComponent={({ error, resetErrorBoundary }) => (
//     <div>
//       <p>Error: {error.message}</p>
//       <button onClick={resetErrorBoundary}>Retry</button>
//     </div>
//   )}
//   onError={(error, info) => Sentry.captureException(error)}
// >
//   <BuggyWidget />
// </ErrorBoundary>
```

