## ⚡ Short Answer
React performance optimization involves reducing unnecessary re-renders, minimizing bundle size, and deferring expensive work. The core tools are `React.memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`, code splitting with `React.lazy`, and proper state architecture. The golden rule: measure first with React DevTools Profiler before optimizing anything.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A set of techniques and APIs to reduce wasted renders, shrink JavaScript payload, and improve perceived performance in React applications.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** React re-renders components on every state/prop change by default. In large trees, this causes cascading renders that block the main thread, slow interactions, and hurt Core Web Vitals (LCP, INP, CLS).

**How it works internally:** React's reconciler (Fiber) diffs the virtual DOM tree on each render. `React.memo` short-circuits this by doing a shallow prop comparison before entering reconciliation. `useMemo`/`useCallback` stabilize references so downstream memoized children don't re-render. `React.lazy` + `Suspense` use dynamic `import()` to split the bundle at route or component boundaries, reducing initial parse/execute time.

**Benefits:** Fewer wasted renders, smaller initial bundle, faster Time to Interactive, better UX on low-end devices, and lower CPU usage during complex interactions.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Memoization adds memory overhead and cognitive complexity. Over-memoizing with `useMemo`/`useCallback` everywhere is a common anti-pattern — the comparison cost can exceed the render cost for cheap components. Code splitting adds network round trips if not preloaded properly.

## 💻 Code Example
```tsx
import React, {
  useState, useMemo, useCallback, lazy, Suspense, memo
} from "react";

// ─── 1. React.memo: skip re-render when props haven't changed ───────────────
const ProductCard = memo(({ product, onAddToCart }: {
  product: { id: number; name: string; price: number };
  onAddToCart: (id: number) => void;
}) => {
  console.log(`Rendering ProductCard ${product.id}`); // fires only when props change
  return (
    <div>
      <h3>{product.name} — ${product.price}</h3>
      <button onClick={() => onAddToCart(product.id)}>Add to Cart</button>
    </div>
  );
});

// ─── 2. Code splitting with React.lazy + Suspense ───────────────────────────
// Heavy chart library only loaded when the Analytics route is visited
const AnalyticsDashboard = lazy(() => import("./AnalyticsDashboard"));

// ─── 3. Parent component — correct use of useMemo & useCallback ─────────────
function ProductList({ rawProducts }: { rawProducts: typeof products }) {
  const [cart, setCart] = useState<number[]>([]);
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState("");

  // useMemo: expensive filter/sort only re-runs when dependencies change
  const filteredProducts = useMemo(() => {
    console.log("Filtering products…");
    return rawProducts
      .filter(p => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(filter.toLowerCase()))
      .sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
  }, [rawProducts, filter]); // ← explicit, minimal deps

  // useCallback: stable reference so ProductCard never re-renders due to this prop
  const handleAddToCart = useCallback((id: number) => {
    setCart(prev => [...prev, id]);
  }, []); // no deps — setCart is stable

  return (
    <>
      <input
        value={filter}
        onChange={e => setFilter(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Filter products…"
      />
      <p>Cart items: {cart.length}</p>

      {filteredProducts.map(p => (
        <ProductCard
          key={p.id}
          product={p}
          onAddToCart={handleAddToCart} // stable ref → memo works correctly
        />
      ))}

      {/* 4. Suspense boundary for lazily loaded route */}
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading analytics…</div>}>
        <AnalyticsDashboard />
      </Suspense>
    </>
  );
}

// ─── 5. State colocation: keep state close to where it's used ───────────────
// BAD: lifting ephemeral UI state to a global store causes app-wide re-renders
// GOOD: local component state stays local — only that subtree re-renders

