## ⚡ Short Answer
`React.memo` is a higher-order component that wraps a functional component and prevents it from re-rendering if its props haven't changed, using shallow equality comparison by default. It's React's component-level memoization tool, similar to how `PureComponent` works for class components. Use it when a component is expensive to render and its parent re-renders frequently without changing the child's props.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `React.memo` is a HOC (higher-order component) that memoizes the rendered output of a functional component. If the parent re-renders but the wrapped component's props are shallowly equal to the previous render, React skips re-rendering it and reuses the last rendered result.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** By default, every React component re-renders whenever its parent re-renders, even if its own props and state haven't changed. This causes unnecessary work — especially costly for components with heavy computations, large lists, or complex DOM trees. `React.memo` solves this by adding a prop-equality gate before the render cycle.

**How it works internally:** React stores the previous props and rendered output. Before re-rendering, it runs a shallow comparison (or your custom comparator) between the previous and next props. If they are equal, React returns the cached fiber (the previously rendered virtual DOM subtree) and skips reconciliation for that subtree entirely. If not equal, it renders normally and updates the cache.

**Benefits:** Eliminates unnecessary re-renders of stable child components, improves perceived performance in large component trees, pairs naturally with `useMemo` and `useCallback` to stabilize object/function props.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Memoization itself has a cost — the comparison runs on every parent render. For cheap components, the comparison overhead may exceed the render cost. It also only does shallow comparison, so object/function props created inline will always be "new" references, defeating the optimization unless you also use `useMemo`/`useCallback`.

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
import React, { useState, useCallback, memo } from "react";

// ─── 1. Expensive child component wrapped with React.memo ───────────────────
// Only re-renders if `items` or `onSelect` change (shallow comparison).
const ItemList = memo(function ItemList({ items, onSelect }) {
  console.log("ItemList rendered"); // watch this in DevTools
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item.id} onClick={() => onSelect(item.id)}>
          {item.name}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
});

// ─── 2. Custom comparator (optional second argument) ────────────────────────
// Use when you need deep equality or want to ignore certain props.
const ExpensiveChart = memo(
  function ExpensiveChart({ data, title }) {
    console.log("ExpensiveChart rendered");
    return <div>{/* ...heavy chart render... */}</div>;
  },
  (prevProps, nextProps) => {
    // Return true  → skip re-render (props are "equal" to us)
    // Return false → allow re-render
    return (
      prevProps.title === nextProps.title &&
      prevProps.data.length === nextProps.data.length
    );
  }
);

// ─── 3. Parent component ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
export default function Dashboard() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [items] = useState([
    { id: 1, name: "Alpha" },
    { id: 2, name: "Beta" },
  ]);

  // ✅ useCallback stabilises the function reference across renders.
  // Without this, a new function is created each render → memo is defeated.
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => {
    console.log("Selected:", id);
  }, []); // empty deps → same reference forever

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Clicking increment re-renders Dashboard, but NOT ItemList
          because `items` and `handleSelect` references are stable. */}
      <button onClick={() => setCount((c) => c + 1)}>
        Increment: {count}
      </button>

      {/* ✅ ItemList will NOT re-render on count change */}
      <ItemList items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} />

      {/* ❌ Anti-pattern: inline object → new reference every render
          → memo is completely useless here */}
      {/* <ItemList items={[{ id: 1, name: "Alpha" }]} onSelect={() => {}} /> */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

