## ⚡ Short Answer
A custom hook is a JavaScript function whose name starts with `use` that calls other React hooks internally, allowing you to extract and reuse stateful logic across multiple components. It's not a React API feature — it's a convention that lets you compose built-in hooks into reusable abstractions. Custom hooks keep components clean by moving complex logic out of JSX and into testable, shareable functions.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A plain JavaScript function prefixed with `use` that can call `useState`, `useEffect`, `useContext`, or any other hook — including other custom hooks — and returns whatever values the consuming component needs.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before hooks, sharing stateful logic between components required patterns like Higher-Order Components (HOCs) or render props, both of which add wrapper layers, complicate the component tree, and make debugging harder. Custom hooks let you share logic without changing your component hierarchy at all.

**How it works internally:** React's rules of hooks are enforced by lint rules and the React runtime, which track hook call order per component instance. When a component calls your custom hook, React doesn't create any special context for it — the hooks called inside your custom hook are associated with the calling component's fiber node, just as if they were called directly in the component. Each component that calls the same custom hook gets its own isolated state.

**Benefits:** Promotes DRY principles for stateful logic, makes components dramatically easier to read, enables unit testing of logic in isolation from UI, and composes cleanly with other hooks.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Can hide complexity if over-abstracted too early; debugging requires understanding the hook's internals; naming discipline (`use` prefix) and hook rules must be followed or React will throw errors.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

// --- Custom Hook: useFetch ---
// Encapsulates fetch lifecycle: loading, data, and error states.
// Each component that calls this gets its OWN isolated state.
function useFetch<T>(url: string) {
  const [data, setData] = useState<T | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState<boolean>(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Reset state on URL change
    setLoading(true);
    setData(null);
    setError(null);

    const controller = new AbortController();

    fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((res) => {
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP error: ${res.status}`);
        return res.json();
      })
      .then((json) => setData(json as T))
      .catch((err) => {
        // Ignore abort errors on cleanup
        if (err.name !== "AbortError") setError(err);
      })
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));

    // Cleanup: cancel the request if URL changes or component unmounts
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [url]);

  return { data, loading, error };
}

// --- Consuming Component ---
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: number }) {
  // All fetch logic lives in the hook — component stays clean
  const { data: user, loading, error } = useFetch<User>(
    `https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/${userId}`
  );

  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error.message}</p>;
  if (!user) return null;

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{user.name}</h2>
      <p>{user.email}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

// --- Another component reusing the SAME hook with its OWN state ---
function UserList() {
  const { data: users, loading } = useFetch<User[]>(
    "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users"
  );

  if (loading) return <p>Loading users...</p>;
  return <ul>{users?.map((u) => <li key={u.id}>{u.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Does each component that calls the same custom hook share the same state?** A: No. Each component invocation gets completely independent state. The hook is just a function — React ties the hook calls to the specific component's fiber, so state is never shared between callers unless you explicitly use something like Context or a global store.
- **Q: Can a custom hook call another custom hook?** A: Yes, and this is a major strength of the pattern. You can compose hooks — for example, a `useUserData` hook might internally call `useFetch` and `useLocalStorage`, building higher-level abstractions from lower-level ones cleanly.
