## ⚡ Short Answer
Prop drilling is the pattern of passing data through multiple intermediate React components that don't need it, just to get it to a deeply nested child that does. It creates tight coupling, makes refactoring painful, and clutters intermediate components with props they never use. You avoid it using React Context, state management libraries (Redux, Zustand, Jotai), or component composition techniques.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Prop drilling occurs when a prop must be threaded through several layers of components — grandparent → parent → child → grandchild — even though only the final consumer actually needs the value.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** React's unidirectional data flow is intentional and predictable, but when component trees grow deep, passing state down becomes a maintenance burden. Every intermediate component becomes a "relay station," aware of data it has no business knowing about.

**How it works internally:** React re-renders components when props change. With drilling, every intermediate component in the chain re-renders when the drilled prop changes, even if that component does nothing with it (unless memoized). This creates both a coupling problem and a potential performance problem.

**Benefits of prop drilling (when shallow):** Explicit, traceable data flow. Easy to debug — you can follow the prop up the tree. No external dependencies needed. Good for 1-2 levels deep.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Intermediate components become polluted with irrelevant props. Refactoring is fragile — adding/removing a layer breaks the chain. Violates the principle of least knowledge. Becomes exponentially worse with deeply nested trees or many shared values.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ❌ PROP DRILLING — theme and user passed through every layer
const App = () => {
  const user = { name: "Alice" };
  const theme = "dark";
  return <Layout user={user} theme={theme} />;
};

const Layout = ({ user, theme }: { user: any; theme: string }) => (
  // Layout doesn't use user or theme — just passes them down
  <Sidebar user={user} theme={theme} />
);

const Sidebar = ({ user, theme }: { user: any; theme: string }) => (
  // Sidebar doesn't use them either
  <UserProfile user={user} theme={theme} />
);

const UserProfile = ({ user, theme }: { user: any; theme: string }) => (
  <div className={theme}>{user.name}</div> // Only this component needed them
);

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ✅ SOLUTION 1: React Context
import React, { createContext, useContext } from "react";

interface AppContextType {
  user: { name: string };
  theme: string;
}

const AppContext = createContext<AppContextType | null>(null);

const useAppContext = () => {
  const ctx = useContext(AppContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error("useAppContext must be used within AppProvider");
  return ctx;
};

const AppProvider = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => {
  const user = { name: "Alice" };
  const theme = "dark";
  return (
    <AppContext.Provider value={{ user, theme }}>
      {children}
    </AppContext.Provider>
  );
};

// Intermediate components are now clean — no props needed
const CleanLayout = () => <CleanSidebar />;
const CleanSidebar = () => <CleanUserProfile />;

const CleanUserProfile = () => {
  const { user, theme } = useAppContext(); // Consumed directly, no drilling
  return <div className={theme}>{user.name}</div>;
};

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ✅ SOLUTION 2: Zustand (lightweight global store — great for larger apps)
import { create } from "zustand";

interface StoreState {
  user: { name: string };
  theme: string;
}

const useStore = create<StoreState>(() => ({
  user: { name: "Alice" },
  theme: "dark",
}));

const ZustandUserProfile = () => {
  const { user, theme } = useStore(); // Pull exactly what you need
  return <div className={theme}>{user.name}</div>;
};

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ✅ SOLUTION 3: Component Composition (often overlooked)
// Pass the fully-rendered child as a prop instead of raw data
const ComposedApp = () => {
  const user = { name: "Alice" };
  const theme = "dark";

  // Compose at the top level; Layout just renders {children}
  return (
    <ComposedLayout>
      <ComposedSidebar>
        <div className={theme}>{user.name}</div>
      </ComposedSidebar>
    </ComposedLayout
