## ⚡ Short Answer
`useTransition` and `useDeferredValue` are React 18 concurrent hooks that let you mark certain state updates as non-urgent, keeping the UI responsive while expensive re-renders happen in the background. `useTransition` wraps the state update itself, while `useDeferredValue` wraps the value you want to defer — both prevent blocking the main thread on slow renders.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Two React 18 hooks that integrate with the Concurrent Renderer to split UI updates into "urgent" (user input, typing) and "non-urgent" (heavy list re-renders, search results) categories.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before React 18, all state updates were synchronous and equally prioritized. A slow component (e.g. rendering 10,000 filtered rows) would block typing in a search input, causing jank. These hooks solve that by letting React interrupt low-priority renders to handle high-priority ones first.

**How it works internally:** React's concurrent renderer can pause, resume, and abandon in-progress renders. Marking an update as a "transition" tells the scheduler it can be interrupted. `useTransition` wraps the setter call; `useDeferredValue` creates a "lagging" copy of a value that React updates only when idle. Both use the same scheduler priority mechanism under the hood — essentially `startTransition` schedules work at a lower lane in React's fiber architecture.

**Benefits:** Eliminates input lag during expensive renders, provides a built-in `isPending` flag for loading states, no external libraries needed, composable with Suspense.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Only helps when the bottleneck is React rendering — not network fetches or JS computation outside React. `useDeferredValue` may cause a "double render" (once with old value, once with new). Adds complexity; overkill for simple/fast components.

