## ⚡ Short Answer
React Server Components (RSCs) are components that render exclusively on the server and never ship their JavaScript to the browser, enabling direct database/filesystem access and zero client-side bundle cost. They differ from Client Components in that RSCs cannot use browser APIs, event handlers, or stateful hooks, while Client Components run in the browser and handle interactivity. The two models compose together — RSCs can render Client Components as children, but not vice versa.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** React Server Components are a rendering paradigm introduced in React 18+ where certain components execute only on the server, producing a serialized component tree (RSC payload) sent to the client — not raw HTML like SSR, and not JavaScript like traditional components.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Traditional React requires shipping component logic to the browser even when that logic only fetches data or formats content. This bloats bundles, forces API layers for data that lives on the server, and waterfalls data fetching through the component tree. RSCs let you co-locate data access with rendering without paying a client bundle cost.

**How it works internally:** When a request hits the server, React renders the Server Component tree and produces an RSC payload — a special JSON-like stream describing the UI. Client Components are serialized as references (not executed server-side). The client React runtime reconstructs the full tree by merging the RSC payload with hydrated Client Components. On navigation, only the RSC payload is sent over the wire, not a full HTML page.

**Benefits:** Zero JS bundle contribution from server components, direct DB/filesystem access without an API layer, async/await at the component level, automatic code splitting between server and client, and improved performance through reduced hydration cost.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Cannot use `useState`, `useEffect`, event handlers, or browser-only APIs. Debugging is more complex across the server/client boundary. The mental model of "which component lives where" adds cognitive overhead. Caching behavior can be subtle and hard to predict. Currently tightly coupled to framework implementations (Next.js App Router being the primary one).

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─── Server Component (default in Next.js App Router) ───────────────────────
// No "use client" directive = Server Component
// Can be async, can query DB directly, ships ZERO JS to browser

import { db } from "@/lib/db";
import { ProductCard } from "./ProductCard"; // Client Component

// This async function runs ONLY on the server
export default async function ProductList() {
  // Direct DB query — no fetch(), no API route needed
  const products = await db.query(
    "SELECT id, name, price FROM products WHERE active = true"
  );

  return (
    <section>
      <h1>Products</h1>
      {products.map((product) => (
        // RSC renders Client Components — passes serializable props only
        <ProductCard key={product.id} product={product} />
      ))}
    </section>
  );
}

// ─── Client Component ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// "use client" marks the boundary — this file + its imports go to the browser

"use client";

import { useState } from "react";

interface Product {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  price: number;
}

// Receives plain serializable data from the Server Component above
export function ProductCard({ product }: { product: Product }) {
  const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);

  const handleAddToCart = () => {
    // Browser interaction — only possible in a Client Component
    setAdded(true);
    console.log(`Added ${product.name} to cart`);
  };

  return (
    <div className="product-card">
      <h2>{product.name}</h2>
      <p>${product.price}</p>
      <button onClick={handleAddToCart} disabled={added}>
        {added ? "Added ✓" : "Add to Cart"}
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

// ─── INVALID: Cannot pass non-serializable props across the boundary ─────────
// ❌ export default async function Bad() {
//   const fn = () => console.log("hi");
//   return <ProductCard onAction={fn} />; // Functions can't cross RSC boundary
// }

// ─── Composing: RSC as children of Client Components ────────────────────────
// This pattern lets you "lift" server data into a client shell

"use client";

export function ClientShell({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  // `children` here can be a Server Component rendered by the parent
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setOpen(!open)}>Toggle</button>
      {open && children}
    </div>
  );
}

// In a Server Component parent:
// <ClientShell>
//   <ServerDataComponent /> ← valid! RSC passed as children prop
