## ⚡ Short Answer
A Higher-Order Component (HOC) is a function that takes a component and returns a new enhanced component, following the higher-order function pattern from functional programming. It's a compositional pattern for reusing component logic across multiple components without modifying the originals. HOCs are not part of the React API — they're a convention that emerges naturally from React's compositional nature.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A function with the signature `const EnhancedComponent = withSomething(WrappedComponent)` that wraps a component to inject props, state, or behavior without altering the original component's source code.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before React Hooks (pre-16.8), sharing stateful logic between class components was painful — mixins were deprecated, and render props added JSX nesting. HOCs solved the cross-cutting concerns problem: authentication guards, logging, data fetching, theming, and permissions could be written once and composed onto any component.

**How it works internally:** The HOC creates a new wrapper component that renders the original wrapped component, passing through its own props plus any injected props. React sees it as two separate components in the tree, which is why display names need to be set manually for DevTools debugging. The wrapper intercepts the render cycle, can add lifecycle behavior (in class HOCs), and uses prop spreading (`{...this.props}`) to remain transparent to the wrapped component.

**Benefits:** Separation of concerns, DRY logic reuse, composability (HOCs can be chained), non-invasive (wrapped component is unaware), works with class and function components.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Prop collision risk (injected props can overwrite existing ones), wrapper hell in DevTools when multiple HOCs are stacked, static methods on wrapped components aren't automatically hoisted, refs don't pass through without `React.forwardRef`, and HOCs obscure where props come from. In modern React, custom Hooks are usually the better alternative for logic reuse.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
import React, { useEffect, useState, ComponentType, FC } from "react";

// --- Types ---
interface WithAuthProps {
  currentUser: { id: string; name: string } | null;
}

// --- HOC: withAuth ---
// Injects `currentUser` prop and redirects if not authenticated
function withAuth<T extends WithAuthProps>(
  WrappedComponent: ComponentType<T>
): FC<Omit<T, keyof WithAuthProps>> {
  const displayName =
    WrappedComponent.displayName || WrappedComponent.name || "Component";

  const WithAuth: FC<Omit<T, keyof WithAuthProps>> = (props) => {
    const [currentUser, setCurrentUser] = useState<WithAuthProps["currentUser"]>(null);
    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

    useEffect(() => {
      // Simulate auth check (in production: call auth service)
      setTimeout(() => {
        setCurrentUser({ id: "u1", name: "Alice" }); // null = not logged in
        setLoading(false);
      }, 500);
    }, []);

    if (loading) return <div>Checking authentication...</div>;

    if (!currentUser) {
      // In production: redirect to /login via React Router
      return <div>Access denied. Please log in.</div>;
    }

    // Spread original props + inject currentUser
    return <WrappedComponent {...(props as T)} currentUser={currentUser} />;
  };

  // Critical: set displayName for React DevTools readability
  WithAuth.displayName = `withAuth(${displayName})`;

  return WithAuth;
}

// --- HOC: withLogger ---
// Logs every render with component name and props
function withLogger<T extends object>(
  WrappedComponent: ComponentType<T>
): FC<T> {
  const displayName =
    WrappedComponent.displayName || WrappedComponent.name || "Component";

  const WithLogger: FC<T> = (props) => {
    useEffect(() => {
      console.log(`[${displayName}] mounted`, props);
      return () => console.log(`[${displayName}] unmounted`);
    });

    return <WrappedComponent {...props} />;
  };

  WithLogger.displayName = `withLogger(${displayName})`;
  return WithLogger;
}

// --- Base Component ---
interface DashboardProps extends WithAuthProps {
  title: string;
}

const Dashboard: FC<DashboardProps> = ({ currentUser, title }) => (
  <div>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p>Welcome, {currentUser?.name}!</p>
  </div>
);

// --- Compose HOCs (right-to-left application) ---
// withLogger wraps the already-auth-guarded Dashboard
const EnhancedDashboard = withLogger(withAuth(Dashboard));

// --- Usage
