## ⚡ Short Answer
The Compound Component pattern is a React design pattern where a parent component manages shared state and exposes a set of child components that work together, communicating implicitly through React Context rather than explicit prop-drilling. It gives consumers a flexible, expressive API — similar to how native HTML elements like `<select>` and `<option>` work together without needing to wire them manually.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A pattern where one "parent" component owns state and renders child sub-components (often attached as static properties) that consume that state via Context, allowing the consumer to compose UI freely without managing the internals.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Traditional component APIs force you to pass many props to a single component, leading to "prop bloat" and inflexible rendering. Compound Components let consumers control structure and layout while the parent handles logic — a clean separation of concerns.

**How it works internally:** The parent creates a React Context and provides shared state (e.g., `isOpen`, `toggle`). Child sub-components call `useContext` to read that state. The parent exposes these children as named exports or static properties (e.g., `Accordion.Item`, `Accordion.Header`). React's tree reconciliation means Context is inherited by any descendant, not just direct children, enabling flexible composition.

**Benefits:** Eliminates prop-drilling, decouples structure from logic, creates expressive and readable JSX, highly flexible for consumers to rearrange or extend UI, and hides internal complexity.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** More verbose to build than a simple component; consumers must know which sub-components exist; misusing Context for high-frequency state updates (e.g., mouse position) can cause re-render issues; tooling like TypeScript requires extra care to type static properties correctly.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
import React, { createContext, useContext, useState, ReactNode } from "react";

// --- 1. Define shared state shape and Context ---
interface AccordionContextValue {
  openIndex: number | null;
  toggle: (index: number) => void;
}

const AccordionContext = createContext<AccordionContextValue | null>(null);

function useAccordion(): AccordionContextValue {
  const ctx = useContext(AccordionContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error("Must be used inside <Accordion>");
  return ctx;
}

// --- 2. Parent component — owns state, provides Context ---
interface AccordionProps {
  children: ReactNode;
}

function Accordion({ children }: AccordionProps) {
  const [openIndex, setOpenIndex] = useState<number | null>(null);

  const toggle = (index: number) =>
    setOpenIndex((prev) => (prev === index ? null : index));

  return (
    <AccordionContext.Provider value={{ openIndex, toggle }}>
      <div className="accordion">{children}</div>
    </AccordionContext.Provider>
  );
}

// --- 3. Sub-components — consume Context, no props needed for state ---
interface ItemProps {
  index: number;
  children: ReactNode;
}

function Item({ index, children }: ItemProps) {
  // Just a structural wrapper; passes index down via its own local Context
  // or directly via props to Header/Panel children (kept simple here)
  return <div className="accordion-item">{children}</div>;
}

interface HeaderProps {
  index: number;
  children: ReactNode;
}

function Header({ index, children }: HeaderProps) {
  const { openIndex, toggle } = useAccordion();
  const isOpen = openIndex === index;

  return (
    <button
      className={`accordion-header ${isOpen ? "open" : ""}`}
      onClick={() => toggle(index)}
      aria-expanded={isOpen}
    >
      {children} {isOpen ? "▲" : "▼"}
    </button>
  );
}

interface PanelProps {
  index: number;
  children: ReactNode;
}

function Panel({ index, children }: PanelProps) {
  const { openIndex } = useAccordion();
  if (openIndex !== index) return null;

  return <div className="accordion-panel">{children}</div>;
}

// --- 4. Attach sub-components as static properties ---
Accordion.Item   = Item;
Accordion.Header = Header;
Accordion.Panel  = Panel;

// --- 5. Consumer usage — expressive, flexible JSX ---
export function App() {
  return (
    <Accordion>
      <Accordion.Item index={0}>
        <Accordion.Header index={0}>What is React?</Accordion.Header>
        <Accordion.Panel index={0}>
          React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces.
        </Accordion.Panel>
      </Accordion.Item>

      <Accordion.Item index={1}>
        <Accordion.Header index={1}>What is TypeScript?</Accordion.Header>
