## ⚡ Short Answer
The Container/Presentational pattern separates React components into two roles: containers handle data fetching, state, and business logic, while presentational components purely render UI based on props. This separation improves reusability, testability, and code organization by keeping concerns isolated. It was popularized by Dan Abramov, though hooks have partially replaced it for simpler cases.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A component design pattern that splits UI into "smart" container components (logic layer) and "dumb" presentational components (view layer). Containers own state and behavior; presentational components are stateless UI renderers.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without this separation, components become bloated with mixed concerns — API calls, state management, and JSX rendering all tangled together. This makes them hard to test, reuse, or redesign. The pattern enforces a clean boundary between "what the data is" and "how it looks."

**How it works internally:** The container component fetches or derives data, manages state (or connects to Redux/Context), and passes everything down as props to the presentational child. The presentational component is often a pure function — same props always produce the same output, no side effects.

**Benefits:** Presentational components are highly reusable across different data sources; they're trivial to unit test since they're pure functions; designers can work on UI without touching logic; containers can swap data sources without changing the view.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds boilerplate and extra files for simple cases. Custom hooks now handle logic extraction more elegantly in many scenarios, making the full pattern overkill for small components. Prop drilling can become painful if the component tree is deep.

