## ⚡ Short Answer
`React.memo` is a higher-order component that memoizes a functional component's rendered output, skipping re-renders when the component receives the same props as the previous render. You should apply it selectively — only when a component is genuinely expensive to render and its parent re-renders frequently with props that haven't changed. Overusing it adds memory overhead and comparison cost without benefit.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** `React.memo` wraps a functional component and caches its last rendered result. On the next render, if props are shallowly equal to the previous render's props, React reuses the cached output instead of calling the component function again.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** React re-renders a component every time its parent re-renders, even if the child's props are identical. For cheap components this is fine, but for components doing heavy computation, rendering large lists, or containing complex subtrees, unnecessary re-renders waste CPU cycles and degrade UX.

**How it works internally:** React stores the previous props and the previous render output. Before each render, it runs a shallow equality check (Object.is comparison) on every prop. If all props pass, React bails out and returns the cached fiber output. You can supply a custom comparison function as the second argument for deep or selective equality checks.

**Benefits:** Eliminates unnecessary re-renders for stable props, reduces reconciliation work, and can meaningfully improve performance in lists, dashboards, and frequently-updating parent trees.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Shallow comparison fails for inline objects/arrays/functions (new reference each render), adding `React.memo` everywhere increases memory usage and adds comparison overhead per render, and it can mask architectural problems (e.g., unstable prop references) that should be fixed instead. It also does nothing if a component uses context or internal state — those still trigger re-renders.

## 💻 Code Example
```tsx
import React, { useState, useCallback, memo } from "react";

// ─── 1. Expensive child component ────────────────────────────────────────────
// Wrapped in React.memo so it only re-renders when `items` or `onSelect` change.
const ProductList = memo(function ProductList({
  items,
  onSelect,
}: {
  items: { id: number; name: string }[];
  onSelect: (id: number) => void;
}) {
  console.log("ProductList rendered"); // watch the console
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item.id} onClick={() => onSelect(item.id)}>
          {item.name}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
});

// ─── 2. Custom comparator (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Use when you want to ignore certain prop changes (e.g., metadata).
const ProductListWithCustomCompare = memo(
  function ProductList({ items, onSelect, metadata }: any) {
    return <ul>{/* ... */}</ul>;
  },
  (prev, next) =>
    // Re-render only when items or onSelect change; ignore metadata changes
    prev.items === next.items && prev.onSelect === next.onSelect
);

// ─── 3. Parent component ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
export default function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<number | null>(null);

  // ✅ useCallback keeps the function reference stable across re-renders.
  // Without this, a new function reference on every render defeats React.memo.
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id: number) => {
    setSelected(id);
  }, []); // no deps → reference never changes

  // ✅ useMemo keeps the array reference stable.
  // Without this, a new array literal defeats React.memo.
  const items = React.useMemo(
    () => [
      { id: 1, name: "Widget A" },
      { id: 2, name: "Widget B" },
    ],
    [] // static data → memoize once
  );

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Clicking this button re-renders App, but NOT ProductList */}
      <button onClick={() => setCount((c) => c + 1)}>
        App render count: {count}
      </button>

      <p>Selected: {selected}</p>

      {/* ProductList is skipped because items and handleSelect are stable */}
      <ProductList items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </div>
  );
}

// ─── When NOT to use React.memo ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// ❌ Wrapping a trivially cheap component: <Badge label="New" />
//    The comparison cost > the render cost.
