## ⚡ Short Answer
Code splitting is the technique of breaking your JavaScript bundle into smaller chunks that are loaded on demand, rather than shipping one massive file upfront. Dynamic `import()` is the ES module syntax that enables this — React leverages it via `React.lazy()` and `Suspense` to defer loading components until they're actually needed, dramatically improving initial load time.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Code splitting divides your app's bundle into multiple chunks. Dynamic `import()` is a Promise-based API that loads a module asynchronously at runtime instead of statically at build time. `React.lazy()` wraps a dynamic import to create a lazily-loaded component, and `Suspense` provides the loading UI while the chunk fetches.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** A typical React SPA bundles every component into one JS file. Users on slow networks must download and parse all code — including routes and features they may never visit — before anything renders. Code splitting lets you pay only for what the user actually needs right now.

**How it works internally:** Bundlers like Webpack or Vite detect a dynamic `import()` call and emit a separate chunk file for that module. At runtime, when the `import()` is executed, the browser makes an HTTP request for that chunk, evaluates it, and resolves the Promise. `React.lazy()` holds the resolved component in a ref and uses React's Suspense mechanism (throwing a Promise) to signal "still loading" to the nearest `<Suspense>` boundary, which renders the fallback UI.

**Benefits:** Smaller initial bundle → faster Time to Interactive (TTI); chunks are cached independently so a component update doesn't bust the entire app cache; aligns perfectly with route-based or feature-based splitting strategies.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Introduces a network waterfall if not preloaded — the user sees a spinner on first visit to a route. Each chunk is an extra HTTP request (mitigated by HTTP/2). Overly granular splitting can hurt performance. SSR requires extra setup (e.g., `loadable-components` or Next.js built-ins) because `React.lazy` doesn't support server rendering natively.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─── 1. Route-based code splitting (most common pattern) ───────────────────
import React, { Suspense, lazy } from "react";
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from "react-router-dom";

// ✅ Each lazy() call becomes its own JS chunk at build time
const HomePage    = lazy(() => import("./pages/HomePage"));
const Dashboard   = lazy(() => import("./pages/Dashboard"));
const AdminPanel  = lazy(() => import("./pages/AdminPanel"));

function App() {
  return (
    <BrowserRouter>
      {/* Suspense fallback renders while the chunk is downloading */}
      <Suspense fallback={<div className="spinner">Loading…</div>}>
        <Routes>
          <Route path="/"        element={<HomePage />} />
          <Route path="/dash"    element={<Dashboard />} />
          <Route path="/admin"   element={<AdminPanel />} />
        </Routes>
      </Suspense>
    </BrowserRouter>
  );
}

// ─── 2. Component-level splitting (heavy feature, e.g. a chart library) ─────
const HeavyChart = lazy(() =>
  // Named export — you must re-export as default, or use a wrapper
  import("./components/HeavyChart").then((mod) => ({ default: mod.HeavyChart }))
);

function ReportPage() {
  const [showChart, setShowChart] = React.useState(false);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setShowChart(true)}>Load Chart</button>
      {showChart && (
        // Nested Suspense keeps the rest of the page interactive
        <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading chart…</p>}>
          <HeavyChart data={[]} />
        </Suspense>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

// ─── 3. Preloading a chunk before the user navigates ────────────────────────
// Call the import() eagerly on hover so the chunk is cached by the time
// the user clicks — eliminates the spinner on navigation.
const preloadDashboard = () => import("./pages/Dashboard");

function NavLink() {
  return (
    <a
      href="/dash"
      onMouseEnter={preloadDashboard} // fires import() → chunk downloads in bg
    >
      Dashboard
    </a>
  );
}

// ─── 4. Error boundary around Suspense (production must-have) ────────────────
class ChunkErrorBoundary extends React.Component<
  { children: React.ReactNode },
  { hasError: boolean }
> {
  state = { hasError: false };

  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { hasError: true };
