## ⚡ Short Answer
The Context API is great for low-frequency updates like themes or auth state, but it has a critical performance flaw: every component that consumes a context re-renders whenever any part of that context value changes, even if the component only cares about an unrelated slice of the data. For high-frequency, granular global state (like a shopping cart, real-time data, or complex UI state), dedicated state managers like Redux, Zustand, or Jotai are far better choices.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** React's built-in mechanism for passing data through the component tree without prop drilling, using `React.createContext`, a `Provider`, and `useContext`.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** It was designed to solve prop drilling for relatively static, app-wide values — things like the current user, locale, or color theme — not as a general-purpose global state solution.

**How it works internally:** When a Provider's `value` prop changes (by reference equality), React schedules a re-render for every component subscribed to that context via `useContext`, regardless of which property within the value they actually use. There is no built-in selector mechanism to bail out of a re-render when only an irrelevant part of the context changed.

**Benefits:**
- Zero dependencies, ships with React
- Simple API, no boilerplate
- Perfect for low-update-frequency data (auth, theme, locale, feature flags)
- Composes well with `useReducer` for simple cases

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- No fine-grained subscriptions — any context change re-renders all consumers
- No built-in middleware, devtools, or time-travel debugging
- Splitting contexts to avoid spurious re-renders increases boilerplate and complexity
- `value` objects must be memoized carefully or cause infinite re-render loops
- Not optimized for frequent updates (e.g., mouse position, form state, real-time feeds)
- No support for derived/computed state out of the box

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
import React, { createContext, useContext, useState, useMemo, useCallback } from "react";

// ─── PROBLEM DEMO ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Both `user` and `cart` live in one context object.
// Updating the cart causes UserGreeting to re-render even though it
// only cares about `user`. There are no selectors to prevent this.

interface AppState {
  user: { name: string };
  cart: { itemCount: number };
  updateCart: (count: number) => void;
}

const AppContext = createContext<AppState | null>(null);

export function AppProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [user] = useState({ name: "Alice" });
  const [cart, setCart] = useState({ itemCount: 0 });

  const updateCart = useCallback(
    (count: number) => setCart({ itemCount: count }),
    []
  );

  // ⚠️ Without useMemo, a new object is created every render,
  // causing ALL consumers to re-render even if nothing changed.
  const value = useMemo(
    () => ({ user, cart, updateCart }),
    [user, cart, updateCart]
  );

  return <AppContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AppContext.Provider>;
}

// This component re-renders on EVERY cart update — wasteful.
function UserGreeting() {
  const ctx = useContext(AppContext)!;
  console.log("UserGreeting rendered"); // fires on every cart change
  return <p>Hello, {ctx.user.name}</p>;
}

// ─── MITIGATION: SPLIT CONTEXTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Each context only holds one concern. Consumers only re-render
// when their specific context changes. More boilerplate, but faster.

const UserContext = createContext<{ name: string } | null>(null);
const CartContext = createContext<{ itemCount: number } | null>(null);

export function SplitProviders({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [user] = useState({ name: "Alice" });
  const [cart, setCart] = useState({ itemCount: 0 });
  // UserGreeting now won't re-render when cart updates ✅
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={user}>
      <CartContext.Provider value={cart}>
        {children}
      </CartContext.Provider>
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}

// ─── REAL SOLUTION FOR HIGH-FREQUENCY STATE: ZUSTAND ─────────────────────────
