## ⚡ Short Answer
Local state lives inside a single component and is invisible to others. Shared state is lifted to a common ancestor so sibling or child components can access it. Global state is application-wide data (auth, theme, cart) managed outside the component tree, typically via a dedicated store or context.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Three distinct scopes for where UI/application data lives and who can read or write it.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** As apps grow, you need a principled way to decide where data should live. Keeping state too high causes unnecessary re-renders and prop drilling; keeping it too low causes data-sharing headaches. Matching state scope to actual need keeps components decoupled and performant.

**How it works internally:** Local state (e.g., `useState`) is owned by one component instance and garbage-collected when it unmounts. Shared state is "lifted" to the nearest common ancestor, then passed down as props — React re-renders the subtree when it changes. Global state (Redux, Zustand, React Context at the root) lives outside any single component; subscribers re-render only when the slice they subscribe to changes.

**Benefits:** Local state is fast and self-contained. Shared state keeps a single source of truth for related sibling components without a full store. Global state avoids prop drilling and provides cross-cutting data anywhere in the tree.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Local state can't be shared without refactoring. Shared state via props causes prop drilling in deep trees. Global state introduces coupling, can trigger widespread re-renders if not carefully subscribed, and adds boilerplate.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// ─── 1. LOCAL STATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// Accordion open/close: only this component cares
function Accordion({ title, children }: { title: string; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = React.useState(false); // local — nobody else needs this

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setIsOpen(o => !o)}>{title}</button>
      {isOpen && <div>{children}</div>}
    </div>
  );
}

// ─── 2. SHARED STATE (lifted) ─────────────────────────────────────
// Both a temperature input and a display need the same value
function TemperatureConverter() {
  const [celsius, setCelsius] = React.useState(0); // lifted to common parent

  return (
    <div>
      <CelsiusInput value={celsius} onChange={setCelsius} />
      <FahrenheitDisplay celsius={celsius} />   {/* receives as prop */}
    </div>
  );
}

function CelsiusInput({ value, onChange }: { value: number; onChange: (v: number) => void }) {
  return <input type="number" value={value} onChange={e => onChange(Number(e.target.value))} />;
}

function FahrenheitDisplay({ celsius }: { celsius: number }) {
  return <p>{(celsius * 9) / 5 + 32} °F</p>;
}

// ─── 3. GLOBAL STATE (Zustand example) ───────────────────────────
// Auth user needed across Header, Dashboard, Settings — prop drilling would be painful
import { create } from "zustand";

interface AuthState {
  user: { name: string; role: string } | null;
  login: (user: AuthState["user"]) => void;
  logout: () => void;
}

const useAuthStore = create<AuthState>(set => ({
  user: null,
  login: user => set({ user }),
  logout: () => set({ user: null }),
}));

// Any component, anywhere in the tree, subscribes directly — no prop passing needed
function Header() {
  const user = useAuthStore(s => s.user);       // fine-grained subscription
  const logout = useAuthStore(s => s.logout);

  return user ? (
    <nav>
      <span>Hello, {user.name}</span>
      <button onClick={logout}>Logout</button>
    </nav>
  ) : null;
}

function Dashboard() {
  const user = useAuthStore(s => s.user);       // same store, independent subscription
  if (!user) return <p>Please log in.</p>;
  return <h1>Welcome back, {user.name} ({user.role})</h1>;
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: When should you lift state vs. reach for global state?** A: Lift when only a small, known subtree needs the data. Go global when the data is needed by components far apart in the tree, or when it persists across route changes (auth, theme, shopping cart).
- **Q: Does React Context count as global state?** A: It provides global *access*, but it's not a state manager — it re-renders all consumers on every change. Pair it
