## ⚡ Short Answer
Infinite scroll loads additional content as the user approaches the bottom of the page, avoiding pagination clicks. In React, you implement it by observing a sentinel element (or the scroll position) and fetching the next page of data when it becomes visible. The modern approach uses the `IntersectionObserver` API via a custom hook, while older approaches relied on `scroll` event listeners.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A UX pattern where content loads continuously as the user scrolls, replacing traditional paginated navigation.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Loading all data upfront is too expensive; pagination adds friction. Infinite scroll gives a seamless, low-latency browsing experience ideal for feeds, search results, and product listings.

**How it works internally:** A "sentinel" div is placed at the bottom of the list. An `IntersectionObserver` watches that sentinel — when it enters the viewport, a callback fires to fetch the next page and append results to state. The observer is disconnected once all pages are loaded.

**Benefits:** No page-reload friction, efficient data fetching (only fetches what's needed), works well with React Query / SWR for caching, and `IntersectionObserver` is non-blocking (runs off the main thread).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Hard to bookmark or share a specific position; back-navigation loses scroll position (unless you restore it manually); accessibility concerns (keyboard/screen reader users struggle); SEO requires server-side rendering of initial content; bad for content where users need to reach the footer.

## 💻 Code Example
```typescript
// useInfiniteScroll.ts — reusable custom hook
import { useEffect, useRef, useCallback } from "react";

interface UseInfiniteScrollOptions {
  onLoadMore: () => void;       // callback to fetch next page
  hasNextPage: boolean;         // stop observing when no more pages
  isLoading: boolean;           // prevent duplicate fetches
  rootMargin?: string;          // how early to trigger (e.g. "200px")
}

export function useInfiniteScroll({
  onLoadMore,
  hasNextPage,
  isLoading,
  rootMargin = "100px",
}: UseInfiniteScrollOptions) {
  // Attach this ref to the sentinel <div> at the bottom of your list
  const sentinelRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);

  const handleIntersection = useCallback(
    (entries: IntersectionObserverEntry[]) => {
      const [entry] = entries;
      // Only fire if visible, more pages exist, and not already loading
      if (entry.isIntersecting && hasNextPage && !isLoading) {
        onLoadMore();
      }
    },
    [onLoadMore, hasNextPage, isLoading]
  );

  useEffect(() => {
    const sentinel = sentinelRef.current;
    if (!sentinel) return;

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(handleIntersection, {
      root: null,         // observe relative to the viewport
      rootMargin,         // trigger slightly before the sentinel is visible
      threshold: 0,       // fire as soon as even 1px is visible
    });

    observer.observe(sentinel);

    // Cleanup: disconnect when deps change or component unmounts
    return () => observer.disconnect();
  }, [handleIntersection, rootMargin]);

  return sentinelRef;
}


