## ⚡ Short Answer
Protected routes in React Router are implemented by creating a wrapper component that checks authentication state before rendering the target route — if the user is authenticated, render the child route; otherwise, redirect to a login page. This pattern uses React Router's `<Navigate>` component (v6) or `<Redirect>` (v5) combined with your auth state from context, Redux, or a custom hook. It's the standard way to guard pages like dashboards, settings, or any resource requiring a logged-in user.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A protected route is a higher-order route component that conditionally renders either the requested page or a redirect to login, based on whether the user holds a valid authentication token or session.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without route guards, any user can navigate directly to `/dashboard` or `/admin` by typing the URL, bypassing login entirely. Protected routes enforce authentication at the routing layer rather than inside every individual page component.

**How it works internally:** React Router v6 renders components as part of the component tree. A `ProtectedRoute` component reads auth state (from context or a store), and either returns `<Outlet />` (to render child routes) or `<Navigate to="/login" replace />` to redirect. The `replace` flag prevents the login page from being pushed onto the browser history stack, so the back button behaves correctly.

**Benefits:** Centralized auth logic in one place, easy to extend with role-based access, clean separation of concerns, works with nested routes via `<Outlet>`.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Auth state must be synchronously available at render time — if you're async-fetching a token (e.g., validating a JWT with the server), you need a loading state to avoid a flash-redirect before auth is confirmed. Also, route-level protection is UI-only — your API endpoints must independently validate tokens; never rely solely on frontend guards for security.

## 💻 Code Example
