## ⚡ Short Answer
Drag-and-drop in React can be implemented using the native HTML5 Drag and Drop API with React's synthetic event handlers (`onDragStart`, `onDragOver`, `onDrop`, etc.), or via purpose-built libraries like `react-beautiful-dnd`, `dnd-kit`, or `react-dnd`. For production apps, libraries are strongly preferred because they handle accessibility, touch support, keyboard navigation, and complex reordering logic that the native API leaves entirely to you.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A UI interaction pattern that lets users pick up an element, move it across the screen, and drop it onto a target — triggering a state update in React.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Users expect tactile, direct manipulation in modern UIs (kanban boards, file uploaders, sortable lists, dashboard widgets). Without drag-and-drop, these interactions require cumbersome click-to-select + click-to-place flows.

**How it works internally:** The HTML5 DnD API fires a sequence of events: `dragstart` → `dragenter/dragover` (on target) → `drop` → `dragend`. Data is passed between source and target via the `DataTransfer` object. React wraps these as synthetic events. Libraries like `dnd-kit` go further by using pointer events and a virtual coordinate system, which works on touch screens and avoids the many quirks of the native API (e.g., no way to customize the drag image cross-browser without hacks).

**Benefits:** Native API has zero bundle cost. Libraries give you accessibility (ARIA live regions, keyboard reorder), animation hooks, collision detection strategies, and touch/mobile support out of the box.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Native API is verbose, buggy across browsers, inaccessible by default, and doesn't work on mobile. Libraries add bundle weight (`dnd-kit` ~10 KB, `react-beautiful-dnd` ~30 KB) and have learning curves. `react-beautiful-dnd` is now in maintenance mode — prefer `dnd-kit` for new projects.

