## ⚡ Short Answer
Accessibility in React is achieved by using semantic HTML elements, ARIA attributes, keyboard navigation support, and focus management. React's JSX maps directly to HTML attributes like `aria-label`, `role`, and `tabIndex`, and libraries like `@radix-ui` or `react-aria` provide accessible primitives out of the box. The goal is to ensure screen readers, keyboard users, and assistive technologies can fully interact with your UI.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Accessibility (a11y) is the practice of designing and building UIs that work for all users, including those using screen readers, keyboard-only navigation, voice control, or other assistive technologies.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** By default, custom React components are just `<div>` soup — they carry no semantic meaning for assistive technologies. Without explicit a11y work, users relying on screen readers or keyboards are often completely locked out of interactive UI elements like modals, dropdowns, tabs, and tooltips.

**How it works internally:** Browsers expose an Accessibility Tree alongside the DOM. Screen readers and tools like VoiceOver or NVDA read from this tree, not the visual render. Semantic elements (`<button>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`) populate this tree automatically. For custom components, ARIA roles and attributes augment the tree. React's `useRef` and `useEffect` enable programmatic focus management (e.g., moving focus into a modal on open).

**Benefits:** Reaches more users, improves SEO, satisfies legal compliance (WCAG 2.1, ADA, Section 508), and often improves overall UX through better keyboard ergonomics.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** ARIA misuse is worse than no ARIA — incorrect roles can actively confuse screen readers. It adds complexity to interactive components. Automated tools (axe, Lighthouse) only catch ~30–40% of real issues; manual testing with actual screen readers is required.

## 💻 Code Example
```tsx
import React, { useRef, useEffect, useId } from "react";

// ─── 1. Accessible Modal Dialog ───────────────────────────────────────────────
interface ModalProps {
  isOpen: boolean;
  onClose: () => void;
  title: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

export function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, title, children }: ModalProps) {
  const dialogRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const titleId = useId(); // stable, unique ID for aria-labelledby

  // Focus trap: move focus into modal when it opens
  useEffect(() => {
    if (isOpen) {
      dialogRef.current?.focus();
    }
  }, [isOpen]);

  // Close on Escape key
  const handleKeyDown = (e: React.KeyboardEvent) => {
    if (e.key === "Escape") onClose();
  };

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  return (
    // role="dialog" + aria-modal tells screen readers this is a dialog
    // aria-labelledby links the dialog to its visible title
    <div
      role="dialog"
      aria-modal="true"
      aria-labelledby={titleId}
      ref={dialogRef}
      tabIndex={-1} // makes the div programmatically focusable
      onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
      style={{ border: "2px solid #333", padding: 24, background: "#fff" }}
    >
      <h2 id={titleId}>{title}</h2>
      <div>{children}</div>
      {/* Use a real <button> — never onClick on a <div> */}
      <button onClick={onClose} aria-label="Close dialog">
        Close
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

