## ⚡ Short Answer
A SQL constraint is a rule enforced at the database level that restricts what data can be stored in a table. Constraints guarantee data integrity by rejecting any INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE that would violate the defined rules. They act as the last line of defense — enforcing correctness even when application-layer validation is bypassed.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A constraint is a declarative rule attached to a column or table in a relational database that defines valid data conditions. Common types include PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK, and DEFAULT.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Application code can have bugs, be bypassed via direct DB access, or be written by multiple teams inconsistently. Constraints enforce correctness at the storage layer, ensuring the database itself is the single source of truth for data validity — not any one application.

**How it works internally:** The database engine evaluates constraints as part of every write operation within the same transaction. If a constraint is violated, the engine raises an error and rolls back the offending statement (or the entire transaction, depending on the DB). For FOREIGN KEYs, the engine maintains referential integrity by checking the referenced table. For UNIQUE and PRIMARY KEY, the engine typically backs the constraint with a unique B-tree index for fast enforcement.

**Benefits:** Guaranteed data integrity regardless of how data enters the DB; self-documenting schema; enables query optimizer hints (e.g., PKs help with join planning); prevents orphaned records via FK constraints.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Constraints add overhead to write operations (especially FKs on high-throughput tables). Some teams disable FK constraints in microservice architectures or data warehouses for performance, pushing integrity responsibility to the application. Bulk data loads often temporarily disable constraints and re-enable them after.

## 💻 Code Example
```sql
-- =====================================================
-- Demonstrating all major SQL constraint types
-- =====================================================

CREATE TABLE departments (
    department_id   INT           PRIMARY KEY,          -- PK: unique + not null
    department_name VARCHAR(100)  NOT NULL UNIQUE        -- NOT NULL + UNIQUE
);

CREATE TABLE employees (
    employee_id     INT           PRIMARY KEY,
    full_name       VARCHAR(150)  NOT NULL,
    email           VARCHAR(255)  NOT NULL UNIQUE,       -- UNIQUE: no duplicate emails
    salary          DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL
                                  CHECK (salary > 0),   -- CHECK: salary must be positive
    department_id   INT           NOT NULL
                                  REFERENCES departments(department_id)  -- FK constraint
                                  ON DELETE RESTRICT
                                  ON UPDATE CASCADE,
    status          VARCHAR(20)   NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'  -- DEFAULT value
);

-- =====================================================
-- Constraint violation examples
-- =====================================================

-- ❌ Violates NOT NULL
INSERT INTO employees (employee_id, full_name, email, salary, department_id)
VALUES (1, NULL, 'a@b.com', 50000, 1);
-- ERROR: null value in column "full_name" violates not-null constraint

-- ❌ Violates CHECK
INSERT INTO employees (employee_id, full_name, email, salary, department_id)
VALUES (1, 'Alice', 'alice@co.com', -500, 1);
-- ERROR: new row violates check constraint "employees_salary_check"

-- ❌ Violates FOREIGN KEY (department 99 doesn't exist)
INSERT INTO employees (employee_id, full_name, email, salary, department_id)
VALUES (1, 'Alice', 'alice@co.com', 50000, 99);
-- ERROR: insert or update on table "employees" violates foreign key constraint

-- ✅ Valid insert
INSERT INTO departments VALUES (1, 'Engineering');
INSERT INTO employees (employee_id, full_name, email, salary, department_id)
VALUES (1, 'Alice', 'alice@co.com', 75000, 1);

-- =====================================================
-- Adding a constraint to an existing table
-- =====================================================
ALTER TABLE employees
ADD CONSTRAINT chk_status CHECK (status IN ('active', 'inactive', 'terminated'));

-- =====================================================
-- Named constraints (best practice — easier to debug)
-- =====================================================
ALTER TABLE employees
ADD CONSTRAINT uq_employee_email UNIQUE (email);
```

