## ⚡ Short Answer
A CROSS JOIN produces a Cartesian product — every row from the left table paired with every row from the right table, with no join condition. A FULL OUTER JOIN returns all rows from both tables, matching where possible and filling NULLs where no match exists — it requires a join condition (ON clause).

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** CROSS JOIN is a Cartesian product (m × n rows). FULL OUTER JOIN is a set union of LEFT and RIGHT OUTER JOINs — all matched rows plus all unmatched rows from both sides padded with NULLs.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** CROSS JOIN is used when you deliberately want every combination (e.g., generating a schedule grid, size × color combinations). FULL OUTER JOIN is used when you need to find matches AND non-matches from both tables simultaneously — a common reporting and data reconciliation need.

**How it works internally:** CROSS JOIN uses a nested-loop with no filter; every row on the outer loop is combined with every row on the inner loop. FULL OUTER JOIN is typically implemented as a HASH JOIN or MERGE JOIN with an additional pass to emit the non-matching rows from both sides (LEFT anti-join rows ∪ RIGHT anti-join rows ∪ matched rows).

**Benefits:** CROSS JOIN is simple and predictable for combinatoric data generation. FULL OUTER JOIN is powerful for reconciliation queries, finding orphaned records on both sides, and outer-merge reporting.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** CROSS JOIN explodes row counts exponentially — 1,000 × 1,000 = 1,000,000 rows. It is almost never appropriate on large tables without a subsequent WHERE filter. FULL OUTER JOIN can be expensive on large tables and is not supported in MySQL (must be emulated with UNION of LEFT and RIGHT joins).

## 💻 Code Example
```sql
-- Setup
CREATE TABLE products  (id INT, name VARCHAR(50));
CREATE TABLE colors    (id INT, color VARCHAR(50));
CREATE TABLE orders    (id INT, product_id INT, color_id INT, qty INT);

INSERT INTO products VALUES (1, 'Shirt'), (2, 'Pants'), (3, 'Hat');
INSERT INTO colors   VALUES (1, 'Red'),  (2, 'Blue'),  (3, 'Green');

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────
-- CROSS JOIN: Every product × every color combo
-- Use case: generate a "catalog grid" of all possibilities
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────
SELECT
    p.name  AS product,
    c.color AS color
FROM products p
CROSS JOIN colors c;
-- Result: 3 × 3 = 9 rows (Shirt/Red, Shirt/Blue, ..., Hat/Green)

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────
-- FULL OUTER JOIN: Compare two tables, find gaps on BOTH sides
-- Use case: data reconciliation — find products with no orders
--           AND orders referencing non-existent products
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1, 1, 1, 5),   -- Shirt/Red ordered
                          (2, 99, 2, 3);  -- references product_id=99 (doesn't exist!)

SELECT
    p.name        AS product_name,
    o.id          AS order_id,
    o.qty         AS quantity,
    CASE
        WHEN p.id IS NULL THEN '⚠️ Orphaned order — no product'
        WHEN o.id IS NULL THEN '⚠️ Product never ordered'
        ELSE                   '✅ Matched'
    END AS status
FROM products p
FULL OUTER JOIN orders o ON p.id = o.product_id;

-- Result:
-- product_name | order_id | quantity | status
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Shirt        | 1        | 5        | ✅ Matched
-- Pants        | NULL     | NULL     | ⚠️ Product never ordered
-- Hat          | NULL     | NULL     | ⚠️ Product never ordered
-- NULL         | 2        | 3        | ⚠️ Orphaned order — no product

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────
-- MySQL workaround: FULL OUTER JOIN via UNION
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────
SELECT p.name, o.id
FROM products p LEFT JOIN orders o ON p.id = o.product_id
UNION
SELECT p.name, o.id
FROM products p RIGHT JOIN orders o ON p.id = o.product_id;
```

