## ⚡ Short Answer
A SQL cursor is a database object that allows row-by-row processing of a result set, essentially giving you a pointer you can advance through query results one record at a time. While powerful, cursors are generally a last resort in SQL because set-based operations are almost always faster and more idiomatic. Use them only when row-by-row logic is genuinely unavoidable.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A cursor is a named database object that encapsulates a SELECT query and provides a mechanism to fetch rows one at a time (or in small batches) into local variables for procedural processing.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** SQL is inherently set-based — most operations work on entire result sets at once. Cursors exist for the rare cases where you need to execute different logic per row, call stored procedures per row, or implement iterative algorithms that can't be expressed as a single set-based statement.

**How it works internally:** The database engine executes the SELECT and materializes the result set (either in a temp structure or keyset). A pointer tracks the current position. Each FETCH moves the pointer and loads column values into declared variables. The cursor lifecycle is: DECLARE → OPEN → FETCH LOOP → CLOSE → DEALLOCATE. The engine holds locks and resources open for the cursor's lifetime, which is why long-running cursors are dangerous under concurrency.

**Benefits:** Enables row-level procedural logic inside T-SQL/PL-pgSQL/PL-SQL; can call external stored procedures per row; useful for administrative scripts (e.g., iterating databases to run maintenance); sometimes clearer for complex multi-step per-row operations.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Dramatically slower than set-based operations due to per-row round-trips and context-switching; holds locks longer, reducing concurrency; consumes server memory; easy to forget CLOSE/DEALLOCATE causing resource leaks; almost always replaceable by JOINs, window functions, CTEs, or APPLY/LATERAL.

## 💻 Code Example
```sql
-- ===========================================================
-- EXAMPLE: T-SQL Cursor (SQL Server)
-- Scenario: Apply a custom per-department salary adjustment
-- that varies by business rule per row (simplified demo).
-- In production, prefer set-based UPDATE with CASE WHEN.
-- ===========================================================

-- Setup
CREATE TABLE #Employees (
    EmployeeID   INT,
    DepartmentID INT,
    Salary       DECIMAL(10,2)
);

INSERT INTO #Employees VALUES (1, 10, 50000), (2, 20, 60000), (3, 10, 55000);

-- Declare variables to hold each fetched row
DECLARE @EmpID       INT;
DECLARE @DeptID      INT;
DECLARE @Salary      DECIMAL(10,2);
DECLARE @Adjustment  DECIMAL(10,2);

-- 1. DECLARE the cursor with its driving SELECT
DECLARE emp_cursor CURSOR
    LOCAL          -- scoped to this batch (best practice)
    FAST_FORWARD   -- forward-only, read-only: fastest option
FOR
    SELECT EmployeeID, DepartmentID, Salary
    FROM   #Employees
    ORDER BY EmployeeID;

-- 2. OPEN executes the query and positions before first row
OPEN emp_cursor;

-- 3. Initial FETCH
FETCH NEXT FROM emp_cursor INTO @EmpID, @DeptID, @Salary;

-- @@FETCH_STATUS = 0 means a row was successfully fetched
WHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0
BEGIN
    -- Per-row business logic (hard to express purely in SET)
    SET @Adjustment = CASE
        WHEN @DeptID = 10 THEN @Salary * 0.05   -- 5% raise
        WHEN @DeptID = 20 THEN @Salary * 0.08   -- 8% raise
        ELSE                   @Salary * 0.03
    END;

    UPDATE #Employees
    SET    Salary = Salary + @Adjustment
    WHERE  EmployeeID = @EmpID;

    PRINT CONCAT('Updated EmpID ', @EmpID, ' | Adj: ', @Adjustment);

    -- 3b. Advance to next row
    FETCH NEXT FROM emp_cursor INTO @EmpID, @DeptID, @Salary;
END;

-- 4. CLOSE releases the result set but keeps the cursor object
CLOSE emp_cursor;

-- 5. DEALLOCATE releases the cursor object and all memory
DEALLOCATE emp_cursor;

SELECT * FROM #Employees;
DROP TABLE #Employees;

