## ⚡ Short Answer
A deadlock occurs when two or more transactions are each waiting for the other to release a lock, creating a circular dependency that never resolves on its own. Both SQL Server and PostgreSQL automatically detect deadlocks and kill the transaction with the lowest cost (the "deadlock victim"), rolling it back. Prevention strategies include consistent lock ordering, keeping transactions short, and using appropriate isolation levels.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A deadlock is a cycle of dependencies where Transaction A holds a lock on Resource 1 and waits for Resource 2, while Transaction B holds Resource 2 and waits for Resource 1. Neither can proceed.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Deadlocks are a fundamental concurrency problem in any multi-user database system. Understanding them is critical because they cause transaction rollbacks, degrade performance, and indicate deeper design issues in your locking strategy.

**How it works internally:** Both databases run a background deadlock detection process. SQL Server's lock monitor checks every 5 seconds (or sooner under load) and builds a wait-for graph. PostgreSQL uses a similar wait-for graph. When a cycle is detected, one transaction is chosen as the victim based on cost (work done, rollback cost) and is terminated with an error. The surviving transaction then completes normally.

**Benefits:** Automatic detection prevents the system from hanging indefinitely. Applications can catch the specific deadlock error code and retry the transaction.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Deadlock detection adds overhead. Frequent deadlocks indicate poor schema design, bad query patterns, or wrong isolation levels. Simply retrying without fixing the root cause leads to thrashing. Row-level locking reduces deadlocks but increases lock management overhead.

## 💻 Code Example
```sql
-- ============================================================
-- DEADLOCK SCENARIO SETUP
-- ============================================================
CREATE TABLE accounts (
    id      INT PRIMARY KEY,
    balance DECIMAL(10, 2)
);

INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (1, 1000.00), (2, 2000.00);

-- ============================================================
-- DEADLOCK SCENARIO (run in two concurrent sessions)
-- ============================================================

-- SESSION A: Locks account 1, then tries to lock account 2
BEGIN;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1; -- acquires lock on row 1
    -- (pause here while Session B runs its first UPDATE)
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2; -- WAITS for lock on row 2
COMMIT;

-- SESSION B: Locks account 2, then tries to lock account 1 → DEADLOCK
BEGIN;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 2; -- acquires lock on row 2
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 1; -- WAITS for lock on row 1 → DEADLOCK!
COMMIT;

-- ============================================================
-- FIX #1: Consistent Lock Ordering (always lock lower id first)
-- ============================================================

-- Session A and Session B both now lock in the SAME order: id=1 first, then id=2
-- This breaks the circular wait

BEGIN;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1; -- lock row 1 first
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2; -- then lock row 2
COMMIT;

-- ============================================================
-- FIX #2: Use SELECT ... FOR UPDATE to acquire all locks upfront
-- ============================================================

BEGIN;
    -- Acquire both locks at the start, in a consistent order
    SELECT id, balance
    FROM accounts
    WHERE id IN (1, 2)
    ORDER BY id                  -- ORDER BY enforces consistent ordering
    FOR UPDATE;                  -- PostgreSQL; use WITH (UPDLOCK) in SQL Server

    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;

-- ============================================================
-- FIX #3: Keep transactions as short as possible
-- ============================================================

-- BAD: Long transaction holds locks while doing unrelated work
BEGIN;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
    -- ... 10 seconds of application logic, API calls, etc. ...
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;

-- GOOD: Do all computation BEFORE the transaction, minimize lock time
-- (Compute amounts in application code first, then open a short transaction)
BEGIN;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
    UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;

-- ============================================================
-- SQL SERVER: View deadlock info via system health session
-- ============================================================

-- SQL Server captures deadlock graphs in the system_health XEvent session
SELECT
    xdr.value('@timestamp', 'datetime2')           AS deadlock_time,
    xdr.query('.')                                 AS deadlock_graph
FROM (
    SELECT CAST
