## ⚡ Short Answer
A slow query is typically caused by missing indexes, full table scans, inefficient joins, or returning more data than needed. The systematic approach is: measure with EXPLAIN/execution plans, identify the bottleneck, apply targeted fixes (indexes, query rewrites, schema changes), then verify improvement with benchmarks.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Query performance optimization is the process of reducing a SQL query's execution time and resource consumption (CPU, I/O, memory) to acceptable levels.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** As data grows, queries that were fast at 10,000 rows become painfully slow at 10 million rows. Even a single slow query can cascade into API timeouts, lock contention, and full system degradation.

**How it works internally:** The database query optimizer generates an execution plan — a tree of operations (scans, seeks, joins, sorts, aggregations). Performance problems arise when the optimizer chooses a bad plan, usually due to missing statistics, missing indexes, or poorly written SQL that prevents index usage. Tools like `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` (PostgreSQL), `EXPLAIN` (MySQL), or execution plan viewers (SQL Server SSMS) reveal exactly which operations are expensive.

**Benefits:** Optimized queries reduce latency, lower DB server load, reduce lock contention, and allow the system to scale further without hardware upgrades.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adding indexes speeds reads but slows writes and increases storage. Over-indexing hurts INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE throughput. Query rewrites can reduce maintainability. Denormalization trades data integrity risk for speed.

## 💻 Code Example
```sql
-- ============================================================
-- SCENARIO: Orders table with 10M rows, running slow
-- ============================================================

-- STEP 1: See the execution plan BEFORE optimization
EXPLAIN ANALYZE
SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS order_count, SUM(o.total) AS revenue
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id
WHERE o.status = 'completed'
  AND o.created_at >= '2024-01-01'
GROUP BY c.name
ORDER BY revenue DESC;
-- ❌ Result: "Seq Scan on orders (cost=0.00..180432.00 rows=2100000)"
-- Full table scan! No index on status or created_at.

-- ============================================================
-- FIX 1: Add a composite index on the WHERE clause columns
-- Rule: (equality columns first, then range columns)
-- ============================================================
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status_created ON orders (status, created_at);

-- If you also filter/join on customer_id frequently:
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_customer_status ON orders (customer_id, status, created_at);

-- ============================================================
-- FIX 2: Avoid SELECT * — only fetch what you need
-- ============================================================
-- ❌ Bad: fetches all columns, wastes I/O
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'completed';

-- ✅ Good: fetch only needed columns
SELECT id, customer_id, total FROM orders WHERE status = 'completed';

-- ============================================================
-- FIX 3: Avoid functions on indexed columns in WHERE clause
-- Functions prevent index usage!
-- ============================================================
-- ❌ Bad: index on created_at is ignored
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE YEAR(created_at) = 2024;

-- ✅ Good: range comparison uses the index
SELECT * FROM orders
WHERE created_at >= '2024-01-01' AND created_at < '2025-01-01';

-- ============================================================
-- FIX 4: Use a covering index (include all SELECTed columns)
-- The query is answered entirely from the index — no table lookup
-- ============================================================
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_covering
    ON orders (status, created_at)
    INCLUDE (total, customer_id);  -- PostgreSQL / SQL Server syntax

-- ============================================================
-- FIX 5: Rewrite correlated subquery as a JOIN
-- ============================================================
-- ❌ Bad: correlated subquery runs once per row in customers
SELECT name,
  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id) AS cnt
FROM customers c;

-- ✅ Good: single pass with JOIN + GROUP BY
SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS cnt
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id
GROUP BY c.id, c.name;

-- ============================================================
-- FIX 6: Paginate large result sets — avoid OFFSET at scale
-- ============================================================
-- ❌ Bad at scale: OFFSET scans and discards rows
SELECT * FROM orders ORDER BY id LIMIT 20 OFFSET 100000;

-- ✅ Good: keyset/cursor pagination — O(log n) with index
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id > 100000 ORDER BY id LIMIT 20;

-- ============================================================
-- FIX 7: Use CTEs or temp tables
