## ⚡ Short Answer
Database transaction isolation levels define how and when changes made by one transaction become visible to other concurrent transactions. They represent a trade-off between data consistency and system performance/concurrency. The four standard ANSI SQL levels are Read Uncommitted, Read Committed, Repeatable Read, and Serializable.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Isolation levels are settings that control the degree to which one transaction must be isolated from resource or data modifications made by other transactions running concurrently.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without isolation controls, concurrent transactions cause anomalies: dirty reads (reading uncommitted data), non-repeatable reads (same query returns different rows within one transaction), and phantom reads (new rows appear mid-transaction). Isolation levels let you tune exactly which anomalies you tolerate in exchange for throughput.

**How it works internally:** Databases implement isolation through locking (shared/exclusive locks held for varying durations) or MVCC — Multi-Version Concurrency Control (Postgres, MySQL InnoDB, Oracle) — where each transaction sees a consistent snapshot of the data at a point in time, avoiding read locks entirely.

**Benefits:** Fine-grained control over consistency vs. concurrency. Higher isolation = stronger guarantees; lower isolation = more throughput and fewer lock waits.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Higher isolation levels increase lock contention, reduce concurrency, and can cause deadlocks. Serializable in particular can drastically reduce throughput under high write loads.

## 💻 Code Example
```sql
-- ============================================================
-- ISOLATION LEVEL DEMONSTRATION (SQL Server / PostgreSQL syntax)
-- ============================================================

-- 1. READ UNCOMMITTED — dirty reads allowed
--    Use case: rough analytics where approximate counts are fine
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ UNCOMMITTED;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    -- Can read rows that another transaction has modified but NOT yet committed
    -- Risk: you might read data that gets rolled back (phantom data)
    SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE status = 'pending';
COMMIT;

-- 2. READ COMMITTED — default in SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Oracle
--    Dirty reads prevented; non-repeatable reads still possible
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 42;  -- returns 1000
    -- Another transaction commits a change HERE
    SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 42;  -- might return 900 (non-repeatable read)
COMMIT;

-- 3. REPEATABLE READ — default in MySQL InnoDB
--    Non-repeatable reads prevented; phantom reads still possible
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 42;  -- returns 1000
    -- Another transaction commits a change HERE
    SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 42;  -- still returns 1000 (locked row)
    -- But a brand-new row inserted by another transaction CAN appear (phantom)
COMMIT;

-- 4. SERIALIZABLE — strictest level
--    All anomalies prevented; transactions appear to run one at a time
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    -- Range lock acquired — no inserts/updates in this range by other transactions
    SELECT SUM(amount) FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 7;
    -- Safe to make decisions based on this — no phantoms possible
    INSERT INTO invoices (customer_id, total) VALUES (7, 450.00);
COMMIT;

-- ============================================================
-- ANOMALY REFERENCE TABLE (comment)
-- ============================================================
-- Level              | Dirty Read | Non-Repeatable | Phantom
-- -------------------|------------|----------------|--------
-- READ UNCOMMITTED   |    YES     |      YES       |  YES
-- READ COMMITTED     |    NO      |      YES       |  YES
-- REPEATABLE READ    |    NO      |      NO        |  YES (NO in MySQL MVCC)
-- SERIALIZABLE       |    NO      |      NO        |  NO

-- ============================================================
-- PRACTICAL: setting per-session in PostgreSQL
-- ============================================================
BEGIN;
SET LOCAL TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
-- ... your queries ...
COMMIT;

-- In application code (C# / EF Core example as comment):
-- using var tx = await context.Database.BeginTransactionAsync(
--     IsolationLevel.RepeatableRead);
```

