## ⚡ Short Answer
Multi-tenant SaaS schemas follow three main strategies: shared database with a tenant discriminator column (most cost-efficient), shared database with separate schemas per tenant (balance of isolation and cost), or separate databases per tenant (highest isolation, highest cost). The right choice depends on your compliance requirements, tenant count, data sensitivity, and budget. Most modern SaaS applications start with the shared-table approach and migrate toward schema or database isolation as enterprise customers demand it.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A multi-tenant schema design allows a single application instance to serve multiple customers (tenants) while keeping their data logically or physically isolated from one another.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Running a separate application stack per customer is prohibitively expensive. Multi-tenancy lets you amortize infrastructure costs across all customers while still appearing to each customer as if they have their own dedicated system.

**How it works internally:** The three canonical models each partition data differently. In the shared-table model, every tenant's rows live in the same tables, distinguished by a `tenant_id` foreign key — Row-Level Security (RLS) in PostgreSQL or filtered indexes in SQL Server enforce isolation at the DB level. In the schema-per-tenant model, each tenant gets their own schema namespace (e.g., `tenant_abc.orders`) inside one database, allowing per-tenant DDL changes without affecting others. In the database-per-tenant model, each tenant is fully isolated in their own database, enabling independent backups, restores, and compliance boundaries.

**Benefits:** Shared tables offer the lowest operational overhead and easiest analytics across tenants. Schema-per-tenant allows customization per tenant and strong namespace isolation. Database-per-tenant gives the strongest compliance posture (GDPR, HIPAA) and the simplest data deletion (drop the database).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Shared tables risk data leaks if `tenant_id` filtering is missed — a single bug exposes all tenants. Schema-per-tenant makes cross-tenant migrations slow and complex (N schemas to migrate). Database-per-tenant is expensive to operate at scale (thousands of connection pools, patching N databases, etc.).

## 💻 Code Example
```sql
-- ============================================================
-- STRATEGY 1: Shared Tables with Row-Level Security (PostgreSQL)
-- ============================================================

-- Core tenant registry
CREATE TABLE tenants (
    tenant_id   UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    name        TEXT NOT NULL,
    plan        TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'starter', -- starter | pro | enterprise
    created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

-- Every shared table carries tenant_id
CREATE TABLE users (
    user_id     UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    tenant_id   UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES tenants(tenant_id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    email       TEXT NOT NULL,
    role        TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'member',
    created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    UNIQUE (tenant_id, email)   -- email unique per tenant, not globally
);

CREATE TABLE projects (
    project_id  UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    tenant_id   UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES tenants(tenant_id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    name        TEXT NOT NULL,
    owner_id    UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(user_id),
    created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

-- Performance: always index tenant_id on every table
CREATE INDEX idx_users_tenant    ON users(tenant_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_projects_tenant ON projects(tenant_id);

-- ============================================================
-- Row-Level Security (RLS) — prevents cross-tenant data leaks
-- at the database layer, not just the application layer
-- ============================================================

ALTER TABLE users    ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE projects ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- Application sets this at the start of every DB session/transaction
-- e.g., SET app.current_tenant_id = 'uuid-of-logged-in-tenant';

CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_users ON users
    USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant_id')::UUID);

CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_projects ON projects
    USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant_id')::UUID);

-- ============================================================
-- STRATEGY 2: Schema-per-Tenant (provisioning script pattern)
-- ============================================================

-- Run this when a new tenant signs up
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE provision_tenant(p_tenant_slug TEXT)
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
BEGIN
    -- Create isolated namespace
    EXECUTE format('CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS %I', p_tenant_slug);

    -- Each tenant gets their own copy of the tables
    EXECUTE format('
        CREATE TABLE %I.users (
            user_id    UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
            email      TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
            role       TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT ''member'',
            created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
        )', p_tenant
