## ⚡ Short Answer
Scalability is a system's ability to handle increased load by adding resources without degrading performance or requiring a redesign. Vertical scaling (scale up) means adding more power to a single machine, while horizontal scaling (scale out) means adding more machines to distribute the load. Most modern distributed systems favor horizontal scaling for fault tolerance and near-unlimited growth potential.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Scalability is the capacity of a system to grow and manage increased demand — more users, more data, more requests — while maintaining acceptable performance and reliability.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** A system built for 1,000 users will collapse under 1,000,000 users if not designed to scale. Scalability ensures the architecture can evolve with business growth without full rewrites or extended downtime.

**How it works internally:**
Vertical scaling works by upgrading CPU cores, RAM, or storage on an existing server — the OS and application see more resources with zero code changes. It has a hard ceiling set by available hardware.
Horizontal scaling works by running multiple instances of an application behind a load balancer. Each node handles a slice of the traffic. It requires the application to be stateless (or to externalize state to a shared store like Redis or a database) so any node can serve any request.

**Benefits:**
Vertical — simple to implement, no code changes, no distributed systems complexity.
Horizontal — theoretically unlimited growth, better fault tolerance, cost-effective with commodity hardware, enables blue/green deployments and rolling updates.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
Vertical — single point of failure, expensive at high tiers, OS/hardware limits cap growth, requires downtime to resize.
Horizontal — requires stateless design, introduces network complexity, needs a load balancer, distributed state management (sessions, caches) adds engineering overhead.

