## ⚡ Short Answer
The CAP theorem states that a distributed system can only guarantee two of three properties simultaneously: Consistency, Availability, and Partition Tolerance. Since network partitions are unavoidable in real distributed systems, the practical choice is always between CP (consistency over availability) or AP (availability over consistency) when a partition occurs.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A fundamental theorem in distributed systems, proven by Eric Brewer (2000) and formally by Gilbert & Lynch (2002), stating that no distributed data store can simultaneously provide all three of: Consistency (every read receives the most recent write), Availability (every request receives a non-error response), and Partition Tolerance (the system continues operating despite network message loss/delay between nodes).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Distributed systems face an unavoidable reality — networks fail, drop packets, and partition. The theorem forces architects to make explicit trade-offs rather than assuming you can have it all, preventing false assumptions in system design.

**How it works internally:** During a network partition, nodes become isolated. A CP system will refuse to answer (or return an error) rather than risk returning stale data — it sacrifices availability. An AP system will continue answering from its local state, potentially returning stale or conflicting data — it sacrifices consistency. There is no option that maintains both perfect consistency AND availability when nodes cannot communicate.

**Benefits:** Forces explicit architectural decisions; helps teams understand why Cassandra, DynamoDB, and CouchDB behave differently from MongoDB or HBase under failure; drives design of conflict resolution strategies (e.g., vector clocks, CRDTs).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** The theorem is often oversimplified — it only applies during a partition event, not normal operation. The PACELC theorem extends CAP to also capture latency vs. consistency trade-offs during normal operation, which is often more relevant in practice.

## 💻 Code Example
```plaintext
==============================================================
 CAP THEOREM — VISUAL OVERVIEW
==============================================================

          CONSISTENCY
          (All nodes see same data)
               /\
              /  \
          CP /    \ CA
            /      \
           /________\
  AVAILABILITY      PARTITION
  (Always responds)  TOLERANCE
                    (Survives splits)

---------------------------------------------------------------
 REAL-WORLD DATABASE PLACEMENT:
---------------------------------------------------------------

 CP  (Consistency + Partition Tolerance)
 → Sacrifice: Availability during partition
 → Systems: HBase, Zookeeper, MongoDB (default), Redis Cluster
 → Behavior: Returns error/timeout rather than stale data

 AP  (Availability + Partition Tolerance)
 → Sacrifice: Consistency (eventual consistency)
 → Systems: Cassandra, DynamoDB, CouchDB, Riak
 → Behavior: Returns possibly stale data, resolves conflicts later

 CA  (Consistency + Availability)
 → Sacrifice: Partition Tolerance
 → Systems: Traditional RDBMS on single node (PostgreSQL, MySQL)
 → Reality: NOT viable for true distributed systems
             (partitions WILL happen)

---------------------------------------------------------------
 PARTITION SCENARIO — CP vs AP:
---------------------------------------------------------------

  [Client] --write--> [Node A]  ~~~X~~~  [Node B]
                      (partition: A and B cannot communicate)

  CP System (e.g. HBase):
    Client reads from Node B
    Node B: "I cannot confirm latest data — returning ERROR"
    ✅ No stale data served
    ❌ Request fails

  AP System (e.g. Cassandra):
    Client reads from Node B
    Node B: "Returning my local value (may be stale)"
    ✅ Request succeeds
    ❌ May return outdated value

  After partition heals:
    AP systems run reconciliation/conflict resolution
    (Last-write-wins, vector clocks, CRDTs, etc.)

---------------------------------------------------------------
 PACELC EXTENSION (more practical):
---------------------------------------------------------------

  If Partition  → choose: Availability (A) or Consistency (C)
  Else (normal) → choose: Latency (L) or Consistency (C)

  DynamoDB    = PA / EL  (available during partition, low latency)
  Cassandra   = PA / EL
  MongoDB     = PC / EC  (consistent during partition, consistent)
  MySQL       = PC / EC

==============================================================
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: Is CA actually a valid option in distributed systems?** A: Not really. Network partitions are inevitable in any multi-node system, so you cannot opt out of partition tolerance. CA databases (like a single-node RDBMS) simply aren't distributed — they avoid partitions by not distributing at all.
- **Q: What is eventual consistency and how does it relate to CAP?** A: Eventual consistency is the model AP systems use — all nodes will converge to the same value *eventually* once the partition heals and replication catches up. It's the trade-off accepted when choosing availability over strong consistency.
- **Q: How
