## ⚡ Short Answer
Strong consistency guarantees that every read sees the most recent write, meaning all nodes in a distributed system agree on the same data at all times. Eventual consistency relaxes this guarantee — writes propagate asynchronously, so different nodes may temporarily return stale data, but the system guarantees all nodes will *eventually* converge to the same value. The choice is a direct trade-off defined by the CAP theorem: strong consistency sacrifices availability, eventual consistency sacrifices immediate accuracy.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Two consistency models in distributed systems describing how and when data changes become visible across all nodes after a write operation.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In a single-machine database, consistency is trivial — there's one source of truth. Distributed systems replicate data across nodes for fault tolerance and performance, which introduces replication lag. Consistency models define the contract between the system and its users about what they can expect to read after a write.

**How it works internally:** With strong consistency, writes are coordinated synchronously — the system uses protocols like Paxos, Raft, or two-phase commit (2PC) to ensure a quorum of nodes acknowledges the write before it is considered committed. No read can return until all (or a quorum of) replicas are updated. With eventual consistency, a write is acknowledged as soon as it lands on one node (or a small subset). That write then propagates to other nodes asynchronously in the background via gossip protocols or replication logs. During the propagation window, different nodes may serve different values — this is called a *stale read*. Systems like Cassandra use vector clocks or last-write-wins (LWW) to resolve conflicts when divergent writes occur.

**Benefits:**
Strong consistency — simple mental model for developers, no stale reads, safe for financial/transactional workloads.
Eventual consistency — lower latency (no write coordination overhead), higher availability, horizontal scalability, tolerates network partitions gracefully.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
Strong consistency — higher write latency, reduced availability during network partitions (per CAP theorem), harder to scale globally across data centers.
Eventual consistency — stale reads, conflict resolution complexity, incorrect behavior if business logic depends on up-to-date state (e.g., inventory checks, balance deductions).

## 💻 Code Example
```plaintext
=== STRONG CONSISTENCY (e.g., PostgreSQL / Raft-based distributed DB) ===

Client --> Leader Node
             |
             |-- Writes to WAL
             |-- Forwards to Replica A (waits for ACK)
             |-- Forwards to Replica B (waits for ACK)
             |
           Quorum achieved --> ACK returned to client
           Any read (from any node) now returns the new value

Timeline: Write latency = network RTT to replicas (e.g., 10-50ms cross-AZ)
Guarantee: No node returns old value after ACK


=== EVENTUAL CONSISTENCY (e.g., Cassandra / DynamoDB) ===

Client --> Node A (primary)
             |
             ACK immediately returned to client  <-- low latency
             |
             [Background async replication]
             |-- propagates to Node B (after 5ms)
             |-- propagates to Node C (after 12ms)

Timeline after write ACK:
  t+0ms:  Node A = "new_value", Node B = "old_value", Node C = "old_value"
  t+5ms:  Node A = "new_value", Node B = "new_value", Node C = "old_value"
  t+12ms: Node A = "new_value", Node B = "new_value", Node C = "new_value"
           ^-- System has converged (eventually consistent)

Stale read window: 0 to ~12ms in this example
Conflict resolution: last-write-wins (LWW) using timestamps or vector clocks


=== TUNABLE CONSISTENCY (Cassandra example) ===

Write with W=QUORUM, Read with R=QUORUM
  --> W + R > N (replication factor)
  --> Effectively strong consistency from an eventually-consistent store

Write with W=ONE, Read with R=ONE
  --> Fastest, but highest chance of stale reads
```

```python
# Simulating stale reads under eventual consistency
import time
import threading

