## ⚡ Short Answer
Database replication is the process of copying and maintaining database data across multiple servers in real time or near-real time. Master-slave (primary-replica) replication sends all writes to one node and distributes reads across replicas, while multi-master replication allows writes on any node and synchronizes changes across all peers. Replication provides high availability, fault tolerance, and horizontal read scalability.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A strategy for keeping multiple copies of a database synchronized across different servers, either for redundancy, read scaling, geographic distribution, or all three.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** A single database server is a single point of failure and a write/read bottleneck. Replication solves availability (failover to a replica if the primary dies), scalability (spread read traffic across replicas), and latency (place replicas closer to users geographically).

**How it works internally:**
In master-slave (primary-replica), the primary logs every write operation to a binary/WAL (Write-Ahead Log). Replicas connect to the primary, stream that log, and replay the operations locally. Replication can be synchronous (primary waits for replica acknowledgment before confirming the write — strong consistency, higher latency) or asynchronous (primary confirms immediately, replica catches up later — eventual consistency, lower latency). In multi-master, every node accepts writes and each node maintains its own log. A conflict resolution layer (last-write-wins, vector clocks, application-level merging) handles concurrent updates to the same row on different nodes.

**Benefits:**
High availability and automatic failover, horizontal read scaling, geographic locality (read from nearest replica), zero-downtime backups (snapshot a replica without touching the primary), and disaster recovery.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
Replication lag causes stale reads on replicas (eventual consistency). Multi-master introduces write conflicts that require conflict resolution logic. Split-brain scenarios (two nodes think they are primary) can cause data corruption. Cross-node distributed transactions become complex and expensive. Multi-master systems are significantly harder to operate correctly.

## 💻 Code Example
```plaintext
=== MASTER-SLAVE (PRIMARY-REPLICA) ARCHITECTURE ===

  [Application]
       |
       |-- WRITES --> [Primary / Master DB]
       |                    |
       |              WAL / Binlog stream
       |              |            |
       |         [Replica 1]  [Replica 2]
       |-- READS --> (round-robin or sticky session)

Flow:
1. App writes to Primary only
2. Primary appends to Write-Ahead Log (WAL)
3. Replicas tail the WAL and replay operations
4. Reads distributed across Replicas
5. If Primary fails → Replica 1 promoted to new Primary

=== MULTI-MASTER ARCHITECTURE ===

  [App Region A]          [App Region B]
        |                       |
  [Master Node A] <--sync--> [Master Node B]
        |                       |
  Writes accepted here      Writes accepted here
        |                       |
  Conflict Resolution Layer (e.g., last-write-wins by timestamp)

Flow:
1. Both nodes accept writes independently
2. Changes are replicated bidirectionally
3. Conflicts detected and resolved by defined strategy
4. Useful for multi-region active-active deployments

=== REPLICATION MODES ===

SYNCHRONOUS:
  Primary --write--> Replica (wait for ACK) --> confirm to client
  Pro: No data loss, strong consistency
  Con: Latency increases, write blocked if replica is slow

ASYNCHRONOUS (default for most systems):
  Primary --write--> confirm to client (immediately)
             |
             +--> Replica (replicates "eventually")
  Pro: Low latency, primary unaffected by slow replicas
  Con: Replica may lag; stale reads possible; data loss on failover

=== REPLICATION LAG MITIGATION STRATEGIES ===

1. Read-your-own-writes: Route reads to Primary for 1-2s after a write
2. Monotonic reads: Always route same user session to same replica
3. Sticky sessions: Pin a user to one replica
4. Sync replication for critical paths only (e.g., payment writes)

=== REAL-WORLD TOOL MAPPING ===

MySQL/MariaDB    → binlog-based primary-replica (GTID mode recommended)
PostgreSQL       → WAL-based streaming replication; Patroni for HA
MongoDB          → Replica Sets (1 primary, N secondaries, automatic election)
CockroachDB      → Multi-master (Raft consensus, no manual conflict resolution)
Galera Cluster   → Multi-master for MySQL (synchronous, wsrep protocol)
AWS RDS          → Managed primary-replica; Multi-AZ = sync standby
AWS Aurora       → Shared storage layer; 6 copies across 3 AZs automatically
Cassandra        → Masterless (peer-to-peer replication, tunable consistency)
```

