## ⚡ Short Answer
A reverse proxy sits in front of backend servers and intercepts client requests on their behalf, while a forward proxy sits in front of clients and makes requests on their behalf. The key distinction is direction: a forward proxy hides the client from the internet, while a reverse proxy hides the server from the internet. Both act as intermediaries, but they protect and serve different sides of the connection.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A reverse proxy is a server that accepts requests from clients, forwards them to one or more upstream backend servers, and returns the response as if it came from the proxy itself. The client never directly communicates with the origin server.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Without a reverse proxy, clients hit your origin servers directly, exposing their IPs, forcing each server to handle TLS termination, load balancing, caching, and rate limiting independently. A reverse proxy centralizes these cross-cutting concerns into one layer, enabling scalability, security, and performance improvements without changing backend code.

**How it works internally:** The proxy listens on a public-facing address (e.g., port 443). When a request arrives, it consults routing rules (host header, path, weighted round-robin, etc.) to select a backend, opens a connection to that backend (often from a connection pool), forwards the request, receives the response, and relays it to the client. TLS is typically terminated at the proxy, so backend traffic can be plain HTTP on a private network.

**Benefits:** Load distribution across multiple backends, TLS/SSL offloading, centralized caching, DDoS mitigation and rate limiting, A/B routing and canary deployments, single entry point for observability (logging, tracing), hides internal topology.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds a network hop and a potential single point of failure (mitigated by running the proxy in HA clusters). Misconfigured headers (especially X-Forwarded-For) can leak internal IPs or break authentication. Increases operational complexity and requires careful health-check tuning.

## 💻 Code Example
```plaintext
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FORWARD PROXY  (proxy hides the CLIENT)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  [Client] ──► [Forward Proxy] ──► [Internet / Target Server]
                  ▲
     Client explicitly configures
     the proxy in browser/OS settings.
     Target server sees proxy IP, not client IP.

  Use cases: corporate internet filtering, bypassing geo-blocks,
             anonymous browsing (Squid, corporate HTTP proxies)

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
REVERSE PROXY  (proxy hides the SERVER)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

                              ┌─► [Backend API  :8001]
  [Client] ──► [Reverse Proxy :443] ─► [Backend API  :8002]
     │              (nginx / Envoy)   └─► [Backend API  :8003]
     │
     Client thinks it's talking directly to "api.example.com".
     It has NO knowledge of the backends behind the proxy.

  Use cases: load balancing, TLS termination, caching, WAF

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NGINX REVERSE PROXY CONFIG (nginx.conf excerpt)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

http {

    # Upstream pool – nginx balances across all three instances
    upstream api_backends {
        least_conn;                        # route to least-busy server
        server 10.0.0.1:8001;
        server 10.0.0.2:8002;
        server 10.0.0.3:8003 backup;      # only used if primaries fail
    }

    server {
        listen 443 ssl;
        server_name api.example.com;

        ssl_certificate     /etc/ssl/api.crt;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/api.key;
        # ↑ TLS terminated HERE – backends receive plain HTTP

        location /api/ {
            proxy_pass         http://api_backends;

            # Preserve real client IP through the proxy layer
            proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP        $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For  $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header   Host             $host;

            # Health-check & timeout tuning
            proxy_connect_timeout  5s;
            proxy_read_timeout    30s;
        }

        # Cache static assets at the proxy layer
        location /static/ {
            proxy_pass         http://api_backends;
            proxy_cache        my_cache;
            proxy_cache_valid  200 1d;
        }
    }
}

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
