## ⚡ Short Answer
A **stateless service** holds no session or request data between calls — every request carries all the context it needs, and any instance can handle it. A **stateful service** remembers data about a client across requests (e.g., session, connection, in-memory cache). Statelessness aids scaling because you can add or remove instances freely without worrying about where a user's data lives.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Stateless services treat each request as independent and self-contained. Stateful services maintain per-client state (in memory, on disk, or via a persistent connection) that subsequent requests depend on.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** As traffic grows, you need to run multiple instances of a service. If state is tied to a specific instance (e.g., a shopping cart in memory), routing a user to a different instance breaks their session. Statelessness eliminates this "sticky session" problem entirely.

**How it works internally:** In a stateless design, all context travels with the request — via JWT tokens, request headers, query parameters, or a shared external store (Redis, a database). The service reads what it needs, processes, responds, and discards everything. No memory of the caller persists. In a stateful design, the server allocates and maintains a session object keyed to a client ID, meaning future requests must return to the same instance — or state must be replicated across instances.

**Benefits:** Any instance can serve any request, so a load balancer can freely distribute traffic. Instances can be spun up, killed, or replaced without data loss. Horizontal scaling (adding more nodes) is trivial. Fault tolerance improves — instance death doesn't lose user data.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Statelessness pushes state *somewhere* — usually to an external store (database, Redis, blob storage), which itself becomes a stateful bottleneck that must be scaled separately. Passing context with every request increases payload size and latency slightly. Some domains (real-time multiplayer, persistent connections like WebSockets, financial transactions) are inherently stateful and awkward to force into a stateless model.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// STATEFUL approach — cart lives in server memory
// Problem: only the instance that created the cart can serve the user
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
public class StatefulCartController : ControllerBase
{
    // ❌ In-memory dictionary: lost on restart, broken with multiple instances
    private static readonly Dictionary<string, List<string>> _carts = new();

    [HttpPost("add/{userId}/{item}")]
    public IActionResult AddItem(string userId, string item)
    {
        if (!_carts.ContainsKey(userId)) _carts[userId] = new();
        _carts[userId].Add(item);
        return Ok(_carts[userId]);
    }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// STATELESS approach — cart lives in Redis (external shared store)
// Any instance can serve any user because state is externalised
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
public class StatelessCartController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IDatabase _redis; // injected Redis connection

    public StatelessCartController(IConnectionMultiplexer redis)
        => _redis = redis.GetDatabase();

    [HttpPost("add/{userId}/{item}")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> AddItem(string userId, string item)
    {
        // ✅ State lives in Redis — survives instance death, works with N instances
        string key = $"cart:{userId}";
        await _redis.ListRightPushAsync(key, item);
        await _redis.KeyExpireAsync(key, TimeSpan.FromHours(24)); // TTL for hygiene

        RedisValue[] items = await _redis.ListRangeAsync(key);
        return Ok(items.Select(i => (string)i));
    }
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// STATELESS auth — JWT carries identity, no server-side session
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Client sends: Authorization: Bearer <jwt>
// Server validates signature and reads claims — no session lookup needed
// Any instance with the same signing key can validate the token
[Authorize]          // middleware validates JWT from header
[HttpGet("profile")]
public IActionResult GetProfile()
{
    // ClaimsPrincipal populated from JWT — zero server-side session storage
    var userId = User.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier)?.Value;
    return Ok(new { userId });
}
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: If stateless services push state to Redis, isn't Redis now the bottleneck?** A: Yes — external stores must be scaled too (Redis Cluster, read replicas, sharding). But scaling a single purpose-built data store is far easier than synchronising state
