## ⚡ Short Answer
A service mesh is a dedicated infrastructure layer that handles service-to-service communication in a microservices architecture, typically implemented via sidecar proxies deployed alongside each service. It solves the cross-cutting concerns of observability, traffic management, security, and resilience without requiring application code changes. Think of it as a programmable network layer that your services talk through, not to.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A service mesh is a configurable, low-latency infrastructure layer designed to handle inter-service communication in a microservices deployment. It is composed of a data plane (the sidecar proxies, e.g., Envoy) and a control plane (the orchestrator, e.g., Istiod) that pushes configuration to those proxies.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** As microservices scale from a handful to hundreds of services, every team ends up re-implementing the same networking logic — retries, timeouts, circuit breakers, mTLS, distributed tracing — in every language and framework. This leads to inconsistency, bugs, and a massive maintenance burden. A service mesh extracts all of that into a single, language-agnostic layer.

**How it works internally:** Each service pod gets a sidecar proxy (typically Envoy) injected automatically via a mutating admission webhook in Kubernetes. All inbound and outbound traffic is transparently intercepted by iptables rules and routed through the sidecar. The control plane (Istio, Linkerd, Consul Connect) pushes xDS configuration to each proxy, telling it how to route traffic, enforce policies, and collect telemetry. The application process itself is completely unaware of the mesh.

**Benefits:** Uniform observability (metrics, logs, traces) across all services without instrumentation code; automatic mTLS for zero-trust security; fine-grained traffic control (canary deployments, A/B testing, fault injection); circuit breaking and retries applied consistently; service discovery and load balancing.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Added latency per hop (typically 1–5ms per sidecar); significant operational complexity — you now operate the mesh itself; increased memory and CPU overhead per pod (Envoy alone can add ~50MB RAM); steep learning curve; can be overkill for small deployments with fewer than ~10 services.

