## ⚡ Short Answer
Consistency patterns define how and when data updates become visible across nodes in a distributed system. Read-your-writes (RYW) is a specific consistency guarantee ensuring that after a client writes data, any subsequent reads by that same client will reflect that write — even if replicas haven't fully synchronized yet.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Consistency patterns are contracts between a distributed system and its clients about the ordering and visibility of reads and writes. They range from strong consistency (every read sees the latest write) to eventual consistency (reads may lag behind). Read-your-writes is a session-level guarantee sitting in the middle of that spectrum.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In replicated systems, a write to a primary may not have propagated to replicas yet. Without RYW, a user who updates their profile might immediately see the old version on refresh if their next request hits a different replica — a confusing, broken UX. RYW eliminates this class of anomaly at the session or client level.

**How it works internally:** RYW is typically implemented using one of three strategies: (1) sticky sessions — route the client's reads to the same node they wrote to; (2) write timestamps / tokens — the client receives a version token after a write and sends it with reads; replicas refuse to serve the read until they've caught up to that version; (3) always read from primary — simple but sacrifices read scalability.

**Benefits:** Eliminates "I just saved that, where did it go?" anomalies. Improves perceived consistency without paying the full cost of linearizability. Works well for user-scoped data where cross-client coordination isn't required.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Sticky sessions create hot spots and reduce load-balancing flexibility. Version-token approaches add complexity to clients and routers. RYW gives no guarantees about what *other* clients see, so it's not suitable for scenarios requiring global ordering (e.g., financial ledgers, inventory counts).

## 💻 Code Example
```plaintext
=== Consistency Spectrum ===

STRONG (Linearizable)
  - Every read reflects the latest write globally
  - Example: Google Spanner, ZooKeeper
  - Cost: High latency, coordination overhead

SEQUENTIAL
  - All operations appear in some global order
  - Clients see a consistent order, not necessarily real-time
  - Example: Multi-leader Raft consensus

READ-YOUR-WRITES (Session Consistency)
  - A client's own writes are always visible to itself
  - Other clients may still see stale data
  - Example: DynamoDB sessions, MongoDB causal sessions

MONOTONIC READS
  - Once a client sees a value, it never sees an older value
  - Prevents "time going backwards" reads
  - Often paired with RYW

EVENTUAL CONSISTENCY
  - All replicas converge given no new writes
  - No ordering guarantees
  - Example: Cassandra default, DNS

=============================================================
RYW Implementation — Version Token Strategy
=============================================================

CLIENT writes:
  POST /profile  { name: "Alice" }
  ← Server responds: { ok: true, write_version: 10042 }
  Client stores: session.write_version = 10042

CLIENT reads:
  GET /profile
  → Request header: X-Min-Version: 10042
  ← Replica checks: my_applied_version >= 10042?
      YES → serve read
      NO  → wait / redirect to primary

=============================================================
RYW Implementation — Sticky Session Strategy
=============================================================

Load Balancer:
  - On first write, tag session cookie with shard ID
  - Route subsequent reads from that session to same shard
  - Failover: promote replica or redirect to primary

Client A: write → Primary  (cookie: shard=primary-us-east-1)
Client A: read  → Primary  (guaranteed RYW)
Client B: read  → Replica  (may see stale, but that's ok — different session)

=============================================================
MongoDB Causal Consistency Example (session-level RYW)
=============================================================

Session opened:
  session = client.startSession({ causalConsistency: true })

Write with session:
  db.users.updateOne(
    { _id: userId },
    { $set: { name: "Alice" } },
    { session }
  )
  // MongoDB records operationTime + clusterTime in session

Read with session:
  db.users.findOne({ _id: userId }, { session })
  // MongoDB routes to a node that has caught up to
  // at least the recorded operationTime — RYW guaranteed
```

## ❓ Follow-Up Questions
- **Q: How does RYW differ from linearizability?** A: Linearizability is a global guarantee — every client sees the latest write immediately. RYW is session-scoped — only the writing client is guaranteed to see its own writes; other clients may still observe stale data.
- **Q: Can you implement RYW without sticky sessions?** A: Yes — use a vector clock or monotonic timestamp returned with writes
