## ⚡ Short Answer
The Circuit Breaker pattern is a resilience design pattern that prevents cascading failures in distributed systems by wrapping calls to remote services and "tripping" (opening) when failure thresholds are exceeded. It acts like an electrical circuit breaker: when too many failures occur, it stops sending requests to the failing service, allowing it time to recover. It has three states: Closed, Open, and Half-Open.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A proxy/wrapper around external calls (HTTP, DB, message queues) that monitors failures and short-circuits requests when a failure threshold is breached, returning a fallback immediately instead of waiting for a timeout.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In distributed systems, a slow or failing downstream service can exhaust thread pools, connection pools, and resources in the caller — causing a cascading failure that takes down the entire system. Without a circuit breaker, every request to a dead service waits for a full timeout (e.g., 30s), multiplied across thousands of concurrent users.

**How it works internally:** The breaker tracks a rolling window of successes and failures. In the **Closed** state, calls pass through normally. If failures exceed a configured threshold (e.g., 50% over 10 seconds), it transitions to **Open**, where all calls immediately fail-fast or return a cached/fallback value. After a cooldown period, it enters **Half-Open**, allowing a limited probe request through. If that probe succeeds, it resets to Closed; if it fails, it returns to Open.

**Benefits:** Fail-fast behavior reduces latency; protects downstream services from being overwhelmed; enables graceful degradation via fallbacks; provides observability into system health.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Adds complexity and configuration overhead (threshold tuning is hard); false positives can trip the breaker under brief spikes; stateful nature is tricky in multi-instance deployments (requires distributed state or per-instance breakers).

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// Using Polly — the de facto Circuit Breaker library for .NET
// Install: dotnet add package Polly

using Polly;
using Polly.CircuitBreaker;

public class OrderService
{
    private readonly HttpClient _httpClient;

    // Build a circuit breaker policy:
    // - Break if 50% of requests in a 10-second window fail
    // - Stay open (broken) for 30 seconds before retrying
    // - Allow 1 probe request in Half-Open state
    private readonly AsyncCircuitBreakerPolicy _circuitBreaker =
        Policy
            .Handle<HttpRequestException>()
            .AdvancedCircuitBreakerAsync(
                failureThreshold: 0.5,        // 50% failure rate triggers OPEN
                samplingDuration: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10),
                minimumThroughput: 5,         // need at least 5 calls before tripping
                durationOfBreak: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30), // stay OPEN for 30s
                onBreak: (ex, duration) =>
                    Console.WriteLine($"Circuit OPENED for {duration.TotalSeconds}s: {ex.Message}"),
                onReset: () =>
                    Console.WriteLine("Circuit CLOSED — service recovered"),
                onHalfOpen: () =>
                    Console.WriteLine("Circuit HALF-OPEN — probing service")
            );

    public OrderService(HttpClient httpClient)
    {
        _httpClient = httpClient;
    }

    public async Task<string> GetInventoryAsync(int productId)
    {
        try
        {
            // The circuit breaker wraps the HTTP call
            return await _circuitBreaker.ExecuteAsync(async () =>
            {
                var response = await _httpClient.GetAsync($"/inventory/{productId}");
                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
                return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
            });
        }
        catch (BrokenCircuitException)
        {
            // Circuit is OPEN — fail fast, return fallback
            Console.WriteLine("Circuit is OPEN. Returning cached/default value.");
            return "{ \"stock\": 0, \"source\": \"fallback\" }";
        }
        catch (HttpRequestException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Request failed: {ex.Message}");
            throw;
        }
    }
}

// --- State machine summary ---
// CLOSED   → normal operation, tracking failure rate
//            ↓ (failure threshold exceeded)
// OPEN     → all calls fail-fast immediately (no network call made)
//            ↓ (durationOfBreak elapses)
// HALF-OPEN → one probe request allowed through
//            ↓ success → CLOSED
//            ↓ failure → OPEN again
```

