## ⚡ Short Answer
The Saga pattern manages distributed transactions across multiple microservices by breaking them into a sequence of local transactions, each publishing an event or message that triggers the next step. If any step fails, compensating transactions are executed in reverse order to undo the completed steps. It avoids the need for distributed locking (2PC) while still maintaining eventual consistency.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A Saga is a sequence of local transactions where each service performs its own transaction and publishes a message/event. If a step fails, the saga executes pre-defined compensating transactions to roll back the work done by previous steps.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Traditional two-phase commit (2PC) requires all participating services to lock resources until the coordinator decides to commit or abort, which kills availability and doesn't work well across service boundaries and network partitions. Sagas replace atomic distributed transactions with a series of smaller, reversible steps that achieve the same business goal without distributed locking.

**How it works internally:** There are two primary coordination styles. In the **Choreography** style, each service listens for events and reacts — no central coordinator; services are loosely coupled but harder to track. In the **Orchestration** style, a central Saga Orchestrator (a service or workflow engine) explicitly tells each participant what to do and handles failures by issuing compensating commands. Both styles rely on idempotent handlers and reliable messaging (e.g., outbox pattern) to guarantee at-least-once delivery.

**Benefits:** No distributed locks, high availability, works across service and database boundaries, each service owns its data, failure handling is explicit and auditable.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Only eventual consistency (no immediate isolation); compensating transactions must be carefully designed and can be complex; debugging distributed saga flows is hard; the system is temporarily inconsistent between steps, which may be visible to users (dirty reads at the saga level).

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// === ORCHESTRATION SAGA EXAMPLE ===
// Scenario: Place Order → Reserve Inventory → Charge Payment
// Each step has a compensating action if something fails later.

// --- Domain Events & Commands ---
public record OrderPlacedEvent(Guid OrderId, Guid CustomerId, decimal Amount, List<OrderItem> Items);
public record InventoryReservedEvent(Guid OrderId, Guid ReservationId);
public record PaymentChargedEvent(Guid OrderId, Guid PaymentId);
public record PaymentFailedEvent(Guid OrderId, string Reason);

// --- Saga State (persisted between steps) ---
public class PlaceOrderSagaState
{
    public Guid OrderId { get; set; }
    public Guid CustomerId { get; set; }
    public decimal Amount { get; set; }
    public Guid? ReservationId { get; set; }   // set after step 1 succeeds
    public Guid? PaymentId { get; set; }        // set after step 2 succeeds
    public SagaStatus Status { get; set; }
}

public enum SagaStatus { Started, InventoryReserved, PaymentCharged, Completed, Compensating, Failed }

// --- Saga Orchestrator ---
public class PlaceOrderSagaOrchestrator
{
    private readonly IInventoryService _inventory;
    private readonly IPaymentService _payment;
    private readonly IOrderService _order;
    private readonly ISagaStateRepository _stateRepo;
    private readonly ILogger<PlaceOrderSagaOrchestrator> _logger;

    public PlaceOrderSagaOrchestrator(
        IInventoryService inventory, IPaymentService payment,
        IOrderService order, ISagaStateRepository stateRepo,
        ILogger<PlaceOrderSagaOrchestrator> logger)
    {
        _inventory = inventory;
        _payment = payment;
        _order = order;
        _stateRepo = stateRepo;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    // Entry point — triggered when an order is placed
    public async Task HandleAsync(OrderPlacedEvent evt)
    {
        var state = new PlaceOrderSagaState
        {
            OrderId = evt.OrderId,
            CustomerId = evt.CustomerId,
            Amount = evt.Amount,
            Status = SagaStatus.Started
        };
        await _stateRepo.SaveAsync(state);

        // Step 1: Reserve Inventory
        var reservation = await _inventory.ReserveAsync(evt.OrderId, evt.Items);
        if (!reservation.Success)
        {
            _logger.LogWarning("Inventory reservation failed for Order {OrderId}", evt.OrderId);
            await _order.CancelAsync(evt.OrderId, "Inventory unavailable");
            state.Status = SagaStatus.Failed;
            await _stateRepo.SaveAsync(state);
            return;
        }

        state.ReservationId = reservation.ReservationId;
        state.Status = SagaStatus.InventoryReserved
