## ⚡ Short Answer
CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) separates the write model (Commands) from the read model (Queries) into distinct paths, allowing each to be optimized independently. It is often paired with Event Sourcing because events serve as the authoritative write store, and those same events can be projected into multiple optimized read models, making the two patterns a natural architectural fit.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** CQRS is an architectural pattern where the model used to mutate state (Command side) is strictly separated from the model used to query state (Query side). Commands express intent ("PlaceOrder"), Queries return data without side effects ("GetOrderSummary").

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Traditional CRUD systems use a single model for reads and writes. This creates tension: the write model must enforce business invariants (complex aggregates, validation), while the read model needs to be flat, fast, and denormalized for UI consumption. Forcing both through one model leads to impedance mismatch, poor read performance, and anemic domain models.

**How it works internally:** Commands are dispatched to a Command Handler which loads an Aggregate, applies business logic, and persists changes. A separate Query Handler reads from a denormalized Read Store (e.g., a materialized view, Redis cache, or Elasticsearch index) that is kept in sync via events or database replication. With Event Sourcing, every state change is stored as an immutable event; projections (read model builders) subscribe to those events and rebuild or update the read store asynchronously, achieving eventual consistency.

**Benefits:** Independent scaling of reads and writes, optimized read models per use case (one for dashboard, one for reporting), clear separation of intent, natural audit trail when paired with Event Sourcing, and support for temporal queries (replay events to reconstruct past state).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Significantly increased complexity — two models, eventual consistency, projection maintenance, and infrastructure overhead. Overkill for simple CRUD apps. Eventual consistency can confuse users who expect to see their own writes immediately (mitigated with read-your-own-writes patterns or synchronous projections for small write volumes).

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// ── DOMAIN EVENTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
public record OrderPlaced(Guid OrderId, string CustomerId, decimal Total, DateTime OccurredAt);
public record OrderShipped(Guid OrderId, string TrackingNumber, DateTime OccurredAt);

// ── COMMAND SIDE (Write Model) ──────────────────────────────────

// Command — expresses intent, no return data
public record PlaceOrderCommand(Guid OrderId, string CustomerId, List<OrderLine> Lines);

// Aggregate — enforces invariants, raises events
public class OrderAggregate
{
    public Guid Id { get; private set; }
    private bool _placed;
    private readonly List<object> _domainEvents = new();

    public IReadOnlyList<object> DomainEvents => _domainEvents;

    public void PlaceOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd)
    {
        if (_placed) throw new InvalidOperationException("Order already placed.");
        decimal total = cmd.Lines.Sum(l => l.Qty * l.UnitPrice);
        // Apply and record event
        Apply(new OrderPlaced(cmd.OrderId, cmd.CustomerId, total, DateTime.UtcNow));
    }

    private void Apply(OrderPlaced e)
    {
        Id = e.OrderId;
        _placed = true;
        _domainEvents.Add(e);
    }
}

// Command Handler — orchestrates aggregate + persistence
public class PlaceOrderHandler
{
    private readonly IEventStore _eventStore;
    private readonly IEventBus _eventBus;

    public PlaceOrderHandler(IEventStore eventStore, IEventBus eventBus)
        => (_eventStore, _eventBus) = (eventStore, eventBus);

    public async Task Handle(PlaceOrderCommand cmd)
    {
        var order = new OrderAggregate();
        order.PlaceOrder(cmd);

        // Persist events as source of truth (Event Sourcing)
        await _eventStore.AppendEventsAsync(cmd.OrderId, order.DomainEvents);

        // Publish so projections can update the read store
        foreach (var evt in order.DomainEvents)
            await _eventBus.PublishAsync(evt);
    }
}

