## ⚡ Short Answer
Blue-green deployments and canary releases are two zero-downtime deployment strategies that reduce risk when shipping new software versions. Blue-green maintains two identical production environments and switches traffic instantly between them, while canary releases gradually roll out changes to a small subset of users before a full rollout.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Blue-green deployment runs two mirrored production environments (blue = current live, green = new version); traffic switches atomically via a load balancer or DNS. Canary release routes a small percentage of real traffic (e.g. 1–5%) to the new version, monitors it, then progressively increases the percentage until full rollout or rollback.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Traditional deployments cause downtime and make rollback painful. Both strategies allow instant or progressive rollback, validate changes against real traffic, and eliminate the "big bang" release risk that causes outages.

**How it works internally:** In blue-green, a load balancer (NGINX, AWS ALB, etc.) points to the active slot. The inactive slot is upgraded, smoke-tested, then the load balancer flips. In canary, weighted routing rules (e.g. 5% → new, 95% → old) are applied at the load balancer or service mesh level; automated metrics (error rate, latency, p99) gate each progressive increase via a deployment controller like Argo Rollouts or Flagger.

**Benefits:** Zero downtime, instant or automated rollback, production-validated testing, reduced blast radius, A/B testing capability.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Blue-green requires double the infrastructure cost and complicates database schema migrations (both versions must be schema-compatible simultaneously). Canary adds observability complexity, requires good metrics and alerting, and can expose a percentage of users to bugs before detection.

## 💻 Code Example
