## ⚡ Short Answer
Service versioning is the practice of managing multiple versions of an API or microservice simultaneously, allowing clients to migrate at their own pace without breaking existing integrations. It exists because APIs evolve — fields change, endpoints are deprecated, and backward-incompatible changes are inevitable in long-lived systems. The three dominant strategies are URI versioning, header versioning, and query parameter versioning, each with different trade-offs around visibility, caching, and coupling.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A set of techniques for exposing multiple iterations of a service contract (API, message schema, or interface) concurrently so that consumers and providers can evolve independently.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In distributed systems, services are owned by different teams and updated on different schedules. A breaking change pushed without versioning forces all consumers to update simultaneously — a coordination nightmare. Versioning decouples producer release cycles from consumer release cycles, enabling independent deployability, a core microservices principle.

**How it works internally:** The routing layer (API gateway, load balancer, or application router) inspects an incoming request's version signal (URI segment, header value, or query param) and forwards it to the appropriate service instance or code path. Internally, services can share a codebase and branch on version, or run entirely separate deployments per version. Schema registries (e.g., Confluent Schema Registry for Kafka) manage versioning for async message contracts.

**Benefits:** Enables zero-downtime migrations, supports long-lived enterprise clients with slow upgrade cycles, allows A/B testing of API designs, and gives teams contractual safety to evolve internals freely.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Maintaining multiple versions multiplies operational burden — bug fixes may need backporting, test surface grows, and deprecated versions create tech debt. Header versioning breaks browser-based caching. URI versioning "leaks" version into resource identity, which purists argue violates REST semantics. Sunset policies and client communication discipline are essential, otherwise versions live forever.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// === 1. URI VERSIONING (most common, cache-friendly) ===
// Routes: /api/v1/orders  and  /api/v2/orders

[ApiController]
[Route("api/v1/orders")]
public class OrdersV1Controller : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id}")]
    public IActionResult Get(int id)
    {
        // V1: returns flat order with string status
        return Ok(new { Id = id, Status = "shipped", CustomerId = 42 });
    }
}

[ApiController]
[Route("api/v2/orders")]
public class OrdersV2Controller : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id}")]
    public IActionResult Get(int id)
    {
        // V2: breaking change — status is now an enum object, address added
        return Ok(new
        {
            Id = id,
            Status = new { Code = 3, Label = "Shipped", TrackingUrl = "https://..." },
            CustomerId = 42,
            ShippingAddress = new { City = "New York", Zip = "10001" }
        });
    }
}

// === 2. HEADER VERSIONING (cleaner URIs, harder to test in browser) ===
// Request: GET /api/orders/1  +  Header: Api-Version: 2

[ApiController]
[Route("api/orders")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id}")]
    public IActionResult Get(int id)
    {
        var version = Request.Headers["Api-Version"].FirstOrDefault() ?? "1";

        return version switch
        {
            "2" => Ok(new { Id = id, Status = new { Code = 3, Label = "Shipped" } }),
            _   => Ok(new { Id = id, Status = "shipped" })  // v1 default
        };
    }
}

// === 3. QUERY PARAMETER VERSIONING (easy for simple APIs) ===
// Request: GET /api/orders/1?version=2

// === 4. MEDIA TYPE / CONTENT NEGOTIATION VERSIONING (REST purist approach) ===
// Request: Accept: application/vnd.myapi.v2+json

// === 5. ASP.NET Core API Versioning (production library approach) ===
// Install: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Versioning

// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddApiVersioning(options =>
{
    options.DefaultApiVersion = new ApiVersion(1, 0);
    options.AssumeDefaultVersionWhenUnspecified = true;
    options.ReportApiVersions = true; // adds api-supported-versions header
    options.ApiVersionReader = ApiVersionReader.Combine(
        new UrlSegmentApiVersionReader(),        // /api/v1/
        new HeaderApiVersionReader("Api-Version") // fallback to header
    );
});

