## ⚡ Short Answer
An authentication and authorization service using OAuth2 and JWT separates identity verification (authn) from permission enforcement (authz) across distributed systems. OAuth2 defines the flow for delegating access (authorization code, client credentials, etc.), while JWTs serve as self-contained, signed tokens that carry claims without requiring a database round-trip on every request. Together they enable stateless, scalable security across microservices with short-lived access tokens and longer-lived refresh tokens.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A centralized Identity Provider (IdP) that issues signed JWT access tokens and opaque refresh tokens following the OAuth2 specification, enabling clients (web apps, mobile, services) to authenticate users and authorize resource access without sharing credentials.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Session-based auth doesn't scale horizontally — every node needs shared session state (Redis, DB). OAuth2 + JWT solves this by making tokens self-verifiable: any service with the public key can validate a token locally, enabling truly stateless microservices. OAuth2 also solves third-party delegation ("Login with Google") without sharing passwords.

**How it works internally:** The Authorization Server (AS) authenticates the user, then issues a short-lived JWT access token (signed with RS256 or ES256 private key) and a long-lived opaque refresh token stored server-side. Resource Servers (APIs) verify the JWT signature using the AS's public key (fetched from a JWKS endpoint), check expiry, audience, and scope claims — all without network calls. When the access token expires, the client POSTs the refresh token to the AS to get a new access token, allowing the AS to rotate or revoke sessions.

**Benefits:**
- Stateless verification on resource servers (no DB lookup per request)
- Standard protocol — works with third-party IdPs (Google, Okta, Auth0)
- Fine-grained scopes and claims embedded in the token
- Supports machine-to-machine (Client Credentials grant) and user-facing (Authorization Code + PKCE) flows

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- JWTs can't be revoked before expiry without a token blocklist (defeats statelessness partially)
- Token size grows with claims — don't put sensitive data in the payload (it's base64, not encrypted)
- RS256 key rotation must be handled gracefully via JWKS caching with TTL
- Refresh token theft is catastrophic — requires HTTPS, HttpOnly cookies, and refresh token rotation

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// =====================================================
// AUTH SERVICE — Core JWT + OAuth2 Design (C# / .NET 8)
// =====================================================

// --- 1. TOKEN SERVICE (Authorization Server core) ---
public class TokenService
{
    private readonly RsaSecurityKey _privateKey;
    private readonly IRefreshTokenRepository _refreshTokenRepo;
    private const string Issuer = "https://auth.myapp.com";
    private const string Audience = "https://api.myapp.com";

    public TokenService(RsaSecurityKey privateKey, IRefreshTokenRepository repo)
    {
        _privateKey = privateKey;
        _refreshTokenRepo = repo;
    }

    // Issue a short-lived JWT access token (15 min)
    public string IssueAccessToken(UserClaims user)
    {
        var claims = new List<Claim>
        {
            new(JwtRegisteredClaimNames.Sub, user.UserId),
            new(JwtRegisteredClaimNames.Email, user.Email),
            new(JwtRegisteredClaimNames.Jti, Guid.NewGuid().ToString()), // unique token ID
            new("roles", string.Join(",", user.Roles)),
            new("scope", string.Join(" ", user.Scopes)), // e.g. "read:orders write:profile"
        };

        var creds = new SigningCredentials(_privateKey, SecurityAlgorithms.RsaSha256);

        var token = new JwtSecurityToken(
            issuer: Issuer,
            audience: Audience,
            claims: claims,
            notBefore: DateTime.UtcNow,
            expires: DateTime.UtcNow.AddMinutes(15), // SHORT expiry — key design decision
            signingCredentials: creds
        );

        return new JwtSecurityTokenHandler().WriteToken(token);
    }

    // Issue an opaque refresh token stored server-side (7 days)
    public async Task<string> IssueRefreshTokenAsync(string userId, string deviceId)
    {
        var rawToken = Convert.ToBase64String(RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(64));
        var hashed = Convert.ToBase64String(SHA256.HashData(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(rawToken)));

        await _refreshTokenRepo.SaveAsync(new RefreshToken
        {
            TokenHash = hashed,         // store hash, never raw
            UserId = userId,
            DeviceId = deviceId,        // family rotation
