## ⚡ Short Answer
A large-scale file upload/download service uses chunked multipart uploads to object storage (S3/GCS/Azure Blob), a metadata database for file tracking, CDN edge caching for download acceleration, and async processing pipelines for post-upload operations like virus scanning and thumbnail generation. The key insight is separating the binary blob storage concern from metadata management while offloading heavy transfer work directly to object storage via presigned URLs, bypassing your application servers entirely.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A distributed system that handles reliable ingestion, storage, retrieval, and lifecycle management of arbitrary binary files at scale — supporting millions of concurrent users uploading/downloading files ranging from kilobytes to gigabytes.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Naively routing file bytes through application servers creates bottlenecks: CPU saturation, memory pressure, and bandwidth exhaustion. Object storage (S3) can sustain millions of requests/second but needs an orchestration layer for auth, deduplication, chunking, resumability, and CDN integration. You need a design that handles partial failures gracefully (resumable uploads), avoids re-uploading duplicate content (deduplication via content hash), and serves downloads at low latency globally (CDN).

**How it works internally:**
Upload flow — client requests a presigned upload URL from your API → API generates a multipart upload ID from S3 and stores metadata in DB with status=PENDING → client uploads chunks directly to S3 in parallel → client calls your API to complete the upload → API triggers async post-processing (virus scan, thumbnail, transcoding) via a message queue → status flips to READY.
Download flow — client requests a file → API checks permissions and file status → returns a short-lived presigned CDN URL or signed S3 URL → client fetches directly from CDN/S3 edge node, not your servers.

**Benefits:** Horizontal scalability with no application-layer bandwidth bottleneck; built-in S3 durability (11 9s); resumable uploads survive network interruptions; CDN dramatically reduces latency and origin egress costs; content-addressable storage enables deduplication.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Presigned URL complexity (expiry, security surface); eventual consistency in metadata vs. storage state requires careful reconciliation; multipart upload orphans need lifecycle cleanup rules; CDN cache invalidation on delete/update is non-trivial; regulatory requirements (GDPR right-to-erasure) require guaranteed deletion pipelines, not just metadata soft-deletes.

