## ⚡ Short Answer
A distributed caching system partitions data across multiple nodes using consistent hashing, replicates data for fault tolerance, and coordinates reads/writes through a client-side or proxy routing layer. Like Redis Cluster, it achieves horizontal scalability by sharding keyspace into slots, uses gossip protocol for node discovery and failure detection, and provides sub-millisecond latency by keeping all data in memory.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A distributed in-memory key-value store where data is sharded across N nodes, each node owns a subset of hash slots, and the system continues operating even when nodes fail — providing low-latency reads/writes at massive scale.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** A single-node cache has a hard memory ceiling and becomes a single point of failure. A distributed cache solves capacity limits by adding nodes, eliminates SPOF via replication, and reduces hotspot pressure by distributing load across the cluster.

**How it works internally:**
Keyspace is divided into fixed slots (Redis uses 16,384). A hash function (CRC16 mod 16384) maps each key to a slot. Each master node owns a range of slots. Replicas shadow their master for failover. Clients use a routing table (CLUSTER SLOTS) to talk directly to the correct node — if they land on the wrong node they receive a MOVED redirect. The gossip protocol (every 100ms) propagates node state; if a master is unreachable by a quorum of peers for a timeout window, a replica is promoted via a Raft-like election.

**Benefits:**
Linear horizontal scalability by adding shards, high availability via automatic failover, sub-millisecond latency, support for complex data structures (strings, hashes, sorted sets, streams), and built-in pub/sub and Lua scripting.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
Multi-key operations (MGET, transactions) only work when all keys map to the same slot (use hash tags `{user}:*` to force co-location). Strong consistency is traded for availability — during a failover window, writes to the failing master are lost. Memory cost is 2× (master + replica minimum). Operational complexity grows with cluster size.

