## ⚡ Short Answer
A search autocomplete feature suggests query completions in real time as a user types, typically using a Trie or prefix-based index backed by a ranked dataset of historical queries or known entities. The system must return relevant, ranked suggestions within ~100ms to feel instantaneous. At scale it combines client-side debouncing, a CDN-cached or in-memory suggestion service, and offline pipelines that compute query frequency rankings.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A system that takes a partial string prefix typed by a user and returns the top-K most relevant completions, ranked by popularity, personalization, or recency.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Users abandon search when they have to type full queries or face misspellings. Autocomplete reduces friction, improves query quality, and increases engagement. It also surfaces trending or popular content the user might not have known to search for.

**How it works internally:** The core data structure is a Trie (prefix tree) where each node represents a character and each terminal node stores the top-K completions and their scores. A min-heap of size K is maintained at each node so suggestions are instantly available without traversal. At large scale (Google, Netflix) this is replaced by a distributed inverted-prefix index sharded by prefix hash, served from in-memory stores like Redis or a custom in-process cache. An offline Hadoop/Spark pipeline periodically recomputes scores (query frequency × recency decay × CTR). A separate online pipeline handles trending queries with a streaming aggregator (Kafka + Flink). The API layer adds personalization (user history, location) at query time via a lightweight re-ranking pass.

**Benefits:** Sub-100ms P99 latency when served from memory; dramatically reduces user effort; increases search quality and click-through rate; can surface trending content in near real time.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Trie memory grows large for massive corpora (mitigated by pruning low-frequency terms); personalization adds latency and infrastructure complexity; must filter harmful/inappropriate suggestions carefully; stale offline indices miss real-time trends unless augmented by a streaming layer.

