## ⚡ Short Answer
Rotating an array right by k positions means each element shifts k indices to the right, with the last k elements wrapping around to the front. The most efficient approach uses the **reversal algorithm** — reverse the whole array, reverse the first k elements, then reverse the remaining — achieving O(n) time and O(1) space.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A right rotation by k moves element at index i to index (i + k) % n, effectively cycling the last k elements to the front of the array.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Array rotation appears in circular buffer management, scheduling algorithms, string pattern matching (rotation checks), and sliding window problems. Understanding it tests knowledge of in-place algorithms and modular arithmetic.

**How it works internally:** The reversal trick exploits the fact that reversing the entire array and then reversing two sub-sections produces the rotated result without extra memory. Mathematically, reversing [0..n-1], then [0..k-1], then [k..n-1] is equivalent to a right cyclic shift by k. The key insight: always normalize k with `k = k % n` to handle k ≥ n cases.

**Benefits:** O(n) time, O(1) space, no extra array needed, handles all edge cases cleanly with normalization.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Mutates the original array (not pure). If immutability is required, use slice/concat (O(n) space). For very small arrays or single rotations, simpler approaches (pop + unshift) are more readable despite being O(n²).

## 💻 Code Example
```javascript
/**
 * RIGHT ROTATE array by k positions — Three Approaches
 * Input:  [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7], k = 3
 * Output: [5, 6, 7, 1, 2, 3, 4]
 */

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// APPROACH 1: Reversal Algorithm (OPTIMAL)
// Time: O(n) | Space: O(1)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
function rotateRight(nums, k) {
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return nums;

  k = k % n; // normalize: k=7 on len-7 array = no-op
  if (k === 0) return nums;

  reverse(nums, 0, n - 1);     // Step 1: reverse entire array
  reverse(nums, 0, k - 1);     // Step 2: reverse first k elements
  reverse(nums, k, n - 1);     // Step 3: reverse remaining elements

  return nums;
}

function reverse(arr, left, right) {
  while (left < right) {
    [arr[left], arr[right]] = [arr[right], arr[left]]; // destructure swap
    left++;
    right--;
  }
}

const arr1 = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7];
console.log(rotateRight(arr1, 3));  // [5, 6, 7, 1, 2, 3, 4]
console.log(rotateRight([1], 99));  // [1]  — normalized k=0
console.log(rotateRight([1,2], 2)); // [1, 2] — full rotation = no-op


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// APPROACH 2: Extra Array (Intuitive)
// Time: O(n) | Space: O(n)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
function rotateRightExtra(nums, k) {
  const n = nums.length;
  k = k % n;
  // New position of element at index i: (i + k) % n
  const result = new Array(n);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    result[(i + k) % n] = nums[i];
  }
  return result;
}

console.log(rotateRightExtra([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7], 3)); // [5, 6, 7, 1, 2, 3, 4]


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// APPROACH 3: Slice + Concat (Immutable/Readable)
// Time: O(n) | Space: O(n)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────
function rotateRightImmutable(nums, k) {
  const n = nums.length;
  k = k % n;
  // Last k elements come first, then the rest
  return [...nums.slice(n - k), ...nums.slice(0, n - k)];
}
