## ⚡ Short Answer
The Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA) of two nodes p and q in a BST is the deepest node that has both p and q as descendants (a node is a descendant of itself). Because a BST has ordering properties, you can determine which subtree to recurse into by comparing node values — no need to search blindly. This makes the BST LCA solution O(h) time where h is the tree height, versus O(n) for a general binary tree.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** The LCA of nodes p and q is the deepest node in the tree that is an ancestor of both. In a BST, the split point (where p and q diverge into different subtrees) is exactly that ancestor.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** LCA is foundational for finding shortest paths between nodes, answering range queries, and is a classic interview problem testing BST traversal understanding. The BST variant is elegant because the ordering property replaces expensive parent-pointer tracking.

**How it works internally:** Walk from the root. At each node, if both p and q are less than the current node's value, the LCA must be in the left subtree. If both are greater, go right. The moment the values split (one is ≤ current and one is ≥ current), the current node IS the LCA. This works because the current node is the first ancestor that "straddles" both values.

**Benefits:** O(h) time — O(log n) for balanced BSTs. O(1) extra space iteratively (no recursion stack). Very clean logic with no backtracking needed.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Only exploits BST ordering — for a general binary tree you need a different O(n) algorithm. Assumes both nodes exist in the tree (no validation). A degenerate (skewed) BST degrades to O(n).

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// Definition for BST node
public class TreeNode
{
    public int Val;
    public TreeNode Left, Right;
    public TreeNode(int val) { Val = val; }
}

public class BSTSolution
{
    // ── Recursive approach ──────────────────────────────────────────
    // Time: O(h)  |  Space: O(h) call stack
    public TreeNode LCA_Recursive(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q)
    {
        if (root == null) return null;

        // Both targets are smaller → LCA is in the left subtree
        if (p.Val < root.Val && q.Val < root.Val)
            return LCA_Recursive(root.Left, p, q);

        // Both targets are larger → LCA is in the right subtree
        if (p.Val > root.Val && q.Val > root.Val)
            return LCA_Recursive(root.Right, p, q);

        // Values split here (or one equals root) → current node is LCA
        return root;
    }

    // ── Iterative approach (preferred in production) ────────────────
    // Time: O(h)  |  Space: O(1) — no recursion stack
    public TreeNode LCA_Iterative(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q)
    {
        TreeNode current = root;

        while (current != null)
        {
            if (p.Val < current.Val && q.Val < current.Val)
                current = current.Left;         // both left
            else if (p.Val > current.Val && q.Val > current.Val)
                current = current.Right;        // both right
            else
                return current;                 // split point = LCA
        }

        return null; // should never reach here if p and q exist
    }
}

