## ⚡ Short Answer
Binary search finds a target value in a sorted array by repeatedly halving the search space, comparing the target to the middle element, and narrowing to the left or right half. It runs in O(log n) time and O(1) space (iterative), making it dramatically faster than linear search for large datasets.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A divide-and-conquer search algorithm that works exclusively on sorted collections by eliminating half the remaining candidates each iteration.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Linear search is O(n) — fine for 100 elements, painful for 1,000,000. Binary search reduces that to ~20 comparisons for a million elements. It solves the "find element efficiently in sorted data" problem.

**How it works internally:** Maintain two pointers, `low` and `high`. Calculate `mid = low + (high - low) / 2` (avoiding integer overflow). If `arr[mid] == target`, return mid. If `arr[mid] < target`, move `low = mid + 1`. If `arr[mid] > target`, move `high = mid - 1`. Repeat until found or `low > high`.

**Benefits:** O(log n) time complexity, O(1) space (iterative), simple to implement, extends to many variants (lower bound, upper bound, rotated arrays).

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Requires a sorted array. Insertion to maintain sort order is O(n). Not suitable for linked lists (no O(1) random access). Overkill for very small arrays where linear search has better cache behavior.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;

public class BinarySearch
{
    // --- Iterative Binary Search --- O(log n) time, O(1) space
    public static int SearchIterative(int[] arr, int target)
    {
        int low = 0, high = arr.Length - 1;

        while (low <= high)
        {
            // Use low + (high - low) / 2 to avoid integer overflow
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (arr[mid] == target)
                return mid;          // Found — return index
            else if (arr[mid] < target)
                low = mid + 1;       // Target is in RIGHT half
            else
                high = mid - 1;      // Target is in LEFT half
        }

        return -1; // Not found
    }

    // --- Recursive Binary Search --- O(log n) time, O(log n) space (call stack)
    public static int SearchRecursive(int[] arr, int target, int low, int high)
    {
        if (low > high) return -1; // Base case: not found

        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (arr[mid] < target)  return SearchRecursive(arr, target, mid + 1, high);
        return SearchRecursive(arr, target, low, mid - 1);
    }

    // --- Lower Bound --- First index where arr[i] >= target
    // Useful for: finding insertion point, counting occurrences
    public static int LowerBound(int[] arr, int target)
    {
        int low = 0, high = arr.Length; // note: high = Length (open end)
        while (low < high)
        {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] < target)
                low = mid + 1;
            else
                high = mid; // don't exclude mid — it could be the answer
        }
        return low; // index of first element >= target
    }

    // --- Upper Bound --- First index where arr[i] > target
    public static int UpperBound(int[] arr, int target)
    {
        int low = 0, high = arr.Length;
        while (low < high)
        {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] <= target)
                low = mid + 1;
            else
                high = mid;
        }
        return low; // index of first element > target
    }

