## ⚡ Short Answer
Sorting algorithms vary widely in complexity: comparison-based sorts are bounded by O(n log n) in the best case, while non-comparison sorts like Radix or Counting Sort can achieve O(n). Space complexity ranges from O(1) for in-place sorts (like Heap Sort) to O(n) for sorts that require auxiliary storage (like Merge Sort). Knowing the trade-offs helps you pick the right algorithm for the data size, distribution, and constraints at hand.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A characterization of how sorting algorithms scale in time (number of operations) and space (memory usage) as input size n grows.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Choosing the wrong sorting algorithm can turn a fast app into an unusably slow one. Complexity analysis gives you a language-agnostic, implementation-agnostic way to reason about scalability before writing a single line of code.

**How it works internally:** Comparison-based sorts must inspect pairs of elements to determine order. Information theory proves you need at least log₂(n!) ≈ n log n comparisons in the worst case, establishing O(n log n) as the theoretical floor. Non-comparison sorts sidestep this by exploiting the structure of the data (e.g., integer range or digit count) to sort in linear time.

**Benefits:** Understanding complexity lets you justify algorithm choices in design reviews, predict bottlenecks, and avoid subtle performance regressions when data size grows unexpectedly.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Big-O hides constant factors. Quick Sort is O(n²) worst-case but beats Merge Sort in practice on random data due to cache locality and low constants. Always profile before optimizing.

## 💻 Code Example
```python
"""
SORTING ALGORITHM COMPLEXITY CHEAT SHEET
=========================================
Algorithm       | Best      | Average   | Worst     | Space  | Stable?
----------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|--------|--------
Bubble Sort     | O(n)      | O(n²)     | O(n²)     | O(1)   | Yes
Selection Sort  | O(n²)     | O(n²)     | O(n²)     | O(1)   | No
Insertion Sort  | O(n)      | O(n²)     | O(n²)     | O(1)   | Yes
Merge Sort      | O(n logn) | O(n logn) | O(n logn) | O(n)   | Yes
Quick Sort      | O(n logn) | O(n logn) | O(n²)     | O(logn)| No
Heap Sort       | O(n logn) | O(n logn) | O(n logn) | O(1)   | No
Counting Sort   | O(n+k)    | O(n+k)    | O(n+k)    | O(k)   | Yes
Radix Sort      | O(nk)     | O(nk)     | O(nk)     | O(n+k) | Yes
Tim Sort        | O(n)      | O(n logn) | O(n logn) | O(n)   | Yes

n = input size, k = range of values (Counting/Radix), logn space for Quick Sort's call stack
"""

# --- Merge Sort: O(n log n) time, O(n) space, Stable ---
def merge_sort(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    mid = len(arr) // 2
    left = merge_sort(arr[:mid])   # recurse left half
    right = merge_sort(arr[mid:])  # recurse right half
    return merge(left, right)

def merge(left, right):
    result = []
    i = j = 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i] <= right[j]:    # <= preserves stability
            result.append(left[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            result.append(right[j])
            j += 1
    result.extend(left[i:])
    result.extend(right[j:])
    return result


# --- Quick Sort: O(n log n) avg, O(n²) worst, O(log n) space ---
def quick_sort(arr, low=0, high=None):
    if high is None:
        high = len(arr) - 1
    if low < high:
        pivot_idx = partition(arr, low, high)
        quick_sort(arr, low, pivot_idx - 1)
        quick_sort(arr, pivot_idx + 1, high)

def partition(arr, low, high):
    pivot = arr[high]              # naive pivot; randomized pivot avoids O(n²)
    i = low - 1
    for j in range(low, high):
        if arr[j] <= pivot:
            i += 1
