## ⚡ Short Answer
Edit Distance (Levenshtein Distance) is the minimum number of single-character operations — insert, delete, or substitute — needed to transform one string into another. It's a classic dynamic programming problem solved in O(m×n) time and space, where m and n are the lengths of the two strings. The DP table builds up optimal subproblem solutions bottom-up, with each cell representing the edit cost between prefixes of both strings.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** A metric measuring how "different" two strings are, quantified as the minimum number of edits (insertions, deletions, substitutions) to convert string A into string B.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Needed wherever approximate string matching matters — spell checkers, DNA sequence alignment, diff tools, fuzzy search, autocorrect, plagiarism detection, and NLP similarity scoring. Exact matching is too brittle for real-world text.

**How it works internally:** Build a 2D DP table `dp[i][j]` = edit distance between the first `i` chars of `s1` and first `j` chars of `s2`. Base cases: `dp[0][j] = j` (insert j chars), `dp[i][0] = i` (delete i chars). Recurrence: if characters match, `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]`; otherwise `dp[i][j] = 1 + min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1], dp[i-1][j-1])` for delete, insert, and substitute respectively.

**Benefits:** Guaranteed optimal solution; works on any alphabet; well-understood mathematical properties; can reconstruct the actual edit sequence by backtracking the DP table.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** O(m×n) time and space can be prohibitive for very long strings (e.g., genomic sequences of millions of chars). Space can be reduced to O(min(m,n)) using two rolling rows, but time remains quadratic. For large-scale fuzzy search, approximate algorithms (BK-trees, SimHash, trigram indexes) are preferred.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
using System;

public class EditDistance
{
    // === Full O(m*n) DP — also reconstructs the edit path ===
    public static int Compute(string s1, string s2)
    {
        int m = s1.Length, n = s2.Length;

        // dp[i][j] = edit distance between s1[0..i-1] and s2[0..j-1]
        int[,] dp = new int[m + 1, n + 1];

        // Base cases: transforming to/from empty string
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i, 0] = i; // delete all chars of s1
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0, j] = j; // insert all chars of s2

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
        {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
            {
                if (s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1])
                {
                    // Characters match — no operation needed
                    dp[i, j] = dp[i - 1, j - 1];
                }
                else
                {
                    dp[i, j] = 1 + Math.Min(
                        dp[i - 1, j],     // Delete from s1
                        Math.Min(
                            dp[i, j - 1], // Insert into s1
                            dp[i - 1, j - 1] // Substitute
                        )
                    );
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[m, n];
    }

