## ⚡ Short Answer
IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS are the three primary cloud service models, each abstracting a different layer of infrastructure management away from the customer. IaaS gives you raw compute/storage/networking, PaaS adds a managed runtime so you focus on code, and SaaS delivers a fully managed application you simply use. Azure offers first-class examples of all three.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Three tiered cloud delivery models defined by how much of the stack the cloud provider manages versus the customer. They form a spectrum from "we manage hardware only" to "we manage everything."

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Before cloud, organizations had to buy, rack, cable, and maintain every server. These models let teams choose exactly how much undifferentiated heavy lifting they want to outsource, trading control for operational simplicity and speed.

**How it works internally:** Think of it as a shared-responsibility ladder. On-premises = you own everything. IaaS = provider owns physical hardware, networking fabric, and hypervisor; you own OS upward. PaaS = provider additionally owns OS, runtime, middleware, and scaling; you own app code and data. SaaS = provider owns the entire stack; you own only your data and user configuration.

**Benefits:** Each layer up the stack reduces ops burden, accelerates time-to-value, and shifts capital expenditure to operational expenditure. PaaS and SaaS in particular eliminate patching, scaling, and availability toil.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Higher abstraction = less control and potential vendor lock-in. IaaS gives maximum portability but maximum ops burden. PaaS can constrain runtime choices or max-out at certain scale patterns. SaaS offers zero infrastructure flexibility and data sovereignty concerns can arise.

