## ⚡ Short Answer
An Azure **subscription** is a billing and access boundary tied to an Azure account, a **management group** is a container for organizing multiple subscriptions with inherited policies, and a **resource group** is a logical container for related Azure resources within a subscription. Together they form a four-level hierarchy: Management Group → Subscription → Resource Group → Resource. Understanding this hierarchy is essential for organizing governance, cost management, and access control at scale.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:**
Azure uses a hierarchical structure to organize cloud resources. At the top sits the **Tenant (Azure AD/Entra ID)**, followed by **Management Groups**, then **Subscriptions**, then **Resource Groups**, and finally individual **Resources**.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:**
Large enterprises need to enforce consistent policies, control costs, and manage access across hundreds of teams and environments without manually configuring each resource. This hierarchy allows governance rules set at a high level to automatically cascade downward.

**How it works internally:**
- **Management Group:** A governance layer above subscriptions. You can nest up to 6 levels deep. Azure Policy and RBAC assigned here are inherited by all child subscriptions. Every tenant has one root management group.
- **Subscription:** The billing unit. All resource costs roll up to a subscription. It also acts as a trust boundary — each subscription trusts one Azure AD tenant. Common patterns are one subscription per environment (dev/staging/prod) or per business unit.
- **Resource Group:** A logical grouping of resources that share the same lifecycle. When you delete a resource group, all resources inside are deleted. Tags, RBAC, and policies can be applied at this level. Resources can only belong to one resource group at a time.

**Benefits:**
- Centralized governance via Azure Policy and RBAC inheritance
- Granular cost tracking and billing separation per subscription
- Lifecycle management — deploy and tear down environments atomically via resource groups
- Scalable access control without per-resource configuration

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
- Too many subscriptions increase administrative overhead and can hit subscription-level service limits
- Resource groups cannot span multiple subscriptions, which can complicate cross-subscription architectures
- Governance hierarchy changes (moving management groups) can be complex and slow in large orgs

