## ⚡ Short Answer
Azure Policy is a governance service that lets you define, assign, and enforce rules (policies) across your Azure resources to ensure they remain compliant with organizational standards. It works by evaluating resources against policy definitions and either auditing violations, denying non-compliant deployments, or automatically remediating drift — without requiring manual oversight.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Azure Policy is a rule-based governance engine built into Azure Resource Manager (ARM). You write policy definitions in JSON that express conditions (e.g., "all storage accounts must use HTTPS") and an effect (Audit, Deny, Append, Modify, DeployIfNotExists, AuditIfNotExists).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** In large enterprises with many teams and subscriptions, resource sprawl and configuration drift are inevitable. Azure Policy provides centralized, automated guardrails so that security, cost, and compliance requirements are enforced consistently across management groups, subscriptions, and resource groups — replacing manual reviews and tribal knowledge.

**How it works internally:** Policy definitions are assigned to a scope (management group → subscription → resource group). The Azure Policy engine hooks into the ARM control plane via a resource provider admission controller. On every create/update operation, ARM calls the policy engine synchronously; if a Deny effect matches, the request is rejected before the resource is created. Background compliance scans run periodically (every ~24 hours) to evaluate existing resources. Remediation tasks can trigger ARM deployments to fix non-compliant resources (via managed identity). Initiatives (policy sets) group multiple definitions for standards like CIS, NIST, or ISO 27001.

**Benefits:** Prevents non-compliant resources from being deployed (shift-left governance), scales across thousands of subscriptions via management groups, integrates with Microsoft Defender for Cloud for security posture scoring, supports built-in policies (thousands available) plus custom definitions, and provides a compliance dashboard with drill-down reporting.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Deny effects can block legitimate work if policies are overly broad — always start with Audit mode. DeployIfNotExists remediation requires a managed identity with contributor rights, which is an attack surface to manage. Policy evaluation lag (~24h) means the compliance dashboard is not real-time. Complex JSON authoring can be error-prone; use the Policy Aliases reference carefully.

