## ⚡ Short Answer
Azure **tags** are key-value metadata labels you attach to resources for organization, cost tracking, and automation — they have no effect on resource behavior or permissions. Azure **resource locks** are governance controls that prevent accidental deletion or modification of resources, regardless of RBAC permissions. Tags answer "what is this resource?", locks answer "can this resource be changed or deleted?"

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Tags are arbitrary key-value pairs (e.g., `Environment=Production`, `CostCenter=Finance`) attached to any Azure resource, resource group, or subscription. Locks are `ReadOnly` or `CanNotDelete` constraints applied at subscription, resource group, or individual resource scope.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Tags solve resource discoverability, cost allocation, and automation targeting at scale — essential when managing hundreds of resources across teams. Locks solve accidental or unauthorized destructive changes; even a Global Administrator can be blocked from deleting a locked resource without first removing the lock.

**How it works internally:** Tags are stored as metadata in Azure Resource Manager (ARM) and propagate into billing data and Azure Policy evaluations. They don't affect the control plane or data plane at all. Locks are enforced by ARM at the control plane level before any write or delete operation is evaluated — they sit above RBAC, meaning a user with `Owner` role still cannot delete a `CanNotDelete`-locked resource without first removing the lock (which itself requires `Microsoft.Authorization/locks/delete` permission).

**Benefits:**
Tags enable cost reporting by team/environment in Azure Cost Management, filtering in the portal, and are used by Azure Policy to enforce compliance. Locks provide a critical safety net for production infrastructure, preventing fat-finger or runaway automation from destroying stateful resources.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
Tags don't inherit automatically to child resources (a tag on a resource group does NOT automatically appear on its child resources unless enforced via Azure Policy's `inheritTagFromResourceGroup` initiative). Locks block ALL users including owners, so they must be managed carefully in CI/CD pipelines — automation that needs to delete/modify a resource must first remove the lock, adding complexity and a potential security gap window.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// Azure SDK for .NET — applying tags and locks via ARM client
using Azure.ResourceManager;
using Azure.ResourceManager.Resources;
using Azure.ResourceManager.Resources.Models;
using Azure.Identity;

var armClient = new ArmClient(new DefaultAzureCredential());

// ── 1. Apply Tags to a Resource Group ──────────────────────────────────────
string subscriptionId = "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000";
string resourceGroupName = "rg-production-eastus";

var resourceGroupResource = armClient
    .GetSubscriptionResource(SubscriptionResource.CreateResourceIdentifier(subscriptionId))
    .GetResourceGroups()
    .Get(resourceGroupName).Value;

// Tags are just a Dictionary<string, string> — no behavioral effect
var tags = new Dictionary<string, string>
{
    ["Environment"]  = "Production",
    ["CostCenter"]   = "Finance-42",
    ["Owner"]        = "platform-team@company.com",
    ["ManagedBy"]    = "Terraform",     // useful for drift detection
    ["DataClass"]    = "Confidential"
};

await resourceGroupResource.SetTagsAsync(tags);
Console.WriteLine("Tags applied successfully.");

// ── 2. Apply a CanNotDelete Lock on the same Resource Group ─────────────────
// Requires Microsoft.Authorization/locks/write permission
var lockCollection = resourceGroupResource.GetManagementLocks();

var lockData = new ManagementLockData(ManagementLockLevel.CanNotDelete)
{
    Notes = "Production resource group — deletion requires CAB approval and lock removal."
};

// Lock name is a logical identifier — make it self-documenting
await lockCollection.CreateOrUpdateAsync(
    waitUntil: Azure.WaitUntil.Completed,
    lockName:  "lock-no-delete-production",
    data:      lockData);

Console.WriteLine("CanNotDelete lock applied.");

// ── 3. ReadOnly Lock — blocks writes AND deletes (use sparingly) ────────────
// Useful for critical shared infrastructure (VNets, Key Vaults)
var readonlyLockData = new ManagementLockData(ManagementLockLevel.ReadOnly)
{
    Notes = "Core network — changes require architecture review board sign-off."
};

// Apply to a specific resource (e.g., a VNet), not the whole RG
// var vnetResource = ... (retrieve specific resource)
// await vnetResource.GetManagementLocks().CreateOrUpdateAsync(...);

// ── Key Behavioral Differences ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Tags:   metadata only, no access control, used for billing/policy/filtering
// Locks:  ARM-enforced, override RBAC, two levels:
