## ⚡ Short Answer
Azure Blob Storage is object storage for unstructured data (images, videos, backups), Azure Files provides managed SMB/NFS file shares mountable like a network drive, and Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 is Blob Storage enhanced with a hierarchical namespace for big data analytics workloads. Choosing between them depends on access patterns, protocol needs, and whether you need analytics-optimized directory semantics.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Three distinct Azure storage services: Blob (object store), Files (managed file share), and Data Lake Gen2 (analytics-optimized object store layered on Blob).

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Different workloads have fundamentally different storage requirements. REST-based cloud apps need cheap object storage; legacy apps and VMs need mountable drives with POSIX-like semantics; Hadoop/Spark analytics jobs need hierarchical directory operations with ACLs at scale. No single design satisfies all three efficiently.

**How it works internally:** Blob Storage partitions data into containers with flat key-value addressing (path is just part of the key). Azure Files runs on SMB 3.0 or NFS 4.1 protocols backed by Azure storage nodes, enabling OS-level mounting. Data Lake Gen2 enables a true hierarchical namespace as a first-class feature on top of Blob — meaning rename/delete of a directory is an O(1) atomic operation rather than copying thousands of blobs, which is critical for ACID-like commit patterns in Spark.

**Benefits:**
Blob — cheapest per GB, tiering (Hot/Cool/Cold/Archive), massive scale, CDN integration, lifecycle policies.
Files — drop-in network share, AD/LDAP auth, mountable on Windows/Linux/macOS, ideal for lift-and-shift.
Data Lake Gen2 — hierarchical namespace with POSIX ACLs, deep integration with Azure Synapse, Databricks, HDInsight, and massive parallel read performance.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
Blob — no mountable share, no fine-grained directory ACLs without Gen2.
Files — higher cost per GB than Blob, latency-sensitive at large scale, not optimized for analytics.
Data Lake Gen2 — cannot disable hierarchical namespace once enabled; some Blob APIs behave differently; adds complexity over plain Blob for simple workloads.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// Azure Storage SDK v12 — practical usage of all three services

using Azure.Storage.Blobs;
using Azure.Storage.Files.Shares;
using Azure.Storage.Files.DataLake;

// ─── 1. BLOB STORAGE ─── unstructured object storage
var blobServiceClient = new BlobServiceClient(connectionString);
var container = blobServiceClient.GetBlobContainerClient("images");
await container.CreateIfNotExistsAsync();

// Upload an image blob
var blobClient = container.GetBlobClient("profile/user123.jpg");
await using var stream = File.OpenRead("user123.jpg");
await blobClient.UploadAsync(stream, overwrite: true);
Console.WriteLine($"Blob URI: {blobClient.Uri}");

// ─── 2. AZURE FILES ─── mountable SMB share
var shareClient = new ShareClient(connectionString, "app-configs");
await shareClient.CreateIfNotExistsAsync();

var directoryClient = shareClient.GetDirectoryClient("prod");
await directoryClient.CreateIfNotExistsAsync();

// Write a config file — identical semantics to a local file share
var fileClient = directoryClient.GetFileClient("appsettings.json");
string config = """{ "FeatureFlags": { "DarkMode": true } }""";
await fileClient.CreateAsync(config.Length);
await fileClient.UploadRangeAsync(
    new Azure.HttpRange(0, config.Length),
    new MemoryStream(System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(config)));
Console.WriteLine("Config written to Azure Files share.");

// ─── 3. DATA LAKE STORAGE GEN2 ─── analytics workloads
var dataLakeServiceClient = new DataLakeServiceClient(
    new Uri($"https://{accountName}.dfs.core.windows.net"),
    new DefaultAzureCredential());

var fileSystemClient = dataLakeServiceClient.GetFileSystemClient("raw-data");
await fileSystemClient.CreateIfNotExistsAsync();

// O(1) atomic rename — impossible with plain Blob at scale
var srcDir  = fileSystemClient.GetDirectoryClient("staging/2024-01-15");
var destDir = fileSystemClient.GetDirectoryClient("processed/2024-01-15");

// Simulate Spark-style atomic commit: write to staging, rename to final
await srcDir.CreateIfNotExistsAsync();
var dataFile = srcDir.GetFileClient("part-00000.parquet");
await dataFile.CreateAsync();
// ... write parquet data
