## ⚡ Short Answer
Azure SQL Database is a fully managed PaaS database engine ideal for modern cloud-native apps with minimal administrative overhead. Azure SQL Managed Instance offers near-100% SQL Server compatibility for lift-and-shift migrations while still being fully managed. SQL Server on IaaS (Azure VMs) gives you full OS and SQL Server control at the cost of managing infrastructure yourself.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Three distinct deployment models for SQL Server workloads in Azure, each representing a different point on the control-vs-management tradeoff spectrum.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Organizations have varying needs — some want zero infrastructure management, others need legacy feature compatibility, and some require complete control over the database engine and OS for compliance or custom configurations.

**How it works internally:**
Azure SQL Database runs on shared/elastic infrastructure managed entirely by Microsoft; you interact only at the database level. Managed Instance runs on a dedicated virtual cluster inside your VNet with an instance-scoped surface area (SQL Agent, CLR, cross-database queries, linked servers). SQL Server on IaaS is a full SQL Server installation on an Azure VM — identical to on-premises, just running in the cloud.

**Benefits:**
Azure SQL DB offers the lowest TCO, automatic patching, built-in HA, serverless/elastic scaling, and hyperscale tiers. Managed Instance adds instance-level features (SQL Agent jobs, Service Broker, SSAS, cross-DB queries) with still-managed infrastructure. IaaS gives unrestricted access — any SQL version, any OS config, custom extensions, full DBA control.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
Azure SQL DB lacks instance-level features and some legacy SQL Server behaviors; some migration paths require refactoring. Managed Instance costs more than SQL DB, has higher minimum compute, and VNet integration adds networking complexity. IaaS requires you to handle patching, HA configuration (Always On, FCI), backups, and OS maintenance — highest operational burden.

## 💻 Code Example
