## ⚡ Short Answer
Azure Service Bus is a fully managed enterprise message broker that supports advanced messaging patterns like publish/subscribe, message sessions, dead-lettering, and exactly-once delivery. Azure Storage Queue is a simpler, cheaper queue built for basic FIFO workloads at massive scale. Choose Service Bus when you need reliability guarantees, ordering, or complex routing; choose Storage Queue when you need simple, cost-effective queueing with queues that can hold billions of messages.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Azure Service Bus is a PaaS messaging middleware supporting queues and topics (pub/sub). It lives in the Azure messaging namespace alongside Event Hubs and Event Grid. Storage Queue is a primitive queue service built on top of Azure Blob Storage infrastructure.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** Distributed systems need asynchronous decoupling. Service Bus solves enterprise-grade decoupling where message reliability, ordering, and routing matter. Storage Queue solves the simpler problem of "I just need a durable queue that scales to billions of messages cheaply."

**How it works internally:** Service Bus uses the AMQP 1.0 protocol over persistent TCP connections, maintaining a broker that tracks message lock tokens, session state, and delivery counts. Storage Queue is HTTP/REST-based, backed by Azure Storage, and uses visibility timeouts (not true locks) to hide messages during processing.

**Benefits:**
Service Bus gives you dead-letter queues (DLQ), message sessions for ordered per-entity processing, topics/subscriptions for fan-out, duplicate detection, transactions spanning multiple operations, and up to 256 KB–100 MB message sizes (Premium tier). Storage Queue gives you near-unlimited scale (unlimited queue depth), lower cost, and geo-redundancy via Azure Storage replication.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:** Service Bus is more expensive, has a max queue depth of 80 GB per entity, and adds operational complexity. Storage Queue has no pub/sub, no built-in DLQ, no message ordering guarantee, and a 64 KB message size limit. Storage Queue visibility timeout max is 7 days; messages expire after 7 days by default.

## 💻 Code Example
```csharp
// === AZURE SERVICE BUS — Send & Receive with Dead-Letter Handling ===
// NuGet: Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus

using Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus;

var connectionString = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("SB_CONNECTION_STRING");
var queueName = "orders";

await using var client = new ServiceBusClient(connectionString);

// --- SENDER ---
await using var sender = client.CreateSender(queueName);

// Send a message with custom properties for routing/filtering
var message = new ServiceBusMessage("{ \"orderId\": 42, \"amount\": 99.99 }")
{
    ContentType = "application/json",
    Subject = "new-order",
    MessageId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString(),       // Enables duplicate detection
    SessionId = "customer-123",                  // Enables ordered processing per customer
    TimeToLive = TimeSpan.FromHours(24)
};

await sender.SendMessageAsync(message);
Console.WriteLine("Message sent to Service Bus queue.");

// --- RECEIVER (with explicit lock and dead-lettering) ---
await using var receiver = client.CreateReceiver(queueName, new ServiceBusReceiverOptions
{
    ReceiveMode = ServiceBusReceiveMode.PeekLock  // Message locked, NOT deleted on receive
});

var received = await receiver.ReceiveMessageAsync(maxWaitTime: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));

if (received != null)
{
    try
    {
        var body = received.Body.ToString();
        Console.WriteLine($"Processing: {body}");

        // Simulate processing...
        bool success = ProcessOrder(body);

        if (success)
            await receiver.CompleteMessageAsync(received);  // Removes from queue
        else
            // Abandons — message becomes visible again, delivery count incremented
            await receiver.AbandonMessageAsync(received);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Unrecoverable error: {ex.Message}");
        // After MaxDeliveryCount exceeded, Service Bus auto-moves to DLQ
        await receiver.DeadLetterMessageAsync(received, "ProcessingFailed", ex.Message);
    }
}

// --- TOPICS & SUBSCRIPTIONS (pub/sub fan-out) ---
// Topic: "events", Subscription: "billing-service" (with SQL filter)
// Create via ARM/Bicep/Portal: filter WHERE Subject = 'new-order'
await using var topicSender = client.CreateSender("events");
await topicSender.SendMessageAsync(new ServiceBusMessage("payload") { Subject = "new-order" });
// Only subscriptions whose filter matches will receive this message

