## ⚡ Short Answer
Azure Event Hub is a high-throughput telemetry ingestion pipeline designed for massive streams of events (think logs, IoT, clickstreams). Azure Event Grid is a lightweight event routing service built for reactive, event-driven architectures using a pub/sub model. Azure Service Bus is an enterprise message broker for reliable, ordered, transactional message delivery between applications.

## 📖 Detailed Explanation
**What it is:** Three distinct Azure messaging services, each optimized for a different communication pattern — streaming, eventing, and messaging respectively.

**Why it exists / problem it solves:** No single messaging technology fits all scenarios. You need a firehose for telemetry, a smart router for discrete domain events, and a reliable broker for business-critical commands. Azure provides all three so architects can pick the right tool.

**How it works internally:**
Event Hub uses a partitioned consumer model backed by Apache Kafka-compatible protocol. Events are retained for 1–90 days and consumers read at their own pace using offsets — it never "delivers" to you, you pull. Event Grid uses a push model: publishers send events to topics, and Grid fans them out to registered subscribers (webhooks, Azure Functions, Logic Apps, etc.) with at-least-once delivery and built-in retry. Service Bus uses traditional AMQP-based queues and topics with broker-side message locking, dead-letter queues, sessions for ordering, and full transaction support.

**Benefits:**
Event Hub — millions of events/sec, cheap per-event cost, replay via retention, Kafka compatibility.
Event Grid — serverless, zero infrastructure, automatic fan-out, near-real-time (sub-second latency), pay-per-event.
Service Bus — guaranteed ordering, transactions, duplicate detection, dead-lettering, sessions, 256 KB–100 MB message size.

**Drawbacks / trade-offs:**
Event Hub — not for individual message processing; consumers must manage offsets; no per-message TTL or dead-lettering.
Event Grid — max event size 1 MB; not suited for high-throughput pipelines; no message ordering guarantees.
Service Bus — more expensive; higher latency than Event Grid; overkill for simple notifications.

